window.BIO004_DUMP_PRACTICE = {"version":1,"modules":[{"n":1,"title":"Module 1. Foundations, Cells, Tissues and Skin","topics":[{"id":"m1-terminology","title":"Language of anatomy: position, directions, planes, regions","mw":"2026-08-17","tr":"2026-08-18","prompts":[{"id":"m1-terminology-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[2,4],"cids":["w1-anatomical-position","w1-directional-terms"],"t":"Directional terms, paired","p":"List every directional term you can and write its opposite beside it. Then draw a stick figure in anatomical position and mark four of your pairs on it.","k":[{"pt":"Superior and inferior","why":"Superior means toward the head and inferior means away from it, so the pair describes up and down on a body in anatomical position."},{"pt":"Anterior and posterior (ventral and dorsal)","why":"Anterior is toward the front and posterior is toward the back, and ventral and dorsal are the same pair by another name."},{"pt":"Medial and lateral","why":"Both are measured from the midline, the imaginary line down the center of the body, so medial is closer to it and lateral is farther away."},{"pt":"Proximal and distal","why":"These compare distance from where a limb attaches to the trunk, which is why they are used on the limbs only."},{"pt":"Superficial and deep","why":"This pair is about depth from the body surface, so the skin is superficial to the muscle under it."},{"pt":"Any extra pair counts: central and peripheral, ipsilateral and contralateral, cranial and caudal","why":"Ipsilateral means the same side and contralateral means the opposite side, which matters when an injury on one side of the brain shows up on the other side of the body."},{"pt":"Figure standing upright, facing forward, arms at the sides, palms forward, feet flat and slightly apart","why":"Every directional term assumes anatomical position, so the figure has to be in it before any arrow on it means anything."}]},{"id":"m1-terminology-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[3,10],"cids":["w1-planes-sections","w1-abdominopelvic-map"],"t":"Planes and abdominal divisions","p":"Draw the three anatomical planes on a body outline. Name each one and write the two parts it divides the body into. Then draw the four abdominal quadrants and name them.","k":[{"pt":"Sagittal plane, including midsagittal (on the midline) and parasagittal (off the midline)","why":"Every sagittal plane is vertical and splits left from right, and only the midsagittal one makes two equal halves."},{"pt":"Frontal or coronal plane","why":"It is vertical too, but it runs side to side, so it separates the front of the body from the back."},{"pt":"Transverse plane","why":"It is the one horizontal plane, so it separates superior from inferior and gives you a cross section, the view a CT slice shows."},{"pt":"What each cut divides: left and right, anterior and posterior, superior and inferior","why":"Naming the two parts a plane creates is how you prove you know which way the cut runs."},{"pt":"Four quadrants named RUQ, LUQ, RLQ, LLQ","why":"The quadrants are named from the patient's right and left, because anatomical position fixes the patient's sides, not yours."},{"pt":"Quadrant lines drawn as a midsagittal line and a transverse line through the umbilicus","why":"Two planes crossing at the navel make the four quadrants, which is why the umbilicus is the center of the grid."}]},{"id":"m1-terminology-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[5],"cids":["w1-regional-terms"],"t":"Regional terms mind map","p":"Draw a body outline. Put anterior regional terms down the left side and posterior terms down the right, with a line from each name to the spot it points to.","k":[{"pt":"Anterior terms such as frontal, orbital, buccal, cervical, thoracic, abdominal, inguinal, patellar, tarsal","why":"These are front view regions, and each word names one spot, for example buccal is the cheek and inguinal is the groin."},{"pt":"Posterior terms such as occipital, scapular, vertebral, lumbar, gluteal, popliteal, sural, calcaneal","why":"These are back view regions, for example popliteal is the back of the knee, sural is the calf, and calcaneal is the heel."},{"pt":"Cervical shows up on both sides of the page","why":"The neck can be seen from the front and from the back, so the same term belongs in both lists."},{"pt":"Each line lands on the correct spot, not just the general area","why":"Regional terms are tested by pointing on a model and on yourself, so a line to the wrong spot is a wrong answer even if the word is right."}]},{"id":"m1-terminology-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[1],"cids":["w1-levels-organization"],"t":"Levels of organization","p":"Write the levels of structural organization from smallest to largest and give one anatomy example at each level.","k":[{"pt":"Chemical, cellular, tissue, organ, organ system, organism, in that order","why":"Each level is built from the one before it, so the order runs from atoms up to the whole person."},{"pt":"A chemical example such as water or a protein","why":"Atoms and molecules are the smallest level, the raw material every cell is built from."},{"pt":"A cell example such as a keratinocyte or a neuron, and a tissue example such as stratified squamous epithelium","why":"A tissue is a group of similar cells doing one job, which is the step up from a single cell."},{"pt":"An organ example such as the skin or the stomach","why":"An organ has two or more tissue types working on one defined job, so a single tissue on its own is not an organ."},{"pt":"An organ system named in full, such as the integumentary system, not just the word system","why":"Naming the system shows you know which organs work together toward one broad function."}]},{"id":"m1-terminology-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[3],"cids":["w1-planes-sections"],"t":"Planes and the parts they make","p":"Make a table of the six planes: sagittal, midsagittal, parasagittal, frontal, transverse, and oblique. For each, write whether it is vertical, horizontal, or angled, and the two parts it divides the body into. Then circle the two that are easiest to mix up and write the difference.","k":[{"pt":"Sagittal: vertical, divides left and right parts","why":"Sagittal is the family name for every vertical cut that separates left from right."},{"pt":"Midsagittal (median): vertical on the midline, equal left and right halves","why":"Because it runs exactly on the midline, it is the only cut that makes the two halves equal."},{"pt":"Parasagittal: vertical off the midline, unequal left and right parts","why":"This cut sits beside the midline, so the two parts come out different sizes."},{"pt":"Frontal (coronal): vertical, anterior and posterior portions","why":"It runs from side to side, so it separates the front of the body from the back."},{"pt":"Transverse (horizontal): superior and inferior portions, a cross section","why":"It is the one horizontal cut, which is why it shows a structure in cross section."},{"pt":"Oblique: an angle other than 90 degrees, angled portions","why":"Any cut that is neither straight up and down nor flat across counts as oblique."},{"pt":"The circled pair, usually midsagittal and parasagittal, with the difference written","why":"Both split left from right, so the only difference is whether the cut sits on the midline and makes equal halves."}]},{"id":"m1-terminology-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[5],"cids":["w1-regional-terms"],"t":"Axial or appendicular","p":"Write these regional terms and say where each one is on your body: brachial, thoracic, popliteal, vertebral, carpal, cervical, femoral, umbilical. Then sort them into axial and appendicular, and write the rule you used.","k":[{"pt":"Axial: thoracic (chest), vertebral (spinal column), cervical (neck), umbilical (navel)","why":"The axial region is the head, neck, and trunk, and each of these sits on that central axis."},{"pt":"Appendicular: brachial (upper arm), popliteal (back of the knee), carpal (wrist), femoral (thigh)","why":"The appendicular region is the limbs, and each of these names a spot on an arm or a leg."},{"pt":"Rule written: axial is the head, neck, and trunk, and appendicular is the limbs","why":"Once you know the rule, any new term can be placed by asking whether it is on a limb."},{"pt":"Each term matched to the right spot on the body","why":"A term you cannot point to on yourself is only a word, and lab asks you to point."}]},{"id":"m1-terminology-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[3],"cids":["w1-planes-sections"],"t":"Read the scan","p":"A scan of the head shows both eyes and both ears in the same image, with the top of the skull missing. A second image of the arm shows one round bone ringed by muscle. Name the plane for each image and explain how you know. Then say which plane you would choose to see the front of the heart against the sternum.","k":[{"pt":"Head image: transverse plane","why":"A horizontal cut at eye and ear level catches both eyes and both ears in one slice, and everything above the cut is gone."},{"pt":"Arm image: transverse plane, a cross section","why":"A round bone ringed by muscle is what a limb looks like when it is cut straight across."},{"pt":"Heart and sternum: a sagittal (midsagittal) plane","why":"A sagittal cut runs from front to back, so the sternum and the heart behind it show in the same image."},{"pt":"Frontal plane ruled out for the heart question","why":"A frontal cut separates front from back, so it puts the sternum and the heart in different slices."},{"pt":"Each answer backed by the two parts the plane divides","why":"Saying what the plane separates, superior from inferior or left from right, is the proof that you picked the right one."}]},{"id":"m1-terminology-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[2,4,5],"cids":["w1-anatomical-position","w1-directional-terms","w1-regional-terms"],"t":"Describe it like a chart","p":"A patient is lying face down on a stretcher with a cut on the back of the forearm close to the wrist and a bruise on the right side of the chest. Name how the patient is lying. Then describe both injuries using directional and regional terms so any clinician would find them, and explain why lying face down does not change your words.","k":[{"pt":"Prone","why":"Prone is lying face down and supine is lying face up."},{"pt":"Cut described as on the posterior, distal forearm (antebrachial region)","why":"Antebrachial names the forearm, posterior names its back surface, and distal says the cut is closer to the wrist than to the elbow."},{"pt":"Distal is allowed here because the forearm is a limb","why":"Proximal and distal are used on the limbs only, so they fit the forearm but would be wrong on the chest."},{"pt":"Bruise described as on the right thoracic region","why":"Thoracic is the chest, and right always means the patient's right, never the viewer's."},{"pt":"Words are based on anatomical position, not on how the patient is lying","why":"Every directional term assumes anatomical position, so the description stays the same whether the patient is standing, supine, or prone."}]}]},{"id":"m1-cavities","title":"Body cavities, membranes and abdominal quadrants","mw":"2026-08-17","tr":"2026-08-18","prompts":[{"id":"m1-cavities-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[6,7],"cids":["w1-body-cavities","w1-serous-membranes"],"t":"Cavities from the outside in","p":"Dump the body cavities from the outside in. Name each cavity, what it holds, and which membrane lines it.","k":[{"pt":"Dorsal (posterior) cavity: cranial cavity with the brain, vertebral (spinal) cavity with the spinal cord","why":"The dorsal cavity runs along the back of the body, and its two parts hold the brain and spinal cord."},{"pt":"Ventral (anterior) cavity: thoracic cavity with the heart and lungs, abdominopelvic cavity with the abdominal and pelvic organs","why":"The ventral cavity holds the organs of the trunk, split into a chest part and an abdominal and pelvic part."},{"pt":"Thoracic cavity subdivided into the pleural cavities, the pericardial cavity, and the mediastinum","why":"Each lung sits in a pleural cavity, the heart sits in the pericardial cavity, and the mediastinum is the central compartment between the lungs."},{"pt":"Diaphragm separating the thoracic cavity from the abdominopelvic cavity","why":"The diaphragm tells you which membrane you are dealing with: pleura and pericardium above it, peritoneum below it."},{"pt":"Membranes named: pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum in the ventral cavity, and the meninges around the brain and spinal cord in the dorsal cavity","why":"Each part of the ventral cavity has its own serous membrane, and the meninges wrap the contents of the dorsal cavity, which is why meningitis is a dorsal cavity problem."},{"pt":"Parietal layer on the wall, visceral layer on the organ","why":"Parietal goes with the wall and visceral goes with the organ, and the meaning never changes from one cavity to the next."}]},{"id":"m1-cavities-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[7],"cids":["w1-serous-membranes"],"t":"Serous membranes","p":"Draw a fist pushed into a balloon. Label the parietal layer, the visceral layer, and the cavity. Then name the three serous membranes and the organ each one wraps.","k":[{"pt":"Visceral layer drawn against the fist","why":"The layer that touches the organ is visceral, the way the balloon touches your fist."},{"pt":"Parietal layer drawn as the outer layer, against the body wall","why":"The parietal layer lines the inner body wall, so it is the outside of the balloon."},{"pt":"Cavity labeled between the two layers, holding serous fluid","why":"The fluid in this space reduces friction so the organ can move against the wall without damage."},{"pt":"Pleura wrapping the lungs","why":"Parietal and visceral pleura surround each lung, with pleural fluid in the pleural cavity between them."},{"pt":"Pericardium wrapping the heart","why":"Parietal and visceral pericardium surround the heart, with pericardial fluid between them."},{"pt":"Peritoneum lining the abdomen and its organs","why":"Parietal and visceral peritoneum line the abdominal wall and cover the organs, with peritoneal fluid between them."}]},{"id":"m1-cavities-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[10],"cids":["w1-abdominopelvic-map"],"t":"Nine regions, filled in","p":"Draw the nine abdominopelvic regions in their grid, name every one, and write one organ that sits in each.","k":[{"pt":"Top row: right hypochondriac, epigastric, left hypochondriac","why":"The top row sits just under the ribs, with the epigastric region in the middle over the stomach."},{"pt":"Middle row: right lumbar, umbilical, left lumbar","why":"The umbilical region is centered on the navel, with a lumbar region on each side of it."},{"pt":"Bottom row: right iliac (inguinal), hypogastric, left iliac (inguinal)","why":"The bottom row sits over the pelvis, with the hypogastric region in the middle over the urinary bladder."},{"pt":"Right and left labeled from the patient's point of view","why":"The grid is drawn on the patient in anatomical position, so the right hypochondriac region is on the patient's right even though it is on your left when you face them."},{"pt":"An organ in each box, for example liver and gallbladder, stomach, spleen, ascending colon, small intestine, descending colon, cecum and appendix, urinary bladder, sigmoid colon","why":"A real organ in each box is what makes the grid useful, because pain over a region points to the organ under it."}]},{"id":"m1-cavities-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[8],"cids":["w1-mediastinum"],"t":"The mediastinum","p":"Draw the thorax in cross section. Mark the mediastinum, name what forms each of its borders, and list everything inside it.","k":[{"pt":"Mediastinum placed between the two lungs, in the center of the thoracic cavity","why":"It is the central compartment of the chest, so it sits between the two pleural cavities."},{"pt":"Anterior border: sternum. Posterior border: vertebral column","why":"The mediastinum runs from the breastbone in front to the spine behind."},{"pt":"Superior border: thoracic inlet. Inferior border: diaphragm","why":"It opens at the top where the chest meets the neck and is closed below by the diaphragm."},{"pt":"Lateral borders: the lungs","why":"The lungs wall the mediastinum in from each side."},{"pt":"Contents: heart, trachea, esophagus, major blood vessels, thymus","why":"These are the structures packed between the two lungs, including the tubes for air and food on their way through the chest."}]},{"id":"m1-cavities-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[9],"cids":["w1-peritoneum-relationships"],"t":"Behind the peritoneum","p":"List every retroperitoneal structure you can. Then write two organs that are intraperitoneal and say what the difference means for where they sit.","k":[{"pt":"Suprarenal (adrenal) glands, aorta and inferior vena cava, duodenum (second and third parts)","why":"These are the S, A, and D of SAD PUCKER, the mnemonic for structures behind the peritoneum."},{"pt":"Pancreas (except the tail), ureters, ascending and descending colon","why":"These are the P, U, and C, and the exception matters, because the tail of the pancreas is not on the list."},{"pt":"Kidneys, esophagus, rectum","why":"These finish the mnemonic with K, E, and R, so the kidneys sit behind the peritoneum against the back wall."},{"pt":"Two intraperitoneal organs, such as the stomach and the spleen","why":"An intraperitoneal organ is fully wrapped in visceral peritoneum and hangs inside the peritoneal cavity, so it will not be on the SAD PUCKER list."},{"pt":"Retroperitoneal means behind the peritoneum and in front of the spine","why":"That is the space the word names, so a retroperitoneal organ sits against the posterior body wall instead of hanging in the cavity."},{"pt":"The difference decides the surgical approach and how an infection or a bleed spreads","why":"Where an organ sits relative to the lining decides the route a surgeon takes in and the path an infection or bleed can follow."}]},{"id":"m1-cavities-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[10],"cids":["w1-abdominopelvic-map"],"t":"Quadrants or regions","p":"Draw one abdomen. Put the four quadrant lines on it in one color and the nine region lines in another. Write how many planes draw each grid, and say when a clinician would use quadrants and when they would use regions.","k":[{"pt":"Quadrants drawn with two planes: a midsagittal plane and a transverse plane through the umbilicus","why":"Two crossing lines make four boxes, and they cross at the navel."},{"pt":"Regions drawn with four planes, two vertical and two horizontal","why":"Two vertical and two horizontal lines make a three by three grid, which is nine regions."},{"pt":"Both grids labeled on the same abdomen","why":"Drawing them together shows how each large quadrant overlaps several smaller regions."},{"pt":"Quadrants used as fast clinical shorthand, such as describing pain in a triage note","why":"Four big areas are quick to say and easy to agree on when speed matters."},{"pt":"Regions used when a location has to be described exactly","why":"Nine smaller areas pin a location down more precisely, which is why they are the anatomical version."},{"pt":"Liver and gallbladder placed in the RUQ and in the right hypochondriac region","why":"Placing one organ on both grids shows that the two systems describe the same body at different levels of detail."}]},{"id":"m1-cavities-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[7,10],"cids":["w1-serous-membranes","w1-abdominopelvic-map"],"t":"A ruptured appendix","p":"A patient has sharp pain low on the right side of the abdomen, and the appendix has ruptured. Name the quadrant and the region where the pain sits, the membrane that is now involved, and the condition that results. Then explain why the problem spreads across the abdomen instead of staying in one spot.","k":[{"pt":"Right lower quadrant (RLQ)","why":"The appendix and cecum are the key organs of the right lower quadrant."},{"pt":"Right iliac (inguinal) region","why":"On the nine-region grid, the cecum and appendix sit in the lower right box."},{"pt":"The peritoneum, both the visceral and the parietal layers","why":"The appendix sits in the abdominopelvic cavity, so its contents spill into the space lined by peritoneum."},{"pt":"Peritonitis","why":"Each serous membrane has a matching inflammation, and inflamed peritoneum is peritonitis."},{"pt":"It spreads because the peritoneal cavity is a closed space with nowhere to drain","why":"Whatever enters the cavity can travel through the whole space, so the problem covers the abdomen instead of staying local."}]},{"id":"m1-cavities-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[9,10],"cids":["w1-peritoneum-relationships","w1-abdominopelvic-map"],"t":"Reaching the kidney","p":"A surgeon plans an operation on the right kidney and wants to stay out of the peritoneal cavity. Say whether the kidney sits inside the peritoneal cavity or behind it, name the term, name the quadrant it sits in, and list three other structures in the same space. Then name one organ the surgeon could not reach without opening the peritoneal cavity.","k":[{"pt":"Behind it: the kidney is retroperitoneal","why":"The kidneys are the K in SAD PUCKER, so they lie behind the peritoneum against the back wall."},{"pt":"Retroperitoneum is the space behind the peritoneum and in front of the spine","why":"Knowing the space tells the surgeon the route, coming in from behind without opening the peritoneal cavity."},{"pt":"Right kidney in the right upper quadrant","why":"The lab list places the right kidney in the RUQ along with the liver and gallbladder."},{"pt":"Three other retroperitoneal structures, such as the suprarenal glands, ureters, aorta, inferior vena cava, or ascending colon","why":"Any structure from SAD PUCKER shares this same space behind the peritoneum."},{"pt":"An intraperitoneal organ such as the stomach or spleen","why":"An intraperitoneal organ is wrapped in visceral peritoneum and hangs inside the cavity, so every route to it passes through the lining."}]}]},{"id":"m1-cell","title":"Anatomy of the cell","mw":"2026-08-19","tr":"2026-08-20","prompts":[{"id":"m1-cell-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[14],"cids":["w1-organelles"],"t":"Organelle inventory","p":"List every organelle you can name and write one job beside each one. Do not stop to make it pretty.","k":[{"pt":"Nucleus and its nucleolus","why":"The nucleus holds the DNA and directs the cell, and the nucleolus inside it assembles ribosomal subunits."},{"pt":"Ribosomes, free and bound to the rough ER","why":"Ribosomes are where proteins are built, and the ones on the rough ER make proteins for export or for membranes."},{"pt":"Rough ER, smooth ER, Golgi apparatus","why":"Rough ER makes membrane and secretory proteins, smooth ER makes lipids and stores calcium, and the Golgi modifies, sorts, and packages."},{"pt":"Lysosomes and peroxisomes","why":"Lysosomes digest worn organelles and pathogens, and peroxisomes break down fatty acids and hydrogen peroxide."},{"pt":"Mitochondria, cytoskeleton, centrioles","why":"Mitochondria make ATP, the cytoskeleton gives support and movement, and centrioles help form the mitotic spindle."},{"pt":"A job written beside every organelle, even a rough one","why":"The job is the point of the list, because every organelle is a structure with a job and a disease when that job fails."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[11,14],"cids":["w1-generalized-cell","w1-organelles"],"t":"Draw the cell","p":"Draw a cell and label everything you can from memory. Then circle the three structures a protein passes through on its way out of the cell.","k":[{"pt":"Plasma membrane, nucleus, and cytoplasm all recognizable","why":"These are the three regions of every generalized cell, and every other label belongs to one of them."},{"pt":"At least eight structures labeled","why":"Eight labels means you went past the obvious ones into ER, Golgi, lysosomes, peroxisomes, ribosomes, or centrioles."},{"pt":"Organelles drawn in the cytoplasm, outside the nucleus","why":"The cytoplasm is everything inside the membrane except the nucleus, so that is where the organelles belong."},{"pt":"Circled: rough ER, Golgi apparatus, secretory vesicle","why":"These are the stations of the way out: rough ER builds the protein, the Golgi packages it, and the vesicle carries it to the membrane."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[13],"cids":["w1-nucleus"],"t":"The nucleus in detail","p":"Draw the nucleus enlarged and label every part. Then write what the nuclear envelope is continuous with.","k":[{"pt":"Nuclear envelope drawn as a double membrane","why":"Two layers of membrane separate the nucleus from the cytoplasm and keep a controlled space inside."},{"pt":"Nuclear pores through the envelope","why":"The pores are protein-lined channels that let RNA and proteins move between the nucleus and the cytoplasm."},{"pt":"Nucleolus as a dense region","why":"The nucleolus is where ribosomal RNA is made and ribosomal subunits are assembled."},{"pt":"Chromatin and nucleoplasm","why":"Chromatin is DNA wrapped on histone proteins, and the nucleoplasm is the fluid that supports it and the nucleolus."},{"pt":"Envelope continuous with the rough ER","why":"The envelope connects to the rough ER, which is why the nuclear envelope counts as part of the endomembrane system."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[14],"cids":["w1-organelles"],"t":"Inside a mitochondrion","p":"Draw a mitochondrion cut open and label everything. Then write two features that make it unlike any other organelle.","k":[{"pt":"Outer membrane and inner membrane, a double membrane","why":"The outer membrane is the wall of the organelle, and the inner membrane is the one that folds into cristae."},{"pt":"Cristae, the folds of the inner membrane","why":"The folds multiply the surface area available for making ATP."},{"pt":"Its own DNA","why":"Having its own DNA is what makes the mitochondrion unlike every other organelle, since it can make some of its own proteins."},{"pt":"Its own ribosomes","why":"Mitochondrial ribosomes build the proteins specific to mitochondrial work."},{"pt":"Job written: makes ATP","why":"The whole structure is built around ATP production, which is why the busiest tissues fail first when mitochondria fail."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[12],"cids":["w1-plasma-membrane"],"t":"The plasma membrane","p":"Dump everything you know about the plasma membrane: what it is built from, what sits in it, and what can cross it without help.","k":[{"pt":"Phospholipid bilayer, hydrophilic heads facing out and hydrophobic tails facing in","why":"The heads face the watery fluid inside and outside the cell, and the tails hide from water in the middle."},{"pt":"Cholesterol between the tails","why":"Cholesterol gives the membrane stability and moderates its fluidity as temperature changes."},{"pt":"Integral proteins spanning the bilayer, peripheral proteins on the surface","why":"Integral proteins pass all the way through and peripheral proteins sit on one face, and both do transport, enzyme, and signaling work."},{"pt":"Glycoproteins and glycolipids with sugar chains facing out","why":"These carbohydrate tags are used for cell recognition and immune responses, so they face the outside."},{"pt":"Oxygen, carbon dioxide, and small lipid-soluble molecules cross freely","why":"They slip through the fatty middle of the bilayer, while charged ions and large molecules need a transport protein."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L1-6","lv":1,"comps":[12,14],"cids":["w1-plasma-membrane","w1-organelles"],"t":"Cytoskeleton and cell surface","p":"List the three cytoskeletal elements with one job each. Then name every motile or surface projection you can and write where you would find it.","k":[{"pt":"Microfilaments, made of actin, for cell shape and movement","why":"Actin filaments are the part of the cytoskeleton that changes the shape of the cell and moves it."},{"pt":"Intermediate filaments for mechanical support, resisting tension","why":"They are the part that holds the cell together when it is pulled."},{"pt":"Microtubules, made of tubulin, forming centrioles, cilia, and flagella","why":"Tubulin tubes are the building material of the moving surface structures, so this branch connects to the second half of the prompt."},{"pt":"Cilia in the respiratory tract","why":"Cilia move substances such as mucus across the cell surface, which is exactly what the airway lining needs."},{"pt":"A flagellum on sperm","why":"A flagellum is long and propels the whole cell, and sperm is the human example."},{"pt":"Microvilli on the lining of the digestive tract","why":"Microvilli sit on the free surface of the simple columnar cells that absorb in the digestive tract."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L1-7","lv":1,"comps":[12],"cids":["w1-plasma-membrane"],"t":"Membrane proteins by job","p":"List the jobs membrane proteins do and draw a quick example of each sitting in a bilayer.","k":[{"pt":"Transport: channels and transport proteins","why":"Charged ions and large molecules cannot cross the bilayer alone, so proteins give them a way through."},{"pt":"Receptors for signals","why":"A receptor sits in the membrane where it can meet a signal arriving from outside the cell."},{"pt":"Enzymes","why":"Some membrane proteins carry out chemical reactions right at the membrane."},{"pt":"Cell identity markers, the glycoproteins","why":"Sugar-tagged proteins let cells recognize each other and take part in immune responses."},{"pt":"Integral and peripheral positions both shown in the drawing","why":"Integral proteins span the bilayer and peripheral proteins bind to its surface, so the sketch should show both."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L1-8","lv":1,"comps":[14],"cids":["w1-organelles"],"t":"Centrosome and spindle","p":"Draw a centrosome and label its parts. Then sketch what it does during cell division.","k":[{"pt":"Centrioles drawn as short cylinders","why":"Centrioles are the short cylindrical structures at the centrosome."},{"pt":"Microtubules in a 9 plus 0 arrangement","why":"Nine groups around the edge with none in the center is the centriole pattern."},{"pt":"Centrosome named as the microtubule-organizing center","why":"Its job is organizing microtubules, not moving the cell."},{"pt":"Mitotic spindle drawn forming from it during division","why":"Centrioles and the centrosome are essential for building the spindle used in cell division."},{"pt":"9 plus 0 contrasted with the 9 plus 2 of cilia and flagella","why":"The 9 plus 0 pattern organizes and does not move, while 9 plus 2 is the pattern that moves."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[13,14],"cids":["w1-nucleus","w1-organelles"],"t":"Protein route, in order","p":"Write the route a protein takes from the gene to the outside of the cell, naming every structure it passes through in order. Then list every membrane-bound organelle you can.","k":[{"pt":"DNA in the nucleus copied into mRNA","why":"The nucleus is the site of transcription, the conversion of DNA to RNA."},{"pt":"mRNA leaves through a nuclear pore","why":"The pores are the channels that let RNA pass from the nucleus into the cytoplasm."},{"pt":"A ribosome on the rough ER builds the protein","why":"Bound ribosomes make proteins meant for secretion or for a membrane."},{"pt":"A transport vesicle carries it to the Golgi apparatus","why":"Vesicles move material between organelles, so the protein rides in one from the ER to the Golgi."},{"pt":"The Golgi packages it into a secretory vesicle that reaches the plasma membrane","why":"The Golgi modifies and tags the protein, and the vesicle carries it to the membrane for release."},{"pt":"Membrane-bound list: nucleus, ER, Golgi, lysosomes, peroxisomes, mitochondria, vesicles","why":"Ribosomes are not on this list, because they are built of RNA and protein with no membrane around them."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[13],"cids":["w1-nucleus"],"t":"Nucleus at the center","p":"Draw a mind map with the nucleus in the middle. Branch out to every structure it directs or supplies, with an arrow on each branch showing which way the material moves.","k":[{"pt":"DNA to mRNA to ribosome, with arrows pointing out of the nucleus","why":"Instructions are copied from DNA into RNA in the nucleus, and the RNA leaves for the ribosome where proteins are made."},{"pt":"Nucleolus branching to ribosomal subunits","why":"The nucleolus builds ribosomal subunits, so the nucleus supplies the very machinery that reads its messages."},{"pt":"Nuclear pores as the exit and entry point","why":"Material moving between nucleus and cytoplasm passes through the pores, RNA going out and proteins coming in."},{"pt":"Chromatin branching to chromosomes during cell division","why":"Chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes when the cell divides."},{"pt":"An arrow on every branch, not bare lines","why":"The arrows show which way material moves, which is the part that proves you understand the map."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[14],"cids":["w1-organelles"],"t":"The endomembrane system","p":"Name every organelle in the endomembrane system and draw arrows showing how material moves from one to the next.","k":[{"pt":"Nuclear envelope, rough ER, smooth ER, Golgi apparatus, vesicles, lysosomes","why":"These are the members of the endomembrane system, the network that makes, modifies, and moves proteins and lipids."},{"pt":"Plasma membrane as the end point","why":"Secretory vesicles deliver their contents to the plasma membrane, where material leaves the cell."},{"pt":"Transport vesicles drawn between the stations","why":"Vesicles are how material travels from one organelle to the next."},{"pt":"Arrows from rough ER to Golgi, then Golgi to vesicle to plasma membrane","why":"That is the secretory pathway, the route a protein takes from where it is made to where it leaves."},{"pt":"Mitochondria and peroxisomes left out on purpose","why":"They are organelles, but they are not part of this production and shipping network."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[14],"cids":["w1-organelles"],"t":"Tell them apart","p":"One line each, no notes: rough ER, smooth ER, Golgi, lysosome, peroxisome, mitochondrion. Say what makes each one different from the others.","k":[{"pt":"Rough ER: studded with ribosomes, makes membrane and secretory proteins","why":"The ribosomes on its surface are why it can build proteins, and their absence is why smooth ER cannot."},{"pt":"Smooth ER: no ribosomes, makes lipids, detoxifies drugs, stores calcium","why":"A cell that detoxifies drugs would be rich in smooth ER, which is how its job shows up in a real cell."},{"pt":"Golgi: modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and lipids","why":"It is the shipping center, tagging each molecule for where it needs to go."},{"pt":"Lysosome: digests macromolecules, damaged organelles, and pathogens","why":"It holds hydrolytic enzymes, so it is the cleanup and recycling organelle."},{"pt":"Peroxisome: breaks down fatty acids, amino acids, and hydrogen peroxide, and detoxifies alcohol","why":"Liver and kidney cells are rich in peroxisomes because those organs detoxify alcohol."},{"pt":"Mitochondrion: makes ATP, double membrane with cristae, its own DNA","why":"Its own DNA and folded inner membrane make it unlike anything else on this list."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L2-5","lv":2,"comps":[12],"cids":["w1-plasma-membrane"],"t":"Three surface extensions","p":"Draw microvilli, cilia, and a flagellum side by side on three cells. Label each, write its microtubule arrangement if it has one, write what it does, and name a location.","k":[{"pt":"Cilia: short, many per cell, 9 plus 2 microtubules","why":"The 9 plus 2 arrangement is what lets cilia move."},{"pt":"Cilia move substances such as mucus across the surface of the respiratory tract","why":"The cell stays put and the cilia sweep material past it, which is why they line the airway."},{"pt":"Flagellum: long, single, the same 9 plus 2 arrangement","why":"It shares the cilia pattern because it also moves."},{"pt":"A flagellum propels the whole cell, as in sperm","why":"Unlike cilia, a flagellum moves the cell itself rather than something on its surface."},{"pt":"Microvilli on the free surface of simple columnar cells in the digestive tract","why":"Microvilli belong to absorptive linings, so they turn up where the gut takes in material."},{"pt":"Relative sizes roughly right, with the flagellum longest","why":"Drawing the flagellum as the long one shows you know cilia are short and flagella are long."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L2-6","lv":2,"comps":[14],"cids":["w1-organelles"],"t":"Free versus bound ribosomes","p":"Draw a cell and mark where free ribosomes sit and where bound ribosomes sit. Write what each group makes and where that product ends up.","k":[{"pt":"Free ribosomes in the cytosol","why":"Free ribosomes are not attached to any membrane."},{"pt":"Proteins from free ribosomes are used inside the cell","why":"Proteins made out in the cytosol stay there for the cell's own use."},{"pt":"Bound ribosomes attached to the rough ER","why":"Their attachment is what makes rough ER rough."},{"pt":"Proteins from bound ribosomes are for secretion or for insertion into a membrane","why":"Building them on the ER puts them straight into the pathway that leads to the Golgi and out of the cell."},{"pt":"Every ribosome built of a large and a small subunit of rRNA and protein","why":"The subunits are assembled in the nucleolus, which ties ribosomes back to the nucleus."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L2-7","lv":2,"comps":[14],"cids":["w1-organelles"],"t":"A lysosome from start to finish","p":"Draw the life of a lysosome: where it is made, what it fuses with, and what happens to the contents.","k":[{"pt":"Packaged by the Golgi apparatus as part of the endomembrane system","why":"The Golgi packages and sorts molecules for delivery, and lysosomes are one of the endomembrane organelles."},{"pt":"Filled with hydrolytic enzymes","why":"The enzymes are what make it a digestive organelle."},{"pt":"Meets material the cell engulfed by phagocytosis, such as pathogens or debris","why":"Phagocytosis is the cell engulfing something, and the lysosome is where that material is broken down."},{"pt":"Breaks down the cell's own worn organelles, called autophagy","why":"Autophagy is the cell recycling its own parts, so the lysosome is also a cleanup crew."},{"pt":"Failure example: Tay-Sachs, a missing lysosomal enzyme lets lipid build up in nerve cells","why":"When the cleanup organelle cannot do its job, material piles up inside the cell until the cell fails."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[14],"cids":["w1-organelles"],"t":"Break one thing","p":"Pick one organelle and build a mind map of what would go wrong in the cell if it stopped working. Branch out at least three consequences.","k":[{"pt":"Organelle named and its normal job stated first","why":"The consequences only make sense once the normal job is on the page."},{"pt":"At least three consequences, each traced back to that job","why":"Every effect should follow from the lost job, not from a guess about the cell."},{"pt":"If it was the lysosome: undigested material and worn organelles build up inside the cell, as in Tay-Sachs","why":"Remove the digestive organelle and nothing else takes over its job, so the material stays put."},{"pt":"If it was the mitochondrion: not enough ATP, and muscle, brain, and kidney show it first","why":"Those tissues have the highest energy demand, so they fail first."},{"pt":"If it was the rough ER: secretory and membrane proteins are not made","why":"The bound ribosomes on the rough ER are where those proteins are built, so the whole export line stops."}]},{"id":"m1-cell-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[12,14],"cids":["w1-plasma-membrane","w1-organelles"],"t":"Read the cell's job","p":"You are shown three cells. Cell A secretes large amounts of protein. Cell B detoxifies drugs. Cell C lines the airway and sweeps mucus. Predict which structure each cell would be packed with and explain your choice from that structure's job. Then predict what happens to cell C if its 9 plus 2 microtubules stop working.","k":[{"pt":"Cell A: rough ER, with a large Golgi apparatus","why":"Rough ER builds proteins for secretion and the Golgi packages them, so a protein exporter needs plenty of both."},{"pt":"Cell B: smooth ER","why":"Detoxifying drugs is a smooth ER job."},{"pt":"Peroxisomes also accepted for cell B if the toxin is alcohol","why":"Peroxisomes detoxify alcohol, which is why liver and kidney cells are rich in them."},{"pt":"Cell C: cilia on its free surface","why":"Cilia move substances such as mucus across the surface of the respiratory tract."},{"pt":"If the 9 plus 2 microtubules fail, the cilia stop and mucus is not cleared","why":"The 9 plus 2 arrangement is what makes movement possible, so losing it stops the sweep."},{"pt":"Result: repeated respiratory infections","why":"Mucus left sitting in the airway is the setting for recurrent infection, the clinical picture of a ciliary problem."}]}]},{"id":"m1-epithelial","title":"Epithelial tissue","mw":"2026-08-19","tr":"2026-08-20","prompts":[{"id":"m1-epithelial-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[16],"cids":["w1-epithelial-id"],"t":"Epithelium by name","p":"List every epithelial type you can, named by layering and cell shape, and give one location for each.","k":[{"pt":"Simple squamous: alveoli, glomeruli, lining of blood vessels, serosa","why":"One thin layer of flat cells suits filtration and diffusion, so it lines places where things cross."},{"pt":"Simple cuboidal: kidney tubules, small glands, ovary surface","why":"A single layer of cube-shaped cells handles secretion and absorption."},{"pt":"Simple columnar: digestive tract from stomach to rectum, gallbladder","why":"Tall single cells, often with microvilli or goblet cells, absorb and secrete mucus and enzymes."},{"pt":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar: trachea, bronchi","why":"It looks layered, but every cell touches the basement membrane, and its cilia and goblet cells move mucus."},{"pt":"Stratified squamous: skin (keratinized), esophagus, mouth, vagina","why":"Many layers with flat cells on top survive abrasion by shedding the top layer."},{"pt":"Stratified cuboidal in sweat, mammary, and salivary glands, and stratified columnar in the male urethra","why":"These two are rare and turn up mostly in ducts, where some protection and secretion are needed."},{"pt":"Transitional: urinary bladder, ureters, part of the urethra","why":"Its cells change from dome-shaped to flat as the organ stretches."}]},{"id":"m1-epithelial-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[16],"cids":["w1-epithelial-id"],"t":"Epithelium mind map","p":"Build a mind map with epithelium at the center. One main branch for layering, one for cell shape. Fill in every combination that actually exists and hang a location off each.","k":[{"pt":"Layering branch: simple (one layer) and stratified (more than one layer)","why":"Layers are named first, so this branch is the first decision when you name any epithelium."},{"pt":"Shape branch: squamous, cuboidal, columnar","why":"The shape you name is always the shape of the cells at the free surface."},{"pt":"Only the eight real types: simple squamous, cuboidal, and columnar; stratified squamous, cuboidal, and columnar; pseudostratified columnar; transitional","why":"Eight epithelia exist, and inventing a ninth means the naming rule is not settled yet."},{"pt":"Pseudostratified and transitional shown as special cases","why":"They do not fit the simple and stratified grid cleanly, since one only looks layered and the other changes shape."},{"pt":"A location on every end point","why":"A tissue without a location cannot be found on a slide or used on an exam."}]},{"id":"m1-epithelial-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[16],"cids":["w1-epithelial-id"],"t":"Polarity and the basement membrane","p":"Draw an epithelial sheet and label the apical surface, the lateral surfaces, the basal surface, and the basement membrane. Write what sits on the other side of the basement membrane.","k":[{"pt":"Apical surface facing the lumen or the outside, sometimes carrying cilia or microvilli","why":"The free surface is where the tissue meets what it covers or lines, so the specializations sit there."},{"pt":"Lateral surfaces between neighboring cells, where the junctions are","why":"Neighboring cells are fastened together along their sides by tight junctions, adherens junctions, and desmosomes."},{"pt":"Basal surface resting on the basement membrane","why":"The bottom of each cell is anchored to the basement membrane by hemidesmosomes."},{"pt":"Basement membrane drawn as a basal lamina over a reticular lamina","why":"The basal lamina is collagen, laminin, and glycoproteins, and the reticular lamina beneath it is collagen and glycoproteins."},{"pt":"Connective tissue below, carrying the blood supply","why":"Epithelium is avascular, so it depends on diffusion from the vascular connective tissue underneath."}]},{"id":"m1-epithelial-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[17],"cids":["w1-cell-junctions"],"t":"Cell junctions","p":"Name the five cell junctions. For each, draw a quick sketch of what it looks like between two cells and write where in the body you would find it.","k":[{"pt":"Tight junction near the apex, sealing the space between cells, in the lining of the stomach, intestines, and bladder","why":"It seals the passage between cells, which keeps organ contents from leaking into the tissue around them."},{"pt":"Adherens junction as a belt just below it, in intestinal epithelial cells","why":"Its belt of cadherins tied to microfilaments helps the surface resist pulling apart."},{"pt":"Desmosome as a spot weld between two cells, in the outer epidermis and cardiac muscle","why":"Desmosomes bind cell to cell under tension, so they are found where tissue is pulled hardest."},{"pt":"Hemidesmosome anchoring a cell to the basement membrane, in skin and heart muscle","why":"It holds a cell to the basement membrane instead of to another cell, which is the difference from a desmosome."},{"pt":"Gap junction as a channel between cells, in cardiac tissue, neurons, and the GI tract","why":"Gap junctions let small ions and molecules pass from cell to cell, which lets impulses spread."}]},{"id":"m1-epithelial-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[16,18,19,20],"cids":["w1-epithelial-id","w1-connective-id","w1-muscle-tissue-id","w1-nervous-tissue-id"],"t":"The four primary tissues","p":"Name the four primary tissue types. For each, write one defining feature and two locations.","k":[{"pt":"Epithelial, connective, muscle, nervous","why":"Every organ in the body is built from these four tissue types."},{"pt":"Epithelial: tightly packed cells on surfaces and linings, such as the epidermis and the lining of the digestive tract","why":"Little matrix and packed cells are what let epithelium cover and line."},{"pt":"Connective: a large matrix with few cells, such as bone and blood","why":"The amount and type of matrix is what separates connective tissue from the other three."},{"pt":"Muscle: cells built to move and generate force, such as skeletal muscle on bones and cardiac muscle in the heart wall","why":"Muscle moves bones, pumps blood, and pushes contents through hollow organs."},{"pt":"Nervous: neurons and glial cells, such as the brain and spinal cord","why":"Branching neurons with long processes are the shape that carries signals over a distance."},{"pt":"Two real locations for each tissue","why":"Locations turn a definition into something you can find on a slide."}]},{"id":"m1-epithelial-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[16],"cids":["w1-epithelial-id"],"t":"Simple columnar versus pseudostratified","p":"Draw a simple columnar and a pseudostratified columnar epithelium side by side and label both. Then write how you tell them apart under the scope.","k":[{"pt":"Both drawn on one basement membrane, with every cell touching it","why":"All cells reach the basement membrane in both, which is why pseudostratified is not truly stratified."},{"pt":"Simple columnar nuclei in one row at about the same level","why":"One layer of tall cells of the same height lines its nuclei up."},{"pt":"Pseudostratified nuclei staggered at different heights","why":"Cells of different heights put their nuclei at different levels, which makes one layer look like several."},{"pt":"Cilia and goblet cells on the pseudostratified side, microvilli and goblet cells possible on the simple columnar side","why":"Cilia sweep mucus in the airway, while microvilli belong to the absorbing lining of the gut."},{"pt":"Locations: simple columnar in the digestive tract, pseudostratified in the trachea and bronchi","why":"The location matches the job, absorbing in the gut and clearing mucus in the airway."}]},{"id":"m1-epithelial-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[16],"cids":["w1-epithelial-id"],"t":"Glands, sorted","p":"Split glands two ways: exocrine versus endocrine, then unicellular versus multicellular. Give an example in every box you can fill.","k":[{"pt":"Exocrine glands have ducts that empty onto a surface or into a lumen","why":"The duct is the defining feature, because it carries the product to where it is used."},{"pt":"Endocrine glands have no duct and release into the bloodstream","why":"With no duct, the product goes straight into the blood."},{"pt":"Unicellular exocrine example: the goblet cell, which secretes mucus","why":"A single cell can be a gland, and the goblet cell is the one you will see on slides."},{"pt":"Multicellular exocrine examples: sweat, salivary, sebaceous glands","why":"Many cells forming a structure with a duct is the kind of gland you will name most often."},{"pt":"Endocrine examples: thyroid, adrenal, pituitary","why":"These are glands with no duct, so they fill the endocrine box."},{"pt":"The pancreas named as a mixed gland","why":"It sends digestive enzymes into a duct and also releases products into the blood, so it fills both boxes."}]},{"id":"m1-epithelial-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[16],"cids":["w1-epithelial-id"],"t":"Two kinds of stratified squamous","p":"Draw keratinized and non-keratinized stratified squamous side by side. Label both and write where each one is found.","k":[{"pt":"Keratinized: flat dead surface cells with no nuclei at the top","why":"Keratin fills the surface cells until they die and flatten, which makes the epidermis tough and dry."},{"pt":"Keratinized location: the epidermis of the skin","why":"The skin faces air and abrasion, so it needs a dead, sealed top layer."},{"pt":"Non-keratinized: surface cells still have nuclei","why":"Without keratin the surface cells stay alive and moist."},{"pt":"Non-keratinized locations: mouth, esophagus, vagina","why":"These surfaces are rubbed but stay wet, so they need layers without a dry dead surface."},{"pt":"A basal layer of dividing cells in both","why":"Both types replace shed cells from the bottom layer, which is how the tissue survives abrasion."},{"pt":"Basement membrane drawn under both","why":"Every epithelium sits on a basement membrane, with connective tissue and blood vessels below it."}]},{"id":"m1-epithelial-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[17],"cids":["w1-cell-junctions"],"t":"When the junction fails","p":"Two patients have blistering skin. In the first, the epidermal cells separate from each other. In the second, the whole epidermis lifts off the basement membrane. Name the junction that failed in each and the protein it uses, and say which blister is deeper. Then predict what would cross the intestinal lining if a toxin opened its tight junctions.","k":[{"pt":"First patient: desmosomes failed","why":"Desmosomes bind cell to cell, so losing them lets epidermal cells pull apart from each other."},{"pt":"Desmosome protein: cadherins, linked to intermediate filaments","why":"Cadherin plaques tied to intermediate filaments hold the cells together under tension."},{"pt":"Second patient: hemidesmosomes failed","why":"Hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basement membrane, so losing them separates the whole epidermis from what lies beneath."},{"pt":"Hemidesmosome protein: integrins","why":"Integrins are the anchoring protein that grips the basement membrane."},{"pt":"The second blister is deeper and more serious","why":"It splits the tissue at the very bottom of the epidermis rather than between cells partway up."},{"pt":"With tight junctions open, material passes between the cells and leaks out of the gut","why":"Tight junctions seal the passage between cells, so opening them lets organ contents leak into the surrounding tissue."}]},{"id":"m1-epithelial-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[16],"cids":["w1-epithelial-id"],"t":"Match the tissue to the place","p":"Predict the epithelium you would find in each place and explain how its structure fits the job: the air sacs of the lungs, the inside of the urinary bladder, the inner surface of the cheek, and the trachea. Then say which of the four is most easily damaged and why.","k":[{"pt":"Air sacs (alveoli): simple squamous","why":"One layer of flat cells is thin enough for substances to diffuse across."},{"pt":"Urinary bladder: transitional","why":"Its cells change from dome-shaped to flat, so the bladder can stretch as it fills."},{"pt":"Inner cheek: non-keratinized stratified squamous","why":"Many layers protect against rubbing from food, and no keratin keeps the surface moist."},{"pt":"Trachea: pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","why":"Goblet cells make mucus and cilia move it, which keeps the airway clear."},{"pt":"Most easily damaged: the simple squamous of the alveoli","why":"A single thin layer has nothing to shed, unlike stratified squamous, which loses its top layer and keeps going."}]}]},{"id":"m1-connective","title":"Connective tissue","mw":"2026-08-24","tr":"2026-08-25","prompts":[{"id":"m1-connective-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[18],"cids":["w1-connective-id"],"t":"What connective tissue is made of","p":"Dump the three ingredients of every connective tissue and name what falls under each. Then name three connective tissue proper subtypes.","k":[{"pt":"Cells: fibroblasts, macrophages, mast cells, plasma cells, adipocytes, white blood cells","why":"These cells live in the matrix and build it, defend it, or store energy in it."},{"pt":"Blasts build and cytes maintain, such as fibroblast and fibrocyte","why":"Blasts divide and secrete matrix, and cytes are the mature form that maintain and repair it."},{"pt":"Fibers: collagen, elastic, reticular","why":"Collagen resists pulling, elastic fibers stretch and recoil, and reticular fibers form fine branching networks."},{"pt":"Ground substance, the material between the cells and fibers","why":"Its water and organic molecules support and bind cells and let nutrients move between blood and cells."},{"pt":"Extracellular matrix named as ground substance plus fibers","why":"The matrix is what changes from one connective tissue to the next, which is why blood is liquid and bone is hard."},{"pt":"Three connective tissue proper subtypes, such as areolar, adipose, reticular, dense regular, dense irregular, elastic","why":"These are the connective tissue proper types on the lab list, separate from cartilage, bone, and blood."}]},{"id":"m1-connective-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[18],"cids":["w1-connective-id"],"t":"Areolar tissue","p":"Draw areolar connective tissue as it looks on a slide and label everything in it. Then write why it is called the packing tissue.","k":[{"pt":"Fibroblasts, macrophages, mast cells, plasma cells, adipocytes","why":"Areolar tissue is a loose mix, so it holds a little of everything, from matrix builders to immune cells."},{"pt":"Thick wavy collagen fibers and thin branching elastic fibers","why":"Collagen gives strength and elastic fibers give stretch, so areolar tissue offers both."},{"pt":"Fine reticular fibers","why":"Reticular fibers surround the cells in areolar tissue and add support."},{"pt":"Loose arrangement with plenty of open ground substance","why":"The open space is what makes it loose connective tissue rather than dense."},{"pt":"Location: under epithelia, around organs and blood vessels","why":"It fills the gaps and wraps structures, which is why it is called the packing tissue."}]},{"id":"m1-connective-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[18],"cids":["w1-connective-id"],"t":"Adipose and reticular","p":"Draw adipose tissue and reticular tissue side by side. Label the cells and fibers in each and name two locations for each.","k":[{"pt":"Adipocytes, each filled with a large fat droplet, the nucleus pushed to the edge","why":"The fat droplet takes up most of the cell, so the nucleus and cytoplasm are squeezed to the rim."},{"pt":"Sparse matrix between adipocytes, with blood vessels","why":"Adipose is mostly cells and very little matrix, and blood vessels run between the fat cells to supply them."},{"pt":"Adipose locations: subcutaneous tissue, around the kidneys and heart","why":"Fat stores energy, insulates, and cushions, so it sits under the skin and around organs."},{"pt":"Reticular fibers drawn as a fine branching mesh","why":"Reticular fibers are collagen coated with glycoproteins, and they branch into networks instead of bundles."},{"pt":"Reticular locations: the stroma (framework) of soft organs, walls of blood vessels, around cells in other tissues","why":"A fine mesh is the right support for soft organs that need a framework without stiffness."}]},{"id":"m1-connective-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[18],"cids":["w1-connective-id"],"t":"Dense regular versus irregular","p":"Draw dense regular and dense irregular connective tissue. Show the fiber direction in each, and write what that direction lets the tissue resist.","k":[{"pt":"Dense regular: collagen bundles all running parallel","why":"Parallel bundles give tensile strength along one line of pull."},{"pt":"Dense regular locations: tendons and ligaments","why":"Tendons and ligaments are pulled along their length, so the fibers line up that way."},{"pt":"Dense irregular: collagen bundles running in every direction","why":"Fibers in all directions resist pulling from any direction."},{"pt":"Dense irregular location: the dermis of the skin","why":"Skin gets pulled every which way, and the dermis is built from this tissue."},{"pt":"Fibroblasts squeezed between the bundles in both","why":"Fibroblasts secrete the collagen fibers, so they sit squeezed between the bundles they made."}]},{"id":"m1-connective-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[16,18,19],"cids":["w1-epithelial-id","w1-connective-id","w1-muscle-tissue-id"],"t":"Name that slide","p":"For each clue write the tissue: lines the trachea and looks layered but is not. Has lacunae in a glassy matrix. Has a central canal ringed by circles. Has one central nucleus and no stripes. Has collagen fibers running in every direction in the dermis of the skin.","k":[{"pt":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","why":"All its cells touch the basement membrane, so the layered look comes only from nuclei at different heights."},{"pt":"Hyaline cartilage","why":"Its collagen fibers are too fine to see with ordinary staining, which gives the matrix its glassy look."},{"pt":"Compact bone, the osteon","why":"Rings of lamellae around a central canal exist only in compact bone."},{"pt":"Smooth muscle","why":"Spindle-shaped cells with one central nucleus and no striations are the smooth muscle signature."},{"pt":"Dense irregular connective tissue","why":"The dermis needs strength in every direction, so its collagen runs every direction."}]},{"id":"m1-connective-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[18],"cids":["w1-connective-id"],"t":"Softest to hardest","p":"Put these in order from the softest matrix to the hardest: blood, areolar tissue, cartilage, bone. For each one, write what its matrix is like and one place it is found. Then write what changes across the order.","k":[{"pt":"Blood: a fluid matrix, in the heart and blood vessels","why":"Blood is a connective tissue with a liquid matrix, which is why it can serve as a transport system."},{"pt":"Areolar tissue: soft, loose ground substance with scattered fibers, under epithelia","why":"Its matrix is open and soft, with fibers running in every direction."},{"pt":"Cartilage: a firm but pliable gel, at the ends of long bones, the nose, the external ear, or the discs","why":"Cartilage matrix is a firm gel with collagen and sometimes elastic fibers, so it bends without breaking."},{"pt":"Bone: a hard calcified matrix, in the skeleton","why":"Bone matrix is calcified, which makes it hard and inflexible."},{"pt":"What changes: the ground substance goes from fluid to gel to calcified","why":"All four share the same plan of cells, fibers, and ground substance, and the matrix alone sets how hard each one is."}]},{"id":"m1-connective-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[18],"cids":["w1-connective-id"],"t":"Two injuries, one day","p":"A person tears a tendon and cuts the skin of the forearm on the same day. Predict which heals faster and explain using what each tissue is built from. Then predict how a torn knee meniscus would heal, and say why.","k":[{"pt":"The skin wound heals faster","why":"A skin wound closes in days, while a tendon heals slowly."},{"pt":"Tendon is dense regular connective tissue with a poor blood supply","why":"Tendon is one of the exceptions to connective tissue being highly vascular, and vascularity sets healing speed."},{"pt":"Skin heals from a well supplied dermis under a living stratum basale","why":"The dermis is full of blood vessels, and the epidermis is fed by diffusion from it."},{"pt":"A torn meniscus also heals slowly","why":"The meniscus is fibrocartilage, and cartilage has no blood vessels of its own."},{"pt":"The rule: more blood supply means faster healing","why":"Tissue structure predicts healing, and vascularity is the part of the structure that sets the speed."}]},{"id":"m1-connective-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[18,26],"cids":["w1-connective-id","w2-cartilage-types"],"t":"Three cartilage biopsies","p":"Three biopsies come back from the lab: one from the external ear, one from an intervertebral disc, and one from a ring of the trachea. Predict the cartilage type in each, what you would see in its matrix, and which one would have no perichondrium.","k":[{"pt":"External ear: elastic cartilage","why":"The ear has to bend and spring back, and elastic cartilage keeps its shape while staying flexible."},{"pt":"Elastic cartilage shows many elastic fibers in the matrix","why":"Those fibers are what separate it from hyaline cartilage, which otherwise looks similar."},{"pt":"Intervertebral disc: fibrocartilage with thick collagen bundles","why":"Discs take compression and shock, which thick collagen bundles are built to resist."},{"pt":"Trachea: hyaline cartilage, glassy, with chondrocytes in lacunae","why":"The airways are held open by hyaline cartilage, and its collagen fibers are too fine to see, so the matrix looks glassy."},{"pt":"Fibrocartilage is the one with no perichondrium","why":"The lab list marks fibrocartilage as the cartilage without a perichondrium."}]}]},{"id":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue","title":"Muscle and nervous tissue (histology)","mw":"2026-08-31","tr":"2026-09-01","prompts":[{"id":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[20],"cids":["w1-nervous-tissue-id"],"t":"Neuron and neuroglia","p":"Draw a neuron, label every part you can, and mark the direction the signal travels. Then write what other cells nervous tissue contains besides neurons, and name the cell you would see wrapping axons on a nerve cross section.","k":[{"pt":"Cell body (soma) with its nucleus and nucleolus","why":"The cell body is the hub of the neuron, and the lab list asks for its nucleus and nucleolus."},{"pt":"Dendrites branching from the cell body","why":"Dendrites are the branching processes that carry signals in toward the cell body."},{"pt":"Axon hillock, axon, and synaptic bulb","why":"The axon leaves the cell body at the hillock and ends at the synaptic bulb, carrying the signal away."},{"pt":"Arrow from the dendrites through the cell body and down the axon to the synaptic bulb","why":"The signal travels one way through a neuron, in through the dendrites and out along the axon."},{"pt":"Glial cells named as the other cells of nervous tissue","why":"Nervous tissue is neurons plus glial cells, the support cells around them."},{"pt":"Schwann cells wrapping axons on a nerve cross section","why":"Schwann cells are the glial cells that wrap axons in a nerve, with gaps called nodes of Ranvier between them."}]},{"id":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[19],"cids":["w1-muscle-tissue-id"],"t":"Cardiac muscle up close","p":"Draw cardiac muscle as it looks on a slide and label everything that identifies it. Write the two features that no other tissue has.","k":[{"pt":"Branching fibers","why":"Cardiac cells branch and connect to their neighbors, which no other muscle does."},{"pt":"Intercalated discs as dark lines across the fibers","why":"The discs are where cardiac cells join end to end."},{"pt":"A single central nucleus in each cell","why":"Cardiac muscle is uninucleated with the nucleus in the center, unlike skeletal muscle."},{"pt":"Striations present","why":"Cardiac shares striations with skeletal muscle, so stripes alone do not identify it."},{"pt":"Intercalated discs and branching named as the giveaway pair","why":"Those two features are found only in cardiac muscle, so seeing them settles the identification."}]},{"id":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[19],"cids":["w1-muscle-tissue-id"],"t":"Skeletal muscle on a slide","p":"Draw skeletal muscle in longitudinal section and label what you can see. Then write where the nuclei sit and why that matters for identifying it.","k":[{"pt":"Long, cylindrical, unbranched fibers","why":"Skeletal fibers run long and straight, unlike branching cardiac cells."},{"pt":"Obvious striations, dark and light bands","why":"The stripes show it is striated muscle, a feature it shares with cardiac."},{"pt":"Many nuclei in each fiber","why":"Skeletal fibers are multinucleated, which no other muscle tissue is."},{"pt":"Nuclei pushed to the edge of the fiber","why":"Peripheral nuclei are the identifier, since cardiac and smooth muscle keep a single nucleus in the center."},{"pt":"Sarcolemma labeled around each fiber","why":"The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the muscle fiber, so it outlines each fiber on the slide."}]},{"id":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[19],"cids":["w1-muscle-tissue-id"],"t":"Three muscle tissues","p":"List the three muscle tissue types. For each, write striations, nuclei per cell, branching, and whether it is voluntary.","k":[{"pt":"Skeletal: striated, many nuclei at the edge, unbranched, voluntary","why":"It is the only voluntary muscle and the only multinucleated one, which makes it easy to separate."},{"pt":"Cardiac: striated, one central nucleus, branched with intercalated discs, involuntary","why":"Branches and discs are unique to cardiac, even though it shares striations with skeletal."},{"pt":"Smooth: no striations, one central nucleus, spindle-shaped and unbranched, involuntary","why":"No stripes and a spindle shape set it apart, even though it shares a central nucleus with cardiac."},{"pt":"Locations: skeletal on bones, cardiac in the heart wall, smooth in the walls of hollow organs such as the intestines and bladder","why":"Each location matches the job: moving bones, pumping blood, and pushing contents through hollow organs."}]},{"id":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[16,18,19,20],"cids":["w1-epithelial-id","w1-connective-id","w1-muscle-tissue-id","w1-nervous-tissue-id"],"t":"Four tissues, one map","p":"Build a mind map with the four primary tissues as main branches. Hang every subtype you can under each branch.","k":[{"pt":"Epithelial branch: named by layers and shape, plus glandular epithelium","why":"Epithelium is named by layers and shape, and glands are epithelium that secretes."},{"pt":"Connective branch: connective tissue proper, cartilage, bone, blood","why":"Cartilage, bone, and blood all count as connective tissue because each one is cells plus matrix."},{"pt":"Muscle branch: skeletal, cardiac, smooth","why":"The three muscle types differ in striations, nuclei, and branching."},{"pt":"Nervous branch: neurons and glial cells","why":"At this level nervous tissue has two cell groups, the signaling neurons and the support cells."},{"pt":"Every subtype hung on the right main branch","why":"Putting blood under connective tissue, rather than on a branch of its own, is the usual test of whether the map is right."}]},{"id":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[21,7],"cids":["w1-body-membranes","w1-serous-membranes"],"t":"Body membranes","p":"Name the four body membranes. For each write which tissues build it and where it is found.","k":[{"pt":"Cutaneous membrane: the skin, keratinized stratified squamous epithelium over dense irregular connective tissue","why":"The skin is the cutaneous membrane, the one that covers the outer surface of the body."},{"pt":"Mucous membrane: epithelium over a lamina propria of loose connective tissue, lining tracts open to the outside","why":"It lines passages such as the digestive and respiratory tracts that connect to the outside world."},{"pt":"Serous membrane: simple squamous epithelium with connective tissue under it, lining closed cavities","why":"Serous membranes line cavities with no opening to the outside, and simple squamous is the epithelium of the serosa."},{"pt":"Synovial membrane: connective tissue only, areolar with elastic fibers, lining joint cavities","why":"It is the one membrane with no epithelium, and it forms the inner layer of a synovial joint capsule."}]},{"id":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[19],"cids":["w1-muscle-tissue-id"],"t":"Name the mystery muscle","p":"A biopsy from the wall of a hollow organ shows spindle-shaped cells with one central nucleus and no stripes. A second biopsy shows striated cells that branch and are joined by dark lines. Name each tissue and the organ each likely came from, and explain which features ruled out the other muscle types. Then say whether each one is voluntary.","k":[{"pt":"First biopsy: smooth muscle","why":"Spindle cells, one central nucleus, and no striations are the smooth muscle features."},{"pt":"First biopsy likely from the wall of the intestines or bladder","why":"Smooth muscle is found in the walls of hollow organs."},{"pt":"Second biopsy: cardiac muscle, from the heart wall","why":"Branching striated cells joined by intercalated discs are found only in the heart."},{"pt":"Skeletal muscle ruled out because its fibers are unbranched with many nuclei at the edge","why":"Neither biopsy shows long multinucleated fibers, so neither one is skeletal."},{"pt":"Both are involuntary","why":"Only skeletal muscle is voluntary, so smooth and cardiac work without conscious control."}]},{"id":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[21,7],"cids":["w1-body-membranes","w1-serous-membranes"],"t":"Which membrane is it","p":"Three patients each have an inflamed membrane: the lining of a knee joint, the lining around the lungs, and the lining of the nasal passages. Name the type of body membrane in each case and the tissues that build it, and explain how its location told you the type.","k":[{"pt":"Knee joint lining: synovial membrane","why":"Synovial membranes line joint cavities."},{"pt":"Synovial membrane is connective tissue only, with no epithelium","why":"That is what sets it apart from the other three body membranes."},{"pt":"Lining around the lungs: a serous membrane, the pleura","why":"The pleural cavity is closed to the outside, and closed cavities are lined by serous membranes."},{"pt":"The pleura is simple squamous epithelium with connective tissue under it","why":"Simple squamous is the epithelium of the serosa."},{"pt":"Nasal passages: mucous membrane, epithelium over a lamina propria","why":"The nasal cavity opens to the outside, and passages open to the outside are lined by mucous membranes."},{"pt":"The rule: open to the outside means mucous, closed cavity means serous, joint cavity means synovial, body surface means cutaneous","why":"Asking where the membrane sits answers what type it is."}]}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary","title":"Integumentary system","mw":"2026-09-02","tr":"2026-09-03","prompts":[{"id":"m1-integumentary-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[23],"cids":["w1-epidermal-strata"],"t":"Epidermis, deep to superficial","p":"Write the epidermal layers from deepest to most superficial and one thing that happens in each. Note which layer is not in thin skin.","k":[{"pt":"Stratum basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum, deep to superficial","why":"This is the order a keratinocyte travels as it is pushed from the base to the surface."},{"pt":"Stratum basale: cells dividing at the base","why":"The basale holds the stem cells that make new keratinocytes, which is why a burn that spares it can heal."},{"pt":"Stratum spinosum: cells with a spiny look, just above the basale","why":"The spiny look is how you pick out this layer on a slide."},{"pt":"Stratum granulosum: granular cells where lamellar granules release their sealant","why":"The water-repellent sealant slows water entry and loss and keeps foreign material out."},{"pt":"Stratum corneum: flat, dead, keratin-filled cells at the surface","why":"The nucleus is gone by this level, and these dead cells take the abrasion and are shed."},{"pt":"Stratum lucidum only in thick skin, between the granulosum and the corneum","why":"Thick skin on the palms, soles, and fingertips adds this clear layer where friction is greatest."},{"pt":"Baby Screams Grandpa Loves Coffee counts if the order is right","why":"The mnemonic is a check on the order, not a replacement for knowing what each layer is."}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[22,24,25],"cids":["w1-skin-layers","w1-dermis-layers","w1-skin-accessory"],"t":"Skin in section","p":"Draw skin in section from the surface down to the hypodermis and label every structure you can.","k":[{"pt":"Epidermis, dermis with papillary and reticular regions, hypodermis","why":"The skin is the epidermis plus the dermis, and the hypodermis below is not part of the skin."},{"pt":"Hair follicle with the shaft, root, and bulb","why":"Follicles run down into the dermis, which shows how deep the epidermal cells reach."},{"pt":"Arrector pili muscle attached to the follicle","why":"This smooth muscle pulls the hair upright to make goose bumps."},{"pt":"Sebaceous gland opening onto the follicle, eccrine sweat gland with its duct reaching the surface","why":"Where each duct ends is the key difference between these two glands."},{"pt":"Dermal papillae with capillary loops and Meissner corpuscles","why":"Capillary loops and touch receptors sit in the papillae, right under the epidermis."},{"pt":"Lamellated (Pacinian) corpuscle and large blood vessels in the hypodermis","why":"The deep pressure receptor and the large vessels sit below the dermis."}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[23],"cids":["w1-epidermal-strata"],"t":"Cells of the epidermis","p":"Name the four cell types in the epidermis. For each, write its job and which layer you would find it in.","k":[{"pt":"Keratinocytes, about 90 percent, make keratin, found in every layer","why":"They are the bulk of the epidermis, and keratin protects against abrasion, heat, microbes, and chemicals."},{"pt":"Melanocytes, about 8 percent, make melanin, in the stratum basale","why":"Their long projections pass melanin to keratinocytes, which shield their nuclei from UV light."},{"pt":"Intraepidermal macrophages (Langerhans or dendritic cells), immune defense, in the stratum spinosum","why":"They come from red bone marrow and alert the immune system to microbes in the skin."},{"pt":"Merkel (tactile) cells, touch, in the deepest layer, the stratum basale","why":"They are the least numerous and contact a sensory neuron at a tactile disc."}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[24],"cids":["w1-dermis-layers"],"t":"The dermis","p":"Draw the dermis and split it into its two layers. Label what is in each and write which one gives you fingerprints.","k":[{"pt":"Papillary region: the thin upper fifth, areolar tissue with thin collagen and fine elastic fibers","why":"It is thin and loose because its job is anchoring and feeding the epidermis by diffusion."},{"pt":"Dermal papillae holding capillary loops, Meissner corpuscles, and free nerve endings","why":"These sit as close to the epidermis as possible, so touch is sensed right at the surface."},{"pt":"Reticular region: the thick lower four fifths, dense irregular connective tissue with thick collagen bundles","why":"Thick collagen running in every direction gives skin its strength and resistance to stretching."},{"pt":"Reticular region holds blood vessels, nerves, hair follicles, sweat glands, sebaceous glands","why":"The deeper layer has room for the larger structures that support the skin."},{"pt":"Dermal papillae raise the epidermal ridges, the fingerprints","why":"Ridges on palms, soles, and fingertips add touch corpuscles and increase grip by adding friction."}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[25],"cids":["w1-skin-accessory"],"t":"A hair and its follicle","p":"Draw a hair in its follicle from the bulb up past the skin surface. Label every part you can.","k":[{"pt":"Shaft above the surface, root below","why":"The root is the part buried in the dermis, sometimes reaching the subcutaneous layer."},{"pt":"Bulb at the base with the hair papilla and the hair matrix","why":"The papilla brings in blood vessels and the matrix is where cells divide, so this is where hair actually grows."},{"pt":"Medulla, cortex, cuticle as three concentric layers","why":"Root and shaft share these layers, from the pigmented core out to the flat cuticle."},{"pt":"External and internal root sheaths, with the dermal root sheath around them","why":"The external sheath continues down from the epidermis, the internal sheath is made by the matrix, and the dermis wraps both."},{"pt":"Arrector pili muscle, sebaceous gland, and hair root plexus attached to the follicle","why":"These raise the hair, oil it, and sense its movement."}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary-L1-6","lv":1,"comps":[25],"cids":["w1-skin-accessory"],"t":"A nail","p":"Draw a nail from above and from the side. Label every named part.","k":[{"pt":"Nail body (plate), the visible part","why":"It looks pink because of the blood vessels in the tissue under it."},{"pt":"Free edge","why":"It looks white because there are no capillaries beneath it."},{"pt":"Nail root, hidden in a fold of skin","why":"The root cannot be seen from above, so the side view is where it shows."},{"pt":"Nail matrix, proximal to the root","why":"The matrix holds the dividing cells that make new nail."},{"pt":"Lunula, the pale crescent at the base of the body","why":"The epithelium there is thickened, so the vessels underneath do not show through."},{"pt":"Eponychium (cuticle) and hyponychium","why":"The eponychium is a band of epidermis at the nail wall, and the hyponychium is thickened stratum corneum under the free edge that secures the nail."}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[25],"cids":["w1-skin-accessory"],"t":"The four skin glands","p":"List the four skin glands. For each, write what it secretes and where the secretion goes. Then write the difference between holocrine, merocrine, and apocrine secretion.","k":[{"pt":"Sebaceous gland: sebum, duct into a hair follicle","why":"Most sebaceous glands connect to a follicle, so the oil coats the hair."},{"pt":"Sebaceous glands are holocrine","why":"The whole cell breaks apart to release sebum, which is what holocrine means."},{"pt":"Eccrine sweat gland: perspiration onto the surface of the epidermis, especially forehead, palms, soles","why":"Eccrine ducts open straight onto the skin surface."},{"pt":"Apocrine sweat gland: perspiration with added lipids and proteins, into hair follicles, in the axillae, groin, and areolae, active from puberty","why":"Opening into follicles and switching on at puberty separates apocrine from eccrine sweat glands."},{"pt":"Ceruminous gland: cerumen in the external auditory canal","why":"These are the glands of the ear canal, making its waxy secretion."},{"pt":"Merocrine releases by exocytosis and the cell is unharmed, apocrine pinches off the top of the cell, holocrine ruptures the whole cell","why":"The three modes differ in how much of the cell is lost to release the product."}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[22,23],"cids":["w1-skin-layers","w1-epidermal-strata"],"t":"Skin color and burn depth","p":"Name the three pigments that contribute to skin color. Then draw skin in section and mark how deep a superficial (first degree), a partial-thickness (second degree), and a full-thickness (third degree) burn reaches.","k":[{"pt":"Melanin, carotene, hemoglobin","why":"Melanin comes from melanocytes, carotene is yellow-orange, and hemoglobin shows pink through the epidermis from dermal capillaries."},{"pt":"Superficial burn: epidermis only","why":"The stratum basale is intact, so its stem cells can rebuild the layers above."},{"pt":"Partial-thickness burn: into the dermis, with blisters","why":"The blister forms because the junction between the dermis and the epidermis fails."},{"pt":"Partial-thickness burns heal from surviving hair follicles and glands","why":"Follicles and glands are lined with epidermal cells and sit deep in the dermis, so they can regrow the surface."},{"pt":"Full-thickness burn: through the dermis and its appendages","why":"With the follicles and glands destroyed there is no source of new epidermis, so grafting is needed."}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[22,24,25],"cids":["w1-skin-layers","w1-dermis-layers","w1-skin-accessory"],"t":"Function to structure","p":"Build a mind map with skin at the center. Branch to each function it performs and, on the end of every branch, name the structure that actually does it.","k":[{"pt":"Protection: keratin, lamellar granules, melanin, immune cells","why":"Each one blocks a different threat, from abrasion and water loss to UV light and microbes."},{"pt":"Thermoregulation: sweat glands and dermal blood vessels","why":"Sweat and the blood vessels near the surface are the two structures that adjust heat loss."},{"pt":"Sensation: tactile discs, Meissner corpuscles, lamellated corpuscles, hair root plexuses, free nerve endings","why":"Name the receptor, not just the word touch, because each structure senses something different."},{"pt":"Vitamin D: skin exposed to UV light starts the synthesis","why":"The pathway begins in the skin, which is why short sun exposure matters."},{"pt":"Excretion and absorption: sweat glands out, lipid-soluble substances in","why":"Small amounts of waste leave in sweat, and only fat-soluble materials cross inward."},{"pt":"Blood reservoir: the network of dermal blood vessels","why":"The dermis holds a large network of vessels, so it stores a real volume of blood."}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[24,23],"cids":["w1-dermis-layers","w1-epidermal-strata"],"t":"Receptors by depth","p":"Draw skin in section and place every sensory receptor you can at the depth where it actually sits. Write what each one detects.","k":[{"pt":"Tactile (Merkel) discs in the deepest layer of the epidermis, touch","why":"Merkel cells sit in the stratum basale and contact a flattened sensory nerve ending."},{"pt":"Free nerve endings in the papillary dermis, temperature, light touch, and itch","why":"They are bare nerve endings just under the epidermis."},{"pt":"Meissner corpuscles (corpuscles of touch) in the dermal papillae, touch and pressure","why":"Sitting in the papillae puts them right under the surface for fine touch."},{"pt":"Hair root plexus around each follicle, light touch","why":"Nerve endings wrap the follicle, so moving a hair is felt."},{"pt":"Lamellated (Pacinian) corpuscles in the hypodermis, pressure","why":"They sit deepest of all, below the dermis."}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[22,25],"cids":["w1-skin-layers","w1-skin-accessory"],"t":"Will it heal?","p":"A burn destroys the epidermis and the upper dermis but leaves the hair follicles and glands deep in the dermis. Predict whether the skin regenerates and where the new cells come from. Then compare a second burn that destroys the whole dermis, and say what treatment it needs and why.","k":[{"pt":"The first burn regenerates","why":"There is still a source of new epidermal cells inside the wound."},{"pt":"New cells come from the surviving hair follicles and glands","why":"Follicles and glands are lined with epidermal cells and sit deep in the dermis, so they survive."},{"pt":"New epithelium spreads out from each follicle and gland until the sheets meet","why":"Each surviving structure is a starting point, so healing grows outward from many spots."},{"pt":"The second burn is full thickness, destroying the dermis and its appendages","why":"With the follicles and glands gone, nothing is left to make new epidermis."},{"pt":"It needs a skin graft, ideally an autograft from the same person","why":"Using the person's own skin avoids tissue rejection."},{"pt":"The question to ask of any burn: is the stratum basale, or a follicle or gland, still there","why":"Burn depth decides healing because it decides whether any epidermal stem cells survive."}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[22,23,24,25],"cids":["w1-skin-layers","w1-epidermal-strata","w1-dermis-layers","w1-skin-accessory"],"t":"Palm or scalp","p":"A dermatologist takes two skin biopsies, one from the palm and one from the scalp. Predict four differences you would see between them under the microscope, and explain what each difference tells you about where the sample came from.","k":[{"pt":"Palm has a stratum lucidum, scalp essentially lacks one","why":"The lucidum is only in thick skin, which covers the palms, soles, and palmar surface of the digits."},{"pt":"Palm has a thicker stratum spinosum and stratum corneum","why":"Thick skin builds more corneum layers in response to greater mechanical stress."},{"pt":"Palm has epidermal ridges over well developed dermal papillae, scalp has none","why":"Organized rows of dermal papillae make ridges only in thick skin."},{"pt":"Scalp has hair follicles, arrector pili, and sebaceous glands, palm has none","why":"Thin skin is hairy skin, so follicles and their oil glands show up only in the scalp sample."},{"pt":"Palm has more sweat glands and denser sensory receptors","why":"Thick skin has more sudoriferous glands and more receptors than thin skin."}]}]}]},{"n":2,"title":"Module 2. The Skeletal System","topics":[{"id":"m2-bone-tissue","title":"Bone tissue, the long bone and bone microanatomy","mw":"2026-09-14","tr":"2026-09-10","prompts":[{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[29],"cids":["w2-long-bone-gross"],"t":"Long bone in section","p":"Draw a long bone cut lengthwise and label every region and covering you can.","k":[{"pt":"Diaphysis, epiphyses, metaphysis","why":"These are the shaft, the two expanded ends, and the region between them that holds the growth plate or its remnant."},{"pt":"Epiphyseal line in an adult, epiphyseal plate in a child","why":"The line is the closed growth plate, a sign that growth in length has ended."},{"pt":"Compact bone forming the shaft wall, spongy bone filling the epiphyses","why":"The shaft is a tube of compact bone, and the ends are spongy bone under a thin compact shell."},{"pt":"Medullary cavity, with red marrow in the spongy bone of the epiphyses and yellow marrow in the cavity","why":"Red marrow is where blood cells form, and yellow marrow is fat stored in the adult shaft."},{"pt":"Periosteum outside, endosteum inside, articular cartilage on the ends","why":"Each covering sits on a different surface: the outer bone, the inner bone, and the joint surface."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[30],"cids":["w2-compact-spongy"],"t":"The osteon, center out","p":"Dump the osteon starting at the center and working out. Name every structure and say what runs through it.","k":[{"pt":"Central (Haversian) canal carrying blood vessels and nerves","why":"It is the core of the osteon and the supply line for everything around it."},{"pt":"Concentric lamellae in rings around the canal","why":"The rings of bony matrix give compact bone its strength."},{"pt":"Lacunae between the lamellae, each holding an osteocyte","why":"Each mature bone cell lives in its own small cavity."},{"pt":"Canaliculi linking the lacunae","why":"These tiny channels let osteocytes exchange materials with each other and with the canal."},{"pt":"Perforating (Volkmann) canals running crosswise between central canals","why":"They connect the supply of one osteon to the next."},{"pt":"Interstitial lamellae between osteons and circumferential lamellae around the whole shaft","why":"Interstitial lamellae are leftover pieces of old osteons, and circumferential lamellae wrap the inner and outer surfaces."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[30,29],"cids":["w2-compact-spongy","w2-long-bone-gross"],"t":"Long bone microanatomy, labeled","p":"Draw a wedge of compact bone at the tissue level and label every structure in it. Then add a patch of spongy bone beside it and label that too.","k":[{"pt":"Osteons with central canal, concentric lamellae, lacunae, canaliculi, osteocytes","why":"The osteon is the structural unit of compact bone, so the wedge is built from them."},{"pt":"Perforating canals crossing between osteons","why":"Crosswise canals tie the osteons into one supplied network."},{"pt":"Interstitial lamellae in the gaps, inner and outer circumferential lamellae at the surfaces","why":"Fragments of older osteons fill the gaps, and the circumferential rings finish the surfaces."},{"pt":"Spongy bone as trabeculae with no osteons and red marrow in the spaces","why":"Spongy bone saves weight with a lattice of struts, and its open spaces hold marrow."},{"pt":"Periosteum with fibrous and osteogenic layers on the outer surface, endosteum on the inner surface","why":"The periosteum covers the outside, anchored by perforating fibers, while the endosteum lines the inside."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[29],"cids":["w2-long-bone-gross"],"t":"Periosteum and endosteum","p":"Draw the surface of a long bone enlarged. Label both coverings, their layers, and what anchors the outer one to the bone.","k":[{"pt":"Periosteum covering the outer surface","why":"It is the connective tissue membrane over the outside of the bone."},{"pt":"Periosteum layers: an outer fibrous layer and an inner osteogenic layer","why":"The fibrous layer is the tough outside, and the osteogenic layer lies against the bone."},{"pt":"Perforating (Sharpey) fibers anchoring it into the bone","why":"These collagen bundles tie the periosteum down so it cannot peel away."},{"pt":"Endosteum lining the medullary cavity and the internal bone surfaces","why":"It is the thin membrane on the inside surfaces, facing the marrow."},{"pt":"Articular cartilage covering the joint ends","why":"Hyaline cartilage caps the epiphysis where it meets another bone."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[28],"cids":["w2-bone-shapes"],"t":"Bones by shape","p":"Name the five bone shape classes. Give an example of each, two where you can, and write one structural feature that goes with the shape.","k":[{"pt":"Long: longer than wide, a shaft with two ends, such as the femur and humerus","why":"Length compared with width is the test, which is why the big limb bones are the examples."},{"pt":"Short: roughly cube-shaped, such as the carpals and tarsals","why":"Small cube-shaped bones fit together in the wrist and ankle."},{"pt":"Flat: thin and often curved, such as the sternum, ribs, and most skull bones","why":"Thin curved plates cover and protect what lies beneath them."},{"pt":"Irregular: complex shapes that fit no other group, such as the vertebrae and hip bones","why":"When no other shape fits, the bone is irregular."},{"pt":"Sesamoid: a small bone formed within a tendon, such as the patella","why":"The patella forms inside the quadriceps tendon, which is what sesamoid means."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L1-6","lv":1,"comps":[26],"cids":["w2-cartilage-types"],"t":"Three cartilages","p":"List the three cartilage types. For each write what is in the matrix, what it looks like under the scope, and two locations.","k":[{"pt":"Hyaline: fine collagen, glassy and smooth, the most common type","why":"Its collagen fibers are too fine to see with ordinary staining, which is why the matrix looks glassy."},{"pt":"Hyaline locations: ends of long bones, nose, airways, costal cartilages","why":"A smooth surface suits joint ends, and flexible support suits the airways and ribs."},{"pt":"Elastic: many elastic fibers, flexible and springy, in the external ear and epiglottis","why":"Elastic fibers let these structures bend and spring back into shape."},{"pt":"Fibrocartilage: thick collagen bundles, tough, in the intervertebral discs, knee menisci, pubic symphysis","why":"Thick collagen resists compression and absorbs shock, the job of discs and menisci."},{"pt":"Chondrocytes in lacunae in all three, perichondrium around most cartilage but not fibrocartilage","why":"Every cartilage keeps its cells in lacunae, and the lab list marks fibrocartilage as the one with no perichondrium."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[30,31],"cids":["w2-compact-spongy","w2-bone-cells"],"t":"Bone cells, compact versus spongy","p":"List the four bone cell types with what each one does. Then write how compact bone differs from spongy bone in structure and in where you find it.","k":[{"pt":"Osteogenic cell: divides to produce new osteoblasts","why":"It is the stem cell of bone tissue, the first step of one lineage."},{"pt":"Osteoblast: builds new bone matrix, then becomes an osteocyte once surrounded","why":"Blasts build and cytes maintain, so the osteoblast becomes an osteocyte once it is walled in."},{"pt":"Osteocyte: maintains the matrix from inside a lacuna","why":"Linked to its neighbors by canaliculi, it keeps the matrix around it healthy."},{"pt":"Osteoclast: breaks down and resorbs bone, large and multinucleated, from a blood-cell line","why":"It comes from a different line than the other three and is the only one that removes bone."},{"pt":"Compact bone: osteons, dense, forming the outer shell and the wall of the diaphysis","why":"Compact bone gets its strength from tightly packed osteons."},{"pt":"Spongy bone: trabeculae, no osteons, red marrow in the spaces, filling the epiphyses","why":"Spongy bone keeps weight down with a lattice of struts lined up along lines of stress."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[29,26],"cids":["w2-long-bone-gross","w2-cartilage-types"],"t":"Bone as an organ","p":"Build a mind map with one long bone at the center. Branch to every tissue type present in it and name where each one sits.","k":[{"pt":"Osseous tissue: compact in the shaft, spongy in the ends","why":"Bone tissue is the main material, arranged two ways."},{"pt":"Hyaline cartilage at the articular surfaces and the epiphyseal plate","why":"Cartilage caps the joint ends and, in a child, forms the growth plate."},{"pt":"Connective tissue membranes: periosteum outside, endosteum inside","why":"A bone is wrapped and lined by connective tissue, part of what makes it an organ rather than a tissue."},{"pt":"Marrow in the cavities: red marrow forming blood cells, yellow marrow storing fat","why":"Marrow adds blood-forming tissue and fat storage to the same organ."},{"pt":"Blood vessels and nerves in the central canals","why":"The central canals carry vessels and nerves deep into the compact bone."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[32],"cids":["w2-ossification-growth"],"t":"Two ways to build bone","p":"Draw intramembranous and endochondral ossification side by side. Write what each one starts from and name two bones formed by each.","k":[{"pt":"Intramembranous ossification starts from a connective tissue membrane","why":"Bone forms directly in the membrane, with no cartilage step."},{"pt":"Intramembranous forms the flat bones of the skull, such as the parietal and frontal bones","why":"The flat skull bones form this way, and the infant skull still has fibrous membrane between them at the fontanelles."},{"pt":"Endochondral ossification replaces a hyaline cartilage model","why":"A cartilage version of the bone forms first, and bone takes its place."},{"pt":"Endochondral forms most bones of the body, such as the femur and humerus","why":"Most of the skeleton, including the long bones, starts as cartilage."},{"pt":"The epiphyseal plate is leftover cartilage where lengthening continues","why":"The plate is hyaline cartilage still being replaced, which ties endochondral formation to growth in length."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[32,27],"cids":["w2-ossification-growth","w2-cartilage-growth"],"t":"How a long bone gets longer","p":"Write how a long bone grows in length. Name the zones of the growth plate in order and say what happens in each.","k":[{"pt":"Growth in length is interstitial growth at the epiphyseal plate","why":"The plate is the one place a long bone lengthens."},{"pt":"Zones from the epiphysis to the diaphysis: resting, proliferation, hypertrophy, calcification, ossification","why":"The order runs from the end of the bone toward the shaft."},{"pt":"Resting zone anchors the plate to the epiphysis, and in the proliferation zone chondrocytes divide and stack in columns","why":"New cartilage cells are made on the epiphysis side of the plate."},{"pt":"Hypertrophy enlarges the chondrocytes, calcification hardens the matrix, ossification replaces cartilage with bone","why":"Cartilage is turned into bone on the diaphysis side, so the shaft lengthens."},{"pt":"When growth ends, the plate closes and becomes the epiphyseal line","why":"An epiphyseal line on an X-ray tells you the skeleton is mature."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L2-5","lv":2,"comps":[27,32],"cids":["w2-cartilage-growth","w2-ossification-growth"],"t":"Wider versus longer","p":"Draw a bone growing in width beside one growing in length. Name the process in each and say which cells are working on which surface.","k":[{"pt":"Width: appositional growth at the outer surface, beneath the periosteum","why":"New bone is added on the outside, so the shaft gets thicker."},{"pt":"Osteoblasts building new matrix at that outer surface","why":"Osteoblasts are the bone cells that build matrix, so they do the adding."},{"pt":"Length: interstitial growth at the epiphyseal plate","why":"The plate grows from within, and bone replaces it on the shaft side."},{"pt":"Chondrocytes dividing inside the plate drive the lengthening","why":"Interstitial means growth from inside, which is exactly where the chondrocytes divide."},{"pt":"Cartilage grows the same two ways: appositional from the perichondrium, interstitial from chondrocytes inside the matrix","why":"The two words mean the same two directions in cartilage and in bone."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L2-6","lv":2,"comps":[26],"cids":["w2-cartilage-types"],"t":"Feeding cartilage","p":"Draw a piece of hyaline cartilage with its covering. Label the covering, the cells, and the matrix, then write how nutrients reach the chondrocytes.","k":[{"pt":"Perichondrium, the connective tissue sheath around the cartilage","why":"It wraps most cartilage, and appositional growth adds new cartilage from it."},{"pt":"Chondrocytes sitting in lacunae","why":"Each cartilage cell lives in its own cavity in the matrix."},{"pt":"Matrix as a firm gel of ground substance with collagen","why":"The gel is what makes cartilage firm but bendable."},{"pt":"No blood vessels inside the cartilage","why":"Cartilage has no blood supply of its own."},{"pt":"Nutrients seep in through the matrix, so cartilage heals slowly","why":"With no vessels, everything has to diffuse in, which is why cartilage injuries take so long."},{"pt":"Articular cartilage is fed by the synovial fluid of the joint","why":"Joint cartilage gets its oxygen and nutrients from synovial fluid instead of from vessels."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[29,30],"cids":["w2-long-bone-gross","w2-compact-spongy"],"t":"Cut off from the supply","p":"Trace the route a nutrient takes from the nutrient artery to an osteocyte deep inside an osteon, naming every opening and canal on the way. Then predict what happens to the osteocytes in one region if their canaliculi are blocked, and what happens to the bone matrix around them.","k":[{"pt":"Nutrient artery entering through the nutrient foramen in the shaft","why":"The nutrient foramen is the opening in the shaft where the nutrient artery enters the bone."},{"pt":"Vessels running in the central canals of the osteons","why":"Central canals carry the blood vessels and nerves along the length of compact bone."},{"pt":"Perforating canals linking central canals crosswise","why":"They pass the supply from one osteon to the next, so no osteon is cut off."},{"pt":"Canaliculi carrying materials from the central canal out to the osteocytes in their lacunae","why":"Osteocytes are walled in by hard matrix, so the canaliculi are their only link to the supply and to each other."},{"pt":"Blocked canaliculi leave those osteocytes cut off from the supply and from their neighbors","why":"With the only channels closed, nothing can be exchanged between the osteocyte and the central canal."},{"pt":"The matrix in that region is no longer maintained","why":"Osteocytes maintain the bone matrix day to day, so when they are cut off the matrix around them loses its upkeep."}]},{"id":"m2-bone-tissue-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[32,29],"cids":["w2-ossification-growth","w2-long-bone-gross"],"t":"Read the X-ray","p":"Two X-rays of the knee are on the screen. One shows a gap near the end of the femur, and the other shows only a thin line there. Say what the gap and the line are, which person is the child, and why the gap shows up as a gap. Then predict what could happen if a fracture ran through the gap.","k":[{"pt":"The gap is the epiphyseal (growth) plate","why":"The plate is hyaline cartilage, not bone, so on an X-ray it looks like a gap."},{"pt":"The thin line is the epiphyseal line","why":"When growth in length ends, the plate closes and leaves this line."},{"pt":"The X-ray with the gap belongs to the child","why":"An open plate means the bone is still growing, and a line means the skeleton is mature."},{"pt":"A fracture through the plate can slow or stop growth in that bone","why":"The plate is where the bone lengthens, so damage there threatens future growth."},{"pt":"Pediatric fractures are described by how they cross the plate","why":"Because the plate matters so much for growth, a child's fracture is classified by its path across it."}]}]},{"id":"m2-skull","title":"The skull","mw":"2026-09-16","tr":"2026-09-15","prompts":[{"id":"m2-skull-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[33],"cids":["w2-skull-bones"],"t":"Cranial then facial","p":"List the cranial bones, then list the facial bones. Mark which ones are paired.","k":[{"pt":"Eight cranial bones: frontal, two parietal, two temporal, occipital, sphenoid, ethmoid","why":"These form the braincase that encloses and protects the brain."},{"pt":"Fourteen facial bones: two maxillae, two palatine, two zygomatic, two nasal, two lacrimal, two inferior nasal conchae, vomer, mandible","why":"These frame the face and hold the teeth."},{"pt":"Single cranial bones marked: frontal, occipital, sphenoid, ethmoid","why":"The single bones sit on the midline, which is a quick way to check your marks."},{"pt":"Single facial bones marked: vomer and mandible","why":"Six facial bones come in pairs, and only these two are single."},{"pt":"Total of 22 skull bones","why":"Eight plus fourteen is 22, a check that nothing is missing or counted twice."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[33,34,35],"cids":["w2-skull-bones","w2-skull-markings","w2-sutures-fontanelles"],"t":"Skull, lateral view","p":"Draw the skull from the side. Label every bone you can and every suture you can.","k":[{"pt":"Frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, sphenoid","why":"These cranial bones all show on the side of the skull."},{"pt":"Zygomatic, maxilla, mandible, nasal, lacrimal","why":"These facial bones show in profile, with the lacrimal tucked into the wall of the orbit."},{"pt":"Coronal, squamous, lambdoid sutures","why":"These three show from the side, while the sagittal suture runs along the top."},{"pt":"Pterion, where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid meet","why":"It is the thinnest part of the skull, with the middle meningeal artery just beneath it."},{"pt":"Mastoid process, styloid process, external acoustic meatus","why":"These temporal bone markings cluster around the ear."},{"pt":"Zygomatic arch","why":"The zygomatic process of the temporal bone joins the zygomatic bone to make the arch."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[37,36],"cids":["w2-skull-foramina","w2-skull-cavities"],"t":"Openings and what goes through","p":"Name every skull foramen or opening you can and write what passes through it. Then list the bones that build the orbit.","k":[{"pt":"Foramen magnum (occipital): spinal cord and vertebral arteries","why":"It is the one large opening between the brain and the spinal cord."},{"pt":"Optic canal (sphenoid): optic nerve, CN II. Superior orbital fissure (sphenoid): nerves to the eye, including those that move it","why":"Both openings are in the sphenoid and both serve the eye, which is why they are easy to mix up."},{"pt":"Cribriform foramina (ethmoid): olfactory nerve filaments, CN I","why":"The plate is perforated like a sieve for the smell fibers passing up to the brain."},{"pt":"Foramen ovale (sphenoid): a branch of the trigeminal nerve to the lower jaw","why":"It is one of the openings in the middle of the cranial floor."},{"pt":"Internal acoustic meatus (temporal): hearing and facial nerves. Carotid canal (temporal): internal carotid artery. Jugular foramen: internal jugular vein","why":"The temporal bone houses the ear and sits beside the jugular foramen, which lies between the temporal and occipital bones."},{"pt":"Orbit: frontal, sphenoid, zygomatic, maxilla, lacrimal, ethmoid, palatine","why":"Seven bones meet to build one eye socket."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[34,37],"cids":["w2-skull-markings","w2-skull-foramina"],"t":"The cranial floor","p":"Draw the inside of the cranial base from above. Outline the three cranial fossae, name each, write which bones form it, and name one landmark or opening in it.","k":[{"pt":"Anterior cranial fossa: orbital plates of the frontal bone, the ethmoid, the lesser wings of the sphenoid","why":"The frontal orbital plates and the lesser wings form the highest step of the floor."},{"pt":"Anterior fossa landmarks: cribriform plate and crista galli","why":"The ethmoid sits in the middle of the anterior floor."},{"pt":"Middle cranial fossa: body and greater wings of the sphenoid, and the temporal bones","why":"The sphenoid is the central wedge of the cranial base, so it builds most of the middle step."},{"pt":"Middle fossa landmarks: sella turcica holding the pituitary, optic canal, foramen ovale","why":"The pituitary sits in the deep seat of the sella, right in the center of the floor."},{"pt":"Posterior cranial fossa: mostly the occipital bone","why":"The occipital bone forms the back wall and much of the cranial base."},{"pt":"Posterior fossa landmarks: foramen magnum, jugular foramen, hypoglossal canal","why":"The openings for the spinal cord, the internal jugular vein, and CN XII leave through the back of the floor."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[34,37],"cids":["w2-skull-markings","w2-skull-foramina"],"t":"The sphenoid alone","p":"Draw the sphenoid bone by itself and label every part and opening on it.","k":[{"pt":"Body containing the sphenoid sinus","why":"The body is the central cube of the bone, hollowed out by a sinus."},{"pt":"Sella turcica with its hypophyseal fossa, dorsum sellae, and tuberculum sellae","why":"The deep seat of the saddle cradles the pituitary gland."},{"pt":"Greater wings and lesser wings","why":"The greater wings form part of the cranial floor, the orbit, and the side wall, and the lesser wings form part of the anterior fossa."},{"pt":"Pterygoid processes with medial and lateral pterygoid plates","why":"These downward projections anchor the chewing muscles."},{"pt":"Optic canal and superior orbital fissure","why":"Both pass nerves to the eye."},{"pt":"Foramen rotundum, foramen ovale, foramen spinosum","why":"These openings let nerves and vessels pass through the middle of the cranial floor."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L1-6","lv":1,"comps":[34,36],"cids":["w2-skull-markings","w2-skull-cavities"],"t":"The ethmoid alone","p":"Draw the ethmoid bone and label its parts. Then write which three cavities it helps build.","k":[{"pt":"Cribriform plate with its olfactory foramina","why":"The olfactory nerve filaments pass through the holes in this horizontal plate."},{"pt":"Crista galli","why":"This upright ridge is where the falx cerebri, a fold of the brain's covering, attaches."},{"pt":"Perpendicular plate","why":"It forms the upper part of the nasal septum."},{"pt":"Lateral masses with the ethmoidal air cells","why":"The air cells are the ethmoidal sinuses, between the orbits."},{"pt":"Superior and middle nasal conchae","why":"These scroll-shaped projections stick into the nasal cavity."},{"pt":"Helps build the cranial floor, the orbit, and the nasal cavity","why":"As the deepest cranial bone, sitting between the orbits, it touches all three spaces."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L1-7","lv":1,"comps":[34],"cids":["w2-skull-markings"],"t":"Mandible and the jaw joint","p":"Draw the mandible and label every named landmark. Then mark where it articulates with the skull and name that joint.","k":[{"pt":"Body, ramus, angle","why":"The body holds the lower teeth, the ramus rises on each side, and they meet at the angle."},{"pt":"Condylar process and coronoid process with the mandibular notch between them","why":"The condylar process forms the jaw joint, and the coronoid process is where the temporalis attaches."},{"pt":"Mental protuberance and mental foramen","why":"The mental foramen passes the nerve and vessels to the chin and lower lip."},{"pt":"Mandibular foramen on the inner surface of the ramus","why":"It is the entry for the nerve to the lower teeth and the target of a dental block."},{"pt":"Alveolar process holding the teeth","why":"The alveolar process is the ridge of tooth sockets."},{"pt":"Temporomandibular joint, the condyle in the mandibular fossa of the temporal bone","why":"This joint is what makes the mandible the only freely movable bone of the skull."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L1-8","lv":1,"comps":[33],"cids":["w2-skull-bones"],"t":"The bone that touches nothing","p":"Draw the hyoid bone, label its parts, and write where it sits and what attaches to it.","k":[{"pt":"Body, greater horns (cornua), lesser horns (cornua)","why":"The body is the front bar of the U, and the greater and lesser horns project back from it on each side."},{"pt":"Sits in the anterior neck, below the mandible","why":"It belongs to the neck, which is why it is counted separately from the 22 skull bones."},{"pt":"Does not articulate with any other bone","why":"It is the one bone in this unit with no bony joint at all."},{"pt":"Slung by ligaments from the styloid processes","why":"Ligaments hang it from the temporal bones, so it hangs rather than joins."},{"pt":"Anchors the tongue and muscles of the neck and larynx","why":"It is a floating anchor point for the muscles that move the tongue and larynx."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L1-9","lv":1,"comps":[35],"cids":["w2-sutures-fontanelles"],"t":"Sutures and soft spots","p":"Draw the skull from above and from the side. Label every suture. Then name the fontanelles of a newborn skull and say why they exist.","k":[{"pt":"Coronal suture between the frontal bone and the two parietals","why":"It crosses the top of the skull just behind the forehead."},{"pt":"Sagittal suture between the two parietals","why":"It runs along the midline of the skull roof."},{"pt":"Lambdoid suture between the parietals and the occipital","why":"It crosses the back of the skull."},{"pt":"Squamous suture between a parietal and a temporal bone","why":"There is one on each side, low on the side of the skull."},{"pt":"Anterior, posterior, two anterolateral (sphenoidal), and two posterolateral (mastoid) fontanelles","why":"The anterior is the largest and closes last, at about 18 to 24 months."},{"pt":"They are fibrous membrane gaps that let the skull grow with the brain","why":"The infant skull bones have not met yet, so membrane fills the gaps until the bones grow together."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L2-2","lv":1,"comps":[36],"cids":["w2-skull-cavities"],"t":"Sinuses, septum, and palate","p":"Name the four paranasal sinuses and the bone each one sits in. Then write which bones build the nasal septum and which build the hard palate, front to back.","k":[{"pt":"Frontal sinus in the frontal bone, above the orbits","why":"Each sinus is named for the bone that holds it."},{"pt":"Ethmoidal sinuses (air cells) in the ethmoid, between the orbits","why":"They sit in the lateral masses of the ethmoid."},{"pt":"Sphenoidal sinus in the body of the sphenoid","why":"The body of the sphenoid is hollowed out by this sinus."},{"pt":"Maxillary sinuses in the maxillae, the largest, in the cheek region","why":"Their size and position put them right above the upper teeth."},{"pt":"Nasal septum: perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, vomer, septal cartilage","why":"Two bones and a cartilage make the partition, with the ethmoid plate above the vomer."},{"pt":"Hard palate: maxillae in front, palatine bones behind","why":"The palatine processes of the maxillae form the front of the roof of the mouth, and the palatine bones finish it at the back."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[33,36,37],"cids":["w2-skull-bones","w2-skull-cavities","w2-skull-foramina"],"t":"What the skull is for","p":"Build a mind map with the skull at the center. Branch to each job it does and name the specific structures that do it.","k":[{"pt":"Protection: the cranium and its fossae around the brain","why":"The eight cranial bones form a closed case around the brain."},{"pt":"Passage: foramina and canals with what runs through them, such as the optic canal and the optic nerve","why":"Every nerve and vessel entering or leaving the skull needs a named opening."},{"pt":"Housing the senses: the orbits, the nasal cavity, and the temporal bone for the ear","why":"Seven bones build each orbit, and the petrous part of the temporal bone holds the middle and inner ear."},{"pt":"Chewing: the mandible, the temporomandibular joint, the coronoid process, the pterygoid processes","why":"The joint and the muscle attachment sites are what turn the mandible into a working jaw."},{"pt":"Lightening the skull: the paranasal sinuses","why":"Air-filled spaces in four bones lighten the skull and drain into the nasal cavity."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[35],"cids":["w2-sutures-fontanelles"],"t":"Fontanelles in closing order","p":"Put the four fontanelles of an infant skull in order from the first to close to the last. For each, write where it sits by the bones around it and about when it closes. Then say which one is the largest.","k":[{"pt":"Posterior (occipital) fontanelle first, about 2 months, where the parietal bones meet the occipital bone","why":"It sits at the back of the skull roof and is the first to close."},{"pt":"Anterolateral (sphenoidal) fontanelles next, about 3 months, one on each side of the skull","why":"They are paired, one on each side, and close soon after the posterior one."},{"pt":"Posterolateral (mastoid) fontanelles, closing from 1 to 2 months and complete by about 12 months, behind each ear","why":"They begin closing early but take until about the first birthday to finish."},{"pt":"Anterior fontanelle last, about 18 to 24 months, where the frontal and parietal bones meet","why":"It is the last to close, so it is the soft spot still open well into the second year."},{"pt":"The anterior fontanelle is the largest","why":"Being the largest and the last to close makes it the one a clinician checks on a newborn."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[35,34],"cids":["w2-sutures-fontanelles","w2-skull-markings"],"t":"A blow to the side of the head","p":"A baseball hits a player on the side of the head, just above the zygomatic arch and in front of the ear. Name the junction that sits there, the four bones that meet at it, and why a blow there is dangerous. Then name the two bones whose processes form the arch the ball just missed.","k":[{"pt":"The pterion","why":"It is the H-shaped junction on the side of the skull."},{"pt":"Frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet there","why":"Four bones meeting in one small spot is part of what makes it a weak point."},{"pt":"It is the thinnest part of the skull","why":"Thin bone breaks more easily under a direct hit."},{"pt":"The middle meningeal artery runs just beneath it, so a fracture can tear it and bleed inside the skull","why":"A bleed inside the rigid skull has nowhere to go, which is why this injury is an emergency."},{"pt":"Zygomatic arch: the zygomatic process of the temporal bone and the temporal process of the zygomatic bone","why":"The two processes reach toward each other and join to make the arch."}]},{"id":"m2-skull-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[36],"cids":["w2-skull-cavities"],"t":"Where did the infection go","p":"A patient has an infected upper tooth, and a week later has pain and pressure in the cheek. Predict which space the infection reached and why it could get there. Then predict where a cold in the nose could spread, and name the bone that holds each sinus you list.","k":[{"pt":"The maxillary sinus","why":"It is the largest sinus and sits in the maxilla, in the cheek region."},{"pt":"The maxillary sinus sits so close to the upper teeth that a tooth infection can reach it","why":"The alveolar process holding the upper teeth is part of the same bone as the sinus."},{"pt":"A cold can spread into the paranasal sinuses as sinusitis","why":"The sinuses drain into the nasal cavity through narrow openings, so infection can travel back into them."},{"pt":"Frontal sinus in the frontal bone, ethmoidal sinuses in the ethmoid, sphenoidal sinus in the sphenoid, maxillary sinuses in the maxillae","why":"Every sinus is named for its bone, so naming the sinus names the bone."},{"pt":"Cheek pain matches the maxillary sinus","why":"The maxillary sinuses sit in the cheek region, so that is where the pressure is felt."}]}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax","title":"Vertebral column and thoracic cage","mw":"2026-09-21","tr":"2026-09-17","prompts":[{"id":"m2-spine-thorax-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[38,40],"cids":["w2-spine-regions","w2-regional-vertebrae"],"t":"Counts and giveaways","p":"Write the vertebral counts by region, top to bottom. Then write one feature that tells you a vertebra came from each region.","k":[{"pt":"7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar","why":"These are the 24 separate vertebrae of the adult column."},{"pt":"Sacrum of 5 fused and coccyx of 4 fused","why":"Fusion leaves 26 bones in the adult column from the 33 vertebrae of early development."},{"pt":"Cervical: transverse foramina, often a bifid spinous process","why":"Only cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina, which pass the vertebral arteries."},{"pt":"Thoracic: costal facets, long spinous process angled sharply down","why":"Costal facets are where ribs articulate, and only the thoracic region carries ribs."},{"pt":"Lumbar: thick kidney-shaped body, short blunt spinous process","why":"Lumbar vertebrae carry the most weight, so they are the largest, with no costal facets and no transverse foramina."}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[39,40],"cids":["w2-typical-vertebra","w2-regional-vertebrae"],"t":"Typical vertebra from above","p":"Draw a typical vertebra viewed from above and label every part. Then mark what you would add to make it cervical.","k":[{"pt":"Body at the front","why":"The body, or centrum, is the thick weight-bearing part."},{"pt":"Vertebral arch made of pedicles and laminae","why":"Pedicles connect the arch to the body, and laminae close it behind."},{"pt":"Vertebral foramen in the middle","why":"The spinal cord runs through it, and stacked foramina form the vertebral canal."},{"pt":"Spinous process pointing back, transverse processes pointing to the sides","why":"These three projections stick out from the arch, one backward and two to the sides."},{"pt":"Superior and inferior articular processes","why":"They join one vertebra to the next."},{"pt":"Cervical additions: a transverse foramen in each transverse process, a bifid spinous process, a small body","why":"These features turn a typical vertebra into a cervical one."}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[41],"cids":["w2-thoracic-cage"],"t":"The thoracic cage","p":"Dump the thoracic cage: parts of the sternum, the rib groups, and the parts of a typical rib.","k":[{"pt":"Sternum: manubrium, body, xiphoid process","why":"Three fused parts make the breastbone, top to bottom."},{"pt":"Jugular notch and sternal angle","why":"The notch is the dip at the top of the manubrium, and the angle is where the manubrium meets the body, level with rib 2."},{"pt":"True ribs 1 to 7, each reaching the sternum by its own costal cartilage","why":"A direct attachment to the sternum is what makes a rib true."},{"pt":"False ribs 8 to 12, with 8 to 10 joining the cartilage of the rib above","why":"False ribs do not reach the sternum on their own."},{"pt":"Floating ribs 11 and 12, with no anterior attachment","why":"They are the subset of false ribs that attach to nothing in front."},{"pt":"Rib parts: head, neck, tubercle, shaft, costal cartilage","why":"The head and tubercle meet the vertebra, and the costal cartilage carries the rib toward the sternum."}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[40],"cids":["w2-regional-vertebrae"],"t":"Sacrum and coccyx","p":"Draw the sacrum from the front and from the back. Label every named feature, then add the coccyx.","k":[{"pt":"Base at the top and apex at the bottom","why":"The sacrum is a triangle, broad at the top and pointed at the bottom."},{"pt":"Sacral promontory","why":"It is the anterior ridge of the first sacral vertebra."},{"pt":"Anterior and posterior sacral foramina","why":"These openings pass the sacral nerves."},{"pt":"Median and lateral sacral crests, sacral canal, sacral hiatus","why":"The median crest is the fused spinous processes, and the canal continues the vertebral canal down to the hiatus."},{"pt":"Auricular surface for the sacroiliac joint","why":"This ear-shaped surface meets the matching auricular surface of the ilium."},{"pt":"Coccyx of 4 fused vertebrae below the apex","why":"The coccyx is the tailbone, an attachment point for pelvic ligaments and muscles."}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[41],"cids":["w2-thoracic-cage"],"t":"Sternum and rib joints","p":"Draw the sternum and label its three parts and its landmarks. Then write what a typical rib meets at its back end and at its front end.","k":[{"pt":"Manubrium, body, xiphoid process","why":"These are the three fused parts of the breastbone."},{"pt":"Jugular notch, clavicular notches, costal notches","why":"The clavicles meet the manubrium at the clavicular notches, and costal cartilages meet the sternum at the costal notches."},{"pt":"Sternal angle level with the second rib","why":"It is the starting point clinicians use to count ribs."},{"pt":"Back end: the rib head meets the vertebral bodies and the tubercle meets a transverse process","why":"A rib touches its vertebrae at two spots."},{"pt":"Front end: costal cartilage carries the rib toward the sternum","why":"The costal cartilage is hyaline cartilage linking the bony rib to the sternum."},{"pt":"Ribs 8 to 10 join the cartilage of the rib above","why":"That indirect connection is what makes them false ribs."}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[38],"cids":["w2-spine-regions"],"t":"The column from the side","p":"Draw the whole vertebral column from the side. Mark the four curvatures, name each, and label which are primary and which are secondary.","k":[{"pt":"Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral curvatures named","why":"Four front-to-back curves let the column act like a spring, giving it strength, flexibility, and balance."},{"pt":"Thoracic and sacral are primary curves","why":"The primary curves are the two the spine already has before an infant ever lifts its head."},{"pt":"Cervical is secondary, developing as an infant holds up its head","why":"Lifting the head builds the neck curve."},{"pt":"Lumbar is secondary, developing as a child stands and walks","why":"Standing upright builds the lower back curve."},{"pt":"Kyphosis is an exaggerated thoracic curve, lordosis an exaggerated lumbar curve, scoliosis a sideways curve","why":"Each abnormal curve is named for where and which way the spine bends too far."}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[40],"cids":["w2-regional-vertebrae"],"t":"Atlas and axis","p":"Draw C1 and C2 from above, side by side. Label every part and write what movement each joint allows.","k":[{"pt":"Atlas (C1): a ring with no body and no spinous process","why":"Without a body, the atlas is a ring that holds up the skull."},{"pt":"Atlas parts: anterior and posterior arches, superior articular facets, facet for the dens","why":"The superior facets meet the occipital condyles, and the facet for the dens faces the peg of the axis."},{"pt":"Axis (C2): the dens, an upward bony peg","why":"The dens rises up into the ring of the atlas."},{"pt":"Atlanto-occipital joint: the yes nod","why":"The occipital condyles rock on the atlas, which nods the head."},{"pt":"Atlanto-axial joint: the no rotation","why":"The atlas pivots around the dens, which turns the head side to side."},{"pt":"A ligament holds the dens against the atlas","why":"The dens sits right next to the brainstem, which is why a fracture of it is a serious injury."}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[39],"cids":["w2-typical-vertebra"],"t":"The disc","p":"Draw two vertebrae with the disc between them, cut in half. Label the parts of the disc and mark where a herniation usually goes and what it presses on.","k":[{"pt":"A fibrocartilage disc between the two vertebral bodies","why":"The disc is a fibrocartilage pad that absorbs shock between the bodies."},{"pt":"Anulus fibrosus, the tough outer ring","why":"The ring holds the soft center in place."},{"pt":"Nucleus pulposus, the soft gel core","why":"The gel center is what pushes out when the ring tears."},{"pt":"Herniation: the nucleus pulposus pushes through a torn anulus fibrosus toward an intervertebral foramen","why":"The intervertebral foramina are where spinal nerves exit, so that is where a bulge does damage."},{"pt":"It presses on a spinal nerve, so pain travels down a limb","why":"The nerve runs out into the limb, so pressure where it exits is felt along its path."}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[38,40],"cids":["w2-spine-regions","w2-regional-vertebrae"],"t":"Region by region","p":"Build a mind map with the vertebral column at the center. One branch per region. On each branch put the count, the identifying feature, and what that region's shape is built for.","k":[{"pt":"Cervical: 7, transverse foramina, small bodies in the neck","why":"Small bodies suit the neck, and the transverse foramina carry the vertebral arteries up to the brain."},{"pt":"Thoracic: 12, costal facets, each one holding a pair of ribs","why":"Each thoracic vertebra articulates with a pair of ribs, which is what the facets are for."},{"pt":"Lumbar: 5, thick kidney-shaped bodies, built for weight bearing","why":"The lower back carries the most body weight, so its bodies are the largest."},{"pt":"Sacrum: 5 fused, wedged between the hip bones","why":"Its auricular surfaces join the hip bones at the sacroiliac joints."},{"pt":"Coccyx: 4 fused, an attachment point for pelvic ligaments and muscles","why":"It is the small fused tailbone at the bottom of the column."}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[40],"cids":["w2-regional-vertebrae"],"t":"Three loose vertebrae","p":"Three loose vertebrae are on a lab table. Vertebra A has a hole in each transverse process. Vertebra B has small smooth facets on the sides of its body and a long spinous process pointing sharply down. Vertebra C has a huge kidney-shaped body and a short blunt spinous process. Name the region of each, the single feature that proved it, and one feature you would check to confirm. Then say which one could be C1 and what it would be missing.","k":[{"pt":"A: cervical, proved by the transverse foramina","why":"Only cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina, which carry the vertebral arteries."},{"pt":"A confirmed by a small oval body or a bifid spinous process","why":"Cervical vertebrae are the smallest and often have a forked spine."},{"pt":"B: thoracic, proved by the costal facets","why":"Costal facets are where ribs articulate, and only thoracic vertebrae carry ribs."},{"pt":"B confirmed by a heart-shaped body and the long downward spinous process","why":"The steep spine and heart-shaped body are the thoracic shape."},{"pt":"C: lumbar, proved by the massive body with no costal facets and no transverse foramina","why":"Lumbar vertebrae are built for weight bearing, so they are the biggest."},{"pt":"Only A could be C1, and C1 would have no body and no spinous process","why":"The atlas is a ring that holds up the skull, so it lacks the parts a typical vertebra has."}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[41],"cids":["w2-thoracic-cage"],"t":"Counting ribs","p":"A nurse places her fingers on the sternal angle to start counting ribs. Name the rib level with her fingers and explain why the sternal angle is used as the starting point. Then, counting down the side of the chest, say where the true ribs end, where the false ribs begin, and which ribs never reach the front. Finally, name the landmark at the lower tip of the sternum used for hand position in CPR.","k":[{"pt":"The second rib is level with the sternal angle","why":"The sternal angle is where the manubrium meets the body, at the level of rib 2."},{"pt":"The angle is used because it is a ridge you can feel through the skin","why":"A landmark you can feel gives a reliable place to start counting."},{"pt":"Ribs 1 to 7 are true ribs","why":"Each true rib reaches the sternum by its own costal cartilage."},{"pt":"False ribs begin at rib 8","why":"From rib 8 down, ribs do not reach the sternum directly, and 8 to 10 join the cartilage above."},{"pt":"Ribs 11 and 12 float, with no attachment at the front","why":"Floating ribs have no anterior attachment, so they cannot be followed to the sternum."},{"pt":"The xiphoid process","why":"The xiphoid is the small inferior tip of the sternum that guides hand position for chest compressions."}]}]},{"id":"m2-upper-extremity","title":"Pectoral girdle and upper limb bones","mw":"2026-09-23","tr":"2026-09-22","prompts":[{"id":"m2-upper-extremity-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[42],"cids":["w2-pectoral-girdle"],"t":"The scapula","p":"Draw the scapula from the back and from the side. Label every named feature.","k":[{"pt":"Superior, medial (vertebral), and lateral (axillary) borders","why":"The scapula is a triangle, so it has three borders."},{"pt":"Superior angle and inferior angle","why":"The angles are the corners where the borders meet."},{"pt":"Spine, acromion, coracoid process","why":"The spine crosses the back of the bone and ends in the acromion at the point of the shoulder, while the coracoid hooks forward."},{"pt":"Supraspinous, infraspinous, and subscapular fossae","why":"These are the hollows above the spine, below the spine, and on the front surface."},{"pt":"Glenoid cavity with the supraglenoid and infraglenoid tubercles","why":"The shallow socket receives the head of the humerus, with a tubercle above it and one below it."},{"pt":"Suprascapular notch on the superior border","why":"It is a notch on the top edge, easy to miss in a back view."}]},{"id":"m2-upper-extremity-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[43],"cids":["w2-arm-forearm"],"t":"The humerus","p":"Draw the humerus from the front and from the back. Label every landmark on both sides.","k":[{"pt":"Head, anatomical neck, surgical neck","why":"The surgical neck is the narrowing below the tubercles and a common fracture site."},{"pt":"Greater and lesser tubercles with the intertubercular sulcus between them","why":"The sulcus holds a biceps tendon between the two bumps."},{"pt":"Deltoid tuberosity on the shaft, radial groove on the back","why":"The deltoid attaches at the tuberosity, and the radial nerve runs in the groove."},{"pt":"Capitulum laterally and trochlea medially","why":"The capitulum meets the radius and the trochlea meets the ulna."},{"pt":"Medial and lateral epicondyles","why":"They are the projections above the trochlea and the capitulum, and the ulnar nerve runs behind the medial one."},{"pt":"Coronoid and radial fossae in front, olecranon fossa behind","why":"The olecranon fossa receives the ulna when the elbow straightens."}]},{"id":"m2-upper-extremity-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[43],"cids":["w2-arm-forearm"],"t":"Radius and ulna","p":"Draw the radius and ulna in anatomical position, label both fully, and show how they articulate with each other and with the humerus.","k":[{"pt":"Ulna: olecranon, coronoid process, trochlear notch, radial notch","why":"The trochlear notch grips the trochlea of the humerus, which makes the ulna the elbow hinge."},{"pt":"Ulna at the wrist: head and a styloid process on the medial side","why":"The ulna is on the little-finger side."},{"pt":"Radius: head, neck, radial tuberosity","why":"The disc-shaped head meets the capitulum and sits in the radial notch of the ulna."},{"pt":"Radius at the wrist: styloid process on the lateral side, ulnar notch on the medial side","why":"The radius is on the thumb side, and its ulnar notch receives the head of the ulna."},{"pt":"Interosseous membrane joining the shafts","why":"This fibrous sheet binds the two bones together and gives muscles attachment."},{"pt":"Proximal and distal radioulnar joints","why":"The radial head sits in the radial notch at the elbow, and the ulnar head sits in the ulnar notch at the wrist."}]},{"id":"m2-upper-extremity-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[42,43,44],"cids":["w2-pectoral-girdle","w2-arm-forearm","w2-hand-bones"],"t":"Upper limb, shoulder to fingertips","p":"List the bones of the upper limb from the shoulder to the fingertips, including the carpals in their two rows.","k":[{"pt":"Pectoral girdle: clavicle and scapula","why":"The girdle attaches the upper limb to the trunk."},{"pt":"Humerus in the arm, radius and ulna in the forearm","why":"One bone runs from shoulder to elbow and two run from elbow to wrist."},{"pt":"Proximal carpal row: scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform","why":"So Long To Pinky runs this row from the thumb side to the little finger."},{"pt":"Distal carpal row: hamate, capitate, trapezoid, trapezium (in either direction)","why":"Here Comes The Thumb runs this row from the little finger side to the thumb."},{"pt":"Metacarpals 1 to 5, then the phalanges","why":"Metacarpals form the palm and are numbered from the thumb, and the phalanges are the finger bones."}]},{"id":"m2-upper-extremity-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[44],"cids":["w2-hand-bones"],"t":"The hand","p":"Draw the bones of the wrist and hand in position and label every one. Keep the carpals in their correct two rows.","k":[{"pt":"Proximal row, thumb side to little finger: scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform","why":"The scaphoid sits at the base of the thumb, which is why it lies under the anatomical snuffbox."},{"pt":"Distal row, little finger side to thumb: hamate, capitate, trapezoid, trapezium","why":"The capitate, the largest carpal, sits in the middle of this row."},{"pt":"Metacarpals numbered 1 to 5 starting at the thumb","why":"Counting from the thumb keeps the numbering the same on both hands in anatomical position."},{"pt":"Fingers 2 to 5 have proximal, middle, and distal phalanges, and the thumb has only proximal and distal","why":"The thumb, the pollex, has no middle phalanx."},{"pt":"Fourteen phalanges in each hand","why":"Four fingers with three each plus a thumb with two makes fourteen."}]},{"id":"m2-upper-extremity-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[45],"cids":["w2-bone-markings-vocab"],"t":"Bone markings","p":"Name as many bone markings as you can, sorted into projections, depressions, and openings. Give an example bone for each.","k":[{"pt":"Projections: process, tubercle, tuberosity, head, epicondyle, and others such as trochanter, crest, spine, condyle","why":"Projections stick out from the bone, often where muscles or ligaments attach."},{"pt":"Depressions: fossa and sulcus (groove), and others such as a notch","why":"Depressions dip into the bone, such as the olecranon fossa that receives the ulna."},{"pt":"Openings: foramen, and others such as a canal, meatus, or fissure","why":"Openings let nerves and vessels pass through bone."},{"pt":"Facet named as a smooth, nearly flat articular surface","why":"A facet is where one bone meets another, like the articular facets of the vertebrae."},{"pt":"A named example for most terms, such as mastoid process, greater tubercle, deltoid tuberosity, olecranon fossa, intertubercular sulcus, nutrient foramen","why":"An example proves you can find the marking on a real bone, which is how it is tested."}]},{"id":"m2-upper-extremity-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[43],"cids":["w2-arm-forearm"],"t":"Radius or ulna","p":"Compare the radius and the ulna. For each, write which side of the forearm it is on, its markings at the elbow end, its markings at the wrist end, and which humerus surface it meets. Then give one feature that tells them apart at the elbow and one at the wrist.","k":[{"pt":"Radius lateral (thumb side), ulna medial (little-finger side)","why":"Fix anatomical position first, palms forward, and the sides follow."},{"pt":"Radius at the elbow: disc-shaped head, neck, radial tuberosity","why":"The round head lets the radius turn against the capitulum."},{"pt":"Ulna at the elbow: olecranon, coronoid process, trochlear notch, radial notch","why":"The trochlear notch wraps the trochlea, making the ulna the hinge of the elbow."},{"pt":"At the wrist: the radius has a lateral styloid process and the ulnar notch, the ulna has its head and a medial styloid process","why":"The two styloid processes sit on opposite sides of the wrist."},{"pt":"Radius meets the capitulum, ulna meets the trochlea","why":"The capitulum is lateral and pairs with the lateral bone, and the trochlea is medial and pairs with the medial bone."},{"pt":"Tell-apart features: the olecranon at the elbow belongs to the ulna, the ulnar notch at the wrist belongs to the radius","why":"The point of the elbow is always ulna, and the notch at the wrist that holds the ulna is always on the radius."}]},{"id":"m2-upper-extremity-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[44],"cids":["w2-hand-bones"],"t":"The carpal tunnel","p":"Draw the wrist in cross section. Label what forms the roof of the carpal tunnel, what forms its floor, and what passes through it. Then explain why narrowing the tunnel causes symptoms in the hand.","k":[{"pt":"Roof: the flexor retinaculum, a strong fibrous band","why":"The band arches over the carpal bones to close the tunnel."},{"pt":"Floor: the carpal bones","why":"The retinaculum roofs the carpals, so the bones form the floor."},{"pt":"Contents: the long flexor tendons of the fingers and thumb","why":"The tendons travel to the fingers and thumb, so they pass through the tunnel."},{"pt":"Contents: the median nerve","why":"The median nerve shares the tunnel with the tendons."},{"pt":"Narrowing compresses the median nerve, which is carpal tunnel syndrome","why":"With a tough fibrous roof over a bony floor, anything that narrows the space squeezes the nerve."}]},{"id":"m2-upper-extremity-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[44,42],"cids":["w2-hand-bones","w2-pectoral-girdle"],"t":"A fall on an outstretched hand","p":"A skateboarder falls on an outstretched hand. The wrist is tender in the hollow at the base of the thumb. Name the hollow, the carpal bone in its floor, and why a missed fracture of that bone is a problem. Then name the bone of the pectoral girdle most likely to break in the same kind of fall and the part of it that usually breaks.","k":[{"pt":"The anatomical snuffbox","why":"It is the hollow at the base of the thumb."},{"pt":"The scaphoid sits in its floor","why":"The scaphoid is in the proximal row on the thumb side, right under the snuffbox."},{"pt":"The scaphoid is the most frequently fractured carpal","why":"A fall on an outstretched hand drives force into it."},{"pt":"Its blood supply is poor, so a missed fracture may fail to heal","why":"Bone with a poor blood supply repairs poorly, so a scaphoid fracture that goes unnoticed may never heal."},{"pt":"The clavicle, usually in its middle third","why":"It is the most commonly broken bone in the body, the slender strut that takes the force of a fall onto the arm."}]},{"id":"m2-upper-extremity-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[43],"cids":["w2-arm-forearm"],"t":"Nerves next to bone","p":"Two patients hurt the arm. The first fractures the humerus at the narrowing just below the tubercles. The second knocks the inside of the elbow and feels a jolt run to the little finger. For each, name the bony landmark involved and the nerve that runs right next to it, and explain why that nerve matters. Then name the nerve that runs in the groove on the back of the humeral shaft.","k":[{"pt":"First patient: the surgical neck of the humerus","why":"The surgical neck is the narrowing below the tubercles and a common fracture site."},{"pt":"The axillary nerve runs close to the surgical neck, so it is checked for injury","why":"A nerve lying against the bone can be damaged when the bone breaks."},{"pt":"Second patient: the medial epicondyle of the humerus","why":"This is the projection on the inner side of the elbow, above the trochlea."},{"pt":"The ulnar nerve passes just behind the medial epicondyle, the funny bone","why":"The nerve is close enough to the surface there that a knock sends a jolt along its path to the little finger."},{"pt":"The radial nerve runs in the radial groove on the posterior shaft","why":"The groove marks the path of the radial nerve along the back of the bone."}]}]},{"id":"m2-lower-extremity","title":"Pelvic girdle and lower limb bones","mw":"2026-09-28","tr":"2026-09-24","prompts":[{"id":"m2-lower-extremity-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[46,47,48,49],"cids":["w2-pelvic-girdle","w2-thigh-knee","w2-leg-bones","w2-foot-bones"],"t":"Lower limb, hip to toes","p":"List the bones of the lower limb from the hip to the toes, including the tarsals.","k":[{"pt":"Coxal (hip) bone: ilium, ischium, pubis","why":"The three fuse at the acetabulum to make one hip bone."},{"pt":"Femur in the thigh, patella at the knee","why":"The femur is the longest and strongest bone, and the patella forms inside the quadriceps tendon."},{"pt":"Tibia and fibula in the leg","why":"The tibia is the medial, weight-bearing shin bone, and the fibula is the slender lateral bone."},{"pt":"Tarsals: talus, calcaneus, navicular, cuboid, medial, intermediate, and lateral cuneiforms","why":"Seven tarsals form the ankle and the back of the foot, with the talus taking the weight from the tibia."},{"pt":"Metatarsals 1 to 5, then the phalanges","why":"Metatarsals are numbered from the great toe, and the hallux has only two phalanges."}]},{"id":"m2-lower-extremity-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[46],"cids":["w2-pelvic-girdle"],"t":"The pelvis","p":"Draw the pelvis and label every part you can. Then write two differences between a male and a female pelvis.","k":[{"pt":"Ilium, ischium, pubis meeting at the acetabulum, with the obturator foramen below","why":"All three bones meet in the acetabulum, and the obturator foramen is framed by the ischium and pubis."},{"pt":"Iliac crest and anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)","why":"These are landmarks you can feel through the skin."},{"pt":"Ischial tuberosity and ischial spine","why":"The tuberosity is the knob you sit on, with the spine above it."},{"pt":"Pubic symphysis where the two pubic bones meet in front","why":"It is a cartilaginous joint in the midline."},{"pt":"Sacrum and coccyx completing the bony pelvis, with the pelvic brim between the false pelvis above and the true pelvis below","why":"The bony pelvis is the two hip bones plus the sacrum and coccyx, and the brim is the dividing rim."},{"pt":"Female pelvis is wider and shallower, with a broader pubic angle and a larger outlet","why":"These are the sex differences, and all of them are about the size and shape of the pelvis."}]},{"id":"m2-lower-extremity-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[47],"cids":["w2-thigh-knee"],"t":"The femur","p":"Draw the femur from the front and from the back and label every named landmark.","k":[{"pt":"Head with the fovea capitis, and the neck","why":"The fovea anchors a ligament to the acetabulum, and the neck is a common fracture site."},{"pt":"Greater and lesser trochanters, with the intertrochanteric line in front and the intertrochanteric crest behind","why":"The greater trochanter is lateral and the lesser is medial, and both are major muscle attachments."},{"pt":"Linea aspera and gluteal tuberosity on the back of the shaft","why":"The rough ridge down the back of the shaft is a muscle attachment line."},{"pt":"Medial and lateral condyles, with the epicondyles above them","why":"The condyles articulate with the tibia, and the epicondyles sit just above them."},{"pt":"Intercondylar fossa behind and the patellar surface in front","why":"The fossa is the notch between the condyles at the back, and the patella sits against the front."}]},{"id":"m2-lower-extremity-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[48],"cids":["w2-leg-bones"],"t":"Tibia and fibula","p":"Draw the tibia and fibula together and label both. Mark which one bears weight and which one does not.","k":[{"pt":"Tibia: medial and lateral condyles and the intercondylar eminence","why":"The condyles articulate with the femur."},{"pt":"Tibial tuberosity and the sharp anterior border","why":"The tuberosity anchors the patellar ligament, and the anterior border is the shin you can feel."},{"pt":"Medial malleolus and the fibular notch at the lower end of the tibia","why":"The medial bump of the ankle belongs to the tibia."},{"pt":"Fibula: head at the top and the lateral malleolus at the ankle","why":"The lateral bump of the ankle belongs to the fibula."},{"pt":"Interosseous membrane between the shafts","why":"A sheet of dense connective tissue binds the two bones."},{"pt":"The tibia bears the weight, the fibula does not","why":"That one fact explains why the tibia is thick and why the fibula can be spared as a graft."}]},{"id":"m2-lower-extremity-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[42,46],"cids":["w2-pectoral-girdle","w2-pelvic-girdle"],"t":"Appendicular mind map","p":"Build a mind map with the appendicular skeleton at the center. Branch to each girdle, then out through the limb bone by bone.","k":[{"pt":"Two main branches: pectoral girdle and pelvic girdle","why":"The appendicular skeleton is the limbs plus the girdles that attach them."},{"pt":"Upper limb in order: clavicle, scapula, humerus, radius and ulna, carpals, metacarpals, phalanges","why":"The chain runs from proximal to distal."},{"pt":"Lower limb in order: hip bone, femur, patella, tibia and fibula, tarsals, metatarsals, phalanges","why":"The lower limb follows the same plan as the upper limb."},{"pt":"Counts if you get there: 8 carpals, 7 tarsals, 5 metacarpals, 5 metatarsals, 14 phalanges in each hand and foot","why":"Carpals and tarsals differ by one, while hands and feet share the same counts for their long bones and phalanges."},{"pt":"No axial bones on the map: no skull, vertebrae, sacrum, ribs, or sternum","why":"The sacrum sits in the pelvis but belongs to the vertebral column, which makes it the usual slip."}]},{"id":"m2-lower-extremity-L2-3","lv":1,"comps":[42,43,46,48,34,40,41],"cids":["w2-pectoral-girdle","w2-arm-forearm","w2-pelvic-girdle","w2-leg-bones","w2-skull-markings","w2-regional-vertebrae","w2-thoracic-cage"],"t":"Find it on yourself","p":"List every bony landmark you can actually feel on your own body. Write the bone it belongs to and roughly where it is.","k":[{"pt":"Clavicle across the top of the chest, and the acromion of the scapula at the point of the shoulder","why":"The clavicle runs from the sternum out to the acromion."},{"pt":"Olecranon of the ulna at the point of the elbow, and the medial epicondyle of the humerus","why":"The elbow has landmarks from two bones, the ulna behind and the humerus on the inner side."},{"pt":"Iliac crest, anterior superior iliac spine, and the ischial tuberosity you sit on","why":"These hip bone landmarks are used to locate injection sites and check pelvic alignment."},{"pt":"Patella, the anterior border of the tibia (shin), and the medial and lateral malleoli","why":"The medial malleolus is tibia and the lateral malleolus is fibula, one on each side of the ankle."},{"pt":"Jugular notch and sternal angle, external occipital protuberance, mastoid process, spinous process of C7","why":"These axial landmarks count too, and C7, the vertebra prominens, has the long spinous process at the base of the neck."}]},{"id":"m2-lower-extremity-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[49],"cids":["w2-foot-bones"],"t":"Arches of the foot","p":"Draw the foot from the side and from below. Mark the three arches and name them. Then draw arrows showing where the body's weight enters the foot and how the arches spread it.","k":[{"pt":"Medial longitudinal, lateral longitudinal, and transverse arches","why":"Two arches run the length of the foot, inner and outer, and one runs across it."},{"pt":"Weight coming down the tibia onto the talus","why":"The talus is the tarsal that articulates with the tibia and fibula and receives the body's weight."},{"pt":"Calcaneus at the heel, the largest tarsal","why":"The heel bone is the back contact point of the foot."},{"pt":"The arches spread body weight across the sole and spring back with each step","why":"Acting like springs, the arches share the load instead of letting it land on one point."},{"pt":"A dropped medial longitudinal arch is flat feet","why":"Flat feet is what happens when the inner arch loses its height."}]},{"id":"m2-lower-extremity-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[42,46],"cids":["w2-pectoral-girdle","w2-pelvic-girdle"],"t":"Two girdles compared","p":"Draw the pectoral girdle and the pelvic girdle side by side. For each, write what it attaches to and whether it favors mobility or stability, and say what structure makes that true.","k":[{"pt":"Pectoral girdle: clavicle and scapula","why":"The clavicle is the only bony link between the upper limb and the trunk."},{"pt":"Pectoral girdle attaches to the axial skeleton only at the sternoclavicular joint, so it favors mobility","why":"With one small joint as its only bony anchor, the girdle trades a firm hold for range of movement."},{"pt":"A shallow glenoid cavity gives range but dislocates more easily","why":"A large humeral head in a shallow socket can move in many directions and can slip out."},{"pt":"Pelvic girdle: two hip bones joined to the sacrum and to each other, so it favors stability","why":"The hip bones meet the sacrum at the sacroiliac joints and each other at the pubic symphysis, making a firm ring."},{"pt":"A deep acetabulum holds the femoral head","why":"A deep cup grips the ball, giving up range of motion for stability under body weight."}]},{"id":"m2-lower-extremity-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[47,46],"cids":["w2-thigh-knee","w2-pelvic-girdle"],"t":"A fall at home","p":"An 80-year-old falls at home and cannot bear weight on one leg. The leg looks shorter than the other and is rotated. Name the most likely fracture site, explain why that spot breaks in older adults, and name the structure whose blood supply is at risk. Then name the socket the femoral head sits in and the three bones that build it.","k":[{"pt":"A fracture of the femoral neck, a broken hip","why":"A broken hip is usually a fracture of the femoral neck."},{"pt":"The neck is narrow and angled","why":"A narrow, angled section is the weak point of the bone in a fall."},{"pt":"Bone thinned by osteoporosis breaks more easily in older adults","why":"Osteoporosis thins bone, which is why this fracture is common in older adults."},{"pt":"The blood supply to the femoral head is at risk","why":"The vessels to the head run close by the neck, so the fracture can threaten the head itself."},{"pt":"The head sits in the acetabulum, built by the ilium, ischium, and pubis","why":"The acetabulum is the deep cup where the three hip bones fuse."}]},{"id":"m2-lower-extremity-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[48],"cids":["w2-leg-bones"],"t":"The rolled ankle","p":"A runner rolls an ankle and feels pain over the bump on the outer side of the ankle. Name the bump and the bone it belongs to. Later a surgeon needs a piece of bone for a graft and takes it from that same bone. Explain why that bone can be spared but the other leg bone cannot, and name the bump on the inner side of the ankle.","k":[{"pt":"The lateral malleolus","why":"It is the lateral bump of the ankle."},{"pt":"It belongs to the fibula","why":"The fibula is the lateral leg bone, so its lower end forms the outer ankle bump."},{"pt":"The lateral malleolus is a common fracture site in a rolled ankle","why":"The lateral malleolus of the fibula is the common ankle fracture site, often from rolling the ankle."},{"pt":"The fibula can be harvested because it does not bear body weight","why":"Taking a length of a non-weight-bearing bone leaves the leg able to stand."},{"pt":"The tibia bears the body's weight, so it cannot be spared","why":"Losing part of the tibia would leave the leg unable to carry the body."},{"pt":"Inner bump: the medial malleolus of the tibia","why":"The tibia is the medial leg bone, so its lower end forms the inner ankle bump."}]}]},{"id":"m2-joints","title":"Joints","mw":"2026-09-28","tr":"2026-09-24","prompts":[{"id":"m2-joints-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[52],"cids":["w2-synovial-structure"],"t":"A synovial joint in section","p":"Draw two bone ends meeting at a synovial joint, cut in half. Label every structure inside and around the joint, and write one job beside each.","k":[{"pt":"Articular cartilage capping each bone end","why":"Hyaline cartilage makes the surfaces smooth and slippery, reducing friction and absorbing shock."},{"pt":"Synovial cavity filled with synovial fluid","why":"The cavity is what makes a joint synovial, and the fluid lubricates the surfaces."},{"pt":"Articular capsule with an outer fibrous membrane and an inner synovial membrane","why":"The fibrous membrane unites the bones, and the synovial membrane secretes the fluid."},{"pt":"Ligaments as thickened bundles of the fibrous membrane","why":"Ligaments resist strain and keep the joint from dislocating."},{"pt":"An accessory structure such as an articular disc (meniscus), a fat pad, or a bursa","why":"Discs improve fit and absorb shock, fat pads cushion, and bursae reduce friction between moving parts."},{"pt":"Synovial fluid feeding the articular cartilage","why":"Articular cartilage has no blood vessels, so the fluid supplies its oxygen and nutrients."}]},{"id":"m2-joints-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[54],"cids":["w2-synovial-types"],"t":"Six synovial joint types","p":"List the six types of synovial joint. For each, sketch the shape of the two surfaces, write how many axes it moves around, and give one joint in the body.","k":[{"pt":"Plane: flat surfaces that glide, such as the intercarpal and intertarsal joints","why":"Flat surfaces slide against each other rather than bending."},{"pt":"Hinge: convex into concave, uniaxial, such as the elbow, ankle, and interphalangeal joints","why":"One axis means it only bends and straightens."},{"pt":"Pivot: a rounded surface in a ring of bone and ligament, uniaxial rotation, such as the atlantoaxial and radioulnar joints","why":"The bone turns around its own long axis, as the atlas turns on the dens."},{"pt":"Condyloid: an oval projection in an oval depression, biaxial, such as the radiocarpal and metacarpophalangeal joints","why":"An oval fit allows movement in two directions."},{"pt":"Saddle: each surface both concave and convex, biaxial, the carpometacarpal joint of the thumb","why":"Two curved saddles fitting together let the thumb move in two directions."},{"pt":"Ball-and-socket: a ball in a cup, triaxial, such as the shoulder and hip","why":"Three axes is the most movement any joint allows."}]},{"id":"m2-joints-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[51],"cids":["w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"],"t":"Fibrous and cartilaginous joints","p":"Name every fibrous joint type and both cartilaginous joint types. For each, write what holds the bones together, how much it moves, and one example.","k":[{"pt":"Suture: thin dense connective tissue with interlocking edges, immovable in adults, between skull bones","why":"Sutures are found only in the skull, and the interlocking edges add strength."},{"pt":"Syndesmosis: bones united by a ligament, slightly movable, the distal tibiofibular joint","why":"There is more tissue and space than in a suture, so a little movement is allowed."},{"pt":"Gomphosis: a peg in a socket held by the periodontal ligament, immovable, a tooth in its socket","why":"The tooth is the peg and the jaw is the socket."},{"pt":"Interosseous membrane: a sheet of dense connective tissue, slightly movable, between the radius and ulna","why":"The broad sheet binds two long bones along their shafts."},{"pt":"Synchondrosis: hyaline cartilage, immovable, the epiphyseal plate and the first rib with the manubrium","why":"Hyaline cartilage holds the bones rigidly, and when bone replaces it the joint becomes a synostosis."},{"pt":"Symphysis: a disc of fibrocartilage, slightly movable, the pubic symphysis and the joints between vertebral bodies","why":"Fibrocartilage gives a little, and every symphysis lies in the midline."}]},{"id":"m2-joints-L2-2","lv":1,"comps":[53],"cids":["w2-joint-movements"],"t":"Movements in pairs","p":"Write each pair of opposite movements and draw a small arrow figure for each: flexion and extension, abduction and adduction, medial and lateral rotation, elevation and depression, protraction and retraction, inversion and eversion, dorsiflexion and plantar flexion, supination and pronation. Then name two movements that have no opposite and say what each one is.","k":[{"pt":"Flexion decreases the angle between bones and extension increases it, with hyperextension going past anatomical position","why":"Both are judged by the angle at the joint, usually in the sagittal plane."},{"pt":"Abduction moves away from the midline and adduction moves toward it","why":"The midline is the reference, the same one medial and lateral use."},{"pt":"Medial rotation turns a bone toward the midline around its own long axis, and lateral rotation turns it away","why":"Rotation turns the bone in place rather than swinging it."},{"pt":"Elevation lifts and depression lowers, protraction moves forward and retraction moves back","why":"These special movements are named for the direction the part travels."},{"pt":"Inversion turns the sole inward and eversion outward, dorsiflexion bends the foot up and plantar flexion points the toes","why":"These are the special movements of the foot and ankle."},{"pt":"Supination turns the palm to face anteriorly and pronation turns it to face posteriorly","why":"In anatomical position the palms already face forward, so the forearm starts out supinated."},{"pt":"No opposite: gliding slides surfaces back and forth or side to side with no change in angle, and circumduction moves the distal end of a part in a circle","why":"Gliding has no angle to reverse, and circumduction traces a full circle and ends where it began, so neither has a single opposite."}]},{"id":"m2-joints-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[50,51],"cids":["w2-joint-classification","w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"],"t":"Two ways to classify a joint","p":"Make a grid with fibrous, cartilaginous, and synovial down the side. Across the top, write whether there is a joint cavity, what tissue holds the bones, and the usual functional class. Then classify a tooth in its socket, the pubic symphysis, and the elbow both ways.","k":[{"pt":"Fibrous: no cavity, held by dense connective tissue rich in collagen","why":"With no cavity, the tissue between the bones is all that holds them, so they barely move."},{"pt":"Cartilaginous: no cavity, held by hyaline cartilage or fibrocartilage","why":"The type of cartilage decides how much the joint gives."},{"pt":"Synovial: cavity present, bones united by an articular capsule, always a diarthrosis","why":"A fluid-filled cavity between the bones is what lets them move freely."},{"pt":"Functional classes: synarthrosis immovable, amphiarthrosis slightly movable, diarthrosis freely movable","why":"Function is about degree of movement, a separate question from what the joint is built of."},{"pt":"Tooth in its socket: fibrous gomphosis, synarthrosis","why":"The periodontal ligament holds the peg tight, so it does not move."},{"pt":"Pubic symphysis: cartilaginous symphysis, amphiarthrosis","why":"A fibrocartilage disc allows a little give, and the two classifications agree."},{"pt":"Elbow: synovial hinge joint, diarthrosis","why":"A convex surface in a concave one moves freely around one axis."}]},{"id":"m2-joints-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[55],"cids":["w2-shoulder-knee"],"t":"Shoulder versus knee","p":"Compare the shoulder (glenohumeral) joint and the knee (tibiofemoral) joint. For each, write the joint type, the bones that meet, the structures that hold it together, and why it is easy to injure.","k":[{"pt":"Shoulder: ball-and-socket, the head of the humerus in the glenoid cavity","why":"A large head in a shallow socket with a loose capsule makes it the most freely movable joint in the body."},{"pt":"Shoulder stability comes mostly from the rotator cuff: supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis","why":"The ligaments add little strength, so these muscles hold the humeral head in the socket."},{"pt":"Glenoid labrum, a fibrocartilage rim around the glenoid cavity","why":"The labrum slightly deepens and enlarges the shallow socket."},{"pt":"Knee: modified hinge, the femoral condyles on the tibial condyles plus the patella on the femur","why":"Three joints share one cavity, which makes it the largest and most complex joint."},{"pt":"Knee held by cruciate ligaments inside the capsule and collateral ligaments outside it, with medial and lateral menisci","why":"The cruciates stop the tibia sliding forward and backward, and the menisci improve fit and absorb shock."},{"pt":"The shoulder gives up stability for mobility, and the knee is the joint most often injured","why":"Freedom of movement is what puts synovial joints at risk, and each of these pays for it in its own way."}]},{"id":"m2-joints-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[55],"cids":["w2-shoulder-knee"],"t":"The unhappy triad","p":"A soccer player's foot is planted when another player hits the outside of the knee. Name the three structures likely torn and the name of this injury. Then predict which bone slides too far forward when one of those structures is gone, and name the structure that stops it sliding backward.","k":[{"pt":"Tibial (medial) collateral ligament","why":"It is the ligament on the medial side of the knee, outside the capsule."},{"pt":"Medial meniscus","why":"The C-shaped medial meniscus sits on the same side as the torn collateral ligament."},{"pt":"Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL)","why":"The ACL crosses inside the joint and is torn in about 70 percent of serious knee injuries."},{"pt":"The injury is the unhappy triad","why":"A lateral blow to a planted knee that tears these three at once is the definition of the triad."},{"pt":"Without the ACL the tibia slides forward on the femur","why":"The ACL is the ligament that stops the tibia sliding forward."},{"pt":"The posterior cruciate ligament stops the tibia sliding backward","why":"The PCL is the other intracapsular ligament, important on stairs and inclines."}]},{"id":"m2-joints-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[50,51,53,54],"cids":["w2-joint-classification","w2-fibrous-cartilaginous","w2-joint-movements","w2-synovial-types"],"t":"Classify the tagged joint","p":"Your lab partner tags three joints: the epiphyseal plate of a child's femur, the atlantoaxial joint, and the carpometacarpal joint of the thumb. For each, give the structural class, the functional class, the synovial type if it has one, and the movement it allows. Then predict what happens to the first joint when growth ends.","k":[{"pt":"Epiphyseal plate: cartilaginous joint, a synchondrosis of hyaline cartilage","why":"No cavity and hyaline cartilage between the bone parts is the definition of a synchondrosis."},{"pt":"Epiphyseal plate: synarthrosis, no movement","why":"Hyaline cartilage holds the parts of the bone rigidly."},{"pt":"Atlantoaxial: synovial pivot joint, diarthrosis, rotation that shakes the head no","why":"The atlas turns around the dens, a rounded surface inside a ring of bone and ligament."},{"pt":"Thumb carpometacarpal: synovial saddle joint, diarthrosis, biaxial","why":"Each surface is both concave and convex, which lets the thumb move in two directions."},{"pt":"When growth ends, bone replaces the plate and the joint becomes a synostosis","why":"The bone parts fuse completely, which shows up as the epiphyseal line."}]}]}]},{"n":3,"title":"Module 3. Heart, Vessels, Muscle and Blood","topics":[{"id":"m3-heart-structure","title":"Heart: pericardium, wall, chambers and surface features","mw":"2026-10-05","tr":"2026-10-01","prompts":[{"id":"m3-heart-structure-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[56],"cids":["cv-surfaces"],"t":"The pericardium","p":"Draw the heart inside its sac, cut in section. Label every layer from the outside in and name the space that holds the fluid.","k":[{"pt":"Fibrous pericardium outside","why":"This tough, inelastic layer of dense connective tissue anchors the heart in the mediastinum and keeps it from overfilling."},{"pt":"Parietal layer of the serous pericardium against it","why":"The serous pericardium is a double membrane, and its parietal layer is the half that lines the inside of the fibrous sac."},{"pt":"Pericardial cavity with serous fluid","why":"The cavity is the space between the two serous layers, and its thin film of fluid lets the heart glide with less friction as it beats."},{"pt":"Visceral layer of serous pericardium, which is the epicardium","why":"This is one membrane with two names: visceral pericardium when you describe the sac, epicardium when you describe the heart wall."},{"pt":"Then myocardium and endocardium","why":"Inside the epicardium come the thick cardiac muscle layer and then the smooth endothelium lining the chambers, which finishes the heart wall."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-structure-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[56,71],"cids":["cv-surfaces","bvn-fetal-remnants"],"t":"Surface features and landmarks","p":"Draw the heart from the front and label every surface feature, sulcus, and external landmark you can. Then name the fetal shunts and the adult remnant of each.","k":[{"pt":"Apex, base, auricles","why":"The apex is the tip of the left ventricle pointing down and to the left, the base is the broad top where the great vessels attach, and an auricle is the small wrinkled pouch on each atrium."},{"pt":"Anterior interventricular sulcus, coronary sulcus","why":"These grooves on the surface show where the chambers meet inside: the coronary sulcus circles between the atria and ventricles, and the anterior interventricular sulcus runs over the septum between the ventricles."},{"pt":"Ligamentum arteriosum, fossa ovalis","why":"The ligamentum arteriosum is the short cord between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, and the fossa ovalis is the oval dip in the interatrial septum seen from inside the right atrium."},{"pt":"Ductus arteriosus, foramen ovale, ductus venosus and their remnants","why":"Each shunt leaves something behind: ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum, foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis, and ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum on the liver."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-structure-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[56,57],"cids":["cv-surfaces","cv-chambers"],"t":"The heart in the chest","p":"Draw the thorax from the front and place the heart in it. Mark the base, the apex, the anterior surface, and the inferior surface, and write which chamber makes up most of each.","k":[{"pt":"In the mediastinum between the two lungs, resting on the diaphragm","why":"The heart sits in the central compartment of the thorax, with a lung on each side and the diaphragm under it."},{"pt":"Tilted to the left, with about two thirds of its mass left of the midline","why":"The tilt is why the apex ends up on the left side of the chest."},{"pt":"Base at the top, mostly left atrium","why":"The base is the broad superior surface where the great vessels enter and leave."},{"pt":"Apex at the bottom, pointing down, forward, and to the left, the tip of the left ventricle","why":"Because the apex is the tip of the left ventricle, the direction it points tells you which side of the heart is the left."},{"pt":"Anterior (sternocostal) surface, mostly right ventricle, under the sternum and ribs","why":"The right ventricle faces forward, so it is the chamber that lies right behind the sternum."},{"pt":"Inferior (diaphragmatic) surface, mostly left ventricle, resting on the diaphragm","why":"The left ventricle makes up the underside of the heart where it sits on the diaphragm."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-structure-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[56,57,59],"cids":["cv-surfaces","cv-chambers","cv-valve-support"],"t":"Inside the four chambers","p":"Draw the heart opened in a frontal section. Label the four chambers, both septa, and every ridge or landmark you can find inside each chamber.","k":[{"pt":"Right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, left ventricle","why":"The two thin-walled atria receive blood and the two thick-walled ventricles pump it out."},{"pt":"Interatrial septum with the fossa ovalis, and the interventricular septum","why":"The septa divide the right heart from the left, and the fossa ovalis in the interatrial septum is the remnant of the fetal foramen ovale."},{"pt":"Pectinate muscles in the atria and auricles, crista terminalis in the right atrium","why":"The crista terminalis is the ridge that separates the smooth back wall of the right atrium, the sinus venarum, from its rough pectinate front wall."},{"pt":"Trabeculae carneae on the ventricle walls","why":"These irregular ridges line the ventricles, so seeing them instead of pectinate muscles tells you that you are inside a ventricle."},{"pt":"Papillary muscles with chordae tendineae","why":"The papillary muscles project from the ventricle wall and anchor the chordae tendineae that tie down the atrioventricular valve cusps."},{"pt":"Moderator band in the right ventricle","why":"This muscular band, the septomarginal trabecula, carries part of the conduction system across to the anterior papillary muscle."},{"pt":"Conus arteriosus and aortic vestibule","why":"These are the smooth outflow tracts of the right and left ventricles, leading up to the pulmonary and aortic valves."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-structure-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[57],"cids":["cv-chambers"],"t":"Why the walls differ","p":"Draw the four heart chambers in cross section with the wall thickness roughly to scale. Write what each chamber pumps to and why its wall is the thickness it is.","k":[{"pt":"Atria thin walled; they receive blood and push it only into the ventricles below","why":"The atria are receiving chambers that move blood a short way down, so they need little muscle."},{"pt":"Right ventricle thicker than the atria; it pumps through the pulmonary trunk to the lungs","why":"The pulmonary circuit is a short loop to the nearby lungs, so the right ventricle needs less muscle than the left."},{"pt":"Left ventricle thickest of the four; it pumps through the aorta to the whole body","why":"The systemic circuit reaches every part of the body, which is why the left ventricle has the thickest wall."},{"pt":"Interventricular septum drawn as a thick wall between the ventricles","why":"The septum keeps the oxygen-poor right side separate from the oxygen-rich left side."},{"pt":"The extra thickness is myocardium","why":"The myocardium is the cardiac muscle layer that contracts and makes up about 95 percent of the wall, so a chamber that does more work has more of it."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-structure-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[56,57,59],"cids":["cv-surfaces","cv-chambers","cv-valve-support"],"t":"Atrium or ventricle","p":"Compare the inside of an atrium with the inside of a ventricle. For each, write what its wall is like, the named ridges and features you would find, and where its blood comes from and goes.","k":[{"pt":"Atria have thin walls and receive blood","why":"The right atrium receives from the superior and inferior venae cavae and the coronary sinus, and the left atrium receives from the four pulmonary veins."},{"pt":"Atrial walls carry pectinate muscles, and each atrium has an auricle","why":"The parallel pectinate ridges line the atrial walls and auricles, and the auricle pouch slightly increases how much the atrium can hold."},{"pt":"Right atrium also has the crista terminalis and the smooth sinus venarum","why":"The venae cavae empty into the smooth sinus venarum, and the crista terminalis marks where it meets the rough pectinate wall."},{"pt":"Ventricles have thick walls and pump blood out","why":"The right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary trunk and the left ventricle pumps into the aorta."},{"pt":"Ventricle walls carry trabeculae carneae and papillary muscles","why":"The papillary muscles rise from the ventricle wall to anchor the chordae tendineae of the atrioventricular valves."},{"pt":"Each ventricle ends in a smooth outflow tract","why":"The conus arteriosus leads to the pulmonary valve and the aortic vestibule leads to the aortic valve."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-structure-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[56],"cids":["cv-surfaces"],"t":"From the blood out to the sac","p":"Start with blood inside the left ventricle and list every layer you would pass through to reach the outside of the fibrous pericardium. For each layer, name the tissue it is made of.","k":[{"pt":"Endocardium","why":"A smooth endothelium lines the chambers and covers the valves, and it is continuous with the lining of the great vessels."},{"pt":"Myocardium","why":"Cardiac muscle in spiraling bundles makes up about 95 percent of the wall thickness, and it is the layer that contracts."},{"pt":"Epicardium, the visceral serous pericardium","why":"This thin serous membrane on the heart surface has fat and the coronary vessels running in it."},{"pt":"Pericardial cavity","why":"This is the space between the two serous layers, holding only a thin film of serous fluid."},{"pt":"Parietal serous pericardium","why":"This serous layer lines the inside of the fibrous sac and faces the visceral layer across the cavity."},{"pt":"Fibrous pericardium","why":"Tough, inelastic dense connective tissue forms the outer sac and anchors the heart in the mediastinum."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-structure-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[56],"cids":["cv-surfaces"],"t":"Fluid around the heart","p":"Fluid builds up around a patient's heart after a chest injury. Name the space it collects in and the layers on each side of that space, then explain from the anatomy why the heart can no longer fill properly.","k":[{"pt":"It collects in the pericardial cavity","why":"The pericardial cavity is the narrow space between the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium, which normally holds only a thin film of fluid."},{"pt":"Outer side: parietal serous pericardium, backed by the fibrous pericardium","why":"The parietal layer lines the fibrous sac, so the fluid presses outward against the toughest layer."},{"pt":"Inner side: visceral serous pericardium, the epicardium, on the heart surface","why":"Because the epicardium is part of the heart wall, the fluid presses directly on the heart."},{"pt":"The fibrous pericardium is inelastic and cannot stretch","why":"With nowhere to expand outward, the extra fluid takes up room the heart needs."},{"pt":"The fluid squeezes the heart from outside and limits filling, called cardiac tamponade","why":"The same fluid film that normally lets the heart glide becomes the problem when there is too much of it."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-structure-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[56,57,58],"cids":["cv-surfaces","cv-chambers","cv-valves"],"t":"Which chamber is this?","p":"On an opened heart in lab you find a chamber with a very thick wall, trabeculae carneae, papillary muscles anchoring a valve with two cusps, and a smooth outflow tract leading up to a valve with three cusps. Name the chamber, explain how each clue points to it, and name the vessel its blood leaves through.","k":[{"pt":"It is the left ventricle","why":"Every clue fits only one of the four chambers, and checking each clue is how you prove it."},{"pt":"The very thick wall points to the left ventricle","why":"Ventricles have thicker walls than atria, and the left ventricle has the thickest wall of all four chambers."},{"pt":"Trabeculae carneae mean you are in a ventricle","why":"Atria are lined with pectinate muscles instead, so these ridges rule out both atria."},{"pt":"A valve with two cusps and chordae is the mitral valve","why":"The mitral, or bicuspid, valve is the only valve with two cusps, and it sits between the left atrium and left ventricle."},{"pt":"The smooth outflow tract is the aortic vestibule, leading to the aortic valve","why":"The aortic valve is a semilunar valve with three cusps, which separates it from the two-cusped mitral valve."},{"pt":"Blood leaves through the aorta to the body","why":"The left ventricle pumps through the aortic valve into the aorta, the start of the systemic circuit."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-structure-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[56,57,62],"cids":["cv-surfaces","cv-chambers","bvn-great-vessels"],"t":"Reading an unlabeled heart","p":"You are handed a heart with no labels. Using only what you can see on the outside, explain how you would find the front, the left side, and the line where the two ventricles meet.","k":[{"pt":"The apex points down, forward, and to the left","why":"The apex is the tip of the left ventricle, so the direction it points tells you which side is left."},{"pt":"The anterior surface is mostly right ventricle, the inferior surface mostly left ventricle","why":"The sternocostal surface lies under the sternum and ribs and the diaphragmatic surface rests on the diaphragm, so knowing which chamber makes each surface tells you front from bottom."},{"pt":"The anterior interventricular sulcus marks where the ventricles meet on the front","why":"This groove lies over the interventricular septum and carries the anterior interventricular artery and the great cardiac vein."},{"pt":"The coronary sulcus circles the heart between the atria and the ventricles","why":"This groove separates the upper receiving chambers from the lower pumping chambers on the surface."},{"pt":"The auricles and great vessels sit at the base, at the top","why":"The base is where the venae cavae, pulmonary trunk, pulmonary veins, and aorta attach, with an auricle on each atrium beside them."}]}]},{"id":"m3-heart-valves-flow","title":"Heart valves and blood flow through the heart","mw":"2026-10-05","tr":"2026-10-01","prompts":[{"id":"m3-heart-valves-flow-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[58,59],"cids":["cv-valves","cv-valve-support"],"t":"Valves, open and shut","p":"List the four heart valves, where each one sits, how many cusps it has, and when it is open versus closed.","k":[{"pt":"Tricuspid: right AV, three cusps","why":"It sits between the right atrium and right ventricle and keeps blood from flowing back into the right atrium."},{"pt":"Mitral or bicuspid: left AV, two cusps","why":"It sits between the left atrium and left ventricle, and it is the only heart valve with two cusps."},{"pt":"Pulmonary and aortic semilunar, three cusps each","why":"They sit at the exits of the right and left ventricles, into the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, and keep blood from flowing back into the ventricles."},{"pt":"AV valves open when ventricles fill and shut when they contract; semilunars the reverse","why":"Every valve opens to let blood move forward and closes to stop it flowing back, so a contracting ventricle shuts its AV valve and pushes its semilunar valve open."},{"pt":"Chordae tendineae and papillary muscles","why":"They hold only the AV valves shut against a contracting ventricle, while the pocket-shaped semilunar cusps fill from above and close themselves."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-valves-flow-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[59,58],"cids":["cv-valve-support","cv-valves"],"t":"What holds an AV valve shut","p":"Draw one atrioventricular valve with everything that holds it in place. Label the cusps, the cords, the muscle the cords attach to, and the ring around the valve opening.","k":[{"pt":"Valve cusps, three for the tricuspid or two for the mitral","why":"The cusps are the flaps that meet to close the opening between an atrium and a ventricle."},{"pt":"Chordae tendineae","why":"These strong fibrous cords tie the free edges of the cusps to the ventricle wall."},{"pt":"Papillary muscles","why":"These cone-shaped projections of the ventricle wall anchor the chordae and hold the cusps shut while the ventricle contracts."},{"pt":"Fibrous skeleton ring around the opening","why":"The ring of dense connective tissue anchors the valve and gives the cardiac muscle a firm point of attachment."},{"pt":"Ventricle wall with trabeculae carneae","why":"The papillary muscles rise out of this ridged wall, which is why the whole support system sits on the ventricle side of the valve."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-valves-flow-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[61,58],"cids":["cv-blood-pathway","cv-valves"],"t":"Trace a drop of blood","p":"Draw the heart and trace one drop of blood from the vena cava all the way to the aorta. Name every chamber, valve, and vessel it passes in order.","k":[{"pt":"SVC and IVC, right atrium","why":"Oxygen poor blood returns from the head, neck, and upper limbs in the SVC and from the trunk and lower limbs in the IVC, and both empty into the right atrium."},{"pt":"Tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve","why":"Blood passes the right AV valve into the right ventricle, which pumps it up through the pulmonary semilunar valve."},{"pt":"Pulmonary trunk, pulmonary arteries, lungs, pulmonary veins","why":"The pulmonary trunk splits into right and left pulmonary arteries, the blood picks up oxygen in the lungs, and four pulmonary veins bring it back."},{"pt":"Left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve, aorta","why":"Oxygen rich blood crosses the left AV valve into the thick-walled left ventricle, which pumps it through the aortic valve into the aorta and out to the body."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-valves-flow-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[61,62,69],"cids":["cv-blood-pathway","bvn-great-vessels","bvn-circulatory-routes"],"t":"The two circuits","p":"Draw the pulmonary and systemic circuits as one loop. Name the great vessels, mark which side is the high pressure side, and mark where the blood is oxygenated.","k":[{"pt":"Right heart to lungs to left heart to body and back","why":"The right side drives the pulmonary circuit and the left side drives the systemic circuit, so the two circuits join into one continuous loop."},{"pt":"SVC, IVC, pulmonary trunk and arteries, pulmonary veins, aorta","why":"These are the great vessels at the base of the heart, and each one belongs to only one of the two circuits."},{"pt":"Left side is high pressure","why":"The left ventricle pumps blood through the aorta to the whole body, which is why its wall is the thickest."},{"pt":"Pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood, pulmonary veins carry oxygenated","why":"Arteries and veins are named for the direction they carry blood relative to the heart, not for how much oxygen it holds."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-valves-flow-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[61,69],"cids":["cv-blood-pathway","bvn-circulatory-routes"],"t":"Pulmonary circulation","p":"Draw the pulmonary circuit on its own. Start in the right ventricle and finish in the left atrium, naming every vessel and structure on the way. Mark clearly where the blood picks up oxygen.","k":[{"pt":"Right ventricle, pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk","why":"The right ventricle pumps through its semilunar valve into the pulmonary trunk, the elastic artery leaving its top."},{"pt":"Right and left pulmonary arteries, carrying deoxygenated blood","why":"The pulmonary trunk splits into one artery for each lung, and they are arteries because they carry blood away from the heart."},{"pt":"Lobar arteries, arterioles, pulmonary capillaries around the alveoli","why":"The vessels follow the usual sequence of artery to arteriole to capillary, getting smaller until they reach the exchange vessels."},{"pt":"Gas exchange marked at the capillary and alveolus","why":"Capillaries are the only vessels thin enough for exchange, so this is the one place the blood can pick up oxygen."},{"pt":"Pulmonary veins, four of them, carrying oxygenated blood to the left atrium","why":"They are veins because they carry blood toward the heart, even though their blood is oxygen-rich."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-valves-flow-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[57,58,62,64],"cids":["cv-chambers","cv-valves","bvn-great-vessels","cv-conduction-anat"],"t":"Heart mind map","p":"Build a mind map with the heart at the center. Four branches: chambers, valves, vessels, conduction. Fill in every branch fully.","k":[{"pt":"Chambers: four, with which side is oxygenated","why":"The right atrium and ventricle hold oxygen-poor blood from the body, and the left atrium and ventricle hold oxygen-rich blood from the lungs."},{"pt":"Valves: two AV, two semilunar, with locations","why":"The tricuspid and mitral valves sit between the atria and ventricles, and the pulmonary and aortic valves sit at the ventricle exits."},{"pt":"Vessels: venae cavae, pulmonary trunk and arteries, pulmonary veins, aorta, coronaries","why":"The great vessels attach at the base, and the coronary arteries leave the base of the aorta to feed the heart wall itself."},{"pt":"Conduction: SA node through to Purkinje fibers","why":"The signal runs SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers, so the atria contract before the ventricles."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-valves-flow-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[59,58],"cids":["cv-valve-support","cv-valves"],"t":"A torn papillary muscle","p":"After a heart attack, a papillary muscle in the left ventricle dies and tears loose. Predict which valve fails, which way blood now leaks, and which structures connected the muscle to the valve.","k":[{"pt":"The mitral valve fails","why":"The papillary muscles of the left ventricle anchor the cusps of the mitral valve, the left AV valve."},{"pt":"The chordae tendineae connected the muscle to the cusps","why":"With the papillary muscle torn, the chordae no longer hold the cusps down."},{"pt":"The cusps can no longer be held shut when the ventricle contracts","why":"Normally the papillary muscles hold the AV valve closed against the pressure of the contracting ventricle."},{"pt":"Blood leaks backward from the left ventricle into the left atrium","why":"The mitral valve's job is to prevent backflow into the left atrium, so that is where the leak goes."},{"pt":"The aortic valve is not affected","why":"Semilunar valves have no chordae or papillary muscles, so losing a papillary muscle cannot affect them."},{"pt":"The leak can be heard as a murmur","why":"Blood slipping backward through a valve that does not close cleanly makes turbulence a stethoscope picks up."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-valves-flow-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[61,70],"cids":["cv-blood-pathway","bvn-vessel-disorders"],"t":"Where a leg clot goes","p":"A clot breaks free from a deep vein in the leg. Trace its path through the heart and name the first place it is likely to get stuck.","k":[{"pt":"Leg veins to the inferior vena cava to the right atrium","why":"The clot follows normal venous return, and the IVC drains the trunk and lower limbs."},{"pt":"Through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle","why":"Blood from the right atrium passes the right AV valve into the right ventricle."},{"pt":"Out through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk","why":"The right ventricle pumps everything it holds up into the pulmonary trunk."},{"pt":"Into a pulmonary artery, where it lodges as a pulmonary embolism","why":"The pulmonary arteries keep branching into smaller vessels in the lung, so the clot gets stuck where a vessel becomes too narrow."},{"pt":"It does not reach the left side of the heart","why":"It lodges in the narrowing lung arteries before it can reach the capillaries and the pulmonary veins that lead to the left atrium."}]},{"id":"m3-heart-valves-flow-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[58],"cids":["cv-valves"],"t":"Which valve is it?","p":"A classmate points to a valve with three cusps and no chordae tendineae, sitting at the exit of the chamber that sends blood to the lungs. Name the valve and what it separates, then name the two valves it is most often confused with and how you would tell each one apart.","k":[{"pt":"It is the pulmonary semilunar valve","why":"The chamber that sends blood to the lungs is the right ventricle, and its exit valve is the pulmonary valve."},{"pt":"It separates the right ventricle from the pulmonary trunk","why":"It prevents blood flowing back into the right ventricle."},{"pt":"No chordae tendineae means it is a semilunar valve","why":"Semilunar cusps are pocket-shaped and close themselves, so they need no cords or papillary muscles."},{"pt":"It is confused with the tricuspid valve, which also has three cusps","why":"The tricuspid is an AV valve between the right atrium and right ventricle, and it has chordae tendineae and papillary muscles."},{"pt":"It is also confused with the aortic valve","why":"The aortic valve looks the same, three semilunar cusps, but it sits at the exit of the left ventricle and opens into the aorta."}]}]},{"id":"m3-coronary-great-vessels","title":"Great vessels and coronary circulation","mw":"2026-10-05","tr":"2026-10-01","prompts":[{"id":"m3-coronary-great-vessels-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[63,56],"cids":["cv-coronary","cv-surfaces"],"t":"Coronary supply and wall layers","p":"Name every coronary vessel you can and the region of the heart it feeds. Then write the layers of the heart wall from the inside out.","k":[{"pt":"Left coronary: LAD or anterior interventricular, circumflex","why":"The left coronary artery leaves the base of the aorta and supplies the left atrium, left ventricle, anterior septum, and apex."},{"pt":"Right coronary: marginal, posterior interventricular","why":"The right coronary artery supplies the right atrium, most of the right ventricle, part of the left ventricle, the posterior septum, and usually the SA and AV nodes."},{"pt":"Coronary sinus and cardiac veins","why":"The cardiac veins run beside the arteries and drain into the coronary sinus on the back of the heart, which empties into the right atrium."},{"pt":"Endocardium, myocardium, epicardium, then pericardial sac layers","why":"The coronary vessels run in the epicardium on the surface and branch down into the myocardium they feed."}]},{"id":"m3-coronary-great-vessels-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[62],"cids":["bvn-great-vessels"],"t":"The great vessels","p":"Draw the base of the heart and label every great vessel attached to it. Next to each one, write the chamber it connects to and whether blood goes in or out.","k":[{"pt":"Superior vena cava into the right atrium","why":"It returns oxygen-poor blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs."},{"pt":"Inferior vena cava into the right atrium","why":"It returns oxygen-poor blood from the trunk and lower limbs."},{"pt":"Pulmonary trunk out of the right ventricle, splitting into right and left pulmonary arteries","why":"It carries blood from the right ventricle toward the lungs, so it starts the pulmonary circuit."},{"pt":"Four pulmonary veins into the left atrium","why":"They return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left side of the heart."},{"pt":"Aorta out of the left ventricle: ascending aorta, aortic arch, descending thoracic aorta","why":"The aorta is the largest artery and carries oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle to the body."},{"pt":"Ligamentum arteriosum between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta","why":"This short cord is the remnant of the fetal ductus arteriosus, so it links the two big arteries leaving the heart."}]},{"id":"m3-coronary-great-vessels-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[63],"cids":["cv-coronary"],"t":"Cardiac veins","p":"Draw the heart from the front and from behind. Label the coronary sinus and every cardiac vein, and next to each vein write the artery it travels with.","k":[{"pt":"Great cardiac vein with the anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","why":"Each cardiac vein travels in the same groove as an artery, so the great cardiac vein lies in the anterior interventricular sulcus."},{"pt":"Middle cardiac vein with the posterior interventricular artery (PDA)","why":"It runs in the posterior interventricular sulcus on the back of the heart."},{"pt":"Small cardiac vein with the right marginal artery","why":"It drains the right margin of the heart alongside the marginal branch of the right coronary artery."},{"pt":"Coronary sinus on the back of the heart, emptying into the right atrium","why":"The coronary sinus is the large vein that collects the cardiac veins, which is why the right atrium receives blood from it as well as from the venae cavae."},{"pt":"Anterior cardiac veins draining straight into the right atrium","why":"These small veins drain the right ventricle directly and bypass the coronary sinus."}]},{"id":"m3-coronary-great-vessels-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[63],"cids":["cv-coronary"],"t":"The heart feeding itself","p":"Trace blood from the aorta through the heart wall's own circulation and back into a chamber. Name every step in order.","k":[{"pt":"The base of the aorta","why":"The two coronary arteries branch off right at the start of the aorta, just above the aortic valve."},{"pt":"Right and left coronary arteries in the grooves on the surface","why":"The arteries run in the coronary and interventricular sulci and branch over the myocardium."},{"pt":"Capillaries of the myocardium","why":"Exchange happens only in capillaries, so this is where the heart muscle gets its oxygen."},{"pt":"Cardiac veins","why":"The great, middle, and small cardiac veins collect the blood and run back alongside the arteries."},{"pt":"Coronary sinus","why":"This large vein on the back of the heart gathers the cardiac veins together."},{"pt":"Right atrium","why":"The coronary sinus empties into the right atrium, so blood from the heart wall joins the rest of the body's returning blood."}]},{"id":"m3-coronary-great-vessels-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[63],"cids":["cv-coronary"],"t":"Right versus left coronary artery","p":"Compare the right and left coronary arteries: the branches each gives off, the groove each branch runs in, and the parts of the heart each supplies.","k":[{"pt":"Both leave the base of the aorta","why":"They branch off right at the start of the aorta, so the heart wall is the first tissue the left ventricle supplies."},{"pt":"Right coronary branches: right marginal and posterior interventricular (PDA)","why":"The right coronary artery runs in the coronary sulcus around the right side, and the PDA turns down the posterior interventricular sulcus."},{"pt":"Left coronary branches: anterior interventricular (LAD), circumflex, left marginal","why":"The LAD runs down the anterior interventricular sulcus, and the circumflex follows the coronary sulcus around to the left and back."},{"pt":"Right coronary supplies the right atrium, most of the right ventricle, part of the left ventricle, and the posterior septum","why":"Its branches follow the right side and the back of the heart, which is the territory it feeds."},{"pt":"Right coronary usually supplies the SA and AV nodes","why":"Both nodes sit in the right atrium, inside the right coronary artery's territory."},{"pt":"Left coronary supplies the left atrium, left ventricle, anterior septum, and apex","why":"The thick left ventricle depends mostly on the left coronary artery, which is why its branches matter so much."}]},{"id":"m3-coronary-great-vessels-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[62,69],"cids":["bvn-great-vessels","bvn-circulatory-routes"],"t":"Sort the great vessels","p":"Sort the great vessels into the pulmonary circuit and the systemic circuit. For each vessel, write whether it carries oxygen-rich or oxygen-poor blood and whether it flows toward or away from the heart.","k":[{"pt":"Pulmonary trunk and right and left pulmonary arteries: pulmonary, oxygen-poor, away","why":"They carry blood from the right ventricle to the lungs to pick up oxygen."},{"pt":"Four pulmonary veins: pulmonary, oxygen-rich, toward","why":"They bring blood back from the lungs to the left atrium."},{"pt":"Aorta: systemic, oxygen-rich, away","why":"It carries blood from the left ventricle out to the body."},{"pt":"Superior and inferior venae cavae: systemic, oxygen-poor, toward","why":"They return blood from the body to the right atrium."},{"pt":"The pulmonary vessels break the usual oxygen pattern","why":"Arteries are named for carrying blood away from the heart and veins for carrying it toward the heart, so the pulmonary artery can be oxygen-poor and the pulmonary vein oxygen-rich."}]},{"id":"m3-coronary-great-vessels-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[63],"cids":["cv-coronary"],"t":"A blocked LAD","p":"A plaque blocks the anterior interventricular artery (LAD) near where it starts. Name the artery it branches from, the groove it runs in, the part of the heart wall at risk, and the vein that runs beside it.","k":[{"pt":"It is a branch of the left coronary artery","why":"The left coronary artery leaves the base of the aorta and divides into the LAD and the circumflex."},{"pt":"It runs in the anterior interventricular sulcus","why":"The groove lies over the interventricular septum on the front of the heart."},{"pt":"The anterior septum and the front of the left ventricle down to the apex are at risk","why":"These lie in the left coronary artery's territory along the path the LAD supplies."},{"pt":"The muscle beyond the block is starved of oxygen and begins to die","why":"That death of a patch of myocardium is a heart attack, a myocardial infarction."},{"pt":"The great cardiac vein runs beside it","why":"Every cardiac vein travels in the same groove as its matching artery."}]},{"id":"m3-coronary-great-vessels-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[63,64],"cids":["cv-coronary","cv-conduction-anat"],"t":"A blocked right coronary artery","p":"A patient's right coronary artery is blocked. Besides the muscle that loses its supply, explain why the heart rhythm may also go wrong, naming the structures involved and where they sit.","k":[{"pt":"Muscle at risk: the right atrium, most of the right ventricle, part of the left ventricle","why":"These regions are the right coronary artery's territory."},{"pt":"The posterior septum is also at risk","why":"The posterior interventricular artery is a branch of the right coronary artery and runs down the back of the septum."},{"pt":"The right coronary artery usually supplies the SA node and AV node","why":"Losing blood supply to the nodes can disturb the rhythm as well as kill muscle."},{"pt":"The SA node sits in the wall of the right atrium near the opening of the superior vena cava","why":"It is the pacemaker, so damage here affects the pace of the whole heart."},{"pt":"The AV node sits in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum","why":"It passes the signal on to the ventricles, so damage here can disconnect the atria from the ventricles."}]},{"id":"m3-coronary-great-vessels-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[63],"cids":["cv-coronary"],"t":"Planning a bypass","p":"A surgeon will use the internal thoracic artery to bypass a blocked coronary artery. Name where the internal thoracic artery comes from, and explain where on the blocked coronary artery the graft must join to bring blood back to the starving muscle.","k":[{"pt":"The internal thoracic artery branches from the subclavian artery","why":"It normally supplies the anterior chest wall, so it is close by and already carries oxygen-rich blood."},{"pt":"Coronary blood normally enters at the base of the aorta","why":"Everything downstream of the block depends on blood that started there and can no longer get through."},{"pt":"The starving muscle is the patch beyond the block","why":"The myocardium fed by the vessel past the block is the part that loses its oxygen."},{"pt":"The graft must join the coronary artery downstream of the block","why":"Joining past the block lets blood skip the narrowed segment and reach that patch of myocardium."},{"pt":"Drainage does not change: cardiac veins to the coronary sinus to the right atrium","why":"The bypass only fixes the arterial side, so the blood still leaves the heart wall by its normal venous route."}]}]},{"id":"m3-conduction","title":"Cardiac muscle and the conduction system","mw":"2026-10-05","tr":"2026-10-01","prompts":[{"id":"m3-conduction-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[64,60,66,67],"cids":["cv-conduction-anat","cv-cardiac-muscle","bvn-vessel-tunics","bvn-vessel-types"],"t":"Conduction and the vessel wall","p":"Write the conduction pathway of the heart in order, naming each structure. Then write the three layers of a vessel wall and how an artery differs from a vein.","k":[{"pt":"SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers","why":"This is the fixed route each heartbeat takes, from the pacemaker in the right atrium to the walls of both ventricles."},{"pt":"Internodal pathways, intercalated discs and gap junctions","why":"The signal spreads across the atria, and gap junctions in the intercalated discs let it pass directly from one cardiac cell to the next."},{"pt":"Tunica intima, media, externa","why":"These are the three tunics from the lumen outward, and a capillary has only the intima."},{"pt":"Artery: thick media, round lumen, no valves. Vein: thin wall, larger flat lumen, valves","why":"Arteries carry blood under high pressure and keep their shape, while veins carry it under low pressure and rely on valves to stop backflow."}]},{"id":"m3-conduction-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[64,56],"cids":["cv-conduction-anat","cv-surfaces"],"t":"Map the conduction system","p":"Draw the heart in frontal section and draw in every part of the conduction system in its correct place. Label each part.","k":[{"pt":"SA node in the wall of the right atrium near the superior vena cava","why":"The pacemaker sits high in the right atrium, which is where every heartbeat begins."},{"pt":"AV node in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum","why":"It sits at the junction of the atria and ventricles, ready to pass the signal down."},{"pt":"AV bundle, the bundle of His, in the top of the interventricular septum","why":"It is the only electrical bridge from the atria to the ventricles."},{"pt":"Right and left bundle branches down both sides of the septum","why":"They carry the signal down toward the apex of the heart."},{"pt":"Purkinje fibers through the walls of both ventricles","why":"They deliver the signal to the working muscle cells of the ventricle walls."},{"pt":"Moderator band in the right ventricle","why":"This muscular band carries part of the conduction system across to the anterior papillary muscle."}]},{"id":"m3-conduction-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[60,77],"cids":["cv-cardiac-muscle","w4-muscle-tissue-types"],"t":"Cardiac muscle on the slide","p":"Draw cardiac muscle as it looks under the microscope. Label the cell, its nucleus, the striations, and the junction between cells, and name the two kinds of connection inside that junction.","k":[{"pt":"Cardiomyocytes, short and branching","why":"The branching is what lets cardiac muscle form a connected network instead of separate parallel fibers."},{"pt":"One central nucleus in each cell","why":"This separates cardiac muscle from skeletal muscle, whose fibers have many nuclei pushed to the edge."},{"pt":"Striations","why":"Cardiac muscle is striped like skeletal muscle because it is also built of ordered sarcomeres."},{"pt":"Intercalated discs where cells meet end to end","why":"These dark lines across the fibers are found only in cardiac muscle, so they are the fastest way to name it."},{"pt":"Desmosomes in the discs","why":"These anchoring junctions keep the cells from pulling apart as the heart contracts."},{"pt":"Gap junctions in the discs","why":"These channels let the signal pass directly from cell to cell, so the heart contracts as one unit."}]},{"id":"m3-conduction-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[64],"cids":["cv-conduction-anat"],"t":"Where each part sits and what it does","p":"Put the parts of the conduction system in order from first to last. For each one, write where it sits and what it does with the signal.","k":[{"pt":"SA node, right atrial wall near the superior vena cava: fires first and sets the pace","why":"It is the natural pacemaker, firing about 60 to 100 times a minute."},{"pt":"Across both atria: the atria contract","why":"The signal spreads through the atrial walls before it reaches the ventricles, so the atria go first."},{"pt":"AV node, floor of the right atrium: the signal pauses briefly","why":"The pause gives the atria time to finish emptying into the ventricles."},{"pt":"AV bundle, top of the interventricular septum: crosses into the ventricles","why":"It is the only electrical link between the atria and the ventricles."},{"pt":"Bundle branches, both sides of the septum: carry it toward the apex","why":"Sending the signal to the apex first lets the ventricles contract from the bottom up."},{"pt":"Purkinje fibers, ventricle walls: deliver it to the contractile cells","why":"Once the working cells receive it, the ventricles contract from the apex upward."}]},{"id":"m3-conduction-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[64,58],"cids":["cv-conduction-anat","cv-valves"],"t":"One bridge between atria and ventricles","p":"Explain how the fibrous skeleton and the AV bundle together control the order in which the chambers contract.","k":[{"pt":"The fibrous skeleton is the set of dense connective tissue rings around the four valve openings","why":"It sits right at the level where the atria meet the ventricles."},{"pt":"It electrically insulates the atria from the ventricles","why":"A signal in the atrial walls cannot spread straight down into the ventricle walls."},{"pt":"The AV bundle passes through it as the only electrical link","why":"Every signal bound for the ventricles has to take this one route."},{"pt":"The AV node holds the signal briefly before the bundle","why":"The pause lets the atria finish emptying before the ventricles get the signal."},{"pt":"Result: atria contract first and ventricles second","why":"Each chamber fills before it empties, which is the point of the conduction order."}]},{"id":"m3-conduction-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[65],"cids":["cv-cardiac-nerves"],"t":"Nerves that adjust the rate","p":"Describe the nerve supply to the heart: where the control comes from, the network the nerves pass through, the two kinds of nerve, and the parts of the conduction system they reach.","k":[{"pt":"Cardiac centers in the medulla oblongata","why":"These regions of the brainstem adjust the heart rate to the body's needs."},{"pt":"Cardiac plexus near the base of the heart","why":"This network of autonomic fibers is the route the nerves take to reach the heart."},{"pt":"Sympathetic nerves speed the rate","why":"They reach the heart through the cardiac plexus."},{"pt":"Parasympathetic fibers in the vagus nerve slow the rate","why":"The vagus nerve carries the parasympathetic supply down from the brainstem."},{"pt":"The nerves reach the SA and AV nodes","why":"Acting on the nodes is how they change the pace the conduction system sets."},{"pt":"They adjust the rate but do not start the beat","why":"The SA node starts every beat on its own, and the nerves only speed it up or slow it down."}]},{"id":"m3-conduction-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[64,65],"cids":["cv-conduction-anat","cv-cardiac-nerves"],"t":"A transplanted heart","p":"A transplanted heart has had all its nerves cut, yet it beats on its own. Explain from the anatomy what keeps it beating and what it has lost.","k":[{"pt":"The conduction system is built into the heart itself","why":"It is a network of specialized cardiac muscle cells, about one percent of the cardiac fibers, so it comes with the donor heart."},{"pt":"The SA node in the right atrial wall still fires on its own","why":"This built-in ability to set a rhythm is called autorhythmicity."},{"pt":"The rest of the pathway is intact: AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers","why":"The signal can still travel its normal route, so the chambers still contract in order."},{"pt":"Lost: the sympathetic and vagal fibers that reached the nodes through the cardiac plexus","why":"Those nerves were cut during the transplant."},{"pt":"The heart can no longer have its rate adjusted by nerves","why":"This is the clearest proof that the heartbeat comes from the conduction system and not the nervous system."}]},{"id":"m3-conduction-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[64],"cids":["cv-conduction-anat"],"t":"A block at the AV node","p":"A patient's signal is stopped at the AV node. Using the conduction system and the fibrous skeleton, explain why the atria and ventricles now beat out of step.","k":[{"pt":"The SA node still fires and the atria still get the signal","why":"Everything above the AV node works normally."},{"pt":"The signal stops at the AV node in the floor of the right atrium","why":"This is the gateway the signal must pass to reach the ventricles."},{"pt":"The fibrous skeleton blocks every other route","why":"It insulates the atria from the ventricles, so the signal cannot go around the block."},{"pt":"The AV bundle, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers no longer receive the atrial signal","why":"The ventricles lose their link to the pacemaker above, so they fall out of step with the atria."},{"pt":"This is heart block, and an artificial pacemaker can carry a signal past it","why":"The device delivers a timed signal that restores an ordered beat."}]},{"id":"m3-conduction-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[64,56],"cids":["cv-conduction-anat","cv-surfaces"],"t":"Signal to a papillary muscle","p":"Trace the signal from the SA node all the way to the anterior papillary muscle of the right ventricle. Name every structure it passes and where each one sits.","k":[{"pt":"SA node, wall of the right atrium near the superior vena cava","why":"Every beat starts at the pacemaker."},{"pt":"Across the atria","why":"The signal spreads through both atrial walls first."},{"pt":"AV node, floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum","why":"The signal pauses here before entering the ventricles."},{"pt":"AV bundle, top of the interventricular septum","why":"This is the only route from the atria into the ventricles."},{"pt":"Right bundle branch down the right side of the septum","why":"The right branch serves the right ventricle."},{"pt":"Moderator band to the anterior papillary muscle","why":"This muscular band crosses the right ventricle and carries part of the conduction system straight to that papillary muscle."}]}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-wall","title":"Blood vessel histology and vessel types","mw":"2026-10-05","tr":"2026-10-01","prompts":[{"id":"m3-vessel-wall-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[68,66],"cids":["bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds","bvn-vessel-tunics"],"t":"Three kinds of capillary","p":"Name the three capillary types. For each, draw the wall, describe what makes it leaky or tight, and name a place you would find it.","k":[{"pt":"Continuous: unbroken endothelium, tight junctions, muscle, skin, brain","why":"The lining is interrupted only by small intercellular clefts, so it keeps most substances in, and it is the most common type."},{"pt":"Fenestrated: pores in the endothelium, kidney, intestine, endocrine glands","why":"The small pores, called fenestrations, let fluid move fast where absorption or filtration happens."},{"pt":"Sinusoidal: large gaps and incomplete basement membrane, liver, spleen, bone marrow","why":"These wide, winding vessels are leaky enough for whole cells to cross the wall."},{"pt":"Basement membrane drawn in each","why":"A capillary wall is just endothelium on a basement membrane, the tunica intima with no media or externa."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-wall-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[66],"cids":["bvn-vessel-tunics"],"t":"The artery wall","p":"Draw an artery in cross section and label every layer and structure in its wall, from the lumen out.","k":[{"pt":"Lumen","why":"The hollow channel the blood flows through, which stays round in an artery."},{"pt":"Tunica intima: endothelium on a basement membrane","why":"This simple squamous lining is continuous with the endocardium of the heart."},{"pt":"Internal elastic lamina","why":"This sheet of elastic fibers marks the boundary between the intima and the media."},{"pt":"Tunica media: smooth muscle and elastic fibers","why":"It is the thickest layer in an artery, and its smooth muscle sets the vessel's diameter."},{"pt":"External elastic lamina","why":"This elastic sheet marks the outer edge of the media, so the two laminae frame the middle layer."},{"pt":"Tunica externa with vasa vasorum","why":"This mostly collagen layer anchors the vessel, and the small vasa vasorum in it supply the wall of a large vessel."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-wall-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[67],"cids":["bvn-vessel-types"],"t":"The five vessel types in order","p":"Put the five vessel types in the order blood flows through them. For each, write the direction of flow, what its wall is like, and its job.","k":[{"pt":"Arteries: away from the heart, thick walls with a heavy tunica media","why":"They carry blood under high pressure, so they need the strongest walls."},{"pt":"Arterioles: the smallest arteries, one to two layers of smooth muscle","why":"They control how much blood enters each capillary bed and are the chief site of resistance."},{"pt":"Capillaries: connect arterioles to venules, a wall of tunica intima only","why":"A single thin layer is what makes them the exchange vessels for gases and nutrients."},{"pt":"Venules: the smallest veins, thin porous walls","why":"They collect blood leaving the capillaries, and they are where white blood cells leave the bloodstream."},{"pt":"Veins: toward the heart, thin walls, wide lumen, valves","why":"They carry blood under low pressure, so valves keep it moving the right way."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-wall-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[68],"cids":["bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"],"t":"Three sizes of artery","p":"Compare elastic arteries, muscular arteries, and arterioles: what fills the tunica media of each, one or two examples, and what each one does.","k":[{"pt":"Elastic artery: thick media full of elastic lamellae, clear internal and external elastic laminae","why":"All that elastic tissue lets the wall stretch with each heartbeat and recoil between beats."},{"pt":"Elastic examples: aorta, pulmonary trunk, common carotid, subclavian, common iliac","why":"These are the aorta, the pulmonary trunk, and their largest branches, the vessels that take the full stretch of each heartbeat."},{"pt":"Elastic job: recoil keeps blood moving while the ventricles relax","why":"That cushioning is what is lost when elastic arteries stiffen with age."},{"pt":"Muscular artery: mostly smooth muscle, up to 40 layers, thin external elastic lamina","why":"Examples are the femoral, axillary, brachial, and radial arteries, which distribute blood to specific organs."},{"pt":"Arteriole: one or two layers of smooth muscle, internal elastic lamina fading at its end","why":"It regulates flow into a capillary bed by narrowing or widening."},{"pt":"Moving down the tree, elastic tissue gives way to smooth muscle and the wall thins","why":"The wall changes from built for recoil, to built for distribution, to built for fine control."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-wall-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[66,67,68],"cids":["bvn-vessel-tunics","bvn-vessel-types","bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"],"t":"Artery and vein side by side","p":"An artery and its companion vein sit next to each other on a slide. List every difference you would use to tell them apart, and explain how each one fits the vessel's job.","k":[{"pt":"Artery has a thick tunica media, vein a thin one","why":"Arteries carry blood under high pressure, so they need more smooth muscle in the middle layer."},{"pt":"The thickest layer in a vein is the tunica externa","why":"Both vessels have the same three tunics, only in different proportions."},{"pt":"Artery holds a round lumen, vein looks collapsed with a wider lumen","why":"The artery's thick wall keeps its shape, while the low pressure vein's thin wall folds."},{"pt":"Artery shows clear internal and external elastic laminae","why":"The laminae frame the tunica media, and the thickness between them tells you which vessel you have."},{"pt":"Vein may show a valve, an artery never does","why":"Venous valves are flaps of the tunica intima that keep low pressure blood from flowing backward."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-wall-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[68],"cids":["bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"],"t":"Matching the capillary to the organ","p":"In one organ whole blood cells must cross the capillary wall, and in another almost nothing should get through. Name a capillary type and an organ for each case, and explain how the wall's structure fits the job.","k":[{"pt":"Whole cells crossing: sinusoids, in the liver, spleen, or red bone marrow","why":"These organs need cells to move between the blood and the tissue."},{"pt":"Sinusoid wall: large fenestrations, an incomplete basement membrane, wide clefts","why":"Gaps that big are the only way a whole cell can pass."},{"pt":"Almost nothing crossing: continuous capillaries, in the brain and central nervous system","why":"The brain keeps most substances out of its tissue."},{"pt":"Continuous wall: unbroken endothelium interrupted only by intercellular clefts","why":"With no pores, the wall stays tight."},{"pt":"In between: fenestrated capillaries in the kidney or small intestine","why":"Their small pores let fluid move fast without letting cells through."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-wall-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[67,68],"cids":["bvn-vessel-types","bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"],"t":"Why blood is drawn from a vein","p":"Explain from the wall structure why blood is drawn and IV lines are placed in veins rather than arteries, and say what the small bumps along a forearm vein are.","k":[{"pt":"Veins sit closer to the surface","why":"A vessel near the skin is easier to find and reach with a needle."},{"pt":"Thin wall and wide lumen","why":"A thin wall is easy to pierce and a wide lumen is easy to enter."},{"pt":"Low pressure","why":"Blood under low pressure is slow to bleed once the needle comes out."},{"pt":"Arteries have a thick muscular media and high pressure","why":"That makes them harder to enter and more likely to bleed."},{"pt":"The bumps are venous valves","why":"These flaps of the tunica intima keep blood from flowing backward and can be seen along limb veins."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-wall-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[66,68],"cids":["bvn-vessel-tunics","bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"],"t":"Name the vessel from its wall","p":"On a slide you find a large vessel whose thick tunica media is made mostly of elastic sheets, with clear internal and external elastic laminae and tiny vessels in its outer layer. Name the kind of vessel, give two examples, and explain what each clue tells you.","k":[{"pt":"It is an elastic artery","why":"A media dominated by elastic tissue only occurs in the largest arteries."},{"pt":"Examples: aorta and pulmonary trunk","why":"Others are the brachiocephalic trunk, subclavian, common carotid, and common iliac arteries."},{"pt":"Elastic sheets in the media mean the wall stretches and recoils","why":"That recoil keeps blood moving between heartbeats."},{"pt":"Clear internal and external elastic laminae mark the edges of the media","why":"Finding them tells you where the middle layer starts and stops."},{"pt":"The tiny vessels are the vasa vasorum in the tunica externa","why":"A wall this thick needs its own blood supply."},{"pt":"It is not a muscular artery","why":"A muscular artery has a media made mostly of smooth muscle and only a thin external elastic lamina."}]}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-organization","title":"Muscle tissue types and muscle organization","mw":"2026-10-07","tr":"2026-10-06","prompts":[{"id":"m3-muscle-organization-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[73,72],"cids":["w4-muscle-organization","w4-ct-coverings"],"t":"Whole muscle down to filament","p":"Dump muscle from the whole organ down to the myofilament. Name every level and every wrapping in order.","k":[{"pt":"Muscle, epimysium","why":"The whole organ is wrapped in the epimysium, the outermost connective tissue sheath."},{"pt":"Fascicle, perimysium","why":"A fascicle is a visible bundle of muscle fibers, and each bundle has its own perimysium."},{"pt":"Muscle fiber or cell, endomysium, sarcolemma","why":"A fiber is one muscle cell, with the fine endomysium wrapped outside its plasma membrane, the sarcolemma."},{"pt":"Myofibril, sarcomere, thick and thin filaments","why":"Myofibrils fill the fiber, and the ordered overlap of their thick and thin filaments forms the sarcomeres."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-organization-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[72],"cids":["w4-ct-coverings"],"t":"Bone to bone","p":"Draw a whole muscle in cross section and label every connective tissue layer from the outside in. Then write what all of those layers become at the end of the muscle.","k":[{"pt":"Epimysium around the whole muscle","why":"This dense irregular connective tissue is the outermost layer of the muscle itself."},{"pt":"Perimysium around each fascicle","why":"It divides the muscle into bundles of fibers."},{"pt":"Endomysium around each fiber","why":"This fine sheath of areolar tissue with reticular fibers wraps every single muscle cell."},{"pt":"All three merge into the tendon","why":"The sheaths are continuous with each other and with the tendon, so every fiber's pull is carried to the bone."},{"pt":"Deep fascia outside the epimysium","why":"This sheet of dense connective tissue wraps and separates muscles and binds those with a common action into groups."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-organization-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[78],"cids":["w4-fascicle-patterns"],"t":"Fascicle patterns","p":"Draw a small muscle for each fascicle pattern, with lines showing which way the fascicles run. Label each with its pattern name and one example muscle.","k":[{"pt":"Parallel: sartorius or rectus abdominis","why":"The fascicles run parallel to the long axis of the muscle."},{"pt":"Fusiform: biceps brachii","why":"The fascicles run parallel, but the belly is thick in the middle and tapers to a tendon at each end."},{"pt":"Circular: orbicularis oris or orbicularis oculi","why":"The fascicles form rings around an opening, which makes the muscle a sphincter."},{"pt":"Convergent: pectoralis major","why":"A broad origin funnels its fascicles onto one tendon, giving a fan shape."},{"pt":"Unipennate: extensor digitorum","why":"Short fascicles attach at an angle to one side of a central tendon."},{"pt":"Bipennate: rectus femoris, and multipennate: deltoid","why":"Bipennate fascicles attach on both sides of the tendon, and multipennate fascicles attach to a branched tendon."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-organization-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[72],"cids":["w4-ct-coverings"],"t":"How muscle attaches","p":"Draw a muscle spanning a joint and label how it attaches at both ends. Name the connective tissue continuity from one bone to the other.","k":[{"pt":"Origin and insertion named","why":"The origin is on the more stationary bone and the insertion is on the bone that gets pulled toward it."},{"pt":"Endomysium to perimysium to epimysium, all continuous","why":"The three sheaths connect without a break, so the pull of each fiber passes outward through every layer."},{"pt":"Continue into the tendon or aponeurosis","why":"The merged sheaths become the attachment that reaches the bone."},{"pt":"Tendon into the periosteum, Sharpey fibers into the bone","why":"The tendon blends into the periosteum, and perforating fibers of collagen anchor the periosteum into the bone beneath."},{"pt":"Tendon is a cord, aponeurosis is a sheet","why":"A broad, flat aponeurosis is used where a muscle is wide, and a cord-shaped tendon where it narrows."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-organization-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[77],"cids":["w4-muscle-tissue-types"],"t":"Three muscle tissues compared","p":"Compare skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle by where each is found, whether it has striations, whether it is under voluntary control, and what its cells look like.","k":[{"pt":"Skeletal: attached to the skeleton, striated, voluntary","why":"It is the only one of the three you control by choice."},{"pt":"Skeletal cells: long cylindrical fibers with many nuclei at the edge","why":"Many peripheral nuclei in a long fiber are the slide clue for skeletal muscle."},{"pt":"Cardiac: the heart wall, striated, involuntary","why":"It has striations like skeletal muscle but works without conscious control."},{"pt":"Cardiac cells: branching, one central nucleus, joined by intercalated discs","why":"Intercalated discs are found only in cardiac muscle."},{"pt":"Smooth: walls of hollow organs and vessels, no striations, involuntary","why":"Without ordered sarcomeres there are no stripes to see."},{"pt":"Smooth cells: spindle-shaped with one central nucleus","why":"The tapered shape and lack of striations separate it from the other two."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-organization-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[78],"cids":["w4-fascicle-patterns"],"t":"Power or range","p":"Compare a parallel muscle with a pennate muscle: how the fascicles are arranged, how long they are, and whether the design gives more power or more range of motion.","k":[{"pt":"Parallel: long fascicles running along the length of the muscle","why":"Sartorius is the classic example."},{"pt":"Parallel gives a larger range of motion","why":"A fiber shortens to about two thirds of its resting length, so a longer fascicle moves the insertion farther."},{"pt":"Parallel gives less power","why":"Fewer fibers fit into each unit of muscle, so fewer pull on the tendon."},{"pt":"Pennate: short fascicles attached at an angle to a central tendon","why":"The deltoid is a multipennate example."},{"pt":"Pennate gives more power but a smaller range","why":"Many more short fibers are packed in to pull on the tendon, but each one shortens only a short distance."},{"pt":"A muscle cannot be built for both at once","why":"Architecture is a trade-off between power and range of motion."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-organization-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[72],"cids":["w4-ct-coverings"],"t":"A strain or a rupture","p":"One athlete strains a thigh muscle and another ruptures a tendon. Using the connective tissue layers, explain where each injury happens and why the rupture stops the muscle from moving the bone at all.","k":[{"pt":"A strain tears muscle fibers and endomysium within the belly","why":"The damage is inside the muscle, so the connection to the bone is still there."},{"pt":"The three sheaths are continuous with each other and the tendon","why":"That continuity is how the pull of every fiber normally reaches the bone."},{"pt":"The tendon is the merged continuation of all three sheaths","why":"It is the one cord that carries all the muscle's pull to the bone."},{"pt":"A rupture separates the muscle from the bone entirely","why":"Once the tendon is broken, contraction pulls on nothing, so the bone does not move."},{"pt":"The two injuries heal on very different timelines","why":"They involve different tissues, muscle in one and dense regular connective tissue in the other."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-organization-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[78],"cids":["w4-fascicle-patterns"],"t":"Read the architecture","p":"A muscle has short fascicles that attach at an angle on both sides of a central tendon, like a feather. Name the pattern and the example from the notes, then predict whether it is built for power or for range of motion and explain why.","k":[{"pt":"The pattern is bipennate","why":"Pennate means feather-shaped, and bi means the fascicles attach on both sides of the tendon."},{"pt":"The example is the rectus femoris","why":"It is the bipennate example in the notes."},{"pt":"It is built for power","why":"Packing many short fascicles at an angle puts many more fibers on the tendon."},{"pt":"Its range of motion is smaller","why":"Short, angled fascicles shorten by only a short distance."},{"pt":"A parallel muscle like the sartorius would be the opposite","why":"Its long fascicles trade power for a long sweeping range."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-organization-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[77,60],"cids":["w4-muscle-tissue-types","cv-cardiac-muscle"],"t":"Identify the muscle tissue","p":"A slide shows striated cells that branch, each with one central nucleus, joined end to end by dark lines that cross the fibers. Name the tissue and where it comes from, and explain how each clue rules out the other two muscle types.","k":[{"pt":"It is cardiac muscle from the myocardium of the heart wall","why":"Only cardiac muscle has all of these features together."},{"pt":"Striations rule out smooth muscle","why":"Smooth muscle has no striations because it lacks ordered sarcomeres."},{"pt":"Branching cells with one central nucleus rule out skeletal muscle","why":"Skeletal fibers are long and cylindrical with many nuclei at the edge."},{"pt":"The dark lines are intercalated discs","why":"These junctions are found only in cardiac muscle, so they confirm the answer."},{"pt":"The discs hold desmosomes and gap junctions","why":"Desmosomes keep the cells together and gap junctions pass the signal from cell to cell."}]}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-microanatomy","title":"Muscle fiber microanatomy and the sarcomere","mw":"2026-10-07","tr":"2026-10-06","prompts":[{"id":"m3-muscle-microanatomy-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[75],"cids":["w4-sarcomere"],"t":"Draw a sarcomere","p":"Draw one sarcomere and label every band, line, and zone. Then mark what gets shorter when the muscle contracts and what does not.","k":[{"pt":"Z discs at both ends, M line in the middle","why":"A sarcomere runs from one Z disc to the next, and the M line holds the thick filaments together in its center."},{"pt":"A band, I band, H zone","why":"The dark A band is as wide as the thick filaments are long, the light I band has thin filaments only, and the H zone in the middle of the A band has thick filaments only."},{"pt":"Thick filament across the A band, thin from the Z disc","why":"Thick filaments are anchored at the M line and thin filaments at the Z disc, and they overlap at each edge of the A band."},{"pt":"I band and H zone shrink, A band does not","why":"The A band is the length of the thick filament, which does not change, so only the regions without overlap narrow."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-microanatomy-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[74],"cids":["w4-muscle-fiber-parts"],"t":"SR and T tubules","p":"Draw a stretch of a muscle fiber cut open. Label the sarcoplasmic reticulum, the terminal cisternae, and the T tubules, and show where the triad sits relative to the sarcomere.","k":[{"pt":"T tubule as an inward fold of the sarcolemma","why":"These narrow tunnels of the plasma membrane dive inward and carry the surface signal deep into the fiber."},{"pt":"SR wrapping each myofibril","why":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the fiber's smooth endoplasmic reticulum, and it stores calcium ions."},{"pt":"Terminal cisternae as the swollen SR ends","why":"These enlarged end sacs sit on either side of a T tubule."},{"pt":"Triad: one T tubule between two terminal cisternae","why":"This is the meeting point where the surface signal reaches the calcium store."},{"pt":"Sits at the A band and I band junction","why":"The triads repeat at the same spot in every sarcomere, which lets the whole fiber respond almost at once."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-microanatomy-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[74,175],"cids":["w4-muscle-fiber-parts","w8-synapse-parts"],"t":"The neuromuscular junction","p":"Draw the neuromuscular junction and label everything on both sides of the gap.","k":[{"pt":"Axon terminal with synaptic vesicles","why":"The motor neuron's ending stores its neurotransmitter in vesicles, ready to release into the gap."},{"pt":"Synaptic cleft","why":"The neuron and the muscle fiber do not touch, and this narrow gap separates them."},{"pt":"Motor end plate with junctional folds","why":"The end plate is the part of the sarcolemma under the axon terminal, and its folds increase the surface facing the nerve."},{"pt":"Receptors on the sarcolemma","why":"The receptors sit on the muscle side of the cleft, where the neurotransmitter arrives."},{"pt":"Mitochondria in the terminal, Schwann cell capping it","why":"The mitochondria fuel the axon terminal, and the Schwann cell covers the terminal from the outside."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-microanatomy-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[76,74],"cids":["w4-myofilament-proteins","w4-muscle-fiber-parts"],"t":"Filament proteins and the triad","p":"List the proteins of the thick filament and the thin filament and what each one does. Then write what a triad is and what it contains.","k":[{"pt":"Thick: myosin, head and tail","why":"A thick filament is a bundle of myosin molecules whose heads stick out as cross bridges, and it is anchored at the M line."},{"pt":"Thin: actin, tropomyosin, troponin","why":"Actin forms two twisted strands with myosin binding sites, tropomyosin lies end to end along them, and troponin is bound at intervals on the tropomyosin."},{"pt":"Titin, nebulin, dystrophin if they get there","why":"Titin runs from the Z disc to the M line for elastic recoil, nebulin sets the length of the thin filament, and dystrophin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma."},{"pt":"Triad: one T tubule between two terminal cisternae of sarcoplasmic reticulum","why":"It is where the surface membrane meets the calcium store of the fiber."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-microanatomy-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[75],"cids":["w4-sarcomere"],"t":"What sits in each region","p":"Make a table of the Z disc, A band, I band, H zone, M line, and zone of overlap. For each one, write which filaments are found there and what, if anything, is anchored there.","k":[{"pt":"Z disc: the border of the sarcomere, anchors the thin filaments","why":"Thin filaments start at the Z disc and reach toward the middle."},{"pt":"A band: thick filaments across its full width, with thin filaments overlapping at each edge","why":"It is the dark band because the thick filaments make it dense."},{"pt":"I band: thin filaments only, with a Z disc through its center","why":"Because a Z disc splits it, one I band is shared by two sarcomeres."},{"pt":"H zone: thick filaments only, in the center of the A band","why":"It looks lighter because no thin filaments reach that far in at rest."},{"pt":"M line: center of the H zone, anchors the thick filaments to one another","why":"It holds the thick filaments lined up in the middle of the sarcomere."},{"pt":"Zone of overlap: both thick and thin filaments, at each end of the A band","why":"This is where the two filament types interleave."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-microanatomy-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[76],"cids":["w4-myofilament-proteins"],"t":"The structural proteins","p":"Sort titin, nebulin, alpha-actinin, myomesin, and dystrophin by where each one sits, and write what each one holds or links.","k":[{"pt":"Titin: from the Z disc to the M line","why":"It anchors the thick filament, gives the sarcomere elastic recoil, and keeps it from overstretching."},{"pt":"Nebulin: along the length of the thin filament","why":"It anchors the thin filament and sets its length."},{"pt":"Alpha-actinin: in the Z disc","why":"It binds the thin filaments and titin to the Z disc."},{"pt":"Myomesin: at the M line","why":"It binds the thick filaments together in the middle of the sarcomere."},{"pt":"Dystrophin: between the thin filaments and the sarcolemma","why":"It passes the force of contraction from the sarcomere out to the connective tissue."},{"pt":"Dystrophin is the protein that is faulty in muscular dystrophy","why":"Without it the sarcomere loses its link to the cell membrane."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-microanatomy-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[75],"cids":["w4-sarcomere"],"t":"Predict the shortened sarcomere","p":"A muscle shortens. Predict what happens to the A band, the I band, the H zone, and the distance between the Z discs, and explain each answer from the filaments in that region.","k":[{"pt":"The A band stays the same width","why":"It is exactly as wide as the thick filaments are long, and the thick filaments do not change length."},{"pt":"The I band narrows","why":"It holds thin filaments only, and it shrinks as the thin filaments slide farther over the thick ones."},{"pt":"The H zone narrows","why":"It holds thick filaments only, and the thin filaments move farther into it."},{"pt":"The Z discs move closer together","why":"The thin filaments are anchored at the Z discs, so as they move toward the M line they pull the discs inward."},{"pt":"The zone of overlap gets wider","why":"More of each thin filament now lies alongside the thick filaments."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-microanatomy-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[76],"cids":["w4-myofilament-proteins"],"t":"When dystrophin is missing","p":"In Duchenne muscular dystrophy one structural protein is faulty. Name it, say exactly what it links, and explain from the anatomy why the fibers damage themselves during ordinary use.","k":[{"pt":"The protein is dystrophin","why":"Duchenne muscular dystrophy is an inherited disease caused by a faulty dystrophin gene."},{"pt":"It links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma","why":"It is the bridge between the contractile machinery and the cell membrane."},{"pt":"Normally it passes the force of contraction out to the connective tissue","why":"From the sarcolemma the pull continues through the endomysium and the other sheaths to the tendon."},{"pt":"Without it the link between sarcomere and sarcolemma fails","why":"The force has no proper route out of the fiber."},{"pt":"The fiber damages itself with ordinary use, so weakness gets worse over time","why":"Every contraction strains a fiber that cannot pass its force on safely."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-microanatomy-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[74],"cids":["w4-muscle-fiber-parts"],"t":"From the surface to the calcium store","p":"A signal arrives on the surface of a muscle fiber, but the calcium store sits deep inside. Name in order the structures that bring the surface membrane to the calcium store, and explain why their arrangement lets the whole fiber respond almost at once.","k":[{"pt":"Sarcolemma at the surface","why":"The plasma membrane of the fiber is where the signal arrives."},{"pt":"T tubule diving inward","why":"A T tubule is a tunnel of the sarcolemma, so it carries the surface membrane deep into the fiber."},{"pt":"Triad","why":"One T tubule lies between two terminal cisternae, putting the surface membrane right next to the store."},{"pt":"Terminal cisternae","why":"These are the enlarged end sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"pt":"Sarcoplasmic reticulum wrapping each myofibril","why":"It is the fiber's smooth endoplasmic reticulum, and it stores the calcium."},{"pt":"Triads repeat tightly along every myofibril","why":"Because the meeting points are everywhere in the fiber, the signal reaches the whole fiber nearly at once."}]}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-naming-levers","title":"Muscle roles, naming and lever systems","mw":"2026-10-07","tr":"2026-10-06","prompts":[{"id":"m3-muscle-naming-levers-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[79],"cids":["w4-muscle-roles-naming"],"t":"Roles in a movement","p":"List the four roles a muscle can play in a movement. Define each one and give the example from the notes.","k":[{"pt":"Agonist, the prime mover: biceps brachii in elbow flexion","why":"It is the muscle mainly responsible for producing the movement."},{"pt":"Antagonist: triceps brachii in elbow flexion","why":"It opposes the agonist, relaxing and lengthening as the agonist contracts, and it can slow or reverse the movement."},{"pt":"Synergist: brachialis assisting elbow flexion","why":"It helps the agonist by adding force or preventing an unwanted movement."},{"pt":"Fixator: scapular muscles steadying the scapula during arm movements","why":"A fixator holds the origin bone still so the agonist can act efficiently on the insertion."},{"pt":"The same muscle can play different roles in different movements","why":"The triceps brachii is the antagonist when the elbow bends but becomes the agonist when the elbow straightens."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-naming-levers-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[80],"cids":["w4-lever-systems"],"t":"The three lever classes","p":"Draw the three classes of lever. Label the fulcrum, effort, and load on each, and draw or write the body example beside it.","k":[{"pt":"In the body the bone is the lever, the joint is the fulcrum, the muscle pull is the effort, and the body part's weight is the load","why":"Every movement is a lever, so each part has a body match."},{"pt":"First class: fulcrum in the middle, like a seesaw","why":"Nodding the head: the atlanto-occipital joint is the fulcrum, the posterior neck muscles give the effort, and the face is the load."},{"pt":"Second class: load in the middle, like a wheelbarrow","why":"Standing on tiptoe: the ball of the foot is the fulcrum, the body is the load, and the calf muscles pull up the heel."},{"pt":"Third class: effort in the middle, like tweezers","why":"Flexing the elbow: the elbow joint is the fulcrum, the biceps pull is the effort, and the weight in the hand is the load."},{"pt":"Third class is the most common lever in the body","why":"Most body movements run on third class levers, which is why most muscles work at a mechanical disadvantage."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-naming-levers-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[79],"cids":["w4-muscle-roles-naming"],"t":"Naming clues","p":"List the clues used to name muscles and give at least one example name for each clue.","k":[{"pt":"Direction of fascicles: rectus, oblique, transversus","why":"These describe how the fibers run relative to the midline: straight, at an angle, or across."},{"pt":"Size: maximus, minimus, longus, brevis","why":"These compare a muscle with its neighbors."},{"pt":"Shape: deltoid, trapezius, rhomboid","why":"These describe the overall shape: triangular, trapezoid, and rhombus shaped."},{"pt":"Location: tibialis anterior, frontalis, intercostals","why":"These name the bone or body region the muscle lies near."},{"pt":"Number of origins: biceps, triceps, quadriceps","why":"These count the heads, two, three, and four."},{"pt":"Origin and insertion: sternocleidomastoid","why":"The name lists its attachments: sternum and clavicle to the mastoid process."},{"pt":"Action: flexor, extensor, abductor, adductor, levator","why":"These name the movement the muscle produces."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-naming-levers-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[79],"cids":["w4-muscle-roles-naming"],"t":"Decode a muscle name","p":"Pick five muscles. For each, break the name apart and write what each piece tells you: shape, size, location, fiber direction, attachments, or action.","k":[{"pt":"Five muscles named","why":"Five gives you enough names to use several different clue types."},{"pt":"Each name broken into its parts","why":"Most names combine two or more clues, so each piece has to be read on its own."},{"pt":"What each part means, for example rectus is straight, oblique is angled","why":"Fiber direction words describe how the fascicles run relative to the midline."},{"pt":"Latissimus dorsi as widest of the back","why":"This name combines a size clue with a location clue."},{"pt":"Sternocleidomastoid read as its three attachments","why":"Sterno and cleido are the sternum and clavicle where it starts, and mastoid is the process where it ends."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-naming-levers-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[79],"cids":["w4-muscle-roles-naming"],"t":"Roles switch","p":"For bending the elbow and then straightening it, name the agonist, the antagonist, a synergist, and a fixator in each movement. Show which muscles switch jobs.","k":[{"pt":"Bending: biceps brachii is the agonist","why":"It is the prime mover of elbow flexion."},{"pt":"Bending: triceps brachii is the antagonist","why":"It relaxes and lengthens as the biceps contracts."},{"pt":"Bending: brachialis is a synergist","why":"It adds force to elbow flexion."},{"pt":"Straightening: triceps becomes the agonist and biceps the antagonist","why":"The same pair works across the elbow, so their roles swap when the movement reverses."},{"pt":"Straightening: anconeus is a synergist","why":"It assists the triceps in extending the elbow."},{"pt":"In both movements scapular muscles act as fixators","why":"Holding the scapula steady gives the arm muscles a stable base to pull from."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-naming-levers-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[80],"cids":["w4-lever-systems"],"t":"Advantage or disadvantage","p":"Compare a lever working at a mechanical advantage with one working at a mechanical disadvantage. Say where the effort sits, what the body gains, and which lever class usually works at a disadvantage.","k":[{"pt":"Advantage: the effort is farther from the fulcrum than the load","why":"With the longer arm on the effort side, a small effort can move a large load."},{"pt":"Disadvantage: the effort is closer to the fulcrum than the load","why":"A large effort is needed to move the load."},{"pt":"Disadvantage buys speed and range","why":"The load moves fast and far for only a small amount of muscle shortening."},{"pt":"Third class levers work at a disadvantage","why":"The effort sits between the fulcrum and the load, so it is always closer to the fulcrum."},{"pt":"Second class levers work at an advantage","why":"The load sits between the fulcrum and the effort, so the effort is always farther away."},{"pt":"Most muscles must pull far harder than the weight they lift","why":"Most body movements run on third class levers, trading force for speed and range."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-naming-levers-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[80],"cids":["w4-lever-systems"],"t":"Classify the movement","p":"You rise up onto your toes to reach a high shelf, then come back down and curl a grocery bag up toward your shoulder. Classify the lever in each movement and name its fulcrum, effort, and load.","k":[{"pt":"Rising onto the toes is a second class lever","why":"The load sits between the fulcrum and the effort."},{"pt":"Toes: the ball of the foot is the fulcrum, the body is the load, the calf muscles are the effort","why":"The calf pulls up on the heel while the body's weight sits in between."},{"pt":"Curling the bag is a third class lever","why":"The effort sits between the fulcrum and the load."},{"pt":"Curl: the elbow joint is the fulcrum, the biceps pull is the effort, the bag in the hand is the load","why":"The biceps inserts close to the elbow, between the joint and the hand."},{"pt":"The curl works at a mechanical disadvantage","why":"The biceps must pull much harder than the bag weighs, but the hand moves fast and far."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-naming-levers-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[79],"cids":["w4-muscle-roles-naming"],"t":"Read a name you have never seen","p":"You meet the muscle name extensor carpi radialis longus for the first time. Without looking it up, predict what it does, the region it acts on, which side it sits on, and how it compares in length with a partner muscle. Then do the same for flexor carpi ulnaris.","k":[{"pt":"Extensor: it extends","why":"An action clue names the movement the muscle produces."},{"pt":"Carpi: it acts on the wrist","why":"Carpi refers to the carpus, the wrist."},{"pt":"Radialis: it sits on the radial, thumb side","why":"Location clues name the bone the muscle runs along."},{"pt":"Longus: it is the longer of a pair","why":"A size clue suggests there is a shorter partner, the extensor carpi radialis brevis."},{"pt":"Flexor carpi ulnaris flexes the wrist on the ulnar, little finger side","why":"The same three clue types, action, region, and side, decode the second name."}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-naming-levers-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[80],"cids":["w4-lever-systems"],"t":"Moving a tendon","p":"A surgeon moves a tendon's insertion on the forearm farther away from the elbow joint. Using lever parts, predict how this changes the effort the muscle needs and what the person gives up in return.","k":[{"pt":"Elbow flexion is a third class lever","why":"The effort, the muscle's insertion, sits between the fulcrum at the elbow and the load in the hand."},{"pt":"Moving the insertion away moves the effort farther from the fulcrum","why":"The insertion is where the effort is applied to the lever."},{"pt":"The muscle now needs less force to lift the same load","why":"The farther the effort is from the fulcrum, the closer the lever gets to a mechanical advantage."},{"pt":"The hand now moves less fast and less far for the same shortening","why":"A lever trades force for speed and range, so gaining one costs the other."},{"pt":"A small change in insertion noticeably changes strength and speed","why":"That is why tendon position matters in surgery."}]}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles","title":"Muscles of the back, thorax and shoulder","mw":"2026-10-07","tr":"2026-10-08","prompts":[{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[85,87],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"],"t":"The back, layer by layer","p":"Draw the back and label the superficial muscles. Then write which layer sits underneath and name one muscle in it.","k":[{"pt":"Trapezius, latissimus dorsi","why":"These two cover most of the back: the trapezius from the occipital bone and spine to the scapula and clavicle, and the latissimus from the lower spine and iliac crest to the humerus."},{"pt":"Levator scapulae, rhomboid major and minor beneath the trapezius","why":"They run from the vertebrae to the superior and medial borders of the scapula, so they only show once the trapezius is lifted."},{"pt":"Deltoid at the shoulder","why":"It caps the shoulder from the clavicle, acromion, and spine of the scapula to the deltoid tuberosity of the humerus."},{"pt":"Deeper: erector spinae group, iliocostalis, longissimus, spinalis","why":"These three vertical columns lie under the superficial layer and run the length of the back."},{"pt":"Actions on the scapula noted","why":"Every superficial back muscle crosses from the axial skeleton to the girdle or humerus, so its action moves the scapula or the arm."}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[85,103],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","resp-thoracic-diaphragm"],"t":"Chest and thoracic wall","p":"Draw the anterior chest and label the muscles of the pectoral region and the thoracic wall, with the action of each.","k":[{"pt":"Pectoralis major, adducts and medially rotates the arm","why":"It runs from the clavicle, sternum, and costal cartilages to the humerus, and it also flexes the arm."},{"pt":"Pectoralis minor, depresses the scapula","why":"It lies under pectoralis major, from ribs 3 to 5 to the coracoid process, and it also protracts the scapula."},{"pt":"Serratus anterior, protracts the scapula, wings if paralysed","why":"It runs from the upper ribs to the medial border of the scapula and holds the scapula flat against the chest wall."},{"pt":"External and internal intercostals, and their fiber directions","why":"External fibers run downward and forward and elevate the ribs, and internal fibers run at right angles to them and depress the ribs."},{"pt":"Diaphragm named as the floor","why":"The diaphragm closes off the bottom of the thoracic cavity."}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[87],"cids":["w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"],"t":"The rotator cuff","p":"Name the four rotator cuff muscles. For each, write where it starts on the scapula, which tubercle of the humerus it reaches, and the movement it makes.","k":[{"pt":"Supraspinatus: supraspinous fossa to the greater tubercle, starts abduction","why":"It begins lifting the arm out to the side, the first fifteen degrees."},{"pt":"Infraspinatus: infraspinous fossa to the greater tubercle, laterally rotates","why":"It sits below the spine of the scapula on the back."},{"pt":"Teres minor: lateral border of the scapula to the greater tubercle, laterally rotates","why":"It lies just below the infraspinatus and does the same job."},{"pt":"Subscapularis: subscapular fossa to the lesser tubercle, medially rotates","why":"It is the only cuff muscle on the front of the scapula, the only one on the lesser tubercle, and the only medial rotator."},{"pt":"Together they hold the head of the humerus in the glenoid cavity","why":"The glenoid is shallow, so the cuff is what keeps this wide-ranging joint in place."}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[103],"cids":["resp-thoracic-diaphragm"],"t":"The diaphragm and its openings","p":"Draw the diaphragm from below. Label its parts and its three openings, with the vertebral level of each opening and what passes through it.","k":[{"pt":"Central tendon, right and left crura, costal and sternal parts","why":"The muscle arises from the xiphoid process, ribs 7 to 12, and the lumbar vertebrae by the crura, and inserts on its own central tendon."},{"pt":"Caval opening at T8: inferior vena cava and right phrenic nerve","why":"The highest opening carries the vena cava."},{"pt":"Esophageal hiatus at T10: esophagus and vagus nerves","why":"The vagus nerves travel down with the esophagus."},{"pt":"Aortic hiatus at T12: aorta, thoracic duct, azygos vein","why":"The lowest opening carries the aorta, along with the thoracic duct and azygos vein."},{"pt":"The levels go 8, 10, 12","why":"The count matches the order: vena cava at 8, esophagus at 10, aorta at 12."}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[87],"cids":["w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"],"t":"Trapezius versus rhomboids","p":"Compare the trapezius and the rhomboids: where each attaches, what each does to the scapula, and the one movement where they work against each other.","k":[{"pt":"Trapezius attaches from the occipital bone, ligamentum nuchae, and spinous processes C7 to T12 to the clavicle, acromion, and spine of the scapula","why":"Its broad origin gives it upper, middle, and lower fibers with different actions."},{"pt":"Rhomboids attach from spinous processes C7 to T5 to the medial border of the scapula","why":"Rhomboid minor comes from C7 to T1 and rhomboid major from T2 to T5."},{"pt":"Both retract the scapula","why":"The middle trapezius fibers and both rhomboids pull the scapula toward the spine."},{"pt":"Trapezius upper fibers elevate and lower fibers depress the scapula","why":"Its different fiber groups pull in different directions."},{"pt":"They oppose each other in rotation","why":"The upper and lower trapezius fibers rotate the glenoid upward, while the rhomboids rotate it downward."}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[103],"cids":["resp-thoracic-diaphragm"],"t":"The intercostal layers","p":"Compare the external, internal, and innermost intercostals: where each attaches, which way its fibers run, and what each does to the ribs.","k":[{"pt":"External intercostals: inferior border of the rib above to the superior border of the rib below","why":"They fill the space between neighboring ribs in the outermost layer."},{"pt":"External fibers run downward and forward, like hands in your pockets, and elevate the ribs","why":"The fiber direction is the fastest way to tell the layers apart."},{"pt":"Internal intercostals: superior border of the rib below to the inferior border of the rib above","why":"They run at right angles to the external layer and depress the ribs."},{"pt":"Innermost intercostals: internal surfaces of neighboring ribs","why":"They act with the internal intercostals."},{"pt":"The neurovascular bundle runs between the internal and innermost layers in the costal groove","why":"This is the layer that separates the two deeper muscles."}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[87],"cids":["w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"],"t":"Superficial and deep back","p":"Sort the back muscles into the superficial layer and the deep layer. Put the three erector spinae columns in order from lateral to medial, with what each attaches to.","k":[{"pt":"Superficial: trapezius, latissimus dorsi, levator scapulae, rhomboids","why":"They connect the spine to the scapula or humerus, so they move the shoulder and arm."},{"pt":"Iliocostalis is the most lateral column","why":"It attaches to the ribs, in lumbar, thoracic, and cervical parts."},{"pt":"Longissimus is the intermediate column","why":"It is the longest of the three, reaching up to the mastoid process."},{"pt":"Spinalis is the most medial column","why":"It runs along the spinous processes and is the smallest of the three."},{"pt":"Other deep muscles: splenius capitis and cervicis, semispinalis, multifidus, quadratus lumborum","why":"Like the erector spinae, they extend, rotate, or bend the head and spine rather than moving the arm."}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[85],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"],"t":"Muscles of the anterior thorax","p":"Compare pectoralis major, pectoralis minor, serratus anterior, and subclavius: where each attaches and what each one moves.","k":[{"pt":"Pectoralis major: clavicle, sternum, and costal cartilages 1 to 6 to the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus","why":"It reaches the humerus, so it flexes, adducts, and medially rotates the arm."},{"pt":"Pectoralis minor: ribs 3 to 5 to the coracoid process","why":"It depresses and protracts the scapula, and lifts the ribs when the scapula is held still."},{"pt":"Serratus anterior: ribs 1 to 8 or 9 to the medial border of the scapula","why":"It protracts the scapula, rotates the glenoid upward, and holds the scapula against the chest wall."},{"pt":"Subclavius: rib 1 to the underside of the clavicle","why":"It depresses and steadies the clavicle."},{"pt":"Only pectoralis major moves the arm directly","why":"The other three attach to the girdle, so they move the scapula or clavicle."}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[85],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"],"t":"A shoulder blade that lifts off","p":"After surgery in the armpit, a patient's shoulder blade lifts off her back when she pushes against a wall. Name the muscle that stopped working, where it attaches, what its normal job is, and why surgery in that area could cause this.","k":[{"pt":"The muscle is serratus anterior","why":"Its job is the one that has disappeared."},{"pt":"It runs from ribs 1 to 8 or 9 on the side of the chest to the medial border of the scapula","why":"It wraps around the chest wall to reach the front of the scapula's medial border."},{"pt":"Its normal job is to hold the scapula flat against the chest wall and protract it","why":"When it fails, pushing forward lifts the medial border off the back."},{"pt":"The sign is called a winged scapula","why":"The muscle's job becomes visible in its absence."},{"pt":"The nerve to serratus anterior runs superficially down the side of the chest wall, right where surgery in the armpit works","why":"This is the long thoracic nerve, and its exposed path on the chest wall is why the muscle can be lost after axillary surgery."}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[85,87],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"],"t":"Trouble lifting the arm","p":"A tennis player cannot start lifting her arm out to the side, but once someone raises it partway she can lift it the rest of the way herself. Name the rotator cuff muscle that is torn and where it runs, explain why she can finish the movement, and name the cuff muscle you would test for medial rotation and the two you would test for lateral rotation.","k":[{"pt":"The torn muscle is supraspinatus","why":"It is the cuff muscle that starts abduction, the first fifteen degrees."},{"pt":"It runs from the supraspinous fossa to the greater tubercle","why":"It sits above the spine of the scapula, which is where its name comes from."},{"pt":"The middle fibers of the deltoid take over once the arm is partway up","why":"The deltoid's middle fibers abduct the arm beyond the start of the movement."},{"pt":"Test subscapularis for medial rotation","why":"It is the only cuff muscle that rotates the arm medially, and it reaches the lesser tubercle."},{"pt":"Infraspinatus and teres minor rotate laterally","why":"Both reach the greater tubercle from the back of the scapula, so testing lateral rotation checks the other two greater tubercle muscles."}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[87],"cids":["w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"],"t":"The swimmer's pull","p":"A swimmer pulls her arm down and back through the water on every stroke. Name the broad back muscle that powers this pull, trace it from origin to insertion, and explain from where it attaches why it moves the arm this way. Then name the smaller shoulder muscle from the inferior angle of the scapula that does the same three actions.","k":[{"pt":"It is the latissimus dorsi","why":"It brings the arm down and back, which is why it is called the swimmer's muscle."},{"pt":"Origin: spinous processes T7 to L5, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest, lower ribs","why":"This wide origin low on the trunk makes it the widest muscle of the back."},{"pt":"Insertion: intertubercular sulcus of the humerus","why":"It reaches the humerus, not the scapula, so its actions are arm actions."},{"pt":"The origin lies below and behind the arm, so pulling the insertion toward it draws the arm down and back","why":"A contracting muscle pulls its insertion toward its origin, so where the origin sits tells you the direction of the pull."},{"pt":"Actions: extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm","why":"The same down and back pull is what pushes the body up out of a chair."},{"pt":"Teres major, from the inferior angle of the scapula to the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus","why":"It shares all three actions with the latissimus dorsi: extension, adduction, and medial rotation of the arm."}]}]},{"id":"m3-arm-muscles","title":"Muscles of the arm (brachium)","mw":"2026-10-07","tr":"2026-10-08","prompts":[{"id":"m3-arm-muscles-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[85],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"],"t":"The arm, front and back","p":"Draw the arm from the front and from the back. Label every muscle in the anterior compartment and every muscle in the posterior compartment, and draw the wall that separates the two.","k":[{"pt":"Anterior compartment: biceps brachii with its long head and short head","why":"Biceps means two heads, and each head starts at a different spot on the scapula, so drawing both heads is part of getting the muscle right."},{"pt":"Brachialis lying deep to the biceps on the lower front of the humerus","why":"It sits under the biceps, so on a model you have to lift the biceps to see it, which is why students often miss it."},{"pt":"Coracobrachialis high on the inner arm, running from the coracoid process down the humerus","why":"It shares the coracoid process with the short head of the biceps, so the two are easiest to find together."},{"pt":"Posterior compartment: triceps brachii with its long, lateral, and medial heads","why":"Triceps means three heads, and all three join into one tendon on the olecranon, so the back of the arm is really one big muscle."},{"pt":"Anconeus as a small muscle at the back of the elbow","why":"It runs from the lateral epicondyle to the olecranon and helps the triceps, so it belongs with the posterior group."},{"pt":"Intermuscular septa drawn between the front and back groups","why":"The septa are the walls that create the compartments, and the compartment tells you the action: front flexes the elbow, back extends it."}]},{"id":"m3-arm-muscles-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[85],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"],"t":"Where the arm muscles start and end","p":"List the muscles of the arm and write where each one starts and where each one ends.","k":[{"pt":"Biceps brachii: long head from the supraglenoid tubercle, short head from the coracoid process, ending on the radial tuberosity and the bicipital aponeurosis","why":"Both heads start on the scapula and the tendon lands on the radius, so the biceps crosses both the shoulder and the elbow."},{"pt":"Brachialis: lower front of the humerus to the coronoid process and tuberosity of the ulna","why":"It starts and ends without touching the scapula, so it acts only at the elbow."},{"pt":"Coracobrachialis: coracoid process to the inner surface of the humeral shaft","why":"It ends on the humerus itself and never reaches the forearm, so it moves the arm at the shoulder but not the elbow."},{"pt":"Triceps brachii: long head from the infraglenoid tubercle, lateral and medial heads from the humerus, all ending on the olecranon","why":"Only the long head starts on the scapula, which is why only that head also acts at the shoulder."},{"pt":"Anconeus: lateral epicondyle to the side of the olecranon and the back of the ulna","why":"It is short and crosses only the elbow, so its one job is to help straighten it."},{"pt":"Brachioradialis: lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus to the styloid process of the radius","why":"It starts on the humerus and ends at the wrist end of the radius, so it crosses the elbow and bends it even though it sits with the posterior group."}]},{"id":"m3-arm-muscles-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[85],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"],"t":"Bend or straighten","p":"Sort the arm muscles into the ones that bend the elbow and the ones that straighten it. For each muscle, write which compartment it sits in and anything else it does.","k":[{"pt":"Biceps brachii, anterior: bends the elbow, turns the palm up (supinates), and also flexes the arm","why":"Its tendon lands on the radius, the forearm bone that rotates, and its heads start on the scapula, so it can act at the elbow, the forearm, and the shoulder."},{"pt":"Brachialis, anterior: bends the elbow and is the prime mover in every forearm position","why":"It pulls on the ulna, which does not rotate, so turning the palm up or down never changes how well it works."},{"pt":"Brachioradialis, posterior: bends the elbow, strongest with the thumb pointing up (forearm in mid position)","why":"It is the exception to the compartment rule: it bends the elbow but belongs to the posterior compartment."},{"pt":"Triceps brachii, posterior: straightens the elbow, and its long head also extends the arm","why":"The long head is the only part that crosses the shoulder, so it is the only part that moves the arm backward."},{"pt":"Anconeus, posterior: helps straighten the elbow","why":"It is a small helper for the triceps at the same joint."},{"pt":"Coracobrachialis, anterior: flexes and adducts the arm but does nothing at the elbow","why":"It ends on the humerus and never crosses the elbow, so it cannot go in either elbow group."}]},{"id":"m3-arm-muscles-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[85,79],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","w4-muscle-roles-naming"],"t":"Biceps and triceps as a pair","p":"Compare the biceps brachii and the triceps brachii: their heads, where each starts and ends, and what each does. Then name the role each one plays when you bend your elbow and when you straighten it.","k":[{"pt":"Biceps: two heads, the long head from the supraglenoid tubercle and the short head from the coracoid process, ending on the radial tuberosity","why":"Both heads start on the scapula, so the biceps crosses the shoulder as well as the elbow and also flexes the arm."},{"pt":"Triceps: three heads, the long head from the infraglenoid tubercle and the lateral and medial heads from the humerus, all ending on the olecranon of the ulna","why":"Only the long head starts on the scapula, so only that head also extends the arm."},{"pt":"Biceps bends the elbow and turns the palm up; triceps straightens the elbow","why":"They sit on opposite sides of the humerus and pull on opposite sides of the elbow, which is what makes them a pair."},{"pt":"Bending the elbow: the biceps works as a mover, with the brachialis beside it, and the triceps is the antagonist","why":"The antagonist opposes the movers, relaxing and lengthening while they contract, so the triceps has to give way for the elbow to bend."},{"pt":"Straightening the elbow: the triceps is the agonist, the anconeus helps, and the biceps and brachialis become the antagonists","why":"The roles swap when the movement reverses, so the same muscle can be a mover in one movement and the antagonist in the other."}]},{"id":"m3-arm-muscles-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[85,79],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","w4-muscle-roles-naming"],"t":"A torn biceps tendon","p":"A weightlifter tears the biceps tendon where it attaches to the radial tuberosity. Predict which movements get weaker, which still work, and which muscles are left to do them.","k":[{"pt":"Turning the palm up (supination) is weakened the most","why":"The biceps is the supinator in the arm, and it works by pulling on the radius, so cutting it off the radius takes that pull away."},{"pt":"The supinator muscle in the posterior forearm can still turn the palm up","why":"It runs from the lateral epicondyle and ulna to the radius, so it never depended on the biceps tendon."},{"pt":"Bending the elbow still works because the brachialis is the prime mover","why":"The brachialis ends on the ulna, not the radius, so a tear at the radial tuberosity leaves it untouched."},{"pt":"Brachioradialis also still bends the elbow, best with the thumb pointing up","why":"It ends on the styloid process of the radius, far from the torn tendon."},{"pt":"Straightening the elbow is not affected","why":"The triceps and anconeus are in the posterior compartment and pull on the olecranon, on the other side of the joint."}]},{"id":"m3-arm-muscles-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[85,86],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"Predict from the compartment","p":"You find an unlabeled muscle in the anterior compartment of the arm that crosses the elbow. Predict its action. Then name the arm muscles that do not follow the compartment rule and explain why each one breaks it.","k":[{"pt":"A muscle in the anterior compartment that crosses the elbow will bend the elbow","why":"Muscles in the same compartment share an action, so the location lets you predict the job before you know the name."},{"pt":"A muscle in the posterior compartment that crosses the elbow will straighten it","why":"The posterior compartment is the extensor side, which is why the triceps and anconeus live there."},{"pt":"Brachioradialis breaks the rule: it bends the elbow but belongs to the posterior compartment","why":"Its fibers run in front of the elbow joint, so it pulls the forearm up even though its compartment is the extensor one."},{"pt":"Coracobrachialis is anterior but does not bend the elbow","why":"It stops on the humerus, so it only acts at the shoulder, where it flexes and adducts the arm."},{"pt":"Intermuscular septa divide the arm into the two compartments","why":"Without these walls there would be no compartments to predict from."}]}]},{"id":"m3-blood-cells","title":"Formed elements: blood cells and platelets","mw":"2026-10-12","tr":"2026-10-20","prompts":[{"id":"m3-blood-cells-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[82,81],"cids":["w4-formed-elements","w4-blood-composition"],"t":"Formed elements","p":"List the formed elements of blood. For each write its job and roughly how common it is.","k":[{"pt":"Erythrocytes: carry oxygen, biconcave discs with no nucleus, by far the most numerous, about 4 to 6 million per microliter","why":"They are the bulk of the formed elements, which is why the red layer makes up almost half of a spun tube."},{"pt":"Leukocytes: five types that defend the body, the least numerous, about 4,500 to 11,000 per microliter","why":"They are the only formed elements that are complete cells with a nucleus."},{"pt":"Platelets: small fragments of megakaryocytes that work in clotting, about 150,000 to 400,000 per microliter","why":"They are pieces of cytoplasm, not whole cells, which is why they have no nucleus."},{"pt":"Ranking from most to least: erythrocytes, then platelets, then leukocytes","why":"Platelets fall in the middle, more common than white cells but far fewer than red cells."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-cells-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[82],"cids":["w4-formed-elements"],"t":"A blood smear","p":"Draw a blood smear as it looks under the scope. Include every formed element in roughly the right proportion and label each one.","k":[{"pt":"Field filled mostly with erythrocytes, each with a pale center","why":"The pale center is the thin middle of the biconcave disc, and red cells far outnumber everything else."},{"pt":"Only one or two white cells in the field","why":"Leukocytes are the least numerous formed element, so a field with many of them would be the wrong proportion."},{"pt":"Platelets as small purple fragments, often in little clumps between the red cells","why":"They are pieces of megakaryocyte cytoplasm, smaller than a red cell and without a nucleus."},{"pt":"Each white cell drawn by its nucleus shape: lobed, two lobed, hidden by granules, round, or kidney shaped","why":"On the smear you identify every white cell by the nucleus first and the granules second."},{"pt":"Proportions right, not one of each cell drawn the same size","why":"The point of the smear is what you really see, and in real blood most fields hold no basophil at all."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-cells-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[82],"cids":["w4-formed-elements"],"t":"White cells under the scope","p":"Split the white cells into granulocytes and agranulocytes. Name each one and write the feature you would use to identify it on a slide.","k":[{"pt":"Granulocytes: neutrophil, eosinophil, basophil. Agranulocytes: lymphocyte, monocyte","why":"The split is based on whether you can see granules in the cytoplasm, so it is the first decision you make on a slide."},{"pt":"Neutrophil: nucleus with three to five lobes joined by thin strands, fine pale granules","why":"The many lobes are the giveaway, and it is the white cell you will see most often."},{"pt":"Eosinophil: two lobed nucleus with coarse red orange granules","why":"The bright granules and the two lobes together separate it from a neutrophil."},{"pt":"Basophil: lobed nucleus hidden under large dark blue purple granules","why":"If you cannot see the nucleus clearly because of the granules, think basophil."},{"pt":"Lymphocyte: large round dark nucleus filling most of the cell with a thin rim of cytoplasm; monocyte: the largest white cell with a kidney or horseshoe shaped nucleus","why":"Both lack visible granules, so size and nucleus shape are what tell them apart."},{"pt":"Order from most to least numerous: neutrophil, lymphocyte, monocyte, eosinophil, basophil (Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas)","why":"The order answers the differential count question and tells you which cells you should expect to find quickly."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-cells-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[82],"cids":["w4-formed-elements"],"t":"Why a red cell is that shape","p":"Draw an erythrocyte from the side and from above. Label its shape, write what it lacks, and give two reasons the shape helps it work.","k":[{"pt":"Biconcave disc, thinner in the center, about 7.5 micrometers across","why":"From the side it looks like a dumbbell and from above it shows the pale center, so both views show the same shape."},{"pt":"No nucleus and no organelles","why":"A mature red cell has thrown out its nucleus and most organelles, which leaves more room for hemoglobin."},{"pt":"More surface area for gas exchange, about thirty percent more than a sphere of the same volume","why":"Oxygen crosses at the surface, so more surface means more exchange."},{"pt":"Flexible membrane that lets it fold through capillaries narrower than itself","why":"Red cells pass single file through the smallest capillaries, so a stiff cell would get stuck."},{"pt":"Packed with hemoglobin: four subunits, each with a heme group holding one iron","why":"Hemoglobin is what binds oxygen, so every feature above is about fitting in more of it."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-cells-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[82,83],"cids":["w4-formed-elements","w4-hematopoiesis"],"t":"Name the mystery cell","p":"On a smear you find a cell about twice the size of a red cell. Its nucleus has four lobes joined by thin strands and its cytoplasm has fine pale granules. Name the cell, sort it into its group and cell line, and explain how you would tell it apart from an eosinophil.","k":[{"pt":"It is a neutrophil","why":"Three to five lobes joined by thin strands is the defining nucleus of the neutrophil."},{"pt":"It is a granulocyte","why":"Its cytoplasm has visible granules, even though they are fine and pale."},{"pt":"It comes from the myeloid line","why":"The myeloid line makes every formed element except the lymphocytes."},{"pt":"It is the most numerous white cell, 50 to 70 percent of white cells","why":"That is why it is the white cell you find first on almost any smear."},{"pt":"An eosinophil has only two lobes and coarse red orange granules","why":"Count the lobes and look at the granule color, and the two cells separate easily."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-cells-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[82],"cids":["w4-formed-elements"],"t":"A smear from a parasite infection","p":"A patient has a parasitic worm infection, and one type of white cell is higher than normal on the smear. Name the cell, describe how you would find it on the slide, and say where it normally ranks in the white cell count.","k":[{"pt":"The eosinophil","why":"Eosinophils attack parasites, so their count rises in a worm infection."},{"pt":"Two lobed nucleus","why":"Two lobes rather than three to five separates it from a neutrophil."},{"pt":"Coarse red orange granules filling the cytoplasm","why":"The granule color is the fastest clue on a stained slide."},{"pt":"Normally only 2 to 4 percent of white cells, fourth in the order neutrophil, lymphocyte, monocyte, eosinophil, basophil","why":"Because it is normally rare, seeing several in one field stands out."},{"pt":"A high count is called eosinophilia","why":"The ending philia means too many of that cell, the same pattern as neutrophilia."}]}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-routes","title":"Circulatory routes, fetal circulation and vessel disorders","mw":"2026-10-14","tr":"2026-10-15","prompts":[{"id":"m3-vessel-routes-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[69,62],"cids":["bvn-circulatory-routes","bvn-great-vessels"],"t":"The two circuits","p":"Draw the pulmonary and systemic circuits as one loop. Name the great vessels, mark which side is the high pressure side, and mark where the blood is oxygenated.","k":[{"pt":"Right heart to lungs to left heart to body and back to the right heart","why":"The pulmonary circuit runs from the right side to the lungs and back to the left side, and the systemic circuit runs from the left side to the body and back to the right side."},{"pt":"Great vessels: superior and inferior venae cavae, pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries, pulmonary veins, aorta","why":"Each great vessel belongs to one circuit, so placing them is how you prove the loop is right."},{"pt":"The left side, the systemic side, is the high pressure side","why":"It pumps blood to the whole body, which is why the left ventricle has the thickest wall."},{"pt":"Pulmonary arteries carry oxygen poor blood and pulmonary veins carry oxygen rich blood","why":"Arteries and veins are named by direction, away from or toward the heart, not by how much oxygen they carry."},{"pt":"Oxygen picked up in the capillaries of the lungs","why":"The blood changes from oxygen poor to oxygen rich only where it meets the air in the lungs."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-routes-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[71],"cids":["bvn-fetal-remnants"],"t":"Fetal structures and what they become","p":"List the five special vessels and openings of fetal circulation. Next to each, write the adult structure it becomes after birth.","k":[{"pt":"Umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver","why":"It ran from the placenta toward the liver, so its remnant sits in the free edge of the falciform ligament."},{"pt":"Ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum","why":"It was the shunt through the liver, so its remnant is a fibrous cord on the underside of the liver."},{"pt":"Foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis","why":"It was a hole in the interatrial septum, so it leaves a shallow oval dent rather than a cord."},{"pt":"Ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum","why":"It linked the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, and the remnant is a short cord in that same spot."},{"pt":"Umbilical arteries become the medial umbilical ligaments","why":"They are the paired cords you can find on the inside of the front abdominal wall."},{"pt":"Every remnant is a cord except the fossa ovalis","why":"The other four were vessels that sealed into ligaments, while the foramen ovale was an opening that closed into a depression."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-routes-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[71],"cids":["bvn-fetal-remnants"],"t":"Placenta to placenta","p":"Trace fetal blood from the placenta through the body and back to the placenta. Name every vessel and shunt in order and write what organ each shunt lets the blood skip.","k":[{"pt":"Placenta to the umbilical vein, carrying oxygen rich blood","why":"In a fetus the placenta does the job of the lungs, so the vessel leaving it carries the best oxygenated blood."},{"pt":"Ductus venosus lets most of that blood skip the liver and flow toward the inferior vena cava","why":"The placenta handles the filtering that the liver will do later, so the liver is largely bypassed."},{"pt":"Inferior vena cava to the right atrium","why":"This is the same entry point as in the adult, which is where the lung shunts begin."},{"pt":"Foramen ovale lets blood pass from the right atrium straight into the left atrium, skipping the lungs","why":"The fetal lungs are collapsed and not used for gas exchange, so there is no reason to send blood through them."},{"pt":"Most blood that reaches the right ventricle goes to the pulmonary trunk, then through the ductus arteriosus into the aorta, skipping the lungs again","why":"This second lung shunt catches the blood that did not cross the foramen ovale."},{"pt":"Two umbilical arteries carry oxygen poor blood back to the placenta","why":"They are named arteries because they carry blood away from the fetal heart, even though that blood is low in oxygen."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-routes-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[69],"cids":["bvn-circulatory-routes"],"t":"Usual route, portal system, anastomosis","p":"Draw three small maps side by side: the usual route blood takes, a portal system, and an anastomosis. Label each map with a named example from the body.","k":[{"pt":"Usual route: artery, arteriole, one capillary bed, venule, vein","why":"Most blood passes through only one capillary bed before heading back to the heart."},{"pt":"Portal system: two capillary beds in a row before the blood returns to the heart","why":"The second bed is what makes it a portal system, so both beds must be drawn."},{"pt":"Hepatic portal example: capillaries of the digestive organs, the hepatic portal vein, then the capillaries of the liver","why":"Blood from the gut goes through the liver before it reaches the rest of the body."},{"pt":"Hypophyseal portal example: links the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary","why":"It is the second named portal system, and it also connects two capillary beds."},{"pt":"Anastomosis: two vessels joining, which gives blood a second route if one path is blocked","why":"The palmar arches are an example, where the radial and ulnar arteries join in the hand."},{"pt":"Arteriovenous anastomosis: an artery connected straight to a vein, skipping the capillaries","why":"This is the one route with no capillary bed at all."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-routes-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[70],"cids":["bvn-vessel-disorders"],"t":"Artery problems and vein problems","p":"Make a table of atherosclerosis, aneurysm, varicose veins, and deep vein thrombosis. For each, write whether it hits an artery or a vein, what part of the vessel is affected, and where in the body it is common.","k":[{"pt":"Atherosclerosis: artery. Fatty plaque (atheroma) builds up within the wall, starting with injury to the tunica intima, and narrows the lumen","why":"The plaque bulges inward, so the channel gets smaller and less blood gets through. It is the root of coronary artery disease and stroke."},{"pt":"Aneurysm: artery. A balloon like bulge in a weakened wall, common in the aorta and the brain arteries","why":"Where a plaque narrows the vessel, an aneurysm weakens the wall and widens it, and it can burst."},{"pt":"Varicose veins: superficial veins, twisted and swollen because their valves fail, most often in the legs","why":"Valves are flaps of the tunica intima that stop backflow, so when they fail, blood pools and the vein bulges."},{"pt":"Deep vein thrombosis: a clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg","why":"The clot can break free and travel with the normal flow of venous blood."},{"pt":"Pattern: artery disorders narrow or weaken the wall; vein disorders involve failed valves and pooled or clotted blood","why":"Arteries carry blood under high pressure and veins under low pressure, and that difference predicts the type of problem."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-routes-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[70,69],"cids":["bvn-vessel-disorders","bvn-circulatory-routes"],"t":"Where a leg clot ends up","p":"A deep vein thrombosis forms in the calf and breaks free. Trace the exact route it takes and name where it lodges.","k":[{"pt":"The loose clot is now an embolus","why":"An embolus is a clot or fragment that travels in the bloodstream and lodges somewhere else."},{"pt":"Up the deep veins of the leg into the inferior vena cava","why":"It follows the normal route of venous return, so it travels toward the heart."},{"pt":"Into the right atrium, through the tricuspid valve, into the right ventricle","why":"Venous blood always enters the right side of the heart, and the clot rides along with it."},{"pt":"Through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk and a pulmonary artery","why":"The right ventricle pumps everything it receives toward the lungs."},{"pt":"Lodges in an artery of the lung, a pulmonary embolism","why":"The pulmonary arteries keep branching into smaller vessels, so the clot gets stuck in the first one too narrow to pass, blocking blood flow to part of the lung."},{"pt":"It does not reach the body arteries","why":"To get to the left heart and the aorta it would have to pass through the lung capillaries first, so the lung is where it stops."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-routes-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[71],"cids":["bvn-fetal-remnants"],"t":"The ductus that stays open","p":"A newborn's ductus arteriosus fails to close. Name the two vessels it still connects, say what that does to the separation of the two circuits, and name the structure that should have formed in its place. Then name the matching problem between the atria and what should have formed there.","k":[{"pt":"It still connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta","why":"Before birth it let blood skip the lungs by shunting it from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta."},{"pt":"At birth the lungs inflate, the shunt is no longer needed, and it should close","why":"Once the lungs take over gas exchange, blood no longer needs a detour around them."},{"pt":"It should have become the ligamentum arteriosum, a short cord between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta","why":"A fetal vessel that seals leaves a ligament in the same spot, and the name keeps a piece of the original."},{"pt":"Left open it is a patent ductus arteriosus, an abnormal connection between the pulmonary and systemic circuits","why":"After birth the two circuits should be separate, so any open link between them is abnormal."},{"pt":"The atrial version is a patent foramen ovale, an opening left in the interatrial septum","why":"Both problems are named for the fetal structure that failed to seal."},{"pt":"The foramen ovale should have become the fossa ovalis, a shallow depression","why":"It was a hole and not a vessel, so it closes into a dent rather than a cord."}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-routes-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[70],"cids":["bvn-vessel-disorders"],"t":"One plaque, three diseases","p":"The same fatty plaque forms in three places: a coronary artery, a brain artery, and an artery of the leg. Name the disease each one causes and explain why one change in the wall gets three different names.","k":[{"pt":"Coronary artery: coronary artery disease, which can lead to a heart attack","why":"The coronary arteries supply the heart wall, so narrowing them starves the myocardium."},{"pt":"Brain artery: stroke","why":"A blocked or burst brain artery cuts off blood to part of the brain, and that tissue begins to die within minutes."},{"pt":"Leg artery: peripheral artery disease, with pain on walking","why":"The leg muscles are starved of blood when they work hardest."},{"pt":"The plaque starts with injury to the endothelium of the tunica intima and grows inward, narrowing the lumen","why":"The wall change is the same in every vessel, so the disease name depends only on where it happens."},{"pt":"A clot (thrombus) can form on a rough plaque and block the vessel suddenly","why":"This is how a slowly growing plaque can cause a sudden event."},{"pt":"A piece can break free as an embolus and lodge further downstream","why":"The blockage then shows up in a smaller vessel beyond the plaque."}]}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-anterior","title":"Anterior forearm muscles","mw":"2026-10-14","tr":"2026-10-08","prompts":[{"id":"m3-forearm-anterior-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[85,86],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"Anterior compartment","p":"Draw the anterior arm and anterior forearm. Label the muscles in each and write the main action of the group.","k":[{"pt":"Arm: biceps brachii, brachialis, coracobrachialis","why":"These three make up the anterior compartment of the arm, the group in front of the humerus."},{"pt":"Biceps and brachialis bend the elbow, and the biceps also supinates","why":"Supination comes from the biceps pulling on the radius, the forearm bone that rotates."},{"pt":"Forearm superficial layer: pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris, with flexor digitorum superficialis just beneath them","why":"Almost all of these start at the medial epicondyle, the common flexor origin, so they fan out from one point."},{"pt":"Forearm deep layer: flexor digitorum profundus, flexor pollicis longus, pronator quadratus","why":"These start on the ulna, radius, and interosseous membrane instead of the epicondyle, which is why they lie deeper."},{"pt":"Forearm group flexes the wrist and fingers and pronates the forearm","why":"Knowing the compartment action lets you predict what an unfamiliar muscle in this group does."}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-anterior-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"Front of the forearm, layer by layer","p":"List the muscles of the anterior forearm from superficial to deep. For each one, write where it ends.","k":[{"pt":"Pronator teres ends on the lateral midshaft of the radius","why":"Pulling the radius across from the lateral side is what turns the palm down."},{"pt":"Flexor carpi radialis ends on the bases of metacarpals 2 and 3; flexor carpi ulnaris ends on the pisiform, hamate, and metacarpal 5","why":"Their names tell you the side: radialis lands on the thumb side and ulnaris on the little finger side."},{"pt":"Palmaris longus ends in the palmar aponeurosis","why":"It is the one superficial flexor that ends in a sheet of fascia, and it is absent in many people."},{"pt":"Flexor digitorum superficialis ends on the middle phalanges of digits 2 to 5","why":"It stops one bone short of the fingertip, so it bends the middle finger joints."},{"pt":"Flexor digitorum profundus ends on the distal phalanges of digits 2 to 5; flexor pollicis longus ends on the distal phalanx of the thumb","why":"These deep flexors reach all the way to the fingertip and thumb tip."},{"pt":"Pronator quadratus, the deepest, runs across the lower front of the ulna to the radius","why":"It is a square muscle just above the wrist and the prime mover of pronation."}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-anterior-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"The two wrist flexors","p":"Compare flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris: where each starts, where each ends, and what each does to the wrist besides bending it.","k":[{"pt":"Both start at the medial epicondyle; flexor carpi ulnaris also starts on the olecranon","why":"Sharing the common flexor origin is what puts them in the same superficial layer."},{"pt":"Flexor carpi radialis ends on the bases of metacarpals 2 and 3, on the thumb side","why":"Its pull on the thumb side tips the hand toward the thumb as it bends the wrist."},{"pt":"Flexor carpi ulnaris ends on the pisiform, hamate, and metacarpal 5, on the little finger side","why":"Its pull on the little finger side tips the hand toward the little finger."},{"pt":"Flexor carpi radialis flexes and abducts the wrist; flexor carpi ulnaris flexes and adducts it","why":"They share flexion but pull in opposite side to side directions."},{"pt":"Working together they bend the wrist straight forward","why":"Their side pulls cancel out, leaving pure flexion."}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-anterior-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"Superficialis and profundus","p":"Compare flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundus: which is on top, where each starts and ends, and which finger joint each one bends.","k":[{"pt":"Superficialis lies on top of profundus","why":"The names mean superficial and deep, so the layer is in the name."},{"pt":"Superficialis starts on the medial epicondyle, the coronoid process, and the radius","why":"It shares the common flexor origin with the superficial group."},{"pt":"Profundus starts on the front and inner ulna and the interosseous membrane","why":"Starting on the forearm bones rather than the epicondyle puts it in the deep layer."},{"pt":"Superficialis ends on the middle phalanges and bends the proximal interphalangeal joints","why":"It stops at the middle bone, so it can only bend the joint before it."},{"pt":"Profundus ends on the distal phalanges and bends the distal interphalangeal joints","why":"It is the only muscle that reaches the fingertip to bend the last joint."},{"pt":"Both send four tendons through the carpal tunnel","why":"That gives eight of the nine tendons that share the tunnel with the median nerve."}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-anterior-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"The two pronators","p":"Compare pronator teres and pronator quadratus: where each sits, where each starts and ends, and which one does most of the work of turning the palm down.","k":[{"pt":"Pronator teres sits high in the forearm, from the medial epicondyle and the coronoid process of the ulna to the lateral midshaft of the radius","why":"It runs diagonally across the forearm, which lets it pull the radius over the ulna."},{"pt":"Pronator quadratus sits low, just above the wrist, from the lower front of the ulna to the lower front of the radius","why":"It is square, as its name says, and it is the deepest muscle of the anterior forearm."},{"pt":"Pronator quadratus is the prime mover of pronation","why":"It does the main work of turning the palm down, and pronator teres helps."},{"pt":"Pronator teres also helps bend the elbow","why":"It starts on the humerus, so it crosses the elbow joint as well as the forearm."},{"pt":"Both pull on the radius","why":"Pronation is the radius rotating across the ulna, so every pronator has to attach to the radius."}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-anterior-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"Golfer's elbow","p":"A golfer has pain right over the bony bump on the inner side of the elbow. Name the structure that is irritated, list the muscles that start there, and predict which movements against resistance will make the pain worse.","k":[{"pt":"The medial epicondyle, the common flexor origin","why":"Golfer's elbow is inflammation at the shared starting point of the wrist flexors."},{"pt":"Muscles that start there: pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris, flexor digitorum superficialis","why":"Almost every superficial flexor shares this one origin, so one sore spot involves the whole group."},{"pt":"Bending the wrist against resistance will hurt","why":"The wrist flexors pull on the medial epicondyle every time they contract."},{"pt":"Turning the palm down against resistance can also hurt","why":"Pronator teres starts at the same spot."},{"pt":"Tennis elbow is the same problem on the outer side, at the lateral epicondyle","why":"That is the common origin of the wrist extensors, so the two conditions are mirror images."}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-anterior-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"Testing the finger flexors","p":"A patient cut the palm side of one finger. You hold the middle joint straight and ask them to bend only the fingertip, and they cannot. Name the tendon that is cut and explain how you would test the other long flexor of that finger.","k":[{"pt":"The cut tendon is flexor digitorum profundus","why":"It is the only muscle that reaches the distal phalanx, so it is the only one that bends the fingertip joint."},{"pt":"Holding the middle joint straight takes superficialis out of the test","why":"Superficialis bends the middle joint, so blocking that joint leaves only profundus to bend the tip."},{"pt":"To test superficialis, hold the other fingers straight and ask the patient to bend the finger at the middle joint","why":"Holding the neighbors straight stops profundus from helping, since its tendons work together."},{"pt":"Superficialis ends on the middle phalanx and bends the proximal interphalangeal joint","why":"That is why the test for it uses the middle joint."},{"pt":"Both tendons run through the carpal tunnel and along the front of the finger","why":"A deep cut on the palm side can reach both, so each one needs its own test."}]}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-vessels","title":"Arteries and veins of the thorax and upper limb","mw":"2026-10-14","tr":"2026-10-08","prompts":[{"id":"m3-upper-limb-vessels-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[88,62],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins","bvn-great-vessels"],"t":"Arteries down the arm","p":"Trace the arterial supply from the aorta to the fingertips, naming every named artery in order and where it changes name.","k":[{"pt":"Subclavian artery, from the brachiocephalic trunk on the right and straight from the arch on the left","why":"The aortic arch is not symmetrical, so the two subclavians start in different places."},{"pt":"Subclavian becomes axillary at the lateral border of the first rib","why":"It is one continuous vessel, and the name changes at a bony landmark rather than at a branch."},{"pt":"Axillary becomes brachial at the lower border of teres major","why":"The landmark is a muscle edge, not a branch point, so you find teres major to find where the name changes."},{"pt":"Brachial divides into the radial and ulnar arteries just below the elbow, in the cubital fossa","why":"This is the one place where the single vessel becomes two."},{"pt":"Radial and ulnar join in the hand as the superficial palmar arch (mostly ulnar, more distal) and the deep palmar arch (mostly radial, more proximal)","why":"The arches join the two forearm arteries, so the hand has two supply routes."},{"pt":"Digital arteries to the fingers","why":"They branch off the arches and are the last named arteries on the route."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-vessels-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[88],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"],"t":"Veins of the upper limb","p":"Draw the arm and label the superficial and the deep veins. Mark where you would draw blood and say why that spot is used.","k":[{"pt":"Superficial: cephalic on the thumb side, basilic on the little finger side, median cubital between them","why":"The superficial veins are the large ones in the upper limb, the reverse of the lower limb."},{"pt":"Median antebrachial vein up the middle of the front of the forearm, and the dorsal venous network on the back of the hand","why":"These are the smaller superficial veins that feed the main ones."},{"pt":"Deep: paired radial, ulnar, and brachial veins running beside the arteries of the same names, then the axillary and subclavian veins","why":"Deep veins travel with the arteries, so if you know the arterial route you know the deep venous route."},{"pt":"Median cubital vein in the cubital fossa is the draw site","why":"It crosses the front of the elbow between the cephalic and basilic veins, close to the surface."},{"pt":"Why a vein: closer to the surface, thinner wall, wider lumen, and low pressure","why":"That makes it easy to enter and slow to bleed, compared with an artery."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-vessels-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[88],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"],"t":"Named branches worth knowing","p":"List the named branches of the subclavian, axillary, brachial, and ulnar arteries. For each, write what it supplies or where it runs.","k":[{"pt":"Internal thoracic artery from the subclavian, supplying the front chest wall and breast","why":"It is the vessel surgeons use as a graft in coronary bypass."},{"pt":"Thoracoacromial artery from the axillary, supplying the pectoral region and the deltoid","why":"Its name gives its targets: the chest (thoraco) and the acromion region of the shoulder."},{"pt":"Lateral thoracic artery from the axillary, running along the side of the chest wall with the long thoracic nerve","why":"Its name tells you its course: lateral and on the thorax."},{"pt":"Subscapular artery, the largest branch of the axillary","why":"It heads toward the scapula, as its name says."},{"pt":"Deep brachial artery from the brachial, running with the radial nerve in the radial groove of the humerus","why":"It travels with the radial nerve, so the two are at risk together in a midshaft fracture."},{"pt":"Common interosseous artery from the ulnar, dividing into the anterior and posterior interosseous arteries","why":"Its branches run on either side of the interosseous membrane between the radius and ulna."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-vessels-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[62,88],"cids":["bvn-great-vessels","w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"],"t":"One artery, two veins","p":"Draw the aortic arch with its branches and the veins that form the superior vena cava. Explain why there is one brachiocephalic artery but two brachiocephalic veins.","k":[{"pt":"Three branches off the arch, right to left: brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery, left subclavian artery","why":"The brachiocephalic trunk comes off first and is the largest, and it is the only branch that serves the right side."},{"pt":"The brachiocephalic trunk divides into the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries","why":"The right side gets one shared stem that later splits in two."},{"pt":"There is no left brachiocephalic trunk","why":"On the left, the common carotid and subclavian come straight off the arch, so no shared stem is needed."},{"pt":"On each side, the subclavian vein joins the internal jugular vein to form a brachiocephalic vein","why":"Both sides drain the head and arm the same way, so both sides have one."},{"pt":"The two brachiocephalic veins join to form the superior vena cava","why":"The veins are symmetrical, while the arteries are not, and that is why the counts differ."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-vessels-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[88],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"],"t":"Superficial and deep veins","p":"Compare the cephalic and basilic veins by side and by where each one ends. Then explain how the deep veins of the arm are arranged and where they join the superficial ones.","k":[{"pt":"Cephalic vein runs up the lateral, thumb side of the forearm and arm","why":"Lateral in anatomical position is the thumb side, which is how you tell the two apart."},{"pt":"Cephalic dives in at the deltopectoral groove to join the axillary vein","why":"The groove between the deltoid and pectoralis major is its landmark."},{"pt":"Basilic vein runs up the medial, little finger side and joins the brachial veins to form the axillary vein","why":"Joining the deep brachial veins is where the superficial and deep systems meet."},{"pt":"Median cubital vein links cephalic and basilic across the cubital fossa","why":"It is the bridge between the two, and the usual blood draw site."},{"pt":"Deep veins come in pairs beside their arteries: radial, ulnar, and brachial veins","why":"Each deep vein has the same name as the artery it travels with."},{"pt":"Axillary vein becomes the subclavian vein at the first rib","why":"The same rib is the landmark on the artery side, where the subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-vessels-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[88,69],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins","bvn-circulatory-routes"],"t":"Why the hand survives a radial line","p":"A nurse places an arterial line in the radial artery at the wrist. Explain, from the anatomy, why the hand still gets enough blood.","k":[{"pt":"The brachial artery splits into the radial and ulnar arteries in the cubital fossa","why":"The hand is fed by two separate forearm arteries, not one."},{"pt":"The radial artery is where the wrist pulse is taken","why":"It sits close to the surface at the wrist, which is why it is used for the line."},{"pt":"Radial and ulnar join in the hand as the superficial and deep palmar arches","why":"The arches link the two arteries, so blood can come in from either side."},{"pt":"The arches are an anastomosis","why":"An anastomosis gives blood an alternate route when one path is blocked."},{"pt":"The ulnar artery keeps supplying the digital arteries through the arches","why":"Even with the radial line in place, blood still reaches the fingers."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-vessels-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[88,89],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins","w4-lab-ue-nerves"],"t":"A blood draw taken too deep","p":"A blood draw at the front of the elbow goes too deep. Name the vein the needle was meant for, the layer that should have stopped it, and the two structures it could hit underneath.","k":[{"pt":"The vein is the median cubital vein in the cubital fossa","why":"It crosses the front of the elbow between the cephalic and basilic veins, close to the surface."},{"pt":"The bicipital aponeurosis lies under the vein","why":"This sheet from the biceps tendon is the only layer between the vein and the deeper structures."},{"pt":"The brachial artery lies just deep to it","why":"The brachial artery divides in the cubital fossa, so it sits right under the draw site."},{"pt":"The median nerve lies just deep to it as well","why":"The nerve runs beside the brachial artery, so both are at risk together."},{"pt":"The vein is used because it is superficial, thin walled, wide, and low pressure","why":"Those features make it easy to enter safely, which is why the depth matters so much."}]}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-nerves","title":"Nerves of the upper limb (brachial plexus)","mw":"2026-10-14","tr":"2026-10-08","prompts":[{"id":"m3-upper-limb-nerves-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[89,191],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-nerves","w8-nerve-plexuses"],"t":"Nerves into the arm","p":"Draw the brachial plexus in simple form and name the five terminal branches. Then write which compartment or region of the limb each one supplies.","k":[{"pt":"Roots C5 to T1","why":"The whole plexus is built from the anterior rami of these five spinal nerves."},{"pt":"Musculocutaneous, from the lateral cord, to the anterior compartment of the arm","why":"It is the nerve of the front of the arm, the elbow flexor group."},{"pt":"Median, from the lateral and medial cords, to most of the anterior forearm and the thenar muscles","why":"It is the only terminal nerve built from two cords."},{"pt":"Ulnar, from the medial cord, to the little finger side of the forearm and most of the intrinsic hand muscles","why":"It runs down the medial side of the limb, so its territory is on the little finger side."},{"pt":"Radial, from the posterior cord, to the posterior compartments of the arm and forearm","why":"It is the nerve of the whole extensor side of the limb."},{"pt":"Axillary, from the posterior cord, to the shoulder region over the deltoid","why":"It wraps the surgical neck of the humerus to reach the shoulder from behind."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-nerves-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[89,191],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-nerves","w8-nerve-plexuses"],"t":"Build the plexus","p":"Draw the brachial plexus from roots to branches. Label every level and write the count at each level.","k":[{"pt":"Five roots: the anterior rami of C5, C6, C7, C8, and T1","why":"The roots are the starting level, and counting them first checks that nothing is missing."},{"pt":"Three trunks: upper from C5 and C6, middle from C7, lower from C8 and T1","why":"The middle trunk is the only one made from a single root."},{"pt":"Six divisions: each trunk splits into an anterior and a posterior division","why":"Three trunks times two divisions gives six."},{"pt":"Three cords: lateral, posterior, and medial","why":"They are named for where they sit around the axillary artery."},{"pt":"Five terminal branches: musculocutaneous, median, ulnar, radial, axillary","why":"These are the nerves you trace into the limb."},{"pt":"Order of levels: roots, trunks, divisions, cords, branches (Read That Damn Cadaver Book)","why":"The counts five, three, six, three, five let you rebuild the plexus from memory."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-nerves-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[89,191],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-nerves","w8-nerve-plexuses"],"t":"Branches before the terminal nerves","p":"List the named nerves that leave the brachial plexus before the five terminal branches, and write the root, trunk, or cord each one comes from.","k":[{"pt":"Dorsal scapular nerve from the C5 root","why":"It leaves at the very first level, so it is the highest branch of the plexus."},{"pt":"Long thoracic nerve from the C5, C6, and C7 roots","why":"It also comes off the roots and runs down the side of the chest wall."},{"pt":"Suprascapular nerve from the upper trunk","why":"It is the branch that comes off at the trunk level."},{"pt":"Lateral pectoral nerve from the lateral cord; medial pectoral nerve from the medial cord","why":"Each pectoral nerve is named for the cord it comes from, not for where it ends up."},{"pt":"Thoracodorsal nerve and the subscapular nerves from the posterior cord","why":"The posterior cord gives these branches as well as the radial and axillary nerves."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-nerves-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[89],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-nerves"],"t":"The radial nerve","p":"Trace the radial nerve from the plexus to the forearm. Name where it wraps the humerus and write what is lost if it is damaged there.","k":[{"pt":"From the posterior cord of the brachial plexus","why":"The posterior cord feeds the extensor side of the limb, which is the radial nerve's territory."},{"pt":"Spirals around the shaft of the humerus in the radial groove, pressed against the bone","why":"Lying directly on the bone is what makes it so easy to injure there."},{"pt":"Travels with the deep brachial artery in the groove","why":"The nerve and that artery share the same path behind the humerus."},{"pt":"Continues into the posterior forearm, where a deep branch reaches the extensor muscles","why":"Every muscle of the posterior forearm is supplied by the radial nerve or its deep branch."},{"pt":"Damage in the groove causes wrist drop, the loss of wrist and finger extension","why":"The wrist and finger extensors are all in the posterior forearm, so without them the hand hangs down."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-nerves-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[89],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-nerves"],"t":"Nerves at their weak spots","p":"Draw the humerus and the wrist and mark the places where the nerves of the upper limb are most easily injured. Beside each, write the nerve and the sign the injury produces.","k":[{"pt":"Axillary nerve at the surgical neck of the humerus: weak abduction of the arm and loss of feeling over the deltoid","why":"The nerve wraps the surgical neck, so a fracture or dislocation there stretches or crushes it."},{"pt":"Radial nerve in the radial groove at midshaft: wrist drop","why":"The nerve lies on the bone, so a midshaft fracture line runs right where the nerve is."},{"pt":"Ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle: tingling in the fourth and fifth fingers, and with lasting damage, clawing of those fingers","why":"It is superficial and unprotected there, which is why this spot is called the funny bone."},{"pt":"Median nerve in the carpal tunnel: wasting of the thenar eminence","why":"The nerve is squeezed under the flexor retinaculum with the nine flexor tendons."},{"pt":"Median nerve also lies deep to the median cubital vein in the cubital fossa","why":"Only the bicipital aponeurosis separates it from the usual blood draw site."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-nerves-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[89],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-nerves"],"t":"Cords around the artery","p":"Draw the axillary artery and place the three cords of the brachial plexus around it. Then write which terminal nerves come off each cord.","k":[{"pt":"Lateral cord on the lateral side of the axillary artery","why":"The cords are named for their position around this artery, so the artery is the landmark."},{"pt":"Posterior cord behind the artery","why":"Behind the artery is posterior, which matches its name."},{"pt":"Medial cord on the medial side of the artery","why":"Medial means toward the midline of the body, and the ulnar nerve that comes off this cord keeps to that side of the limb as it travels down."},{"pt":"Lateral cord gives the musculocutaneous nerve and part of the median nerve; medial cord gives the ulnar nerve and the rest of the median","why":"The median nerve gets a contribution from both of these cords, the only terminal nerve built that way."},{"pt":"Posterior cord gives the radial and axillary nerves","why":"Both of these supply the back of the limb, matching the cord's position."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-nerves-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[89],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-nerves"],"t":"Midshaft fracture","p":"A patient breaks the shaft of the humerus. Afterward they cannot extend the wrist or the fingers, but they can still bend the elbow. Name the nerve and explain the pattern.","k":[{"pt":"The nerve is the radial nerve","why":"Wrist and finger extension come from the posterior forearm, and one nerve supplies that whole compartment."},{"pt":"Start from what is lost: the posterior forearm extensors","why":"The lost movement points to the compartment, and the compartment points to the nerve."},{"pt":"Elbow bending is intact, so the musculocutaneous nerve is fine","why":"The anterior arm muscles that bend the elbow are on a different nerve, which rules it out."},{"pt":"The radial nerve spirals in the radial groove against the midshaft of the humerus","why":"The fracture site and the nerve are in the same place, which confirms it."},{"pt":"The sign is called wrist drop","why":"Without the extensors, nothing holds the wrist up against gravity."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-nerves-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[89],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-nerves"],"t":"A knock on the funny bone","p":"A student bangs the inner side of the elbow on a desk and feels tingling shoot into the ring and little fingers. Name the nerve, say exactly where it was hit, and predict what lasting damage at that spot would do to the hand.","k":[{"pt":"The nerve is the ulnar nerve","why":"The tingling goes to the ring and little fingers, the medial side of the hand, which is ulnar nerve territory."},{"pt":"It was hit behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus","why":"There the nerve is superficial and unprotected, which is why the spot is called the funny bone."},{"pt":"Lasting damage causes clawing of the fourth and fifth fingers","why":"The ulnar nerve reaches most of the intrinsic hand muscles, and without them those fingers curl."},{"pt":"Lasting damage also weakens the grip","why":"Losing most of the intrinsic hand muscles takes away the strength and fine control of the grip."},{"pt":"Feeling is lost on the medial hand","why":"The sensory loss matches the same fingers that tingled after the knock."}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-nerves-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[89],"cids":["w4-lab-ue-nerves"],"t":"A dislocated shoulder","p":"A shoulder dislocation damages a nerve at the surgical neck of the humerus. Name the nerve, trace where it came from, and predict what the patient will notice.","k":[{"pt":"The nerve is the axillary nerve","why":"It wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus, right where the dislocation pushes the head of the bone."},{"pt":"It comes from the posterior cord","why":"The posterior cord also gives the radial nerve, and both supply the back of the limb."},{"pt":"Weak abduction of the arm","why":"The deltoid is the main muscle the axillary nerve reaches, and its middle fibers abduct the arm."},{"pt":"Loss of feeling over the deltoid","why":"The patch of skin over the shoulder is also axillary nerve territory."},{"pt":"The nerve is named for the region, the axilla, but the injury is named for the bone","why":"The region name tells you where the nerve ends up, and the bone name tells you where it gets hurt."}]}]},{"id":"m3-blood","title":"Blood: composition, plasma, hematopoiesis, blood types and disorders","mw":"2026-10-19","tr":"2026-10-20","prompts":[{"id":"m3-blood-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[81,82],"cids":["w4-blood-composition","w4-formed-elements"],"t":"Blood mind map","p":"Build a mind map with blood at the center. Two main branches, plasma and formed elements. Fill both out completely.","k":[{"pt":"Plasma branch: water, proteins, electrolytes, nutrients, wastes, gases","why":"Plasma is the liquid matrix of blood, and each of these is something dissolved in it."},{"pt":"Plasma proteins named: albumin, globulins, fibrinogen","why":"These three are the main plasma proteins and the most abundant solutes after water."},{"pt":"Formed element branch: erythrocytes, five leukocytes, platelets","why":"These are the cells and cell fragments suspended in the plasma."},{"pt":"Percentages on the main split, about 55 percent plasma and 45 percent formed elements","why":"This is the split you see when a tube of blood is spun."},{"pt":"Buffy coat named, the thin pale layer of leukocytes and platelets, less than 1 percent","why":"It sits between the plasma and the red cells and is where the white cells end up in a spun tube."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[81],"cids":["w4-blood-composition"],"t":"What is in plasma","p":"List everything in plasma you can name, grouped as proteins, electrolytes, nutrients, wastes, and gases.","k":[{"pt":"Water, about 92 percent of plasma","why":"Water is the solvent that carries every other component."},{"pt":"Albumin, about 60 percent of plasma protein, made by the liver","why":"It is the most abundant plasma protein, holds water in the blood, and carries hormones, fatty acids, and drugs."},{"pt":"Globulins, about 35 percent: alpha and beta transport proteins and gamma globulins, the antibodies","why":"Gamma globulins are the only plasma proteins not made by the liver; plasma cells make them."},{"pt":"Fibrinogen, about 4 percent, the clotting protein","why":"It turns into fibrin threads that form the framework of a clot, and plasma with the clotting proteins removed is called serum."},{"pt":"Electrolytes: sodium, potassium, calcium, chloride, bicarbonate","why":"These are the dissolved ions in the plasma."},{"pt":"Nutrients: glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, vitamins. Wastes: urea, creatinine, bilirubin, ammonia","why":"Plasma carries what the cells need and what they throw away."},{"pt":"Gases: dissolved oxygen, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen","why":"A small amount of each gas travels dissolved in the plasma itself."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[83,29],"cids":["w4-hematopoiesis","w2-long-bone-gross"],"t":"Red marrow, yellow marrow","p":"Draw a long bone and a flat bone and mark where red marrow and yellow marrow sit in each. Write what each kind of marrow does and where a marrow sample is usually taken.","k":[{"pt":"Red marrow makes blood cells","why":"Red bone marrow is the site of hematopoiesis in adults."},{"pt":"Red marrow fills the spaces of spongy bone, such as the epiphyses of a long bone and the inside of a flat bone","why":"Spongy bone is built from trabeculae, and the spaces between them hold the red marrow."},{"pt":"Yellow marrow is stored fat in the medullary cavity of the adult long bone shaft","why":"It sits in the hollow core of the diaphysis, not in the spongy ends."},{"pt":"In adults, red marrow is found in the axial skeleton and the proximal limb bones","why":"The adult long bone shafts hold yellow marrow, so active red marrow is left in these bones."},{"pt":"A marrow sample is drawn from the iliac crest or the sternum","why":"Both are flat bones of the axial skeleton or girdle with red marrow close to the surface."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[83],"cids":["w4-hematopoiesis"],"t":"Hemopoiesis","p":"Start at the hemopoietic stem cell and branch out as far as you can name. Get every cell line you can onto the page.","k":[{"pt":"Hemocytoblast in the red bone marrow","why":"Every formed element starts from this one stem cell."},{"pt":"Two lines: myeloid and lymphoid","why":"The stem cell splits into these two lines, and each line makes a different set of cells."},{"pt":"Myeloid line: erythrocytes, platelets, neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, monocytes","why":"The myeloid line makes every formed element except the lymphocytes."},{"pt":"Platelets come from the megakaryocyte, a giant marrow cell that breaks into thousands of fragments","why":"The megakaryocyte stays in the marrow; only its pieces enter the blood."},{"pt":"Reticulocyte as the young red cell that has just lost its nucleus","why":"It still shows leftover ribosomes and makes up about 1 to 2 percent of circulating red cells."},{"pt":"Lymphoid line: lymphocytes, including T cells and B cells","why":"This is the only line that makes lymphocytes, which is why lymphocytes sit apart from the other white cells."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[83,82],"cids":["w4-hematopoiesis","w4-formed-elements"],"t":"A red cell from birth to breakdown","p":"Build a mind map following one red blood cell from stem cell to disposal. Put every structure and organ it passes on the branches.","k":[{"pt":"Hemocytoblast in the red bone marrow","why":"The red cell starts as the same stem cell that makes every other formed element."},{"pt":"Myeloid line","why":"Red cells come from the myeloid line, not the lymphoid line."},{"pt":"Reticulocyte, then mature erythrocyte","why":"The reticulocyte has just lost its nucleus and still holds ribosome remnants before it matures."},{"pt":"About 120 days in circulation","why":"Without a nucleus the cell cannot make new parts to repair itself, so it wears out."},{"pt":"Worn cells are broken down in the spleen and the liver","why":"These organs remove old red cells from the circulation."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[84],"cids":["w4-blood-disorders"],"t":"Disorders by component","p":"Sort these disorders by the part of the blood each one affects: anemia, sickle cell disease, polycythemia, leukemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, hemophilia. For each, write whether the problem is too few, too many, or abnormal.","k":[{"pt":"Anemia: erythrocytes, too few red cells or too little hemoglobin","why":"Either way the blood carries less oxygen."},{"pt":"Polycythemia: erythrocytes, too many","why":"Extra red cells thicken the blood and slow its flow."},{"pt":"Sickle cell disease: erythrocytes and hemoglobin, abnormal","why":"An inherited abnormal hemoglobin bends the cell into a sickle shape."},{"pt":"Leukemia: leukocytes, abnormal; leukopenia: leukocytes, too few","why":"Leukemia is a cancer of white cell formation that crowds the marrow, while leukopenia is simply a low count."},{"pt":"Thrombocytopenia: platelets, too few","why":"With fewer platelets the risk of bleeding goes up."},{"pt":"Hemophilia: clotting proteins, an inherited missing clotting factor","why":"It is the one disorder on the list that affects a plasma protein instead of a formed element."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[81],"cids":["w4-blood-composition"],"t":"Why blood is a connective tissue","p":"Blood is classified as a connective tissue. Defend that using its cells and its matrix, then name the layers you would see if you spun a tube of it.","k":[{"pt":"Every connective tissue is cells suspended in a matrix","why":"That definition is what blood has to meet."},{"pt":"Plasma is the matrix, a liquid, about 55 percent of the volume","why":"Blood is the only connective tissue with a fluid matrix."},{"pt":"Formed elements are the cells, about 45 percent of the volume","why":"Red cells, white cells, and platelets play the role the fibroblasts or chondrocytes play in other connective tissues."},{"pt":"Spun tube: plasma on top, buffy coat in the middle, red cells at the bottom","why":"The heaviest part, the red cells, sinks to the bottom."},{"pt":"Hematocrit is the percent of blood volume made of red cells, about 42 to 52 percent in males and 37 to 47 percent in females","why":"It is read straight off the height of the red layer."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[84,82],"cids":["w4-blood-disorders","w4-formed-elements"],"t":"A rigid red cell","p":"An abnormal hemoglobin makes a red cell rigid and curved. Predict where in the circulation it causes trouble and explain why, starting from the shape of a normal red cell.","k":[{"pt":"A normal red cell is a flexible biconcave disc","why":"Its flexible membrane lets it bend and fold."},{"pt":"It has to fold to pass single file through capillaries narrower than itself","why":"Capillaries are the narrowest vessels the cell travels through."},{"pt":"The sickle cell is rigid and curved, so it cannot fold","why":"The abnormal hemoglobin takes away the flexibility the cell depends on."},{"pt":"It jams in the capillaries and blocks them","why":"Trouble starts in the smallest vessels, not in the large arteries."},{"pt":"The tissue downstream loses its blood supply, causing pain and tissue damage","why":"A blocked capillary means the cells it feeds go without oxygen."},{"pt":"This is sickle cell disease, an inherited disorder of the erythrocytes and hemoglobin","why":"It is named for the shape the cell takes."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[81,84],"cids":["w4-blood-composition","w4-blood-disorders"],"t":"Two spun tubes","p":"Two blood samples are spun. In sample A the red layer is much shorter than normal. In sample B the pale middle layer is much thicker than normal. Name the three layers, say what each change means, and name a disorder that fits each one.","k":[{"pt":"Layers: plasma on top, buffy coat in the middle, red cells at the bottom","why":"Spinning separates blood by weight, so the red cells sink and the plasma rises."},{"pt":"Sample A has a low hematocrit","why":"The hematocrit is the percent of blood made of red cells, read from the height of the red layer."},{"pt":"A low red layer fits anemia","why":"Anemia means too few red cells or too little hemoglobin."},{"pt":"Sample B has extra leukocytes or platelets in the buffy coat","why":"The buffy coat holds the white cells and platelets, normally less than 1 percent of the volume."},{"pt":"A high white cell count points toward infection or leukemia","why":"Leukemia crowds the marrow with abnormal white cells, so a thick buffy coat is one of its signs."}]},{"id":"m3-blood-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[83,84],"cids":["w4-hematopoiesis","w4-blood-disorders"],"t":"Every count is low","p":"A patient's blood count shows low red cells, low white cells, and low platelets all at the same time. Reason out which single tissue is most likely the problem and where you would take a sample to look at it.","k":[{"pt":"All three formed elements come from the hemocytoblast","why":"If every line is low, the problem is most likely where they all start."},{"pt":"That stem cell lives in the red bone marrow","why":"Red marrow is the site of hematopoiesis in adults."},{"pt":"The myeloid line makes red cells, platelets, and most white cells, and the lymphoid line makes lymphocytes","why":"A problem in only one formed element would point to one line, but here both lines are affected."},{"pt":"Marrow failure or marrow crowded by leukemia can lower all the counts","why":"Both shut down normal blood cell production at the source."},{"pt":"Sample the marrow from the iliac crest or the sternum","why":"These bones hold red marrow close to the surface, so the blood forming tissue itself can be examined."}]}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-posterior","title":"Posterior forearm muscles","mw":"2026-10-19","tr":"2026-10-15","prompts":[{"id":"m3-forearm-posterior-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[85,86],"cids":["w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"Posterior compartment","p":"Draw the posterior arm and posterior forearm. Label the muscles and write the action of each group.","k":[{"pt":"Arm: triceps brachii with its three heads, extending the elbow","why":"The triceps is the main muscle of the posterior arm, and all three heads end on the olecranon."},{"pt":"Anconeus at the back of the elbow, helping extension","why":"It is the small muscle that links the arm group to the forearm group."},{"pt":"Forearm superficial: extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, extensor carpi ulnaris","why":"Most of these start at the lateral epicondyle, the common extensor origin, so they fan out from one point."},{"pt":"Forearm deep: supinator, abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus, extensor indicis","why":"Most of these start on the back of the radius, ulna, and interosseous membrane rather than the common extensor origin, which puts them deeper; the supinator also takes origin from the lateral epicondyle."},{"pt":"Group extends the wrist and fingers, and the supinator turns the palm up","why":"Knowing the compartment action lets you predict what an unfamiliar muscle here does."}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-posterior-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"Where the extensor tendons land","p":"Draw the back of the hand and wrist with the extensor tendons arriving. Label where each tendon ends.","k":[{"pt":"Extensor carpi radialis longus to the base of metacarpal 2; brevis to the base of metacarpal 3","why":"Both radialis muscles land on the thumb side, matching their name."},{"pt":"Extensor carpi ulnaris to the base of metacarpal 5","why":"Ulnaris lands on the little finger side."},{"pt":"Extensor digitorum to the extensor expansions of digits 2 to 5","why":"It ends in the fibrous expansions on the backs of the fingers rather than on a single bone."},{"pt":"Extensor digiti minimi to the extensor expansion of digit 5; extensor indicis to the extensor expansion of digit 2","why":"These two give the little finger and index finger their own extra extensor."},{"pt":"Thumb: abductor pollicis longus to the base of metacarpal 1, extensor pollicis brevis to the proximal phalanx, extensor pollicis longus to the distal phalanx","why":"Brevis is short and stops early, longus is long and reaches the tip."},{"pt":"Extensor retinaculum drawn as the band across the back of the wrist that the tendons pass under","why":"It holds the tendons down against the wrist as they cross it."}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-posterior-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"The three wrist extensors","p":"Compare extensor carpi radialis longus, extensor carpi radialis brevis, and extensor carpi ulnaris: where each starts, where each ends, and what each does besides extending the wrist. Then match each to its partner on the front of the forearm.","k":[{"pt":"Extensor carpi radialis longus starts on the lateral supracondylar ridge, just above the lateral epicondyle","why":"It is the one wrist extensor that starts above the common extensor origin."},{"pt":"Extensor carpi radialis brevis starts on the lateral epicondyle; extensor carpi ulnaris starts on the lateral epicondyle and the back of the ulna","why":"Both share the common extensor origin."},{"pt":"Radialis longus ends on metacarpal 2, brevis on metacarpal 3, ulnaris on metacarpal 5","why":"The landing side decides the side to side pull."},{"pt":"Both radialis muscles extend and abduct the wrist; ulnaris extends and adducts it","why":"Thumb side muscles tip the hand toward the thumb, and the little finger side muscle tips it the other way."},{"pt":"They mirror the flexors on the front: flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris","why":"Same naming pattern, same side pulls, opposite direction of bending."}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-posterior-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[86,44],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments","w2-hand-bones"],"t":"The thumb muscles on the back","p":"Compare abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, and extensor pollicis longus: where each starts, where each ends, and what each does. Then show how their tendons frame the hollow at the base of the thumb.","k":[{"pt":"All three start on the back of the forearm bones and the interosseous membrane","why":"They are deep posterior muscles, not part of the common extensor origin."},{"pt":"Abductor pollicis longus ends on the base of metacarpal 1 and abducts and extends the thumb","why":"It lands on the first bone of the thumb, so it moves the thumb as a whole."},{"pt":"Extensor pollicis brevis ends on the proximal phalanx and extends the thumb at the metacarpophalangeal joint","why":"It is short, so it stops at the first phalanx and works the knuckle."},{"pt":"Extensor pollicis longus ends on the distal phalanx and extends the thumb at the interphalangeal joint","why":"It is the only one that reaches the thumb tip."},{"pt":"Their tendons outline the anatomical snuffbox: abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis on the thumb side, extensor pollicis longus on the other side","why":"The hollow appears between these tendons when you extend the thumb."},{"pt":"The scaphoid lies in the floor of the snuffbox","why":"That is why tenderness there after a fall points to a scaphoid fracture."}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-posterior-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"Tennis elbow","p":"A tennis player has pain over the bony bump on the outer side of the elbow, and it gets worse when she extends her wrist against resistance. Name the structure involved, list the muscles that start there, and explain why that movement hurts.","k":[{"pt":"The lateral epicondyle, the common extensor origin","why":"Tennis elbow is inflammation at the shared starting point of the wrist extensors."},{"pt":"Muscles that start there include extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, and extensor carpi ulnaris","why":"Almost every superficial extensor shares this origin, so one sore spot involves the whole group."},{"pt":"Supinator and anconeus also start on the lateral epicondyle","why":"They share the same bony origin even though they are not wrist extensors."},{"pt":"Extending the wrist against resistance pulls on the irritated origin","why":"Every time the extensors contract, they tug on the lateral epicondyle."},{"pt":"Golfer's elbow is the same problem at the medial epicondyle","why":"That is the common flexor origin, so the two conditions mirror each other."}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-posterior-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[86,85],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments","w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"],"t":"Turning a screwdriver","p":"A carpenter tightens a screw with the right hand by turning the palm from facing down to facing up, with the elbow bent. Name the two muscles doing the turning, the compartment each one sits in, and the muscles that would turn the palm back down.","k":[{"pt":"The movement is supination","why":"Turning the palm from down to up is supination, a rotation of the radius."},{"pt":"Supinator: posterior forearm, from the lateral epicondyle and proximal ulna to the proximal lateral radius","why":"It wraps the top of the radius, so pulling on it rolls the radius into supination."},{"pt":"Biceps brachii: anterior arm, ending on the radial tuberosity","why":"It also pulls on the radius, which is why the biceps is a supinator as well as an elbow flexor."},{"pt":"Both muscles end on the radius","why":"The radius is the bone that rotates, so any muscle that turns the palm must attach to it."},{"pt":"Turning back down uses pronator quadratus, the prime mover, and pronator teres, both in the anterior forearm","why":"Pronation is the opposite rotation, done by muscles on the front of the forearm."}]}]},{"id":"m3-hand-muscles","title":"Hand muscles and wrist landmarks","mw":"2026-10-19","tr":"2026-10-15","prompts":[{"id":"m3-hand-muscles-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"Hand muscles by group","p":"List the intrinsic muscles of the hand by group, and draw the palm showing where each group sits.","k":[{"pt":"Thenar group: abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis","why":"These make the bulge at the base of the thumb, the thenar eminence, and they move the thumb."},{"pt":"Hypothenar group: abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi brevis, opponens digiti minimi","why":"These make the bulge on the little finger side of the palm and mirror the thenar group."},{"pt":"Midpalmar group: lumbricals, palmar interossei, dorsal interossei","why":"They sit between the two bulges, among the metacarpals and flexor tendons."},{"pt":"Adductor pollicis on its own in the web space of the thumb","why":"It is not part of the thenar group, even though it moves the thumb."},{"pt":"Each group drawn in its region of the palm","why":"Intrinsic muscles lie entirely within the hand, so the location in the palm is how you identify the group."}]},{"id":"m3-hand-muscles-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[44,86],"cids":["w2-hand-bones","w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"The carpal tunnel","p":"Draw the wrist in cross section. Label the roof, the floor, and everything passing through the tunnel. Then name one structure that passes over the top and is therefore spared.","k":[{"pt":"Flexor retinaculum as the roof","why":"This strong fibrous band arches over the carpal bones, and the space beneath it is the tunnel."},{"pt":"Carpal bones forming the arched floor","why":"The carpals make a curved trough, which the retinaculum closes into a tunnel."},{"pt":"Nine tendons: four flexor digitorum superficialis, four flexor digitorum profundus, one flexor pollicis longus","why":"These are the long flexors of the fingers and thumb, all heading to the palm side of the digits."},{"pt":"Median nerve, the tenth structure","why":"It shares the tight space with the tendons, which is why narrowing the tunnel squeezes it."},{"pt":"Palmaris longus tendon passes over the top of the retinaculum into the palmar aponeurosis","why":"It runs outside the tunnel, so it is not squeezed when the tunnel narrows."}]},{"id":"m3-hand-muscles-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[86,44],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments","w2-hand-bones"],"t":"Landmarks of the elbow, wrist, and hand","p":"Name and locate each of these: cubital fossa, carpal tunnel, flexor retinaculum, extensor retinaculum, palmar aponeurosis, anatomical snuffbox, extensor expansion.","k":[{"pt":"Cubital fossa: the hollow at the front of the elbow","why":"The median cubital vein crosses it, and the brachial artery divides into the radial and ulnar arteries there."},{"pt":"Carpal tunnel under the flexor retinaculum, on the palm side of the wrist","why":"The retinaculum is the roof, so the two are always found together."},{"pt":"Extensor retinaculum across the back of the wrist","why":"It holds the extensor tendons down as they cross onto the back of the hand."},{"pt":"Palmar aponeurosis: the triangular sheet of fascia in the palm","why":"Palmaris longus ends in it and tenses it."},{"pt":"Anatomical snuffbox: the hollow at the base of the thumb on the back of the wrist","why":"It shows best when the thumb is extended, and the scaphoid lies in its floor."},{"pt":"Extensor expansion: the fibrous hood on the back of each finger","why":"Extensor digitorum ends in it, and the lumbricals and interossei join it too."}]},{"id":"m3-hand-muscles-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"PAD and DAB","p":"Compare the palmar and dorsal interossei: where each starts, which way it moves the fingers, and the line the movement is measured from. Then add what the lumbricals do.","k":[{"pt":"Palmar interossei start on metacarpals 2, 4, and 5 and adduct the fingers toward the middle finger (PAD)","why":"Palmar ADduct: they pull the fingers together."},{"pt":"Dorsal interossei start on the adjacent sides of metacarpals 1 to 5 and abduct the fingers away from the middle finger (DAB)","why":"Dorsal ABduct: they spread the fingers apart."},{"pt":"The reference line is the middle finger, not the midline of the body","why":"Abduction and adduction in the hand are measured from the middle finger."},{"pt":"The middle finger has no palmar interosseous and gets two dorsal interossei","why":"It is the reference line, so it never moves toward itself, but it can move to either side."},{"pt":"Lumbricals start on the tendons of flexor digitorum profundus and end on the extensor expansions","why":"They are the only hand muscles that start on a tendon instead of a bone."},{"pt":"Lumbricals bend the knuckles and straighten the finger joints","why":"Their path runs in front of the knuckle and then joins the extensor expansion behind the finger joints."}]},{"id":"m3-hand-muscles-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[86],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"Thenar and hypothenar","p":"Compare the thenar and hypothenar muscles: where each group starts, where each group ends, and what each muscle does. Then explain why adductor pollicis is not counted with the thenar group.","k":[{"pt":"Thenar muscles start on the flexor retinaculum and the trapezium, and abductor pollicis brevis also on the scaphoid","why":"They start on the thumb side of the retinaculum and the carpal bones under it."},{"pt":"Hypothenar muscles start on the pisiform or on the hook of the hamate and the flexor retinaculum","why":"They start on the little finger side carpals, mirroring the thenar group."},{"pt":"Abductor and flexor brevis of each group end on the proximal phalanx of their digit","why":"Short muscles reach only the first phalanx."},{"pt":"Opponens pollicis ends on the side of metacarpal 1; opponens digiti minimi on the side of metacarpal 5","why":"Pulling on the metacarpal swings the whole digit across the palm, which is what opposition needs."},{"pt":"Each group abducts, flexes, and opposes its digit","why":"The names give the actions: abductor, flexor, opponens."},{"pt":"Adductor pollicis starts on the capitate and metacarpals 2 and 3 and sits in the web space","why":"It lies deeper and away from the thenar eminence, so it is grouped on its own."}]},{"id":"m3-hand-muscles-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[86,44,89],"cids":["w4-lab-forearm-compartments","w2-hand-bones","w4-lab-ue-nerves"],"t":"A flat thenar eminence","p":"A patient with long standing carpal tunnel syndrome has a flat thenar eminence. Name what is compressed and what forms the tunnel around it, then name the muscles that have wasted and the thumb movements that are now weak.","k":[{"pt":"The median nerve is compressed inside the carpal tunnel","why":"Carpal tunnel syndrome is narrowing of the tunnel that squeezes the median nerve."},{"pt":"Flexor retinaculum forms the roof and the carpal bones form the floor","why":"There is no room to expand, so anything that narrows the tunnel presses on the nerve."},{"pt":"Thenar muscles wasted: abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis","why":"These make up the thenar eminence, so their wasting is what flattens it."},{"pt":"Weak abduction, flexion, and opposition of the thumb","why":"Each wasted muscle is named for the movement that is now weak."},{"pt":"Adductor pollicis, in the web space, is not part of the thenar group, so it does not waste and the thumb can still pull in","why":"Carpal tunnel syndrome wastes the thenar group, and adductor pollicis sits apart from that group in the web space."},{"pt":"The flat thenar eminence is a structural sign of a nerve problem","why":"You can see the nerve injury in the shape of the hand."}]},{"id":"m3-hand-muscles-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[44,86],"cids":["w2-hand-bones","w4-lab-forearm-compartments"],"t":"A fall on an outstretched hand","p":"After a fall on an outstretched hand, a patient is tender in the hollow at the base of the thumb on the back of the wrist. Name the hollow, the tendons that frame it, the bone in its floor, and explain why that tenderness matters.","k":[{"pt":"The hollow is the anatomical snuffbox","why":"It shows clearly when the thumb is extended."},{"pt":"Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis tendons frame the thumb side","why":"These two run together toward the base of the thumb."},{"pt":"Extensor pollicis longus tendon frames the other side","why":"It runs to the thumb tip, so its tendon makes the far border of the hollow."},{"pt":"The scaphoid lies in the floor","why":"Pressing into the snuffbox presses directly on this carpal bone."},{"pt":"The scaphoid is the most frequently fractured carpal bone","why":"Tenderness in the snuffbox after this kind of fall is treated as a scaphoid fracture until proven otherwise."}]}]}]},{"n":4,"title":"Module 4. Respiratory, Lymphatic, Endocrine, Lower Limb and Digestive","topics":[{"id":"m4-upper-respiratory","title":"Nose, pharynx and larynx","mw":"2026-10-26","tr":"2026-10-27","prompts":[{"id":"m4-upper-respiratory-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[98],"cids":["resp-upper-tract"],"t":"Inside the nose","p":"Draw the lateral wall of the nasal cavity and label everything on it. Mark where each sinus drains.","k":[{"pt":"Superior, middle and inferior nasal conchae on the lateral wall","why":"The superior and middle conchae are parts of the ethmoid bone and the inferior concha is its own bone, and all three make the air tumble so more of it touches the warm, moist lining."},{"pt":"Superior, middle and inferior meatuses, the air passages under each concha","why":"Each meatus is named for the concha above it, which is how you know where each sinus opening lands."},{"pt":"Olfactory mucosa in a small patch in the roof","why":"The smell receptors sit only in the roof, and the rest of the cavity is lined with respiratory mucosa that traps and sweeps particles."},{"pt":"Frontal sinus, maxillary sinus, and the front and middle ethmoidal cells drain into the middle meatus","why":"The middle meatus takes most of the sinus drainage, so swelling there affects several sinuses at once."},{"pt":"Back ethmoidal cells drain into the superior meatus, and the sphenoidal sinus drains above the superior concha into the sphenoethmoidal recess","why":"These are the only sinus openings that do not land in the middle meatus."},{"pt":"Choanae at the back, opening into the nasopharynx","why":"The choanae are where the nasal cavity ends and the pharynx begins, so they mark the exit on your drawing."}]},{"id":"m4-upper-respiratory-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[98,113],"cids":["resp-upper-tract","w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"],"t":"Three regions of the pharynx","p":"Draw the pharynx from the side and split it into its three parts. Label the boundaries of each and every opening into it.","k":[{"pt":"Nasopharynx behind the nasal cavity, down to the soft palate","why":"The soft palate is the floor of the nasopharynx, and it lifts during swallowing to seal off this air only region."},{"pt":"Auditory tube openings on the side walls and the pharyngeal tonsil on the back wall of the nasopharynx","why":"The auditory tubes connect the nasopharynx to the middle ears, so these openings link the throat to the ear."},{"pt":"Oropharynx from the soft palate to the epiglottis, with the palatine tonsils in its side walls and the lingual tonsil on the back of the tongue","why":"The oropharynx sits behind the mouth, so its tonsils guard what is swallowed as well as what is breathed in."},{"pt":"Laryngopharynx from the epiglottis down to the esophagus, behind the larynx","why":"At its lower end air turns forward into the larynx and food goes back into the esophagus, so this is where the two paths divide."},{"pt":"Openings: choanae from the nasal cavity, fauces from the mouth, the laryngeal inlet in front, and the esophagus below","why":"Each region of the pharynx is defined by the cavity it sits behind, and these openings are where those cavities connect to it."},{"pt":"Only the oropharynx and laryngopharynx carry both air and food","why":"Where food passes the lining is stratified squamous to resist rubbing, while the air only nasopharynx keeps a ciliated lining."}]},{"id":"m4-upper-respiratory-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[98,99],"cids":["resp-upper-tract","resp-larynx"],"t":"Sinuses and larynx","p":"Name the paranasal sinuses and the bone each is in. Then name every laryngeal cartilage you can and the two sets of folds.","k":[{"pt":"Frontal sinus in the frontal bone, sphenoidal sinus in the sphenoid, ethmoidal air cells in the ethmoid, maxillary sinus in the maxilla","why":"Each sinus is named for the bone it sits in, so knowing the bone gives you the name, and the maxillary in the cheek is the largest."},{"pt":"Single cartilages: thyroid, cricoid, and epiglottis","why":"These three are unpaired; the thyroid is the largest and the cricoid just below it is the only complete ring of cartilage in the airway."},{"pt":"Paired cartilages: arytenoid, corniculate, and cuneiform, nine cartilages in all","why":"Three single plus three pairs makes nine, and the arytenoids matter most because they anchor and move the vocal folds."},{"pt":"Vestibular folds above (false vocal cords) and vocal folds below (true vocal cords)","why":"Only the lower vocal folds vibrate to make sound; the vestibular folds help close the airway during swallowing."},{"pt":"Glottis, the vocal folds and the opening between them, and the cricothyroid ligament between the thyroid and cricoid cartilages","why":"The opening of the glottis is the narrowest point air passes in the larynx, and the cricothyroid ligament fills the gap in front below it."}]},{"id":"m4-upper-respiratory-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[97,98,100],"cids":["resp-zones","resp-upper-tract","resp-tree"],"t":"Nostril to alveolus","p":"Trace one molecule of air from the nostril to the alveolus. Name every structure it passes through in order.","k":[{"pt":"Nostril (nares), nasal vestibule, nasal cavity, then out through the choanae","why":"The nose warms, moistens and filters the air before it ever reaches the throat."},{"pt":"Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx","why":"Air passes all three regions of the pharynx, and from the oropharynx down it shares the path with food."},{"pt":"Larynx through the glottis, then the trachea down to the carina","why":"The larynx is the first structure of the lower tract, so the line between the upper and lower tracts falls where the pharynx meets the larynx."},{"pt":"Main, lobar and segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles","why":"Each step is smaller and more numerous, and the terminal bronchiole is the last tube that only carries air."},{"pt":"Respiratory bronchiole, alveolar duct, alveolar sac, alveolus","why":"The respiratory zone starts at the respiratory bronchiole, the first airway with alveoli budding from its wall."}]},{"id":"m4-upper-respiratory-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[97,100],"cids":["resp-zones","resp-tree"],"t":"Two zones","p":"Build a mind map splitting the airway into the conducting zone and the respiratory zone. Put every structure on the correct branch and mark where the line falls.","k":[{"pt":"Conducting: nose, nasal cavity, pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi, bronchioles and terminal bronchioles","why":"These walls are too thick for gases to cross into the blood, so they only carry, warm, moisten and filter the air."},{"pt":"Respiratory: respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, alveoli","why":"These are the structures with alveoli in their walls, where air comes close enough to the blood for gas exchange."},{"pt":"The line falls where a terminal bronchiole gives way to a respiratory bronchiole","why":"This boundary lies deep inside the lung, not at the larynx where the upper and lower tract line falls."},{"pt":"Paranasal sinuses placed beside the conducting branch as air spaces with no exchange","why":"They open off the nasal cavity and share its lining, but air does not pass through them on its way to the lungs."},{"pt":"Structural changes down the tree noted on the branch: cartilage rings, then plates, then none, as smooth muscle takes over","why":"The wall thins as you go deeper, which is what lets the respiratory zone exchange gas."}]},{"id":"m4-upper-respiratory-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[97,100,103],"cids":["resp-zones","resp-tree","resp-thoracic-diaphragm"],"t":"One molecule of air","p":"Build a mind map following one molecule of air in and back out. Branch at every point where the pathway divides and note what changes at each level.","k":[{"pt":"Nose or mouth, pharynx, larynx, trachea","why":"The nose and the mouth both lead into the pharynx, and from there a single path runs down through the larynx into the trachea."},{"pt":"Branch at the carina into the main bronchi, then lobar (three right, two left), then segmental","why":"Each branch serves a smaller piece of lung, ending with one segmental bronchus for each bronchopulmonary segment."},{"pt":"Cartilage disappearing, smooth muscle increasing, epithelium thinning","why":"C rings become plates and then vanish in the bronchioles, while pseudostratified epithelium thins to simple cuboidal."},{"pt":"Terminal to respiratory bronchiole, the exchange line","why":"This is where the conducting zone ends and the respiratory zone begins."},{"pt":"Same route back out, with the muscles that drive it","why":"The diaphragm and external intercostals enlarge the chest to draw air in, quiet expiration is elastic recoil, and the internal intercostals and abdominal muscles push air out when breathing is forced."}]},{"id":"m4-upper-respiratory-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[99,104],"cids":["resp-larynx","resp-disorders"],"t":"The emergency airway","p":"Something is stuck in a patient's larynx above the vocal folds and they cannot breathe. Run your finger down the front of the neck from the hyoid bone and name each structure you feel, then name the spot used for an emergency airway and explain why it works.","k":[{"pt":"Hyoid bone first, the U shaped bone the larynx hangs from","why":"The hyoid sits above the larynx and is not part of it, so it is your starting landmark."},{"pt":"Thyroid cartilage with the laryngeal prominence (Adam's apple)","why":"It is the largest laryngeal cartilage, and its front ridge is the easiest point on the larynx to find."},{"pt":"Cricoid cartilage just below, the only complete ring in the airway","why":"Its firm ring sits directly under the gap you are looking for."},{"pt":"Cricothyroid ligament in the gap between the thyroid and cricoid cartilages","why":"It lies just under the skin with no cartilage over it, which makes it the landmark for an emergency airway."},{"pt":"The ligament sits below the vocal folds, so an opening there bypasses the blockage","why":"The obstruction is above the vocal folds, so air let in at the cricothyroid ligament reaches the trachea from below it."},{"pt":"The trachea continues down from the lower edge of the cricoid","why":"Air entering at the ligament passes the cricoid ring and goes straight into the trachea."}]},{"id":"m4-upper-respiratory-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[98,104],"cids":["resp-upper-tract","resp-disorders"],"t":"A blocked middle meatus","p":"A cold makes the lining of the nasal cavity swell shut over the middle meatus. Predict which sinuses can no longer drain, which ones still can and where they open, and name the disorder that can follow.","k":[{"pt":"Frontal sinus is blocked because it drains into the middle meatus","why":"The frontal sinus sits in the forehead above the orbits, and its opening lands in the middle meatus."},{"pt":"Maxillary sinus is blocked because it also drains into the middle meatus","why":"The maxillary is the largest sinus, in the cheek below each orbit, and its opening also lands in the middle meatus."},{"pt":"Front and middle ethmoidal air cells are blocked","why":"The ethmoidal cells split their drainage, and only the front and middle cells use the middle meatus."},{"pt":"Back ethmoidal cells still drain into the superior meatus","why":"Their opening is above the middle concha, out of reach of the swelling."},{"pt":"Sphenoidal sinus still drains above the superior concha into the sphenoethmoidal recess","why":"It sits deep behind the nasal cavity and opens higher than the middle meatus."},{"pt":"Sinusitis, inflammation of the sinus lining","why":"It often follows when the sinus openings into the nasal cavity are blocked, and the sinuses share the same mucosa as the nasal cavity."}]}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory","title":"Trachea, bronchial tree, lungs and pleurae","mw":"2026-10-26","tr":"2026-10-27","prompts":[{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[101],"cids":["resp-lungs-pleura"],"t":"Lungs and pleurae","p":"Draw both lungs with their lobes and fissures. Label the pleurae and their cavity, then write what sits in the space between the lungs.","k":[{"pt":"Right lung three lobes, horizontal and oblique fissures","why":"The horizontal fissure separates the superior lobe from the middle lobe, and the oblique fissure separates the inferior lobe from the other two."},{"pt":"Left lung two lobes, oblique fissure, cardiac notch and lingula","why":"The heart tilts left and takes up room, so the left lung has a notch for it and the lingula sits where a middle lobe would be."},{"pt":"Visceral pleura on the lung and into the fissures, parietal pleura on the chest wall, diaphragm and mediastinum","why":"They are one continuous sheet that turns back on itself at the hilum, which is why your drawing shows them meeting there."},{"pt":"Pleural cavity between the layers with a thin film of pleural fluid","why":"The fluid lets the lung glide on the chest wall and holds the two layers together so the lung follows the wall."},{"pt":"Mediastinum: heart, great vessels, trachea, esophagus, thymus","why":"The mediastinum keeps the right and left pleural cavities apart, so a problem in one cavity does not collapse the other lung."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[102],"cids":["resp-histo"],"t":"The respiratory membrane","p":"Draw the barrier between alveolar air and blood, enlarged. Label every layer the oxygen must cross.","k":[{"pt":"Type I alveolar cell, a thin simple squamous cell","why":"Type I cells cover nearly all of the alveolar surface, and gases cross the wall through them."},{"pt":"Fused basement membranes of the alveolus and the capillary","why":"The two basement membranes are joined into one layer, which keeps the barrier very thin."},{"pt":"Capillary endothelial cell of a pulmonary capillary","why":"This is the last layer before the blood, the wall of the capillary net that wraps each alveolus."},{"pt":"Three layers in all, in order from air to blood","why":"Type I cell, fused basement membranes, endothelium: that sequence is the respiratory membrane."},{"pt":"Type II cells and alveolar macrophages drawn nearby, not in the barrier","why":"Type II cells sit scattered in the wall making surfactant and macrophages move over the surface, but oxygen does not cross either one."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[100,101],"cids":["resp-tree","resp-lungs-pleura"],"t":"Segments and the root","p":"Name the lobes of each lung and write how many bronchopulmonary segments each lung has. Then draw the root of the lung and label what enters and leaves at the hilum.","k":[{"pt":"Right three lobes, left two with the cardiac notch and lingula","why":"Each lobe gets its own lobar bronchus, so the lobe count matches the lobar bronchi."},{"pt":"Right lung 10 segments, left lung about 8 to 10","why":"Each segment is served by its own segmental bronchus and blood vessels, so the count follows the segmental bronchi."},{"pt":"Root of the lung: main bronchus, pulmonary artery, pulmonary veins","why":"The root is the bundle of everything passing through the hilum on the mediastinal surface."},{"pt":"Bronchial vessels, nerves and lymphatic vessels also in the root","why":"The hilum is the only doorway into the lung, so the smaller vessels and nerves use it too."},{"pt":"Pulmonary veins are the lowest structures in both hila","why":"This holds for both lungs, so the veins are a reliable first landmark at any hilum."},{"pt":"Left hilum: pulmonary artery highest. Right hilum: a bronchus at or above the artery","why":"This difference in what sits on top is how you tell a right lung from a left on the cadaver."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[103],"cids":["resp-thoracic-diaphragm"],"t":"The diaphragm","p":"Draw the diaphragm from below. Label its attachments and the three major openings, and name what passes through each.","k":[{"pt":"Muscle fibers attach to the lower edge of the rib cage, the sternum, and the lumbar vertebrae by the crura","why":"All the fibers pull toward the top of the dome, so when they contract the dome flattens and moves down."},{"pt":"Central tendon at the top of the dome","why":"It is the flat sheet of tendon the fibers pull toward, and the highest opening sits in it."},{"pt":"Caval opening at T8, inferior vena cava","why":"It sits in the central tendon, the highest of the three openings."},{"pt":"Esophageal hiatus at T10, esophagus and vagus nerves","why":"This opening is in the muscle, and the muscle around it helps the lower esophageal sphincter stay closed."},{"pt":"Aortic hiatus at T12, aorta and thoracic duct","why":"It lies behind the diaphragm between the crura, and the thoracic duct climbs through it beside the aorta."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[100,102],"cids":["resp-tree","resp-histo"],"t":"The bronchial tree, in order","p":"Write the branching order of the bronchial tree from the trachea to the alveolus. Note where cartilage stops and where gas exchange starts.","k":[{"pt":"Trachea, carina, main (primary) bronchi","why":"The carina is the internal ridge where the trachea forks, at about the level of the sternal angle."},{"pt":"Lobar (secondary) bronchi, three on the right and two on the left","why":"There is one lobar bronchus for each lobe, so the count matches the lobes."},{"pt":"Segmental (tertiary) bronchi","why":"There is one for each bronchopulmonary segment."},{"pt":"Bronchioles, terminal bronchioles, respiratory bronchioles","why":"Bronchioles are the tubes under 1 mm across, and the terminal bronchiole is the last conducting tube."},{"pt":"Cartilage ends at the bronchioles, exchange starts at the respiratory bronchiole","why":"Without cartilage the bronchioles depend on smooth muscle, and the first alveoli in the wall mark the start of exchange."},{"pt":"Alveolar duct, alveolar sac, alveolus","why":"These finish the respiratory zone, ending at the air sac where gases cross into the blood."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L1-6","lv":1,"comps":[100],"cids":["resp-tree"],"t":"Trachea in cross section","p":"Draw the trachea cut across and label every layer and structure from the lumen outward.","k":[{"pt":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells lining the lumen","why":"Goblet cells make the mucus that traps particles, and the cilia sweep it up toward the pharynx."},{"pt":"C shaped rings of hyaline cartilage, 16 to 20 of them","why":"The rings keep the trachea from collapsing."},{"pt":"The open part of each C faces back","why":"The soft back wall lets the esophagus bulge forward a little when food passes."},{"pt":"Trachealis smooth muscle closing the gap posteriorly","why":"It bridges the open ends of each ring, so the back wall is muscle instead of cartilage."},{"pt":"Esophagus sitting just behind that gap","why":"The esophagus runs down the mediastinum behind the trachea, which is why the open part of the C faces it."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[100,102],"cids":["resp-tree","resp-histo"],"t":"Epithelium down the airway","p":"Write the epithelium at each level of the airway and note what changes as you go deeper. Then name the cells you find in an alveolus.","k":[{"pt":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells in the trachea and main bronchi","why":"Mucus from the goblet cells traps particles and the cilia move it up and out."},{"pt":"Fewer goblet cells in the smaller bronchi","why":"The mucus making cells thin out as the tubes narrow."},{"pt":"Simple columnar, then simple cuboidal in the bronchioles and terminal bronchioles","why":"The lining gets thinner and simpler as the tubes get smaller."},{"pt":"Simple squamous in the alveolus","why":"Thin, flat type I cells give the shortest path between air and blood."},{"pt":"Type I alveolar cell, type II surfactant cell, alveolar macrophage","why":"Type I cells are for exchange, type II cells make surfactant that keeps alveoli from collapsing, and macrophages clean up debris."},{"pt":"Cartilage disappears and smooth muscle takes a larger share of the wall","why":"Rings, then plates, then none, which is why the bronchioles can narrow so much when their muscle contracts."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[101,102],"cids":["resp-lungs-pleura","resp-histo"],"t":"The blood side of the lung","p":"Draw the pulmonary circulation as it reaches the lung tissue. Show the artery arriving, the capillary bed around an alveolus, and the vein leaving. Label what kind of blood each vessel carries.","k":[{"pt":"Pulmonary artery enters at the hilum carrying blood low in oxygen","why":"In the root of the lung, the pulmonary artery brings blood from the heart into the lung."},{"pt":"Pulmonary capillaries form a dense net wrapping each alveolus","why":"The net is fed by branches of the pulmonary arteries and holds blood right against the alveolar wall."},{"pt":"Respiratory membrane where a capillary presses against an alveolus","why":"This thin barrier of type I cell, fused basement membranes and endothelium is where gases cross."},{"pt":"Pulmonary veins leave carrying oxygen rich blood back to the heart","why":"The capillaries drain into the pulmonary veins, which are the lowest structures at the hilum."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[103],"cids":["resp-thoracic-diaphragm"],"t":"Muscles of breathing","p":"Name the muscles of quiet breathing and the muscles brought in for forced breathing. For each, say what it does to the size of the thoracic cavity.","k":[{"pt":"Quiet inspiration: diaphragm and external intercostals","why":"The diaphragm flattens and moves down to make the cavity taller, and the external intercostals lift the ribs."},{"pt":"External intercostal fibers run downward and forward (hands in pockets), and internal intercostals run at right angles to them","why":"The fiber direction is how you tell the two layers apart on the cadaver."},{"pt":"Quiet expiration is passive, elastic recoil","why":"The lungs and chest wall spring back on their own, so no muscle has to work."},{"pt":"Forced inspiration: scalenes, sternocleidomastoid, pectoralis minor","why":"These accessory muscles lift the rib cage higher than the main muscles can alone."},{"pt":"Forced expiration: internal intercostals, abdominal wall muscles","why":"The internal intercostals pull the ribs down, as in a hard cough or blowing out candles."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[101,104],"cids":["resp-lungs-pleura","resp-disorders"],"t":"Pleural recesses","p":"Draw the pleural cavity of one lung from the front and mark its lowest recess. Name it and write where fluid would collect in someone sitting upright, and what that condition is called.","k":[{"pt":"Parietal pleura lining the ribs, the top of the diaphragm and the mediastinum","why":"This outer layer lines the walls of the space, so the recess forms where it changes direction."},{"pt":"Visceral pleura on the lung, with the pleural cavity between the two layers","why":"The cavity is a thin space that normally holds only a film of fluid."},{"pt":"Costodiaphragmatic recess, where the parietal pleura turns from the ribs onto the diaphragm","why":"This low pocket is the lowest part of the pleural cavity."},{"pt":"Fluid pools in the costodiaphragmatic recess when upright","why":"Gravity carries extra fluid to the lowest point of the cavity."},{"pt":"Pleural effusion, extra fluid in the pleural cavity pressing on the lung","why":"The structure affected is the pleural cavity, not the lung tissue itself."},{"pt":"Only that side is affected","why":"The mediastinum keeps the right and left pleural cavities apart."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[100,101,104],"cids":["resp-tree","resp-lungs-pleura","resp-disorders"],"t":"The inhaled peanut","p":"A child inhales a peanut and it lodges in a bronchus. Trace the peanut from the mouth to where it stops, predict which lung it most likely ends up in and why, and say what part of the lung loses its air if it blocks a segmental bronchus.","k":[{"pt":"Mouth, oropharynx, laryngopharynx, then past the epiglottis into the larynx","why":"The epiglottis should cover the laryngeal inlet during a swallow, and the peanut slipped past it."},{"pt":"Through the glottis between the vocal folds into the trachea","why":"The glottis is the narrowest point in the larynx, so an object that gets past it can travel on down."},{"pt":"Down to the carina, which sets off a strong cough","why":"The lining over the carina is very sensitive, and coughing is the airway trying to clear the object."},{"pt":"Into the right main bronchus","why":"The right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left, so inhaled objects fall into it more easily."},{"pt":"Lobar bronchus, then segmental bronchus","why":"The peanut follows the branches until it is too big for the next one."},{"pt":"One bronchopulmonary segment loses its air","why":"Each segment is served by its own segmental bronchus, so blocking that bronchus cuts off that one segment."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[101,103,104],"cids":["resp-lungs-pleura","resp-thoracic-diaphragm","resp-disorders"],"t":"A puncture through the chest wall","p":"A knife wound goes between two ribs on the left side and into the pleural cavity. Name every layer the blade crosses from the muscle inward, predict what happens to the left lung, and explain why the right lung keeps working.","k":[{"pt":"External intercostal muscle, then internal intercostal muscle","why":"Two layers of intercostal muscle fill each space between the ribs, with the external layer on the outside."},{"pt":"Parietal pleura lining the inside of the chest wall","why":"This is the outer layer of the pleura, so piercing it opens the pleural cavity."},{"pt":"Air enters the pleural cavity: a pneumothorax","why":"The cavity is sealed and holds only a thin film of fluid, so a puncture lets air in."},{"pt":"The layers separate and the left lung collapses","why":"Pleural fluid normally holds the lung to the chest wall, and once air separates the layers the lung no longer follows the wall."},{"pt":"The right lung keeps working because it has its own pleural cavity","why":"The mediastinum keeps the right and left cavities apart, so air in one does not collapse the other lung."}]}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-vessels","title":"Lymph, lymphatic vessels, trunks and ducts","mw":"2026-10-28","tr":"2026-10-29","prompts":[{"id":"m4-lymph-vessels-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[105],"cids":["w4-lymph-pathway"],"t":"Which duct drains what","p":"Draw a body outline. Shade what the right lymphatic duct drains and label where each duct empties.","k":[{"pt":"Right lymphatic duct: upper right quadrant (right arm, right side of head, neck and thorax)","why":"This right upper quarter is the only part of the body the thoracic duct does not drain."},{"pt":"Thoracic duct: the rest of the body, about three quarters","why":"Both lower limbs, the abdomen and the whole left side above drain into it, which makes it the largest lymphatic vessel."},{"pt":"Thoracic duct begins at the cisterna chyli, in front of the upper lumbar vertebrae","why":"The lumbar and intestinal trunks empty into this sac, so lymph from the lower body starts its climb there."},{"pt":"Thoracic duct passes up through the diaphragm with the aorta","why":"It goes through the aortic hiatus at T12 and climbs the thorax beside the vertebral column."},{"pt":"Both empty where the subclavian and internal jugular veins meet, each on its own side","why":"The right duct empties on the right and the thoracic duct on the left, and there lymph becomes part of the blood plasma again."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-vessels-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[105],"cids":["w4-lymph-pathway"],"t":"The lymphatic trunks","p":"List the lymphatic trunks, say which ones come in pairs, and write the region each one drains and where it empties.","k":[{"pt":"Lumbar trunks, left and right: lower limbs, pelvis and abdominal wall","why":"They drain the lower body, so they empty into the cisterna chyli at the start of the thoracic duct."},{"pt":"Intestinal trunk, single: digestive organs of the abdomen","why":"It is the one unpaired trunk, and it also empties into the cisterna chyli, so chyle from the small intestine travels this way."},{"pt":"Bronchomediastinal trunks, left and right: thoracic organs and the thoracic wall","why":"Each trunk is named for the region it drains, which makes the names easy to match."},{"pt":"Subclavian trunks, left and right: upper limbs","why":"They drain the upper limbs, and each one empties into the duct on its own side."},{"pt":"Jugular trunks, left and right: head and neck","why":"They drain the head and neck, the same region the internal jugular veins drain."},{"pt":"Nine trunks in all; the right jugular, right subclavian and right bronchomediastinal go to the right lymphatic duct, and every other trunk goes to the thoracic duct","why":"Four pairs plus the single intestinal trunk makes nine, and the three right upper trunks match the right upper quarter the right duct drains."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-vessels-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[105,106],"cids":["w4-lymph-pathway","w4-lymph-vessels-node"],"t":"Lymph, start to finish","p":"Trace lymph from a tissue space back to the blood. Name every vessel, node and duct on the way.","k":[{"pt":"Interstitial fluid becomes lymph once it enters a lymphatic vessel","why":"The fluid left behind by leaky blood capillaries is interstitial fluid until a lymphatic capillary takes it in."},{"pt":"Lymphatic capillaries, then valved collecting vessels","why":"The blind ended capillaries take up the fluid, and the valves in the collecting vessels keep it moving one way."},{"pt":"Filtered through lymph nodes","why":"Nodes along the collecting vessels filter the lymph, usually several in a row, before it reaches the blood."},{"pt":"Trunks, then the right lymphatic duct or the thoracic duct","why":"Collecting vessels merge into trunks that each drain one region, and the trunks empty into one of the two ducts."},{"pt":"Back to the blood where the subclavian and internal jugular veins meet","why":"This is a one way system, not a loop, so lymph rejoins the blood near the heart instead of returning to the tissues."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-vessels-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[105,106],"cids":["w4-lymph-pathway","w4-lymph-vessels-node"],"t":"How lymph moves without a pump","p":"Lymph has no heart. Explain everything that keeps it moving toward the blood.","k":[{"pt":"Overlapping endothelial cells act as one way flaps in the capillaries","why":"Fluid pushes the flaps open to get in, and pressure inside presses them shut so it cannot flow back out."},{"pt":"Valves in the collecting vessels","why":"They let lymph move forward but not backward, and the stretches between them bulge to give a full vessel a beaded look."},{"pt":"Squeeze of nearby skeletal muscles","why":"Contracting muscles press on the vessels that run between them, which is the skeletal muscle pump."},{"pt":"Breathing movements","why":"Pressure changes in the thorax with each breath pull lymph upward, which is the respiratory pump."},{"pt":"Smooth muscle in the walls of the larger vessels and the pulsing of nearby arteries","why":"These add squeezes of their own, and the valves turn every squeeze into flow toward the heart."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-vessels-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[106],"cids":["w4-lymph-vessels-node"],"t":"Lymphatic capillary or blood capillary","p":"Draw a lymphatic capillary beside a blood capillary and compare them: their ends, their walls, what holds them open, what can get in, and which way fluid flows.","k":[{"pt":"Lymphatic capillary is blind ended; a blood capillary is open at both ends as part of a loop","why":"Lymph starts in the tissues and only flows away from them, while blood flows through from the artery side to the vein side."},{"pt":"Lymphatic wall of overlapping endothelial cells that act as flap valves; blood capillary cells joined more tightly","why":"The overlapping edges are what let fluid in and keep it from leaking back out."},{"pt":"Anchoring filaments hold a lymphatic capillary open; blood pressure holds a blood capillary open","why":"When the tissue swells the filaments pull on the cells, so the capillary opens wider instead of collapsing."},{"pt":"Fluid, proteins, cells, pathogens and even cancer cells get into lymphatic capillaries; mostly small molecules get into blood capillaries","why":"The large gaps make lymphatic capillaries more permeable, which is how cancer cells can travel in the lymph."},{"pt":"Lymphatic capillaries are wider","why":"Wider, blind ended, and leakier are the quickest ways to tell a lymphatic capillary from a blood capillary."},{"pt":"Lacteal named as the special lymphatic capillary in each villus","why":"Lacteals take in dietary fat, which is packaged in particles too large to enter blood capillaries."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-vessels-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[105,108],"cids":["w4-lymph-pathway","w4-lymphatic-disorders"],"t":"Swelling after surgery","p":"A patient has many lymph nodes removed from the right armpit during cancer surgery, and months later the right arm stays swollen. Name the disorder and the structure affected, then trace the route lymph from the right hand should take back to the blood, and name the node a surgeon checks first to see whether a cancer has begun to spread.","k":[{"pt":"Lymphedema, long term swelling from blocked, damaged or missing lymphatic vessels","why":"The vessels that passed through the removed nodes no longer drain the arm, so fluid stays in the tissue."},{"pt":"Lymphatic capillaries in the hand take up the fluid as lymph","why":"This first step still works, but the fluid has nowhere to go past the removed nodes."},{"pt":"Collecting vessels up the arm to the axillary nodes in the armpit","why":"Lymph from the arm normally passes through these nodes, so removing them breaks the route."},{"pt":"Right subclavian trunk","why":"The subclavian trunks drain the upper limbs."},{"pt":"Right lymphatic duct, into the junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins","why":"The right arm is in the right upper quarter, the region the right lymphatic duct drains."},{"pt":"Sentinel node named as the first node a region drains into","why":"Because lymph passes through nodes in order, checking that first node tells a surgeon whether a cancer has begun to travel."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-vessels-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[105,106,108],"cids":["w4-lymph-pathway","w4-lymph-vessels-node","w4-lymphatic-disorders"],"t":"An infected cut on the foot","p":"A cut on the sole of the left foot gets infected and a tender lump appears in the left groin. Name the lump, then trace lymph from the foot all the way to the blood, naming every vessel, trunk and duct.","k":[{"pt":"The lump is an enlarged inguinal lymph node, lymphadenopathy","why":"Nodes swell as they trap and fight pathogens, and the groin nodes filter the lymph coming up from the lower limb."},{"pt":"Lymphatic capillaries in the foot, then valved collecting vessels up the leg","why":"The infection's debris and bacteria get into the lymph easily because lymphatic capillaries are so permeable."},{"pt":"Into the inguinal nodes through afferent vessels and out at the hilum through efferent vessels","why":"More vessels enter a node than leave it, so lymph moves slowly through and the immune cells have time to work."},{"pt":"Left lumbar trunk to the cisterna chyli","why":"The lumbar trunks drain the lower limbs and empty into the sac at the start of the thoracic duct."},{"pt":"Thoracic duct up through the aortic hiatus and the thorax","why":"The left leg is outside the right upper quarter, so its lymph goes to the thoracic duct."},{"pt":"Into the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins","why":"The thoracic duct empties on the left side, where the lymph rejoins the blood."}]}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-organs","title":"Lymphatic organs and tissues","mw":"2026-10-28","tr":"2026-10-29","prompts":[{"id":"m4-lymph-organs-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[106],"cids":["w4-lymph-vessels-node"],"t":"A lymph node in section","p":"Draw one lymph node cut open and label everything you can.","k":[{"pt":"Capsule of dense connective tissue, with trabeculae reaching inward","why":"The trabeculae divide the node into compartments."},{"pt":"Cortex with rounded clusters of lymphocytes (lymphatic follicles), many with germinal centers","why":"The follicles of the outer cortex are mostly B cells, and the deep cortex is mostly T cells."},{"pt":"Medulla with medullary cords and medullary sinuses lined with macrophages","why":"The macrophages lining the sinuses engulf bacteria, debris and cancer cells as the lymph passes."},{"pt":"Afferent vessels entering through the capsule on the rounded surface","why":"There are several of them, entering at many points around the node."},{"pt":"Efferent vessels leaving at the hilum, the indented side where blood vessels also enter and leave","why":"There are fewer efferent than afferent vessels, so lymph backs up and moves slowly through the node."},{"pt":"Job: filters lymph and houses immune cells","why":"The slow flow gives the lymphocytes and macrophages time to check the lymph before it reaches the blood."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-organs-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[107,108,126],"cids":["w4-lymphatic-organs","w4-lymphatic-disorders","w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"],"t":"The spleen","p":"Draw the spleen in place and write everything you know about it.","k":[{"pt":"Largest lymphatic organ","why":"It is a secondary organ that checks the blood, the way the nodes check lymph."},{"pt":"Upper left abdomen against the diaphragm, to the left of and behind the stomach, under the lower left ribs","why":"The lower left ribs protect it, which matters because its tissue tears easily."},{"pt":"Splenic artery and splenic vein at its hilum","why":"The splenic artery is a branch of the celiac trunk, and the splenic vein runs behind the pancreas to help form the hepatic portal vein."},{"pt":"White pulp: islands of lymphocytes around central arteries, for immune functions","why":"This is the lymphatic tissue of the spleen, where it responds to pathogens in the blood."},{"pt":"Red pulp: most of the organ, filters blood and removes worn out red blood cells","why":"Its venous sinuses and cords are full of red blood cells and macrophages."},{"pt":"Rupture causes heavy internal bleeding","why":"It holds a large volume of blood, and an enlarged spleen ruptures even more easily."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-organs-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[107],"cids":["w4-lymphatic-organs"],"t":"Tonsils and MALT","p":"Map the lymphatic tissue that guards the body's openings.","k":[{"pt":"Palatine tonsils in the side walls of the oropharynx, between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches","why":"They are the largest tonsils and the ones usually infected and removed."},{"pt":"Lingual tonsils on the base of the tongue","why":"They face the oropharynx and guard swallowed material."},{"pt":"Pharyngeal tonsil (adenoid) on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx, and tubal tonsils around the auditory tube openings","why":"These sit in the air only part of the pharynx and meet what is breathed in."},{"pt":"Tonsils guard against inhaled and swallowed pathogens, with deep pits called crypts","why":"The crypts trap bacteria and debris so the lymphatic tissue can deal with them."},{"pt":"MALT guards the mucous membranes of the digestive, respiratory, urinary and reproductive tracts","why":"These are open tracts where pathogens can get in, so lymphatic tissue sits in their linings."},{"pt":"Peyer patches in the wall of the ileum, and the appendix off the cecum","why":"Along with the tonsils, these are the largest and best organized pieces of MALT."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-organs-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[107],"cids":["w4-lymphatic-organs"],"t":"Primary versus secondary organs","p":"Sort every lymphatic organ you can name into primary and secondary, and say where each one is.","k":[{"pt":"Primary: red bone marrow, in spongy bone, where all lymphocytes form and B cells mature","why":"It counts as primary because lymphocytes are made and matured there."},{"pt":"Primary: thymus, in the mediastinum above the heart and behind the sternum, where T cells mature","why":"T cell precursors leave the marrow and travel in the blood to the thymus to finish maturing."},{"pt":"Secondary: lymph nodes along the collecting vessels, clustered in the cervical, axillary and inguinal regions","why":"The nodes check the lymph."},{"pt":"Secondary: spleen in the upper left abdomen","why":"The spleen checks the blood."},{"pt":"Secondary: tonsils around the pharynx and MALT in the linings of open tracts","why":"These guard the places where pathogens enter through a mucous membrane."},{"pt":"Primary means made or matured; secondary means where mature lymphocytes meet invaders","why":"Only two organs make or mature lymphocytes, so everything else on the list is secondary."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-organs-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[108],"cids":["w4-lymphatic-disorders"],"t":"When drainage fails","p":"Name the lymphatic disorders you know and the structure each one affects.","k":[{"pt":"Edema: interstitial space","why":"Fluid leaks out of the blood capillaries faster than the lymphatic vessels can carry it away."},{"pt":"Lymphedema: lymphatic vessels, sometimes after node removal","why":"Blocked, damaged or missing vessels leave the region they drain swollen for the long term."},{"pt":"Lymphadenopathy: lymph nodes","why":"Nodes enlarge while fighting an infection or when cancer cells lodge in them."},{"pt":"Lymphoma: lymphatic tissue, usually the nodes","why":"Lymphocytes become cancerous, and the two main groups are Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma."},{"pt":"Splenomegaly: spleen","why":"It can happen with mononucleosis or leukemia, and an enlarged spleen ruptures more easily."},{"pt":"Tonsillitis: tonsils, usually the palatine tonsils","why":"The tonsils become infected, red and swollen, which is common in childhood."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-organs-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[107,108,126],"cids":["w4-lymphatic-organs","w4-lymphatic-disorders","w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"],"t":"A blow to the left side","p":"A cyclist crashes and breaks ribs on the lower left side, then shows signs of heavy internal bleeding. Name the organ most likely torn, describe where it sits, and explain from its structure why it bleeds so much.","k":[{"pt":"The spleen","why":"It sits right under the lower left ribs, so broken ribs there can tear it."},{"pt":"Upper left abdomen against the diaphragm, to the left of and behind the stomach","why":"This places it exactly under the injured ribs."},{"pt":"Soft tissue that tears easily and holds a large volume of blood","why":"That combination is why a ruptured spleen bleeds so heavily."},{"pt":"Red pulp makes up most of the organ, with venous sinuses and cords full of red blood cells","why":"Most of the spleen is blood filled tissue, so a tear opens many blood spaces at once."},{"pt":"Splenic artery and splenic vein at the hilum","why":"The splenic artery is one of the three branches of the celiac trunk, which comes straight off the aorta."},{"pt":"An enlarged spleen, as in mononucleosis, ruptures even more easily","why":"Splenomegaly makes the same blow more dangerous."}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-organs-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[107,108],"cids":["w4-lymphatic-organs","w4-lymphatic-disorders"],"t":"A sore throat and neck lumps","p":"A child has a red, swollen throat and tender lumps under the jaw on both sides. Name the structures that are inflamed and enlarged, say where each sits, and explain why the lumps showed up in the neck.","k":[{"pt":"Tonsillitis, usually of the palatine tonsils","why":"Tonsillitis is common in childhood and usually involves the palatine tonsils."},{"pt":"Palatine tonsils sit in the side walls of the oropharynx, between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches","why":"These are the tonsils you see when you look into an open mouth."},{"pt":"The neck lumps are enlarged cervical lymph nodes, lymphadenopathy","why":"Nodes swell while they trap and fight pathogens nearby, which is why a clinician feels the neck."},{"pt":"The cervical nodes filter lymph from the head and neck","why":"Lymph from the throat passes through the nearest nodes first, so those are the ones that swell."},{"pt":"Tonsils and lymph nodes are both secondary lymphoid organs","why":"They are where mature lymphocytes meet pathogens, not where lymphocytes are made."}]}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine","title":"Endocrine glands","mw":"2026-11-02","tr":"2026-11-03","prompts":[{"id":"m4-endocrine-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[128,132],"cids":["w6-endocrine-locate","w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"],"t":"Gland map","p":"List every endocrine gland or endocrine tissue you can and write where in the body it sits. Draw a rough body outline and place them on it.","k":[{"pt":"Hypothalamus, pituitary, pineal in the brain","why":"The hypothalamus forms the floor of the third ventricle, the pituitary hangs below it in the sella turcica, and the pineal projects from the back of the roof of the third ventricle."},{"pt":"Thyroid and parathyroids in the neck","why":"The thyroid sits below the larynx in front of the trachea, with the parathyroids on its back surface."},{"pt":"Thymus in the thorax","why":"It sits in the mediastinum, behind the sternum and above the heart."},{"pt":"Adrenals and pancreatic islets in the abdomen","why":"Each adrenal sits on top of a kidney, and the islets are scattered through the pancreas behind the stomach."},{"pt":"Ovaries in the pelvis, testes in the scrotum","why":"The gonads make gametes and also contain endocrine cells."},{"pt":"Organs with endocrine cells: heart, kidneys, skin, stomach and small intestine","why":"These organs have another main job, so they are not counted as endocrine glands, but their cells still release hormones into the blood."}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[128,129],"cids":["w6-endocrine-locate","w6-pituitary"],"t":"Hypothalamus and pituitary","p":"Draw the hypothalamus and pituitary and label the parts, including how each lobe connects to the brain. Then write what kind of tissue each lobe is made of.","k":[{"pt":"Pituitary in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, with the optic chiasm just above and in front","why":"The bony pocket protects the gland on its floor, front and back."},{"pt":"Infundibulum, the stalk from the hypothalamus down to the pituitary","why":"It is the physical link between brain and gland; nerve fibers run down through it and small vessels run down along it."},{"pt":"Anterior lobe (adenohypophysis), glandular epithelial tissue","why":"It grows up from the roof of the embryonic mouth, so it is gland tissue and not brain."},{"pt":"Posterior lobe (neurohypophysis), nervous tissue with axons and pituicytes","why":"It grows down from the floor of the brain and is an extension of the hypothalamus itself."},{"pt":"Hypophyseal portal system to the anterior lobe","why":"The anterior lobe has no nerve link, so blood vessels carry the hypothalamic signals to it."},{"pt":"Hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract down the infundibulum to the posterior lobe","why":"The posterior lobe is nervous tissue, so it connects by nerve fibers."}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[128,132],"cids":["w6-endocrine-locate","w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"],"t":"The other endocrine organs","p":"Draw and place the pineal gland, the thymus, and the gonads. For each, write where it sits and which part holds its endocrine cells, and note which one shrinks with age.","k":[{"pt":"Pineal: small cone shaped gland in the epithalamus, projecting from the back of the roof of the third ventricle","why":"Its position on the third ventricle is how you identify it."},{"pt":"Thymus: mediastinum, behind the sternum and above the heart","why":"This is the same spot the lymphatic chapter gives, because the thymus is also a primary lymphoid organ."},{"pt":"Thymus is large in childhood, shrinks after puberty, and is mostly fat in older adults","why":"This is the one in the set that shrinks with age."},{"pt":"Ovaries in the pelvic cavity on each side of the uterus; endocrine cells in the developing follicles and the corpus luteum","why":"Like the testes, the ovaries are mixed organs: they make gametes, and the cells of their follicles and the corpus luteum are the endocrine part."},{"pt":"Testes in the scrotum; endocrine interstitial (Leydig) cells between the seminiferous tubules","why":"The testes sit outside the abdominopelvic cavity, and their hormone cells fill the spaces between the tubules."}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[131],"cids":["w6-adrenal"],"t":"Adrenal in section","p":"Draw an adrenal gland cut in half. Label the capsule, the three cortical zones in order, and the medulla, with how the cells are arranged in each region.","k":[{"pt":"Capsule of connective tissue outside","why":"It covers the whole gland, and the zones are counted inward from it."},{"pt":"Zona glomerulosa: thin outer zone of rounded clusters and arches","why":"Glomerulus means a little ball, which describes the clusters."},{"pt":"Zona fasciculata: thick middle zone of long, straight columns of pale cells","why":"Fascicle means a bundle, and the cells look pale and foamy because they hold many lipid droplets."},{"pt":"Zona reticularis: thin inner zone of cells in a branching network, against the medulla","why":"Reticulum means a network, and these cells stain darker than the fasciculata."},{"pt":"Medulla: clusters of chromaffin cells around blood vessels","why":"Chromaffin cells are modified sympathetic neurons, so the medulla works like a sympathetic ganglion."},{"pt":"Sits on the superior pole of the kidney, behind the peritoneum","why":"It is retroperitoneal, enclosed with the kidney in fat and the renal fascia."}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[132],"cids":["w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"],"t":"Hidden endocrine tissue","p":"Name every organ that is not primarily endocrine but still contains hormone-secreting cells. Write the cells and where in the organ they sit.","k":[{"pt":"Kidney: endocrine cells in the renal cortex, including the juxtaglomerular cells in the walls of the afferent arterioles","why":"These cells are built into the walls of the small arteries entering the kidney's filters, so they sit right on its blood supply."},{"pt":"Heart: cardiac muscle cells in the walls of the atria","why":"The hormone cells are the atrial muscle cells themselves, not a separate gland."},{"pt":"Stomach and small intestine: enteroendocrine cells scattered in the mucosa","why":"They are scattered one by one in the lining, so the gut has no single gland you can point to."},{"pt":"Skin: keratinocytes of the epidermis, which begin making a hormone when exposed to sunlight","why":"Sunlight starts the process in the epidermis, which is why skin counts as an organ with endocrine tissue."},{"pt":"Liver hepatocytes and the adipocytes of adipose tissue","why":"Their main jobs are processing blood and storing fat, so they are not counted as endocrine glands even though their cells release hormones."}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[130,131,132],"cids":["w6-thyroid-parathyroid","w6-adrenal","w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"],"t":"Four glands, region by region","p":"For the thyroid, parathyroid, adrenal, and pancreas: describe the gross structure, name the regions or cell types inside it, and say how you would tell each one apart on a slide.","k":[{"pt":"Thyroid: right and left lobes joined by the isthmus; follicles of colloid lined by follicular cells, with parafollicular (C) cells between","why":"Rings of cells around pink colloid mean thyroid."},{"pt":"Parathyroids: usually four small glands on the back of the thyroid lobes; chief cells and oxyphil cells","why":"Packed cords of cells with no colloid mean parathyroid."},{"pt":"Adrenal: capsule, cortex in three zones (glomerulosa, fasciculata, reticularis), and a medulla of chromaffin cells","why":"Layered bands around a different looking core mean adrenal."},{"pt":"Pancreas: head, body and tail; acini make up most of it, with islets scattered through","why":"Pale round islets surrounded by darker acini mean pancreas."}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[124,132],"cids":["w5-pancreas","w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"],"t":"Two pancreases on one slide","p":"Draw a pancreas slide showing both tissue types. Label the exocrine and endocrine parts and write what each one releases and where it goes.","k":[{"pt":"Acini, clusters of gland cells around tiny ducts, the exocrine majority, about 99 percent","why":"Most of the pancreas is exocrine, so the acini fill most of the slide."},{"pt":"Acini make pancreatic juice with digestive enzymes, sent through ducts to the duodenum","why":"Exocrine glands release through a duct, and the main pancreatic duct runs the length of the gland to the ampulla."},{"pt":"Pancreatic islets (islets of Langerhans), pale round patches, the endocrine minority, about 1 percent","why":"On a stained slide the islets look pale against the darker acini around them."},{"pt":"Islet hormones go into the capillaries running through them, with no duct","why":"Endocrine glands are ductless and have a rich capillary supply."}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[128,129],"cids":["w6-endocrine-locate","w6-pituitary"],"t":"Hypothalamus at the center","p":"Build a mind map with the hypothalamus in the middle. Branch to every structure it connects to, and mark whether each link runs through blood vessels or down nerve fibers.","k":[{"pt":"Where it sits: the lowest part of the diencephalon, below the thalamus, forming the floor and lower walls of the third ventricle","why":"Its place at the base of the brain puts it directly above the pituitary."},{"pt":"Infundibulum, the stalk down to the pituitary","why":"Every link to the pituitary runs through or along this stalk."},{"pt":"Anterior pituitary through the hypophyseal portal system: primary capillary plexus, portal veins, secondary capillary plexus","why":"Signals picked up at the base of the hypothalamus ride down along the infundibulum and are delivered in a second capillary bed in the anterior lobe."},{"pt":"Posterior pituitary down the axons of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract","why":"The posterior lobe stores and releases what the hypothalamic neurons make and send down their axons."},{"pt":"Link type marked on every branch: blood vessels for the anterior lobe, nerve fibers for the posterior lobe","why":"The tissue of each lobe explains its link: gland tissue connects by blood, nervous tissue connects by nerve fibers."}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[128,129],"cids":["w6-endocrine-locate","w6-pituitary"],"t":"A tumor in the sella turcica","p":"A patient slowly loses peripheral vision, and a scan shows a pituitary tumor. Explain from the location of the pituitary why the tumor grows the way it does and why a change in vision can be the first sign.","k":[{"pt":"The pituitary sits in the sella turcica, a saddle shaped pocket in the body of the sphenoid bone","why":"Bone forms the floor and the front and back walls, so the gland is boxed in on most sides."},{"pt":"The tumor usually expands upward, out of the sella","why":"Upward is the direction not walled in by bone."},{"pt":"The optic chiasm lies just above and in front of the pituitary","why":"A tumor growing upward runs straight into it."},{"pt":"Pressure on the optic chiasm causes a loss of peripheral vision","why":"That vision change can be the first sign that a pea sized gland has enlarged."}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[128,130],"cids":["w6-endocrine-locate","w6-thyroid-parathyroid"],"t":"Thyroid surgery","p":"A surgeon is removing a large goiter from the front of the neck. Describe where the thyroid sits and its parts, name the small glands that must be spared and where they are, and say how a pathologist could tell the two tissues apart on a slide.","k":[{"pt":"Thyroid in the anterior neck below the thyroid cartilage, with its right and left lobes beside the larynx and trachea","why":"It sits just under the skin, which is why a goiter can be seen and felt and why surgery here is accessible."},{"pt":"Isthmus crossing the front of the upper tracheal rings, just below the cricoid cartilage","why":"It crosses the front of the trachea in the midline, so it lies right under the skin at the front of the neck."},{"pt":"Parathyroid glands, usually four, a superior and an inferior on the back of each lobe","why":"They sit on the posterior surface of the thyroid, right where the surgeon is working."},{"pt":"Each parathyroid has its own thin capsule, and their number and position vary","why":"That variation is why the surgeon has to look for them instead of assuming where they are."},{"pt":"Thyroid slide: follicles of pink colloid lined by follicular cells, with C cells between","why":"Colloid filled follicles are the mark of thyroid tissue."},{"pt":"Parathyroid slide: packed cords of small chief cells with larger dark pink oxyphil cells and no colloid","why":"No follicles and no colloid means the tissue is parathyroid."}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[128,131],"cids":["w6-endocrine-locate","w6-adrenal"],"t":"A mass found by accident","p":"A patient has a CT scan of the kidneys to look for a kidney stone, and the report mentions a small mass on top of the right kidney. Name the gland, describe where it sits and its shape on that side, and explain why kidney imaging found it.","k":[{"pt":"Adrenal (suprarenal) gland","why":"Suprarenal means above the kidney, which tells you where it is."},{"pt":"Sits on the superior pole of the kidney, on the posterior abdominal wall behind the peritoneum","why":"It is retroperitoneal like the kidney, the S in SADPUCKER."},{"pt":"Enclosed with the kidney in fat and the renal fascia","why":"Because the two share this wrapping, imaging aimed at the kidney also captures the adrenal."},{"pt":"The right adrenal is shaped like a pyramid, the left like a crescent","why":"The shape on each side helps you name which adrenal you are looking at."},{"pt":"Two glands in one: a cortex of glandular epithelium outside and a medulla of modified nervous tissue inside","why":"A mass could start in either part, and the two parts develop from different embryonic tissues, mesoderm and neural crest."}]}]},{"id":"m4-hip-thigh-muscles","title":"Gluteal and thigh muscles","mw":"2026-11-02","tr":"2026-11-03","prompts":[{"id":"m4-hip-thigh-muscles-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[91],"cids":["w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"],"t":"Anterior thigh","p":"Draw the anterior thigh and label every muscle. Write the action of each and name the group they belong to.","k":[{"pt":"Quadriceps: rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, vastus intermedius, extend the knee","why":"The four share one insertion, so they pull on the knee together."},{"pt":"Rectus femoris also flexes the hip","why":"It is the only quadriceps muscle that starts on the pelvis, at the anterior inferior iliac spine, so it crosses the hip as well as the knee."},{"pt":"Sartorius, from the anterior superior iliac spine to the medial upper tibia: flexes, abducts and laterally rotates the thigh and flexes the knee","why":"It runs from the hip bone down to the inner side of the knee, so it acts on both joints."},{"pt":"Iliopsoas (iliacus and psoas major) to the lesser trochanter, flexes the thigh at the hip","why":"The psoas major starts on vertebrae T12 to L5, so it can also flex the trunk when the thigh is fixed."},{"pt":"All quadriceps insert on the tibial tuberosity through the quadriceps tendon, patella and patellar ligament","why":"The patella sits inside this tendon, which is why it moves when the quadriceps contract."},{"pt":"Tensor fasciae latae at the side, pulling on the IT band","why":"The IT band is not a muscle; it is a thick band of fascia from the iliac crest to the lateral condyle of the tibia."}]},{"id":"m4-hip-thigh-muscles-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[91],"cids":["w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"],"t":"Posterior thigh","p":"Draw the posterior thigh and label the hamstring group plus any other muscle you can place. Write the two actions the group shares and where they attach proximally.","k":[{"pt":"Biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus","why":"These three are the hamstrings, the whole posterior compartment of the thigh."},{"pt":"All from the ischial tuberosity except the short head of biceps femoris, from the linea aspera","why":"Starting on the pelvis is what lets them act on the hip."},{"pt":"Extend the hip and flex the knee","why":"Each crosses the back of both the hip and the knee; the short head of the biceps femoris crosses only the knee."},{"pt":"Biceps femoris to the head of the fibula, semitendinosus to the medial upper tibia, semimembranosus to the back of the medial condyle of the tibia","why":"The biceps femoris goes to the lateral side and the other two go medial, which is how you sort them on the cadaver."},{"pt":"Adductor magnus: the part that starts on the ischial tuberosity, beside the hamstrings","why":"The part of the adductor magnus from the ischial tuberosity shares that origin and also extends the thigh."}]},{"id":"m4-hip-thigh-muscles-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[91],"cids":["w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"],"t":"Medial thigh","p":"Draw the medial thigh and label every muscle in the adductor group. Write where each one starts and ends and the action they share.","k":[{"pt":"Adductor longus, from the body of the pubis near the pubic symphysis to the middle of the linea aspera","why":"It forms the medial border of the femoral triangle, so it is a key landmark in the groin."},{"pt":"Adductor brevis, from the body and inferior ramus of the pubis to the upper linea aspera","why":"Brevis means short, and it ends higher on the femur than the longus."},{"pt":"Adductor magnus, from the inferior ramus of the pubis, the ramus of the ischium and the ischial tuberosity to the linea aspera and adductor tubercle","why":"Its part from the ischial tuberosity also extends the thigh, and the femoral artery passes through an opening in it, the adductor hiatus."},{"pt":"Gracilis, from the body and inferior ramus of the pubis to the medial upper tibia","why":"It is the one adductor that crosses the knee, so it also flexes the knee."},{"pt":"Pectineus, from the superior ramus of the pubis to just below the lesser trochanter","why":"It sits in the medial compartment and adducts and flexes the thigh."},{"pt":"Shared action: adduct the thigh","why":"They all run from the pubis or ischium to the femur, or to the tibia for the gracilis, so they pull the thigh toward the midline."}]},{"id":"m4-hip-thigh-muscles-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[91],"cids":["w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"],"t":"Three compartments of the thigh","p":"Sort the quadriceps, sartorius, the adductor group, and the hamstrings into the anterior, medial, or posterior compartment of the thigh, and write the main action each group shares.","k":[{"pt":"Anterior: rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus intermedius, vastus medialis, sartorius","why":"These sit on the front of the femur."},{"pt":"The quadriceps extend the knee; the rectus femoris and sartorius also flex the hip, and the sartorius flexes the knee","why":"Those two start on the pelvis, so they cross the hip as well, and the sartorius is the one anterior muscle that bends the knee instead of straightening it."},{"pt":"Medial: adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, gracilis, pectineus","why":"These run from the pubis and ischium to the inner thigh."},{"pt":"Medial group adducts the thigh","why":"Pulling from the pelvis to the inner femur draws the thigh toward the midline."},{"pt":"Posterior: biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus","why":"These are the hamstrings, starting at the ischial tuberosity."},{"pt":"Posterior group extends the hip and flexes the knee","why":"That is the opposite of the quadriceps at the knee and of the hip flexors at the hip."}]},{"id":"m4-hip-thigh-muscles-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[91],"cids":["w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"],"t":"Three muscles, one insertion","p":"The sartorius, gracilis and semitendinosus all end on the medial surface of the upper tibia. Compare them: which compartment each sits in, where each one starts, and the one action at the knee they all share.","k":[{"pt":"All three insert on the medial upper tibia at the pes anserinus","why":"Muscles from three different compartments meet at one spot on the inside of the knee."},{"pt":"Sartorius: anterior compartment, from the anterior superior iliac spine","why":"It starts at the front of the hip bone and crosses the thigh to the inner knee."},{"pt":"Gracilis: medial compartment, from the body and inferior ramus of the pubis","why":"It is the adductor that reaches past the knee."},{"pt":"Semitendinosus: posterior compartment, from the ischial tuberosity","why":"It is a hamstring, so it shares the hamstring origin."},{"pt":"All three flex the knee","why":"Each one crosses the knee to reach the tibia, and knee flexion is the action they have in common."},{"pt":"Gracilis and semitendinosus medially rotate the leg; sartorius laterally rotates the thigh","why":"Their other actions differ because they start in three different places."}]},{"id":"m4-hip-thigh-muscles-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[91],"cids":["w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"],"t":"The gluteal muscles and the IT band","p":"Compare the gluteus maximus, gluteus medius and tensor fasciae latae: where each starts and ends, its main action at the hip, and which two of them pull on the IT band.","k":[{"pt":"Gluteus maximus: posterior ilium, sacrum and coccyx to the gluteal tuberosity and the IT band","why":"It is the large muscle of the buttock."},{"pt":"Gluteus maximus is the main hip extensor, used for climbing and rising from a chair, and it laterally rotates the thigh","why":"Its fibers run from the back of the pelvis to the femur, so they pull the thigh back."},{"pt":"Gluteus medius: outer surface of the ilium to the greater trochanter","why":"It sits on the side of the pelvis, above the hip joint."},{"pt":"Gluteus medius abducts and medially rotates the thigh and keeps the pelvis level when the opposite foot is off the ground","why":"Pulling from the side of the ilium on the greater trochanter holds the pelvis steady over the standing leg."},{"pt":"Tensor fasciae latae: anterior iliac crest and anterior superior iliac spine to the IT band; flexes, abducts and medially rotates the thigh","why":"It works at the front and side of the hip."},{"pt":"Gluteus maximus and tensor fasciae latae both pull on the IT band","why":"The band runs down to the lateral condyle of the tibia, and pulling on it steadies the knee."}]},{"id":"m4-hip-thigh-muscles-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[91,127],"cids":["w5-lab-thigh-gluteal","w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"],"t":"Gluteal region","p":"Draw the gluteal region and label the muscles from superficial to deep. Then mark the safe quadrant for an intramuscular injection and say what you are avoiding.","k":[{"pt":"Gluteus maximus, medius, minimus","why":"The gluteus maximus is the large muscle of the buttock, and the medius and minimus lie deeper on the outer ilium."},{"pt":"Piriformis deep to the gluteus maximus, with the sciatic nerve usually coming out below it","why":"The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, usually below the piriformis."},{"pt":"Upper outer quadrant is the safe zone","why":"Staying high and lateral keeps the needle away from the nerve."},{"pt":"Avoiding the sciatic nerve, which runs through the lower, medial part of the buttock deep to the gluteus maximus","why":"It is the largest nerve in the body, and a needle placed low and toward the middle can hit it."},{"pt":"The ventrogluteal site on the side of the hip, which many clinicians now prefer","why":"It keeps the needle away from the sciatic nerve for the same reason."}]},{"id":"m4-hip-thigh-muscles-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[91],"cids":["w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"],"t":"The dropping hip","p":"A patient stands on the right foot and lifts the left foot, and the left side of the pelvis drops. Name the weak muscle and its side, give its origin and insertion, and explain why it is the muscle that keeps the pelvis level.","k":[{"pt":"The right gluteus medius is weak","why":"The gluteus medius on the standing side keeps the pelvis level when the opposite foot is off the ground."},{"pt":"Origin: outer (lateral) surface of the ilium","why":"It starts on the side of the pelvis above the hip joint."},{"pt":"Insertion: greater trochanter of the femur","why":"It ends on the bump at the top of the femur on the standing leg."},{"pt":"Action: abducts and medially rotates the thigh","why":"Pulling between the ilium and the greater trochanter of the standing leg holds the pelvis from tipping toward the lifted side."},{"pt":"Tensor fasciae latae is a second abductor on the same side","why":"It runs from the anterior iliac crest to the IT band and also abducts the thigh."}]},{"id":"m4-hip-thigh-muscles-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[91],"cids":["w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"],"t":"A pulled hamstring","p":"A sprinter feels a sharp pull at the back of the thigh just below the buttock. Name the muscles that start at that spot, the two joints they cross, the two movements that will hurt against resistance, and the one hamstring part that does not start there.","k":[{"pt":"The spot is the ischial tuberosity","why":"It is the bony point under the buttock where the hamstrings arise."},{"pt":"Long head of biceps femoris, semitendinosus and semimembranosus","why":"All three start on the ischial tuberosity."},{"pt":"They cross both the hip and the knee","why":"Starting on the pelvis and ending on the tibia or fibula means they span two joints."},{"pt":"Extending the hip and flexing the knee against resistance will hurt","why":"Those are the two actions the hamstrings share, so using them loads the injured muscle."},{"pt":"Short head of biceps femoris starts on the linea aspera of the femur","why":"It crosses only the knee, so it does not act on the hip."}]}]},{"id":"m4-leg-muscles","title":"Leg and foot muscles","mw":"2026-11-02","tr":"2026-11-03","prompts":[{"id":"m4-leg-muscles-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[92],"cids":["w5-lab-leg-muscles"],"t":"Front and outer leg","p":"Draw the leg from the front and side. Label the muscles of the anterior compartment and the lateral compartment, with where each one ends.","k":[{"pt":"Tibialis anterior, to the medial cuneiform and the base of metatarsal 1","why":"It runs down beside the tibia to the inner side of the foot."},{"pt":"Extensor hallucis longus, to the distal phalanx of the great toe","why":"Hallucis means great toe, so the name tells you where it ends."},{"pt":"Extensor digitorum longus, to the middle and distal phalanges of toes 2 to 5","why":"Digitorum means toes, here the four smaller toes."},{"pt":"Fibularis longus, by a tendon that crosses the sole to the medial cuneiform and the base of metatarsal 1","why":"Its tendon wraps under the foot, which is how it helps support the arches."},{"pt":"Fibularis brevis, to the base of metatarsal 5","why":"Brevis is the shorter one; it starts lower on the fibula and ends on the outer side of the foot."},{"pt":"Anterior compartment muscles start on the tibia, fibula and interosseous membrane; lateral compartment muscles start on the fibula","why":"The lateral compartment lies along the fibula, which is where both fibularis muscles begin."}]},{"id":"m4-leg-muscles-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[92],"cids":["w5-lab-leg-muscles"],"t":"The calf","p":"Draw the posterior leg and label every muscle in the posterior compartment. Write where each one ends and circle the ones that reach the calcaneus.","k":[{"pt":"Gastrocnemius, from the medial and lateral condyles of the femur to the calcaneus by the calcaneal tendon","why":"Starting above the knee lets it flex the knee as well as plantar flex the foot."},{"pt":"Soleus, from the head of the fibula and the soleal line of the tibia to the calcaneus by the calcaneal tendon","why":"It starts below the knee, so it acts only at the ankle."},{"pt":"Plantaris, from the femur just above the lateral condyle to the calcaneus beside the calcaneal tendon","why":"It is the third muscle that reaches the calcaneus, and it only weakly helps the other two."},{"pt":"Tibialis posterior to the navicular, plus the cuneiforms, cuboid and bases of metatarsals 2 to 4","why":"Its broad insertion under the foot lets it invert and plantar flex the foot."},{"pt":"Flexor digitorum longus to the distal phalanges of toes 2 to 5","why":"It flexes the four smaller toes."},{"pt":"Flexor hallucis longus to the distal phalanx of the great toe","why":"Hallucis means great toe, so the name tells you where it ends."}]},{"id":"m4-leg-muscles-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[92],"cids":["w5-lab-leg-muscles"],"t":"Three compartments of the leg","p":"Draw the lower leg in cross section. Label the anterior, lateral, and posterior compartments, name the muscles in each, and write the shared action of each group.","k":[{"pt":"Anterior: tibialis anterior, extensor digitorum longus, extensor hallucis longus, dorsiflex","why":"They sit in front of the ankle, so they lift the top of the foot."},{"pt":"Lateral: fibularis longus and brevis, evert","why":"Both also plantar flex the foot."},{"pt":"Posterior: gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris, plantarflex","why":"These three end on or beside the calcaneus, so they pull the heel up."},{"pt":"Posterior: tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus","why":"These also plantar flex and invert the foot, and the two long flexors bend the toes."},{"pt":"Interosseous membrane between the tibia and fibula","why":"The tibialis anterior in front and the tibialis posterior behind both start on it, so it sits between the anterior and posterior compartments."}]},{"id":"m4-leg-muscles-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[92],"cids":["w5-lab-leg-muscles"],"t":"Two muscles, one landing spot","p":"The tibialis anterior and the fibularis longus both end on the medial cuneiform and the base of metatarsal 1. Compare their compartments, their starting points, and their actions, and explain how one insertion can produce opposite movements.","k":[{"pt":"Tibialis anterior: anterior compartment, from the lateral condyle and upper lateral tibia and the interosseous membrane","why":"It runs down the front of the leg."},{"pt":"Fibularis longus: lateral compartment, from the head and upper lateral fibula","why":"It runs down the outer side of the leg."},{"pt":"Tibialis anterior dorsiflexes and inverts the foot","why":"It pulls the inner edge of the foot up."},{"pt":"Fibularis longus everts and plantar flexes the foot","why":"It pulls the inner edge of the foot down from underneath."},{"pt":"They reach the same bones from opposite sides: the tibialis anterior from the front and inner side, the fibularis longus by a tendon crossing under the sole","why":"Pulling on the same spot from opposite directions produces opposite movements."}]},{"id":"m4-leg-muscles-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[92],"cids":["w5-lab-leg-muscles"],"t":"A torn Achilles tendon","p":"A basketball player hears a pop at the back of the ankle and cannot push off or rise onto the toes. Name the tendon that tore, the muscles that lose their pull, the movement that is weak, and which of those muscles also acts at the knee.","k":[{"pt":"Calcaneal (Achilles) tendon, attaching to the calcaneus","why":"It is the thick tendon at the back of the ankle."},{"pt":"Gastrocnemius and soleus join to form it","why":"Both end on the calcaneus through this one tendon, so one tear disconnects both."},{"pt":"Plantar flexion is weak","why":"Rising onto the toes and pushing off are plantar flexion, the shared action of the calf muscles."},{"pt":"Gastrocnemius also flexes the knee","why":"It starts on the femoral condyles above the knee, unlike the soleus."},{"pt":"Tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus still help plantar flex","why":"Their tendons do not use the calcaneal tendon, so some weak plantar flexion can remain."}]},{"id":"m4-leg-muscles-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[92],"cids":["w5-lab-leg-muscles"],"t":"A rolled ankle","p":"A runner rolls the ankle so the sole turns inward. Name the muscles that normally pull the foot the opposite way, their compartment and attachments, and the muscles that turn the sole inward.","k":[{"pt":"The sole turning inward is inversion; the opposite movement is eversion","why":"Naming the movement first tells you which muscle group to look for."},{"pt":"Fibularis longus and fibularis brevis evert the foot","why":"They are the evertors on your list."},{"pt":"They sit in the lateral compartment along the fibula","why":"Their position on the outer leg is what lets them lift the outer edge of the foot."},{"pt":"Fibularis brevis ends on the base of metatarsal 5; fibularis longus crosses the sole to the medial cuneiform and the base of metatarsal 1","why":"Both tendons pass the outer side of the ankle, the side that was stretched."},{"pt":"Inverters: tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior, helped by the long toe flexors","why":"These pull from the inner side, the direction the ankle rolled."}]}]},{"id":"m4-lower-limb-nav","title":"Nerves and vessels of the lower limb","mw":"2026-11-02","tr":"2026-11-03","prompts":[{"id":"m4-lower-limb-nav-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[127],"cids":["w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"],"t":"The femoral triangle","p":"Draw the femoral triangle and label its three borders. Then name the contents from lateral to medial.","k":[{"pt":"Superior border: inguinal ligament","why":"The femoral vessels and nerve pass under this ligament to enter the thigh."},{"pt":"Lateral: sartorius. Medial: adductor longus","why":"These two muscles meet below to close the triangle."},{"pt":"Femoral nerve most lateral","why":"It enters the thigh under the inguinal ligament on the lateral side of the femoral artery."},{"pt":"Femoral artery in the middle, its pulse felt about halfway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis","why":"This is where the external iliac artery has just become the femoral artery."},{"pt":"Femoral vein medial to the artery, then lymphatics","why":"In the triangle the vein always lies on the medial side of the artery."},{"pt":"NAVEL: Nerve, Artery, Vein, Empty space, Lymphatics","why":"It is the order from lateral to medial, so the vein always lies medial to the artery."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-limb-nav-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[127,191],"cids":["w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves","w8-nerve-plexuses"],"t":"Nerves of the lower limb","p":"List the femoral, obturator, sciatic, tibial and common fibular nerves. For each, write the plexus it comes from, its spinal nerves, and where you find it on the cadaver.","k":[{"pt":"Lumbar plexus forms inside the psoas major from L1 to L4","why":"That is why its branches appear coming out of the psoas on the posterior abdominal wall."},{"pt":"Femoral nerve: lumbar plexus, L2 to L4; comes out of the lateral side of the psoas major and passes under the inguinal ligament lateral to the femoral artery","why":"It is the largest branch of the lumbar plexus and the lateral structure in the femoral triangle."},{"pt":"Obturator nerve: lumbar plexus, L2 to L4; comes out of the medial side of the psoas major and runs to the obturator foramen","why":"It comes from the same spinal nerves as the femoral nerve but leaves the psoas on the opposite side."},{"pt":"Sciatic nerve: sacral plexus, L4 to S3; through the greater sciatic foramen, deep to the gluteus maximus, then deep to the hamstrings","why":"It is the largest nerve in the body."},{"pt":"Tibial nerve: branch of the sciatic; straight down the back of the knee, deep to the calf muscles, behind the medial malleolus","why":"It runs beside the posterior tibial artery all the way down."},{"pt":"Common fibular nerve: branch of the sciatic; along the biceps femoris tendon and around the neck of the fibula","why":"It lies just under the skin at the fibular neck, where it divides."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-limb-nav-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[127],"cids":["w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"],"t":"Veins of the lower limb","p":"Draw the lower limb and trace its main veins: the deep veins that run with the arteries and the two superficial saphenous veins. Label where each one ends.","k":[{"pt":"Anterior and posterior tibial veins join to form the popliteal vein","why":"Deep veins run beside the arteries and share their names."},{"pt":"Popliteal vein becomes the femoral vein","why":"It changes name as it moves up from behind the knee into the thigh, the same way the artery does."},{"pt":"Femoral vein lies medial to the femoral artery in the femoral triangle and becomes the external iliac vein at the inguinal ligament","why":"The deep veins carry most of the blood out of the lower limb."},{"pt":"Great saphenous vein from the medial side of the top of the foot, in front of the medial malleolus, up the medial leg and thigh","why":"It is the longest vein in the body and runs in the fat under the skin, which is why surgeons can take it for bypass grafts."},{"pt":"Great saphenous vein empties into the femoral vein just below the inguinal ligament","why":"It passes through an opening in the deep fascia to reach the deep vein."},{"pt":"Small saphenous vein up the back of the calf into the popliteal vein","why":"It is the smaller superficial vein, ending behind the knee."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-limb-nav-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[127,191],"cids":["w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves","w8-nerve-plexuses"],"t":"The sciatic nerve","p":"Trace the sciatic nerve from where it forms to where it divides. Name its two divisions and write the route each one takes.","k":[{"pt":"From the sacral plexus, L4 to S3","why":"The sacral plexus serves the buttock, the back of the thigh, and most of the leg and foot."},{"pt":"Exits through the greater sciatic foramen, usually below piriformis, deep to the gluteus maximus","why":"Its path through the lower, medial buttock is why injections are given high and lateral."},{"pt":"Runs down the posterior thigh deep to the hamstrings","why":"On the cadaver you find it by moving the hamstrings aside."},{"pt":"Splits into tibial and common fibular nerves, usually just above the knee","why":"The split is the point where the sciatic nerve ends."},{"pt":"Tibial nerve: straight down the back of the knee and posterior leg beside the posterior tibial artery, then behind the medial malleolus into the sole","why":"It continues the straight line of the sciatic nerve."},{"pt":"Common fibular nerve: along the biceps femoris tendon to the lateral knee, around the neck of the fibula, then into superficial and deep branches","why":"It swings to the outside of the leg, which puts it just under the skin at the fibular neck."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-limb-nav-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[126,127],"cids":["w5-lab-abdominal-vessels","w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"],"t":"One artery, many names","p":"Trace the main artery of the lower limb from the common iliac artery to the foot. Write each name in order and the landmark where the name changes.","k":[{"pt":"Common iliac divides into the internal and external iliac in front of the sacroiliac joint","why":"The internal iliac dives into the pelvis, and the external iliac heads for the thigh along the psoas major."},{"pt":"External iliac becomes the femoral artery as it passes under the inguinal ligament","why":"The ligament is the landmark that changes the name."},{"pt":"Femoral artery runs through the femoral triangle, gives off the deep femoral artery to most thigh muscles, then runs under the sartorius","why":"The deep femoral branch is how most of the thigh gets its blood."},{"pt":"Femoral becomes popliteal after passing through the adductor hiatus in the adductor magnus","why":"Going through that opening takes the artery from the front of the thigh to the back of the knee."},{"pt":"Popliteal divides just below the knee into the anterior and posterior tibial arteries","why":"The posterior tibial is the larger of the two branches."},{"pt":"Anterior tibial becomes the dorsalis pedis on the top of the foot; posterior tibial passes behind the medial malleolus and divides into plantar arteries in the sole","why":"One branch serves the top of the foot and the other serves the sole."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-limb-nav-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[92,127],"cids":["w5-lab-leg-muscles","w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"],"t":"A cast that is too tight","p":"A patient in a tight leg cast says it presses hard just below the outer side of the knee, and now the toes catch on the floor when walking. Name the nerve at risk and where it runs, name the problem, and say which movement of the foot is weak.","k":[{"pt":"Common fibular nerve","why":"It wraps around the neck of the fibula just under the skin, right where the cast presses."},{"pt":"It comes from the sciatic nerve and follows the biceps femoris tendon to the lateral side of the knee","why":"Its route takes it to the outside of the knee, where it has little padding."},{"pt":"It divides there into superficial and deep branches","why":"Pressure at the fibular neck catches the nerve right where it divides, so both branches can be affected."},{"pt":"Foot drop","why":"The muscles that lift the foot are weakened, so the toes drag."},{"pt":"Dorsiflexion is weak","why":"Lifting the foot and toes is the job of the anterior compartment muscles: tibialis anterior and the long toe extensors."}]},{"id":"m4-lower-limb-nav-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[127],"cids":["w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"],"t":"Checking the pulses in a leg","p":"After surgery on the leg, a nurse checks blood flow at the groin and at the ankle. Name the artery felt at each spot and the landmark used, then trace how blood gets from the first spot to the second.","k":[{"pt":"Femoral artery in the groin, about halfway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis","why":"Here it has just passed under the inguinal ligament and lies in the femoral triangle."},{"pt":"Posterior tibial artery behind the medial malleolus","why":"At the ankle it passes behind the medial malleolus on its way into the sole."},{"pt":"Femoral artery runs down under the sartorius and through the adductor hiatus","why":"This takes it from the front of the thigh to the back of the knee."},{"pt":"It becomes the popliteal artery behind the knee","why":"The name changes once it passes through the adductor magnus."},{"pt":"Popliteal divides below the knee, and the posterior tibial branch runs down the back of the leg deep to the calf muscles with the tibial nerve","why":"It is the larger branch, and it reaches the ankle on the medial side."}]}]},{"id":"m4-gi-wall","title":"Digestive tract wall and peritoneum","mw":"2026-11-04","tr":"2026-11-10","prompts":[{"id":"m4-gi-wall-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[109,110],"cids":["w5-canal-wall","w5-enteric-plexuses"],"t":"The four layers of the gut wall","p":"Draw the GI wall in cross section and name the four layers from the lumen outward. Write what is inside each layer.","k":[{"pt":"Mucosa: epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae","why":"The epithelium touches the food, the lamina propria holds capillaries and lymphatic tissue, and the muscularis mucosae is a thin layer of smooth muscle."},{"pt":"Submucosa: vessels, glands, submucosal (Meissner) plexus","why":"This areolar connective tissue (OpenStax describes it as dense connective tissue) carries the larger blood and lymphatic vessels."},{"pt":"Muscularis externa: circular and longitudinal layers, myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","why":"The plexus sits between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers it controls."},{"pt":"Serosa or adventitia depending on location","why":"The serosa is visceral peritoneum and covers the tube in the abdominal cavity; the esophagus above the diaphragm has a fibrous adventitia instead."},{"pt":"Main tissue of each layer: epithelium, areolar connective tissue (OpenStax: dense), smooth muscle, mesothelium","why":"Knowing the main tissue of each layer is the quickest way to tell the layers apart on a slide."}]},{"id":"m4-gi-wall-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[111,112],"cids":["w5-peritoneum-folds","w5-retroperitoneal"],"t":"Peritoneum and what sits behind it","p":"Name every peritoneal fold, mesentery, and omentum you can. Then list the retroperitoneal structures.","k":[{"pt":"Parietal and visceral peritoneum, peritoneal cavity","why":"The parietal layer lines the walls, the visceral layer covers the organs, and the cavity between them holds a little serous fluid."},{"pt":"Greater and lesser omentum, mesentery proper, mesocolon, falciform ligament","why":"These are the five named folds, each a double layer of peritoneum."},{"pt":"Retroperitoneal: suprarenal glands, aorta and IVC, duodenum, pancreas, ureters, colon (ascending and descending), kidneys, esophagus (the short part in the abdomen), rectum","why":"SADPUCKER gives you the list in order."},{"pt":"Duodenum except its first part, and pancreas except the tip of its tail","why":"These two are the exceptions inside the list, because a small part of each is covered by peritoneum."},{"pt":"Retroperitoneal means behind the parietal peritoneum, against the posterior abdominal wall, with peritoneum on the front only","why":"Intraperitoneal organs hang on a fold and can move, while retroperitoneal organs have no mesentery and are held in place."}]},{"id":"m4-gi-wall-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[110],"cids":["w5-enteric-plexuses"],"t":"The gut has its own nerves","p":"Draw the gut wall and mark the two nerve plexuses. Name each, write which layer it sits in, and say what it controls.","k":[{"pt":"Submucosal or Meissner plexus, in the submucosa","why":"Sub mucosal tells you the layer: it sits just deep to the mucosa."},{"pt":"It controls secretion and local blood flow","why":"Its position right next to the lining matches the glands and blood flow of the mucosa it controls."},{"pt":"Myenteric or Auerbach plexus, between the two muscularis layers","why":"Myo means muscle, so it sits in the muscularis, between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers."},{"pt":"It controls motility","why":"It runs the contractions of the two muscle layers it sits between, which mix food and move it along."},{"pt":"Together called the enteric nervous system","why":"The two plexuses run the whole length of the tube inside its wall."},{"pt":"Runs on its own but is modulated by the vagus and sympathetics","why":"Parasympathetic fibers, mostly from the vagus nerve, and sympathetic fibers reach the wall and adjust what the plexuses do."}]},{"id":"m4-gi-wall-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[111],"cids":["w5-peritoneum-folds"],"t":"Mesenteries and omenta","p":"Draw the abdomen in sagittal section and label every peritoneal fold. Name what each one connects and what runs inside it.","k":[{"pt":"Greater omentum hanging from the greater curvature","why":"It drapes in front of the intestines and folds back up to attach to the transverse colon, a fatty apron with many lymph nodes."},{"pt":"Lesser omentum from the lesser curvature to the liver","why":"Its free edge carries the hepatic artery, the hepatic portal vein and the common bile duct."},{"pt":"Mesentery proper suspending the small intestine","why":"It fans out from the posterior wall to hold the jejunum and ileum."},{"pt":"Transverse and sigmoid mesocolon","why":"Only the two parts of the colon with a mesocolon are intraperitoneal."},{"pt":"Vessels, nerves, and lymphatics running between the two layers","why":"Each fold is a double layer, and the space between the layers is the route vessels and nerves take to the organs."},{"pt":"Falciform ligament with the round ligament in its edge","why":"It attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm, and the round ligament is the remnant of the fetal umbilical vein."}]},{"id":"m4-gi-wall-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[111,112],"cids":["w5-peritoneum-folds","w5-retroperitoneal"],"t":"Behind or in front","p":"For the liver, pancreas, and duodenum, write whether each is intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal, and name the peritoneal structure attached to it.","k":[{"pt":"Liver intraperitoneal, with the falciform ligament on its front and the lesser omentum on its underside","why":"The liver is wrapped in peritoneum and held by these folds rather than fixed to the back wall."},{"pt":"Pancreas retroperitoneal except the tip of its tail","why":"It lies across the posterior wall behind the stomach, with peritoneum only on its front."},{"pt":"Duodenum retroperitoneal after the first part","why":"It is fixed to the posterior wall in a C around the head of the pancreas."},{"pt":"The lesser omentum attaches to the first part of the duodenum","why":"That first part is the one piece of the duodenum that is not retroperitoneal."},{"pt":"The duodenum and pancreas are on the SADPUCKER list and the liver is not","why":"The D and the P in SADPUCKER are the duodenum and the pancreas, while the liver hangs from its folds."}]},{"id":"m4-gi-wall-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[109,117,119],"cids":["w5-canal-wall","w5-large-intestine","w5-canal-disorders"],"t":"A tumor in the colon wall","p":"A tumor that started in the lining of the transverse colon has grown through the muscularis externa. Name, in order, every layer it has already crossed and the next one it will reach, then say where it can spread once it breaks through.","k":[{"pt":"It began in the mucosa: epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae","why":"Colorectal cancer starts in the cells of the lining, usually as a small growth called a polyp."},{"pt":"Then the submucosa","why":"This layer carries the larger blood and lymphatic vessels, which gives the tumor a route to spread."},{"pt":"Then the inner circular layer of the muscularis","why":"The muscularis has two layers in the colon, and the circular one comes first from the inside."},{"pt":"Then the outer longitudinal layer, gathered into the teniae coli","why":"The large intestine bunches this layer into three bands instead of a complete sheet."},{"pt":"Next it reaches the serosa, the visceral peritoneum","why":"The transverse colon hangs on its mesocolon and is intraperitoneal, so its outer layer is serosa, not adventitia."},{"pt":"Through the serosa lies the peritoneal cavity","why":"Breaking through the outer layer opens the way into the space between the peritoneal layers."}]},{"id":"m4-gi-wall-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[111,112,124],"cids":["w5-peritoneum-folds","w5-retroperitoneal","w5-pancreas"],"t":"Opening the abdomen","p":"A surgeon opens the front abdominal wall to reach the pancreas. Name the peritoneal layers and folds met on the way in, which ones have to be moved, and explain why the pancreas is behind all of them.","k":[{"pt":"Parietal peritoneum lining the inside of the abdominal wall","why":"This is the first peritoneal layer cut to enter the peritoneal cavity."},{"pt":"Greater omentum is the first thing seen, a fatty apron over the intestines","why":"It hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach down in front of the small intestine."},{"pt":"Lifting it upward shows the transverse colon on its back surface and the small intestine below","why":"The greater omentum attaches to the transverse colon, so the two lift together."},{"pt":"The stomach lies in front of the pancreas, hanging between the lesser and greater omenta","why":"The pancreas lies across the posterior wall behind the stomach."},{"pt":"The pancreas is retroperitoneal, behind the parietal peritoneum with no mesentery","why":"Peritoneum covers only its front surface, so it is fixed against the back wall instead of hanging in the cavity."},{"pt":"Its head sits in the C of the duodenum, which is also retroperitoneal","why":"The duodenum and pancreas lie together against the posterior wall."}]}]},{"id":"m4-mouth-esophagus","title":"Mouth, teeth, pharynx and esophagus","mw":"2026-11-04","tr":"2026-11-10","prompts":[{"id":"m4-mouth-esophagus-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[113,120,121],"cids":["w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","w5-teeth-tongue","w5-salivary-glands"],"t":"Inside the mouth","p":"Draw the open mouth and label everything you can, including what forms its walls and floor. Then name the three pairs of salivary glands and mark where each duct opens.","k":[{"pt":"Boundaries: lips in front, cheeks with the buccinator at the sides, the palate as the roof, the mylohyoid muscle as the floor, and the fauces behind","why":"The tongue rests on the floor and fills most of it, but the mylohyoid under it is the actual floor."},{"pt":"Hard and soft palate, uvula","why":"The soft palate is skeletal muscle, so it can rise with the uvula to close off the nasopharynx."},{"pt":"Palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches framing the fauces","why":"The fauces is the opening from the mouth into the oropharynx."},{"pt":"Tongue with papillae, lingual frenulum","why":"The frenulum anchors the tongue to the floor of the mouth and limits how far it can move back."},{"pt":"Palatine and lingual tonsils","why":"The palatine tonsils sit between the two arches and the lingual tonsils cover the back of the tongue."},{"pt":"Parotid duct opposite the second upper molar","why":"The parotid gland sits in front of and below the ear, so its duct runs forward across the cheek to reach the mouth."},{"pt":"Submandibular ducts beside the lingual frenulum and sublingual ducts along the floor under the tongue","why":"Both glands sit in the floor of the mouth, so their ducts open there."}]},{"id":"m4-mouth-esophagus-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[120],"cids":["w5-teeth-tongue"],"t":"Teeth","p":"Draw the adult dental arch and label the tooth types with how many of each. Then draw one tooth in section and label every layer.","k":[{"pt":"Incisors 8, canines 4, premolars 8, molars 12, total 32","why":"The 12 molars include the four third molars, the wisdom teeth."},{"pt":"Deciduous set of 20 named","why":"Children have 20 baby teeth before the 32 permanent teeth."},{"pt":"Crown, neck, root","why":"The crown is above the gum, the neck sits at the gum line, and the root is held in the socket."},{"pt":"Enamel, dentin, pulp cavity, root canal","why":"Enamel is the hardest substance in the body, dentin forms most of the tooth, and the pulp holds the vessels and nerves."},{"pt":"Cementum, periodontal ligament, gingiva","why":"Cementum covers the root and anchors it to the periodontal ligament, making the tooth and socket a fibrous joint called a gomphosis."}]},{"id":"m4-mouth-esophagus-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[113,114,118],"cids":["w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","w5-canal-sphincters","w5-food-pathway"],"t":"Swallowing route","p":"Trace a bolus from the mouth to the stomach, naming every structure it passes. Mark the point where the airway has to be shut off and name what does it.","k":[{"pt":"Oral cavity, fauces, oropharynx, laryngopharynx","why":"The tongue pushes the bolus back through the fauces into the throat."},{"pt":"Soft palate and uvula rise to close off the nasopharynx","why":"This keeps food from going up into the nasal cavity."},{"pt":"Epiglottis folds over the laryngeal inlet","why":"This is where the airway is shut off, so the bolus slides behind the larynx toward the esophagus."},{"pt":"Upper esophageal sphincter","why":"It is skeletal muscle at the level of the cricoid cartilage and relaxes to let the bolus in."},{"pt":"Esophagus behind the trachea, through the esophageal hiatus at T10","why":"The esophagus runs down the mediastinum in front of the vertebral column."},{"pt":"Lower esophageal or cardiac sphincter into the stomach","why":"It opens into the cardia, the entrance region of the stomach."}]},{"id":"m4-mouth-esophagus-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[120],"cids":["w5-teeth-tongue"],"t":"Muscles of the tongue","p":"Compare the extrinsic and intrinsic tongue muscles. Name the four extrinsic muscles, where each starts, and what each does, then say what the intrinsic muscles do instead.","k":[{"pt":"Extrinsic muscles start outside the tongue and end inside it, and move the whole tongue","why":"Because they anchor on bone or the soft palate, they can pull the tongue out, back, up, down and side to side."},{"pt":"Genioglossus, from the inside of the mandible at the chin: pushes the tongue out and lowers it","why":"Genio refers to the chin, where it starts."},{"pt":"Hyoglossus, from the hyoid bone: pulls the tongue down and back","why":"The hyoid sits below and behind the tongue, so pulling toward it moves the tongue down and back."},{"pt":"Styloglossus, from the styloid process of the temporal bone: pulls the tongue up and back","why":"The styloid process is above and behind the tongue."},{"pt":"Palatoglossus, from the soft palate: raises the back of the tongue","why":"It starts on the soft palate, above the back of the tongue, so pulling toward it lifts the back of the tongue."},{"pt":"Intrinsic muscles start and end inside the tongue and change its shape","why":"Their fibers run lengthwise, across and up and down, so they curl, flatten, narrow and shorten the tongue for speech and swallowing."}]},{"id":"m4-mouth-esophagus-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[109,113,114],"cids":["w5-canal-wall","w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","w5-canal-sphincters"],"t":"The esophagus from top to bottom","p":"Describe how the esophagus changes from its top to its bottom: where it starts and ends, what it passes through, its muscle, its lining, and its outer layer.","k":[{"pt":"Starts at the lower end of the laryngopharynx at the level of the cricoid cartilage, about 25 cm (10 inches) long","why":"The upper esophageal sphincter marks this starting point."},{"pt":"Runs down the mediastinum behind the trachea and in front of the vertebral column","why":"It stays flat except when food is passing through."},{"pt":"Passes through the esophageal hiatus at T10 and joins the stomach at the cardia","why":"A short abdominal segment lies below the diaphragm before the stomach."},{"pt":"Muscularis: skeletal muscle above, a mix in the middle, smooth muscle below","why":"This is why the upper esophageal sphincter is skeletal muscle and the lower one is smooth muscle."},{"pt":"Lining: stratified squamous epithelium","why":"It stands up to rubbing by food but is not protected against stomach acid."},{"pt":"Outer layer: adventitia above the diaphragm, serosa on the short segment below","why":"Only the part inside the abdominal cavity is covered by visceral peritoneum."}]},{"id":"m4-mouth-esophagus-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[113,114,119],"cids":["w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","w5-canal-sphincters","w5-canal-disorders"],"t":"Burning behind the sternum","p":"A patient has a burning feeling behind the sternum after meals, and imaging shows part of the stomach pushed up above the diaphragm. Name the two disorders, the opening and the sphincter involved, and explain why the burning is felt behind the sternum.","k":[{"pt":"Hiatal hernia: part of the stomach pushes up through the esophageal hiatus into the thorax","why":"The opening the esophagus normally uses lets the stomach slide up too."},{"pt":"The esophageal hiatus is in the muscle of the diaphragm at T10","why":"This is the opening involved."},{"pt":"The lower esophageal sphincter loses the support of the diaphragm","why":"The diaphragm around the hiatus normally helps this sphincter stay closed."},{"pt":"GERD: acidic stomach contents flow back up into the esophagus","why":"A sphincter that does not close well lets reflux happen."},{"pt":"The stratified squamous lining of the esophagus is not protected against acid, so it becomes inflamed","why":"Stratified squamous epithelium is built to stand up to rubbing by food, not to acid."},{"pt":"The burning is felt behind the sternum because the esophagus lies in the thorax just behind it","why":"Where the structure sits predicts where the patient feels the problem."}]},{"id":"m4-mouth-esophagus-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[98,113,118],"cids":["resp-upper-tract","w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","w5-food-pathway"],"t":"Milk out the nose","p":"A child laughs while swallowing milk and some comes out of the nose. Name the structures that should have sealed the nose off, then trace the route the milk took from the mouth to the nostrils.","k":[{"pt":"Soft palate and uvula should rise to close off the nasopharynx","why":"That seal is what normally keeps food out of the nasal cavity during a swallow."},{"pt":"Oral cavity, through the fauces into the oropharynx","why":"This first part of the route is the normal swallowing path."},{"pt":"Up into the nasopharynx above the soft palate","why":"The nasopharynx normally carries air only and is not on the food path."},{"pt":"Forward through the choanae into the nasal cavity","why":"The choanae are the back openings of the nasal cavity into the nasopharynx."},{"pt":"Out through the nasal vestibule and the nares","why":"The milk leaves by the same openings air comes in through."}]}]},{"id":"m4-stomach-intestines","title":"Stomach, small intestine and large intestine","mw":"2026-11-04","tr":"2026-11-10","prompts":[{"id":"m4-stomach-intestines-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[114,115],"cids":["w5-canal-sphincters","w5-stomach"],"t":"The stomach","p":"Draw the stomach and label every region, curvature, and sphincter. Then list its three muscle layers in order from the lumen outward.","k":[{"pt":"Cardia, fundus, body, pyloric part, greater and lesser curvature, rugae","why":"Food enters at the cardia and collects in the body, the fundus domes above, and the rugae flatten as the stomach fills."},{"pt":"Pyloric part in order: pyloric antrum, pyloric canal, pylorus","why":"The funnel narrows from the wide antrum to the outlet surrounded by the sphincter."},{"pt":"Lesser curvature: the short, concave medial (right) border. Greater curvature: the long, convex lateral (left) border","why":"The lesser omentum attaches along the lesser curvature, and the greater omentum hangs from the greater curvature."},{"pt":"Cardiac and pyloric sphincters","why":"The cardiac sphincter guards the entrance from the esophagus and the pyloric sphincter guards the exit to the duodenum."},{"pt":"Third muscle layer: oblique, innermost, then circular, then longitudinal","why":"The extra oblique layer lets the stomach twist and churn food, not just push it forward."}]},{"id":"m4-stomach-intestines-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[116],"cids":["w5-small-intestine"],"t":"The small intestine","p":"Name the three regions of the small intestine in order with one identifying feature of each. Then name every structure that increases surface area, largest to smallest.","k":[{"pt":"Duodenum, jejunum, ileum","why":"The order is shortest, middle and longest: about 25 cm (10 inches), 1 m (3 feet) and 2 m (6 feet)."},{"pt":"Duodenum retroperitoneal and C shaped, with the major duodenal papilla","why":"It wraps around the head of the pancreas, and bile and pancreatic juice enter through the papilla."},{"pt":"Jejunum mostly in the upper left abdomen, hanging on the mesentery","why":"It gradually becomes the ileum with no sharp border."},{"pt":"Ileum with Peyer patches, ending at the ileocecal valve","why":"It lies mostly in the lower right abdomen and joins the cecum."},{"pt":"Circular folds (plicae), villi, microvilli or brush border","why":"Fold, finger, fuzz: each level is smaller than the last and adds more surface for absorption."},{"pt":"Lacteals in the villi","why":"Each villus has a lacteal in its core that takes in fats too large for the blood capillaries."}]},{"id":"m4-stomach-intestines-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[117],"cids":["w5-large-intestine"],"t":"The large intestine","p":"Draw the large intestine and label every region in order. Then name the three features unique to it and mark them on your drawing.","k":[{"pt":"Cecum with the vermiform appendix, ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid colon, rectum, anal canal","why":"The colon frames the small intestine: up the right, across the top, down the left, then an S into the pelvis."},{"pt":"Right colic or hepatic flexure, left colic or splenic flexure","why":"The flexures are the bends between parts of the colon, not separate parts."},{"pt":"Teniae coli, three bands of longitudinal muscle","why":"They are the outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis gathered into three ribbons."},{"pt":"Haustra, the pouches they create","why":"The teniae are shorter than the wall, so the wall bunches up between them."},{"pt":"Epiploic or omental appendages","why":"These small fat filled pouches of visceral peritoneum hang mostly along the teniae coli."},{"pt":"Rectum with no haustra","why":"The teniae coli spread out into a full layer again at the rectum."}]},{"id":"m4-stomach-intestines-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[117,119],"cids":["w5-large-intestine","w5-canal-disorders"],"t":"The right lower quadrant","p":"Draw the cecum, appendix, and ileocecal junction. Label every part and mark the surface point that overlies the appendix.","k":[{"pt":"Ileum entering at the ileocecal valve","why":"The ileocecal sphincter and folds of mucosa keep the contents of the large intestine from moving back into the ileum."},{"pt":"Cecum as the blind pouch below it","why":"It is the first part of the large intestine, in the lower right abdomen."},{"pt":"Appendix hanging off the cecum","why":"It is a narrow blind tube with a wall full of lymphatic tissue, so food never passes through it."},{"pt":"Teniae coli converging on the appendix base","why":"Following the three bands down to where they meet is one way to find the appendix on a specimen."},{"pt":"McBurney point, about one third of the way from the ASIS to the umbilicus","why":"It is the surface landmark for the base of the appendix, and tenderness there suggests appendicitis."}]},{"id":"m4-stomach-intestines-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[114,118],"cids":["w5-canal-sphincters","w5-food-pathway"],"t":"Mouth to anus","p":"Trace a bite of food from the mouth to the anus. Name every organ and every sphincter it passes in order.","k":[{"pt":"Oral cavity, pharynx, esophagus","why":"Food passes the oropharynx and laryngopharynx but not the nasopharynx, which carries air only."},{"pt":"Upper and lower esophageal sphincters","why":"These sit at the top and bottom of the esophagus; only the upper one is skeletal muscle."},{"pt":"Stomach, pyloric sphincter","why":"Food enters at the cardia, is churned in the body, and leaves through the pyloric part."},{"pt":"Duodenum, jejunum, ileum, ileocecal valve","why":"The ileocecal valve lets the remaining material into the large intestine."},{"pt":"Cecum, colon segments, rectum","why":"Ascending, transverse, descending and sigmoid colon, with the hepatic and splenic flexures between them."},{"pt":"Anal canal with the internal and external anal sphincters, then the anus","why":"The internal sphincter is smooth muscle and the external one is skeletal and under your control."}]},{"id":"m4-stomach-intestines-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[118],"cids":["w5-food-pathway"],"t":"GI mind map","p":"Build a mind map with the GI tract at the center. One branch per organ in order. On each branch write one structural feature and one thing that happens there.","k":[{"pt":"Mouth: teeth and tongue; food is chewed, mixed with saliva and shaped into a bolus","why":"The teeth cut and grind, and the tongue shapes the bolus and pushes it back toward the pharynx."},{"pt":"Pharynx: oropharynx and laryngopharynx; the epiglottis covers the larynx as the bolus passes","why":"Food uses only the lower two regions, and the nasopharynx is sealed off by the soft palate."},{"pt":"Esophagus: upper and lower esophageal sphincters; carries the bolus down through the esophageal hiatus","why":"The sphincters sit at its two ends and control when food enters and leaves."},{"pt":"Stomach: rugae and the extra oblique muscle layer; food is churned into chyme","why":"The three muscle layers squeeze and twist the food, and the rugae flatten as it fills."},{"pt":"Small intestine: circular folds, villi and microvilli; most absorption happens here","why":"Each level of folding adds surface, and more surface means more absorption."},{"pt":"Large intestine: teniae coli and haustra; water is absorbed and feces are formed and stored","why":"These features are found only on the large intestine, so they mark its branch."},{"pt":"Accessory organs on side branches, and the fundus and appendix marked as off the path","why":"Food never passes through the accessory organs or the appendix, and it collects in the fundus without having to pass through it."}]},{"id":"m4-stomach-intestines-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[114,117],"cids":["w5-canal-sphincters","w5-large-intestine"],"t":"The rectum and the anal sphincters","p":"Compare the rectum with the anal canal, and the internal anal sphincter with the external one: where each sits, what it is made of or lined with, and which sphincter you control.","k":[{"pt":"Rectum, the straight part in the pelvis in front of the sacrum and coccyx","why":"It stores feces before they leave the body."},{"pt":"No haustra on the rectum","why":"The teniae coli spread out into a complete layer here."},{"pt":"Anal canal, the last few centimeters, passing through the pelvic floor to the anus","why":"It is lined with stratified squamous epithelium near the anus, which stands up to friction."},{"pt":"Internal anal sphincter, smooth muscle, involuntary","why":"It is a thickening of the circular smooth muscle of the canal, so it works without conscious control."},{"pt":"External anal sphincter, skeletal muscle, voluntary","why":"This is the one you control, and it sits outside the internal sphincter."},{"pt":"Lining: simple columnar in the rectum, stratified squamous in the anal canal near the anus","why":"The rectum is lined like the rest of the large intestine, and the lining changes near the anus where it has to stand up to friction."}]},{"id":"m4-stomach-intestines-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[116,118,123],"cids":["w5-small-intestine","w5-food-pathway","w5-bile-path"],"t":"A fatty meal","p":"Build a mind map following a fatty meal. Branch to every organ that handles it and name the structure in each organ doing the work.","k":[{"pt":"Stomach churns, pyloric sphincter releases slowly","why":"The three muscle layers turn food into chyme, and the sphincter lets small amounts into the duodenum at a time."},{"pt":"Gallbladder contracts, bile through the bile duct","why":"Fatty food entering the duodenum is what makes the gallbladder squeeze its stored bile down the cystic and common bile ducts."},{"pt":"Pancreas sends enzymes through the pancreatic duct","why":"The main pancreatic duct joins the common bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla."},{"pt":"Duodenum and jejunum, villi and microvilli","why":"Bile and enzymes enter the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla, and the folded surface absorbs what they release."},{"pt":"Lacteals in the villi take the fat, not the capillaries","why":"Most absorbed fats are packaged in particles too large to enter blood capillaries."},{"pt":"Thoracic duct returns it to the blood","why":"The milky chyle drains to the cisterna chyli and up the thoracic duct to the left subclavian and internal jugular junction."}]},{"id":"m4-stomach-intestines-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[109,115,119],"cids":["w5-canal-wall","w5-stomach","w5-canal-disorders"],"t":"An ulcer that goes too deep","p":"A peptic ulcer starts in the lining of the stomach and keeps eroding. Name, in order, each layer it passes through, what can happen at each step, and where the stomach contents go if it breaks all the way through.","k":[{"pt":"Starts in the mucosa, an open sore where the lining is worn away","why":"Common causes are infection with Helicobacter pylori and regular use of NSAID pain relievers."},{"pt":"Submucosa, where it can open a blood vessel and bleed","why":"The submucosa carries the larger blood vessels of the wall."},{"pt":"Muscularis: oblique, circular, then longitudinal","why":"The stomach has the extra oblique layer, so the ulcer has three muscle layers to cross here."},{"pt":"Serosa, the visceral peritoneum","why":"The stomach is intraperitoneal, so its outer layer is serosa."},{"pt":"A perforated wall spills the stomach contents into the peritoneal cavity","why":"Once all four layers are breached there is nothing between the lumen and the cavity."}]}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs","title":"Salivary glands, liver, gallbladder and pancreas","mw":"2026-11-04","tr":"2026-11-10","prompts":[{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[123],"cids":["w5-bile-path"],"t":"The biliary tree","p":"Draw the whole biliary tree from the liver to the duodenum. Label every duct and the gallbladder, and mark where bile is stored and where it enters the gut.","k":[{"pt":"Right and left hepatic ducts joining to the common hepatic duct","why":"The hepatic ducts drain the two sides of the liver and leave at the porta hepatis."},{"pt":"Cystic duct to and from the gallbladder","why":"Bile runs both ways in it: up into storage when the sphincter of Oddi is closed, and back down when the gallbladder contracts."},{"pt":"Common hepatic plus cystic makes the bile duct","why":"The common bile duct is where the liver's duct and the gallbladder's duct come together."},{"pt":"Joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla","why":"The two ducts meet in a short widened chamber in the duodenal wall."},{"pt":"Major duodenal papilla, sphincter of Oddi","why":"The ampulla opens into the descending duodenum at the papilla, and the sphincter around it controls the flow."},{"pt":"Stored in the gallbladder, enters the gut at the major duodenal papilla","why":"The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile between meals."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[122],"cids":["w5-liver-gross-lobule"],"t":"The liver, gross to microscopic","p":"Draw the liver and label the lobes and ligaments. Then draw one lobule and label what runs in the portal triad.","k":[{"pt":"Right, left, caudate, quadrate lobes","why":"The right and left show from the front; the quadrate is underneath beside the gallbladder, and the caudate is on the back beside the IVC."},{"pt":"Falciform ligament, round ligament","why":"The falciform ligament marks the line between the right and left lobes, and the round ligament in its free edge is the remnant of the fetal umbilical vein."},{"pt":"Hexagonal lobule, central vein, sinusoids, hepatocyte plates","why":"The plates radiate from the central vein like spokes, with the sinusoids running between them."},{"pt":"Portal triad: hepatic portal vein, hepatic artery branch, bile duct","why":"The two vessels bring blood into the lobule, and the bile duct carries bile away."},{"pt":"Porta hepatis between the quadrate and caudate lobes","why":"This doorway is where the portal vein and hepatic artery enter and the hepatic ducts leave."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[123,124],"cids":["w5-bile-path","w5-pancreas"],"t":"Bile out, enzymes in","p":"Trace bile from the hepatocyte to the duodenum, naming every duct. Then name the parts of the gallbladder and the pancreatic ducts.","k":[{"pt":"Bile canaliculi, hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct","why":"The canaliculi between hepatocytes feed the bile ducts of the portal triads, which join into the right and left hepatic ducts."},{"pt":"Cystic duct, gallbladder fundus, body, neck","why":"The neck narrows into the cystic duct, and the fundus sticks out past the lower edge of the liver."},{"pt":"Bile duct joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla","why":"This shared chamber is why one stone there can block both bile and pancreatic juice."},{"pt":"Main pancreatic duct (duct of Wirsung) runs from the tail to the head","why":"It collects pancreatic juice along the whole length of the gland."},{"pt":"Major and minor duodenal papilla, accessory pancreatic duct, sphincter of Oddi","why":"Match the size words: the main duct opens at the major papilla, and the accessory duct opens at the minor papilla."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[124,126],"cids":["w5-pancreas","w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"],"t":"The pancreas in place","p":"Draw the pancreas and label its three regions. Then name what it sits against in front and behind.","k":[{"pt":"Head in the C of the duodenum","why":"The head is the wide end, to the right of the midline."},{"pt":"Body crossing the midline, tail reaching the spleen","why":"The tail tapers toward the left and ends near the spleen."},{"pt":"Retroperitoneal except the tip of the tail","why":"Peritoneum covers only its front, so it is fixed against the posterior wall."},{"pt":"Stomach in front; the aorta and vertebral column behind the body","why":"It lies across the posterior abdominal wall behind the stomach."},{"pt":"Hepatic portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas","why":"The superior mesenteric and splenic veins join there, so the pancreas is a landmark for the portal system."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[122],"cids":["w5-liver-gross-lobule"],"t":"Portal triad at high power","p":"Draw one liver lobule and label everything. Zoom in on a corner and draw the portal triad with all three of its structures.","k":[{"pt":"Hexagonal lobule with the central vein in the middle","why":"The central vein collects blood from the sinusoids at the center of each lobule."},{"pt":"Plates of hepatocytes radiating outward","why":"These plates are the hepatic laminae, and the hepatocytes in them make bile."},{"pt":"Sinusoids between the plates, with Kupffer cells","why":"The sinusoids are wide, leaky capillaries, and the Kupffer cells remove worn out blood cells and bacteria."},{"pt":"Portal triad at each corner: portal venule, hepatic arteriole, bile ductule","why":"Two vessels bring blood in and the bile duct carries bile out."},{"pt":"Bile canaliculi running the opposite direction to the blood","why":"Blood flows in toward the central vein while bile flows out toward the portal triad."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L1-6","lv":1,"comps":[123,124],"cids":["w5-bile-path","w5-pancreas"],"t":"Where the ducts meet the gut","p":"Draw the second part of the duodenum opened up. Label the papillae, the ampulla, and the sphincter, and mark which duct arrives at each.","k":[{"pt":"Descending duodenum, retroperitoneal","why":"The second part of the duodenum is fixed to the posterior wall around the head of the pancreas."},{"pt":"Major duodenal papilla with the hepatopancreatic ampulla","why":"The ampulla is the chamber in the wall, and the papilla is the bump where it opens."},{"pt":"Bile duct plus main pancreatic duct arriving there","why":"Both ducts deliver through this one opening."},{"pt":"Sphincter of Oddi controlling the opening","why":"It is a ring of smooth muscle around the ampulla."},{"pt":"Minor duodenal papilla above it for the accessory pancreatic duct","why":"The accessory duct drains part of the head and opens on its own, a little above the major papilla."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[122,123],"cids":["w5-liver-gross-lobule","w5-bile-path"],"t":"Liver and the bile route","p":"Draw the liver and label the lobes and the ligaments. Then trace bile from a hepatocyte to the duodenum, naming every duct.","k":[{"pt":"Right, left, caudate, quadrate lobes, falciform ligament, round ligament","why":"Two lobes show from the front, and the quadrate and caudate are seen from below and behind."},{"pt":"Lobule with a central vein, portal triad: portal vein, hepatic artery, bile duct","why":"Blood flows toward the central vein and bile flows toward the portal triad, in opposite directions."},{"pt":"Bile canaliculi, right and left hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct","why":"The hepatic ducts leave the liver at the porta hepatis and join into one."},{"pt":"Cystic duct and gallbladder, bile duct","why":"Bile can turn up the cystic duct for storage, then come back down and continue in the common bile duct."},{"pt":"Hepatopancreatic ampulla, major duodenal papilla","why":"The common bile duct meets the main pancreatic duct at the ampulla and opens into the duodenum at the papilla."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[123],"cids":["w5-bile-path"],"t":"Where bile comes from and goes","p":"Write which cells make bile, where it is stored and concentrated, and what triggers its release. Draw the route as a line with the structures named along it.","k":[{"pt":"Hepatocytes secrete it into bile canaliculi","why":"The canaliculi are tiny channels between neighboring hepatocytes inside each plate."},{"pt":"Canaliculi to bile ductules to hepatic ducts","why":"The ducts of the portal triads join into larger ducts that drain each side of the liver."},{"pt":"Stored and concentrated in the gallbladder","why":"The gallbladder removes water from bile between meals."},{"pt":"Released when the duodenum receives fat","why":"The smooth muscle in the gallbladder wall contracts and squeezes the bile out."},{"pt":"Down the cystic and bile ducts to the duodenum","why":"It passes the hepatopancreatic ampulla and enters at the major duodenal papilla."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[121,123,124],"cids":["w5-salivary-glands","w5-bile-path","w5-pancreas"],"t":"Accessory organ mind map","p":"Build a mind map with the three accessory organs as branches. On each, name its duct, what it delivers, and exactly where that lands in the gut.","k":[{"pt":"Liver: hepatic ducts, bile, to the duodenum","why":"The hepatic ducts join into the common hepatic duct and then the common bile duct."},{"pt":"Gallbladder: cystic duct, stored bile, to the duodenum","why":"The gallbladder does not make bile; it stores and concentrates what the liver makes."},{"pt":"Pancreas: main and accessory pancreatic ducts, enzymes","why":"The acini make pancreatic juice and send it into the ducts."},{"pt":"All converge at the major duodenal papilla except the accessory duct","why":"The accessory pancreatic duct opens on its own at the minor duodenal papilla."},{"pt":"The salivary glands are accessory organs too, and their ducts land in the mouth","why":"Their ducts land in the mouth: parotid opposite the upper second molar, submandibular beside the frenulum, sublingual along the floor."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[121],"cids":["w5-salivary-glands"],"t":"Three pairs of salivary glands","p":"Compare the parotid, submandibular and sublingual glands: where each one sits, where its duct opens, and the kind of saliva it makes.","k":[{"pt":"Parotid: in front of and below the ear, between the skin and the masseter; the largest","why":"Parotid means beside the ear."},{"pt":"Parotid duct crosses the masseter, passes through the buccinator, and opens inside the cheek opposite the upper second molar","why":"The gland sits outside the mouth, so its duct has to cross the cheek to get in."},{"pt":"Submandibular: floor of the mouth along the inner surface of the mandible; duct opens on each side of the lingual frenulum","why":"Submandibular means below the mandible."},{"pt":"Sublingual: under the tongue above the submandibular gland, the smallest; many small ducts open along the floor of the mouth","why":"Sublingual means below the tongue."},{"pt":"Saliva: parotid watery (serous), submandibular mixed, sublingual mostly mucous","why":"The type of saliva changes from watery to mucous as you go from the largest gland to the smallest."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[123,124,125],"cids":["w5-bile-path","w5-pancreas","w5-accessory-disorders"],"t":"A stone at the ampulla","p":"A gallstone travels down from the gallbladder and lodges in the hepatopancreatic ampulla. Trace the route it took, then explain why the patient can develop both jaundice and pancreatitis from one stone.","k":[{"pt":"Gallbladder neck, cystic duct, common bile duct","why":"Stones form from bile inside the gallbladder and can move into its ducts."},{"pt":"Common bile duct passes behind the first part of the duodenum and through the head of the pancreas","why":"This is the last stretch before the duct enters the duodenal wall."},{"pt":"Lodges where the common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct meet in the duodenal wall","why":"The ampulla is a shared chamber, so a stone there plugs both ducts."},{"pt":"Bile backs up into the liver and blood, causing jaundice","why":"Bilirubin builds up and stains the skin and the whites of the eyes yellow."},{"pt":"Pancreatic juice backs up into the pancreas and its enzymes damage the gland: pancreatitis","why":"The enzymes become active inside the gland instead of in the duodenum."},{"pt":"Both are blocked because the two ducts share one exit, the major duodenal papilla","why":"One stone in that shared passage stops both bile and pancreatic juice at once."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[123,125],"cids":["w5-bile-path","w5-accessory-disorders"],"t":"A stone in the cystic duct","p":"A gallstone blocks the cystic duct instead. Name the disorder this most often causes, and predict whether bile from the liver can still reach the duodenum, tracing the route it would take.","k":[{"pt":"Cholecystitis: the gallbladder wall becomes inflamed and swollen","why":"A stone in the cystic duct is the most common cause, because it traps bile inside the gallbladder."},{"pt":"Yes, bile from the liver can still reach the duodenum","why":"The cystic duct is only the side branch to the gallbladder; the main route from the liver does not pass through it."},{"pt":"Right and left hepatic ducts to the common hepatic duct","why":"These drain the liver above the blockage."},{"pt":"Common hepatic duct continues as the common bile duct past where the cystic duct joins","why":"The stone sits in the side branch, so the main duct stays open."},{"pt":"Hepatopancreatic ampulla and major duodenal papilla into the duodenum","why":"The end of the route is the same as always."},{"pt":"The gallbladder can no longer store bile or send it out","why":"Bile reaches and leaves the gallbladder only through the cystic duct."}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[121,125],"cids":["w5-salivary-glands","w5-accessory-disorders"],"t":"Swelling in front of the ears","p":"A child's face puffs out in front of and below both ears. Name the infection and the glands involved, describe where those glands sit, and trace the path their saliva takes into the mouth.","k":[{"pt":"Mumps, a viral infection of the parotid glands","why":"The parotids are the glands that swell in mumps."},{"pt":"Parotid gland, the largest salivary gland, under the skin in front of and below the ear, over the masseter","why":"That position is why the swelling shows in front of and below the ears."},{"pt":"Parotid duct runs forward across the masseter","why":"It leaves the front edge of the gland and crosses the chewing muscle."},{"pt":"It passes through the buccinator muscle of the cheek","why":"The buccinator forms the wall of the cheek, so the duct pierces it to reach the inside of the mouth."},{"pt":"It opens inside the cheek opposite the upper second molar","why":"This is where parotid saliva enters the mouth."},{"pt":"Parotid saliva is watery (serous)","why":"The parotid makes the most watery saliva of the three large glands."}]}]},{"id":"m4-abdominal-vessels","title":"Vessels of the abdomen and pelvis","mw":"2026-11-09","tr":"2026-11-10","prompts":[{"id":"m4-abdominal-vessels-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[122,126],"cids":["w5-liver-gross-lobule","w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"],"t":"Two blood supplies","p":"Draw the liver and show both of its blood supplies arriving. Name each vessel, where it comes from, and what kind of blood it delivers.","k":[{"pt":"Hepatic artery proper, oxygen rich blood from the aorta","why":"It comes from the common hepatic artery, a branch of the celiac trunk."},{"pt":"Hepatic portal vein, nutrient rich, oxygen poor blood from the gut and spleen","why":"It forms behind the pancreas from the superior mesenteric and splenic veins."},{"pt":"Both enter at the porta hepatis and send branches to the portal triad at each corner of the lobules","why":"Every lobule gets both kinds of blood at its corners."},{"pt":"Both drain into the sinusoids","why":"The two bloods mix in the sinusoids as they flow past the hepatocytes."},{"pt":"Central veins to hepatic veins to the IVC","why":"Two supplies come in, and one drainage leads out to the inferior vena cava just below the diaphragm."}]},{"id":"m4-abdominal-vessels-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[126],"cids":["w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"],"t":"Branches of the abdominal aorta","p":"Draw the abdominal aorta from the diaphragm to where it ends. Label every branch on your list in order from top to bottom, mark each as paired or unpaired, and write its vertebral level.","k":[{"pt":"Enters through the aortic hiatus at T12","why":"Above the diaphragm it is the thoracic aorta, and below it is the abdominal aorta."},{"pt":"Celiac trunk, unpaired, T12, splitting into the left gastric, splenic and common hepatic arteries","why":"It is short and splits almost at once."},{"pt":"Superior mesenteric artery, unpaired, L1","why":"It leaves just below the celiac trunk and runs in the mesentery."},{"pt":"Renal arteries, paired, L1 to L2; gonadal arteries, paired, L2","why":"Paired branches leave the sides of the aorta to reach organs on the right and left."},{"pt":"Inferior mesenteric artery, unpaired, L3","why":"The three unpaired branches leave the front of the aorta and supply the gut."},{"pt":"Ends at L4 by dividing into the right and left common iliac arteries","why":"These carry blood on to the pelvis and the lower limbs."}]},{"id":"m4-abdominal-vessels-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[126],"cids":["w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"],"t":"Veins of the abdomen","p":"Draw the inferior vena cava from where it forms to where it ends. Label every vein that joins it on your list, and mark the two veins that do not drain straight into it.","k":[{"pt":"Formed at L5 by the right and left common iliac veins","why":"Each common iliac vein is made of an internal iliac vein from the pelvis and an external iliac vein from the lower limb."},{"pt":"Renal veins drain the kidneys into the IVC; the left one is longer","why":"The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta to reach the IVC on the right."},{"pt":"Right gonadal vein drains straight into the IVC","why":"It is on the same side as the IVC."},{"pt":"Left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein, not the IVC","why":"This is one of the two veins that do not empty straight into the IVC."},{"pt":"Hepatic veins join just below the diaphragm","why":"They carry blood out of the liver after it has passed through the sinusoids."},{"pt":"Hepatic portal vein carries gut blood into the liver, not the IVC","why":"The stomach, intestines, spleen and pancreas send their blood to the liver first."},{"pt":"IVC runs up the right side of the aorta, through the diaphragm at T8, to the right atrium","why":"It passes through the caval opening in the central tendon."}]},{"id":"m4-abdominal-vessels-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[126],"cids":["w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"],"t":"Feeding the gut","p":"Draw the abdominal aorta and its three unpaired branches. For each, name the gut region it supplies and write where one territory hands over to the next.","k":[{"pt":"Celiac trunk: stomach, liver, gallbladder, spleen, pancreas and the start of the duodenum","why":"Its three branches, the left gastric, splenic and common hepatic arteries, cover the top of the gut."},{"pt":"Superior mesenteric: most of the small intestine, part of the pancreas, the cecum, appendix, ascending colon and most of the transverse colon","why":"It feeds the middle of the gut."},{"pt":"Inferior mesenteric: the last part of the transverse colon, the descending colon, sigmoid colon and upper rectum","why":"It feeds the end of the gut."},{"pt":"Celiac to superior mesenteric: in the duodenum, since the celiac trunk feeds only its start","why":"The celiac trunk covers the top of the gut, and the superior mesenteric picks up most of the small intestine."},{"pt":"Superior to inferior mesenteric: near the end of the transverse colon","why":"The three branches feed the gut in the same order as the gut itself."}]},{"id":"m4-abdominal-vessels-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[122,126],"cids":["w5-liver-gross-lobule","w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"],"t":"The portal system","p":"Draw the hepatic portal system. Name the veins that form the portal vein and trace the blood through the liver and back to the heart.","k":[{"pt":"Superior mesenteric plus splenic vein makes the hepatic portal vein","why":"The two join behind the neck of the pancreas."},{"pt":"Inferior mesenteric draining into the splenic","why":"It drains the descending colon, sigmoid colon and upper rectum, and usually empties into the splenic vein."},{"pt":"Portal vein to the liver sinusoids","why":"It runs up in the free edge of the lesser omentum and enters at the porta hepatis."},{"pt":"Central veins to hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava","why":"The hepatic veins leave the back of the liver and empty into the IVC just below the diaphragm, which carries the blood to the right atrium."},{"pt":"Blood passes two capillary beds before reaching the heart","why":"The first bed is in the gut wall and the second is the hepatic sinusoids, and that is what makes it a portal system."}]},{"id":"m4-abdominal-vessels-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[122,126],"cids":["w5-liver-gross-lobule","w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"],"t":"Why gut cancers reach the liver","p":"Cancer cells break off a tumor in the sigmoid colon and enter its small veins. Trace them to the first organ where they are likely to lodge, and explain why that organ and not the lungs comes first.","k":[{"pt":"Small veins of the sigmoid colon to the inferior mesenteric vein","why":"The inferior mesenteric vein drains the descending colon, sigmoid colon and upper rectum."},{"pt":"Inferior mesenteric vein usually empties into the splenic vein","why":"It does not go straight to the IVC."},{"pt":"Splenic vein joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the neck of the pancreas to form the hepatic portal vein","why":"All gut blood funnels into this one vein."},{"pt":"Portal vein runs up in the lesser omentum and enters the liver at the porta hepatis","why":"This carries the cells straight to the liver."},{"pt":"The cells reach the hepatic sinusoids, the second capillary bed","why":"Blood from the stomach and intestines passes through the liver before it goes anywhere else, which is why digestive tract cancers often spread to the liver."},{"pt":"Only after the liver does the blood reach the IVC and the heart","why":"The hepatic veins carry it to the IVC, so the lungs come later on the route."}]},{"id":"m4-abdominal-vessels-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[126],"cids":["w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"],"t":"A blocked gut artery","p":"A clot blocks the inferior mesenteric artery where it leaves the aorta. Name which parts of the gut lose their main supply, which artery supplies the gut just before that territory, and where along the aorta each of these arteries leaves.","k":[{"pt":"The inferior mesenteric artery leaves at about L3","why":"It is the lowest of the three unpaired gut branches."},{"pt":"Lost: the last part of the transverse colon, the descending colon, the sigmoid colon and the upper rectum","why":"This is the whole territory of the inferior mesenteric artery."},{"pt":"The superior mesenteric artery, at L1, supplies the gut just before, through most of the transverse colon","why":"The handover between the two arteries is near the end of the transverse colon."},{"pt":"The celiac trunk at T12 supplies the stomach, liver, spleen and start of the duodenum, and is untouched","why":"It is the highest branch, well above the clot."},{"pt":"The small intestine and the right side of the colon are spared","why":"They belong to the superior mesenteric territory, not the blocked one."}]},{"id":"m4-abdominal-vessels-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[126],"cids":["w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"],"t":"A clot in the left renal vein","p":"A clot blocks the left renal vein just before it reaches the inferior vena cava. Predict which organs on the left can no longer drain normally, and explain why the same clot on the right would affect fewer structures.","k":[{"pt":"The left kidney, drained by the left renal vein","why":"The renal vein is the kidney's only route into the IVC."},{"pt":"The left testis or ovary, because the left gonadal vein empties into the left renal vein","why":"Its blood has to pass the clot to reach the IVC."},{"pt":"The left renal vein is the longer one and crosses in front of the aorta","why":"The IVC lies to the right of the aorta, so the left vein has farther to travel."},{"pt":"On the right, the gonadal vein drains straight into the IVC","why":"So a clot in the right renal vein would affect only the right kidney."}]}]}]},{"n":5,"title":"Module 5. Urinary, Reproductive and Nervous Systems","topics":[{"id":"m5-kidney","title":"Kidney gross anatomy and blood supply","mw":"2026-11-18","tr":"2026-11-19","prompts":[{"id":"m5-kidney-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[134,139],"cids":["w6-kidney-internal","w6-urine-path"],"t":"Kidney in coronal section","p":"Draw a kidney cut front to back and label every region and structure. Then trace urine from a collecting duct to the outside of the body.","k":[{"pt":"Renal capsule on the surface, with the grainy renal cortex just under it","why":"The cortex holds the renal corpuscles and the coiled tubules, which is what makes it look grainy."},{"pt":"Renal medulla made of striped renal pyramids, each tip a renal papilla","why":"The pyramids look striped because tubules and collecting ducts run side by side through them toward the papilla."},{"pt":"Renal columns between the pyramids","why":"Renal columns are cortex tissue reaching inward, so they look like the outer layer even though they sit deep in the kidney."},{"pt":"Minor calyx, major calyx, and renal pelvis in the renal sinus, with the hilum on the medial border","why":"Each papilla drains into a minor calyx, minor calyces join into major calyces, and those join into the funnel of the renal pelvis at the hilum."},{"pt":"Urine path inside the kidney: collecting duct, papillary duct, minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis","why":"Urine leaves the tissue through openings on the papilla and from then on only moves through collecting spaces."},{"pt":"Then ureter, urinary bladder, urethra, external urethral orifice","why":"Once urine leaves the renal pelvis, the rest of the system only carries it, stores it, and releases it."}]},{"id":"m5-kidney-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[133],"cids":["w6-kidney-gross"],"t":"The kidney in place","p":"Draw the kidney in position from behind. Mark which vertebral levels it spans, which one sits lower and why, and label every covering layer from the kidney outward.","k":[{"pt":"Roughly T12 to L3, on the posterior abdominal wall beside the vertebral column","why":"The kidneys sit one on each side of the vertebral column, and the lowest ribs partly cover their upper ends."},{"pt":"Retroperitoneal, behind the parietal peritoneum","why":"Retroperitoneal means the kidney sits outside the peritoneal cavity, right against the muscles of the back."},{"pt":"Right kidney lower, pushed down by the liver","why":"The liver takes up the space above the right kidney, so the right one ends up a little lower than the left."},{"pt":"Renal capsule directly on the kidney","why":"This thin, tough layer of dense fibrous tissue holds the kidney's shape and protects its surface."},{"pt":"Adipose capsule, the perirenal fat, around the renal capsule","why":"The fat in the middle layer cushions the kidney and helps hold it in place."},{"pt":"Renal fascia as the outermost layer","why":"The fascia is farthest out because its job is to anchor the whole package to the posterior abdominal wall."},{"pt":"Adrenal gland sitting on top, wrapped in the same renal fascia","why":"The adrenal gland belongs to the endocrine system, but the renal fascia encloses it together with the kidney."}]},{"id":"m5-kidney-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[134,139],"cids":["w6-kidney-internal","w6-urine-path"],"t":"From papilla to pelvis","p":"Draw the collecting system on its own, without the rest of the kidney. Name every part in order from the collecting duct to the ureter, and say how many major calyces a kidney usually has.","k":[{"pt":"Collecting ducts running down through a pyramid","why":"Many nephrons drain into each collecting duct, so the ducts are shared as they run through the pyramid."},{"pt":"Papillary ducts opening on the renal papilla","why":"The collecting ducts merge into larger papillary ducts, and their openings sit on the tip of the pyramid."},{"pt":"One minor calyx cupping each papilla","why":"The minor calyx is the first space outside the kidney tissue, so every papilla drains into its own cup."},{"pt":"Major calyces, usually 2 or 3","why":"Several minor calyces join to form each major calyx, so there are far fewer major calyces than minor ones."},{"pt":"One renal pelvis, narrowing into the ureter at the hilum","why":"The major calyces join into a single funnel in the renal sinus, and that funnel becomes the ureter."}]},{"id":"m5-kidney-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[138,135,136,137],"cids":["w6-kidney-bloodsupply","w6-nephron-parts","w6-nephron-types","w6-jga-filtration"],"t":"Renal blood route and the corpuscle","p":"List the blood vessels in order from the renal artery to the renal vein. Then name the parts of the renal corpuscle and its filtration barrier.","k":[{"pt":"Renal artery, segmental arteries, interlobar arteries","why":"The renal artery enters at the hilum and branches in the renal sinus, and the interlobar arteries run up through the renal columns between the pyramids."},{"pt":"Arcuate arteries, then cortical radiate arteries","why":"Arcuate arteries arch over the bases of the pyramids at the border of cortex and medulla, and the cortical radiate arteries fan out from them into the cortex."},{"pt":"Afferent arteriole, glomerulus, efferent arteriole","why":"The glomerulus is unusual because it has an arteriole on both sides instead of draining into a venule."},{"pt":"Peritubular capillaries or vasa recta as the second capillary bed","why":"Blood leaving the glomerulus passes through a second capillary bed around the tubules before it reaches any vein."},{"pt":"Cortical radiate veins, arcuate veins, interlobar veins, renal vein","why":"The veins repeat the artery names in reverse, and the renal vein leaves at the hilum and empties into the inferior vena cava."},{"pt":"Corpuscle: glomerulus plus the glomerular capsule, with podocytes in its visceral layer","why":"The renal corpuscle is the filtering head of the nephron, made of the capillary ball and the cup that surrounds it."},{"pt":"Filtration barrier: fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, filtration slits","why":"Fluid crosses these three layers in this order on its way from the blood into the capsular space."}]},{"id":"m5-kidney-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[134,135,138],"cids":["w6-kidney-internal","w6-nephron-parts","w6-kidney-bloodsupply"],"t":"Kidney mind map","p":"Build a mind map with the kidney at the center. Branch to filter, turn filtrate into urine, and drain. Put the exact structure that does each job on the branch.","k":[{"pt":"Filter: renal corpuscle, glomerulus, glomerular capsule, filtration membrane","why":"Blood is filtered out of the glomerular capillaries across the filtration membrane and into the capsular space."},{"pt":"Turn filtrate into urine: PCT, nephron loop, DCT, in that order","why":"The renal tubule leaves the corpuscle as one long tube, and the filtrate is turned into urine as it flows through each segment."},{"pt":"Collecting duct and papillary duct, shared by many nephrons","why":"Fluid from many nephrons joins in the collecting duct, and once it leaves the collecting duct it is urine."},{"pt":"Drain: papilla, minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter","why":"These are collecting spaces rather than working tissue, so they carry the finished urine without changing it much."},{"pt":"Blood supply on its own branch, renal artery in and renal vein out at the hilum","why":"Every filtering job depends on blood arriving through the chain of arteries and leaving through the matching veins."}]},{"id":"m5-kidney-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[134],"cids":["w6-kidney-internal"],"t":"Working tissue or collecting space","p":"Sort the parts of a sectioned kidney into two groups: the parenchyma that holds nephrons, and the spaces that only collect urine. Write where each part sits.","k":[{"pt":"Parenchyma: renal cortex and renal medulla","why":"All of the nephrons sit in the cortex and medulla, so together they are the working tissue of the kidney."},{"pt":"Renal pyramids and their papillae belong to the medulla","why":"The medulla is made of the pyramids, and each papilla is just the tip of a pyramid."},{"pt":"Renal columns go in the cortex group, even though they sit deep","why":"A renal column is cortex reaching inward between two pyramids, so it counts as cortex."},{"pt":"Collecting spaces: minor calyces, major calyces, renal pelvis","why":"These spaces hold urine after it leaves the papilla, and no nephrons sit in them."},{"pt":"Renal sinus just inside the hilum, holding the pelvis, calyces, vessels, and fat","why":"The collecting spaces and the branching vessels sit in the sinus, not in the parenchyma."}]},{"id":"m5-kidney-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[133,140],"cids":["w6-kidney-gross","w6-urinary-disorders"],"t":"A kidney that drops","p":"A very thin patient loses most of the fat around one kidney, and the kidney drops lower than normal. Name the layer that was lost and the layers on either side of it, then predict what could happen to urine flow and why.","k":[{"pt":"The lost layer is the adipose capsule, the perirenal fat","why":"The adipose capsule cushions the kidney and helps hold it in place, so losing it lets the kidney slip."},{"pt":"Renal capsule inside it and renal fascia outside it","why":"The fat sits between the capsule on the kidney surface and the fascia that anchors the kidney to the body wall."},{"pt":"The condition is nephroptosis","why":"Nephroptosis means the kidney drops out of its normal position, often when its cushion of fat is thin or lost."},{"pt":"The ureter below can kink","why":"The ureter leaves at the hilum and runs down the posterior abdominal wall, so a dropping kidney can bend it."},{"pt":"Urine backs up into the renal pelvis and calyces, called hydronephrosis","why":"Any blockage along the urine path makes urine back up into the parts above it, and the swollen pelvis and calyces are hydronephrosis."}]},{"id":"m5-kidney-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[138,134],"cids":["w6-kidney-bloodsupply","w6-kidney-internal"],"t":"A clot in an arcuate artery","p":"A small clot lodges in one arcuate artery. List, in order, the vessels past the clot that stop getting blood, up to the second capillary bed, and say which kidney tissue is at risk.","k":[{"pt":"The clot sits in a vessel that arches over the base of a pyramid, at the border of cortex and medulla","why":"Arcuate means arched, and these arteries run along the border between the cortex and the medulla."},{"pt":"Cortical radiate arteries branching from that arcuate artery lose flow","why":"The cortical radiate arteries come off the arcuate arteries and radiate outward into the cortex."},{"pt":"Afferent arterioles, then their glomeruli, lose flow","why":"Each afferent arteriole carries blood into one glomerulus, so those glomeruli stop filtering."},{"pt":"Efferent arterioles, then the peritubular capillaries and vasa recta that come off them, lose flow","why":"Both capillary beds around the tubules are supplied by the efferent arterioles leaving the glomeruli."},{"pt":"The wedge of cortex fed by those cortical radiate arteries, with its renal corpuscles and convoluted tubules, is at risk","why":"Only the cortex reached by the branches of the blocked artery loses its supply, not the whole kidney."},{"pt":"The medulla beside the long loops of those deep nephrons is also short of blood","why":"The vasa recta come from the efferent arterioles of the juxtamedullary nephrons in the deep cortex, so their blood passed through the blocked artery first."}]}]},{"id":"m5-nephron","title":"The nephron and juxtaglomerular apparatus","mw":"2026-11-18","tr":"2026-11-19","prompts":[{"id":"m5-nephron-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[135,136],"cids":["w6-nephron-parts","w6-nephron-types"],"t":"The nephron in order","p":"Name every segment of the nephron in order from the glomerular capsule to the collecting duct. Mark which parts sit in the cortex and which dip into the medulla.","k":[{"pt":"Glomerulus inside the glomerular (Bowman) capsule, in the cortex","why":"The renal corpuscle is the filtering head of the nephron, and it always sits in the cortex."},{"pt":"Capsular space collecting the filtrate","why":"Filtrate collects between the parietal and visceral layers of the capsule before it flows into the tubule."},{"pt":"Proximal convoluted tubule, coiled, in the cortex","why":"Proximal means near the corpuscle, so the PCT is the first segment the filtrate meets."},{"pt":"Nephron loop: descending limb down and ascending limb back up, dipping toward or into the medulla","why":"The loop is the part of the nephron that leaves the cortex, and it returns to the cortex before the next segment."},{"pt":"Distal convoluted tubule back in the cortex, then the collecting duct","why":"Distal means farther from the corpuscle, and the DCT empties into a collecting duct shared by many nephrons."},{"pt":"Cortical nephron with a short loop and juxtamedullary nephron with a long loop","why":"Both types have the same parts in the same order, and they differ in how deep the loop reaches."}]},{"id":"m5-nephron-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[135,137],"cids":["w6-nephron-parts","w6-jga-filtration"],"t":"The renal corpuscle up close","p":"Draw a renal corpuscle large enough to label. Label the glomerulus, both layers of the capsule, the capsular space, the podocytes, the two arterioles, and where the tubule leaves.","k":[{"pt":"Glomerulus, a ball of fenestrated capillaries","why":"The capillary walls are full of small pores, which makes them very leaky and suited to filtering."},{"pt":"Parietal layer of the capsule, simple squamous epithelium, forming the outside wall of the cup","why":"The parietal layer is the outer wall of the double cup and does not touch the capillaries."},{"pt":"Visceral layer made of podocytes hugging the capillaries","why":"Podocytes wrap the glomerular capillaries with extensions shaped like little feet."},{"pt":"Filtration slits in the gaps between the podocyte feet","why":"The slits are the last part of the filtration membrane that fluid passes before it enters the capsule."},{"pt":"Capsular space between the two layers","why":"Filtrate collects in this space and then flows straight into the tubule."},{"pt":"Afferent arteriole in, efferent arteriole out, and the PCT leaving the capsule","why":"The corpuscle sits between its two arterioles, and the PCT carries the filtrate away as the first tubule segment."}]},{"id":"m5-nephron-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[136],"cids":["w6-nephron-types"],"t":"Two kinds of nephron","p":"Draw a cortical and a juxtamedullary nephron side by side in a wedge of kidney. Label the difference in loop length and in the capillaries around each.","k":[{"pt":"Cortical nephrons, about 85 percent, with the corpuscle in the outer cortex","why":"Most nephrons are cortical, which is why their corpuscles fill the outer part of the cortex."},{"pt":"Short loop that barely dips into the outer medulla","why":"A corpuscle high in the cortex sits far from the medulla, and its short loop only reaches the edge of it."},{"pt":"Juxtamedullary nephrons, about 15 percent, with the corpuscle deep in the cortex next to the medulla","why":"Juxta means next to, which names where the corpuscle sits, right beside the medulla."},{"pt":"Long loop reaching deep into the medulla near the papilla","why":"Starting next to the medulla lets the loop run all the way down toward the papilla."},{"pt":"Peritubular capillaries wrapping the cortical nephron's tubules","why":"The depth of the loop decides which capillary bed runs alongside it, and a short loop stays among the peritubular capillaries."},{"pt":"Vasa recta running down and back up beside the long loop","why":"Recta means straight, and these long straight vessels have to reach deep because the loop does."},{"pt":"Long loops with the vasa recta let the kidney make concentrated urine","why":"This is why the small share of juxtamedullary nephrons matters so much to the kidney."}]},{"id":"m5-nephron-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[137],"cids":["w6-jga-filtration"],"t":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus","p":"Draw the glomerulus with the tubule looping back to touch it. Label the two cell populations of the juxtaglomerular apparatus and write what each one senses.","k":[{"pt":"Afferent and efferent arterioles drawn on either side of the glomerulus","why":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus is the spot where the tubule touches these two arterioles of its own corpuscle."},{"pt":"Tubule passing between the arterioles where the ascending limb ends and the DCT begins","why":"After the loop climbs back to the cortex, the tubule runs right past its own renal corpuscle."},{"pt":"Macula densa cells in the tubule wall, tall and closely packed","why":"Macula densa means dense spot, which describes how the crowded cells look on a slide."},{"pt":"Macula densa senses the sodium chloride level of the fluid passing by","why":"Sitting in the tubule wall puts these cells in direct contact with the fluid they monitor."},{"pt":"Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells, modified smooth muscle in the wall of the afferent arteriole","why":"Sitting in the arteriole wall puts these cells where they can respond to the blood pressure arriving at the glomerulus."},{"pt":"JG cells hold renin in their granules and release it when blood pressure drops","why":"The granules are what give these cells their other name, granular cells."}]},{"id":"m5-nephron-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[137],"cids":["w6-jga-filtration"],"t":"Three layers of the filter","p":"Name the three layers of the filtration membrane in order from the blood to the capsular space. Beside each, write what it holds back. Then list what makes it all the way through.","k":[{"pt":"Fenestrated endothelium of the glomerular capillary holds back blood cells and platelets","why":"The pores in the capillary wall are too small for cells, so the cells are stopped first."},{"pt":"Basement membrane holds back large plasma proteins","why":"This layer sits between the capillary wall and the podocytes and catches the big proteins that pass the pores."},{"pt":"Filtration slits of the podocytes hold back most of the smaller proteins","why":"The slits are bridged by thin slit membranes that stop most proteins small enough to get past the basement membrane."},{"pt":"Each layer catches something smaller than the one before","why":"Cells first, then big proteins, then smaller proteins, is the order the fluid meets them."},{"pt":"Filtrate: water and small dissolved substances such as ions, glucose, and urea","why":"Only things small enough to pass all three layers end up in the capsular space."},{"pt":"Healthy filtrate has almost no protein and no blood cells","why":"Protein or blood in the urine is a sign that one of these layers is damaged."}]},{"id":"m5-nephron-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[137,140],"cids":["w6-jga-filtration","w6-urinary-disorders"],"t":"Protein and blood in the urine","p":"A urine test shows protein and red blood cells, and the doctor suspects the glomeruli are inflamed. Name the disorder, name which filtration layers must be failing to let each one through, and trace the leak to the urine.","k":[{"pt":"The disorder is glomerulonephritis, inflamed glomeruli","why":"Inflammation of the glomeruli damages the filtration membrane that sits right on those capillaries."},{"pt":"Red blood cells getting through means all three layers are breached, starting with the fenestrated endothelium","why":"Cells are the largest things in the blood, so for them to reach the capsular space the pored capillary wall and both layers past it must all be letting things through."},{"pt":"Protein getting through means the basement membrane and filtration slits are failing","why":"These two layers normally hold back the large and then the smaller plasma proteins."},{"pt":"The leak enters the capsular space","why":"Whatever crosses the filtration membrane lands in the space between the two capsule layers."},{"pt":"It follows the filtrate through the PCT, loop, DCT, and collecting duct into the urine","why":"The tubule has one fixed path, so anything that leaks into the capsule ends up in the urine."}]},{"id":"m5-nephron-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[135,136,137],"cids":["w6-nephron-parts","w6-nephron-types","w6-jga-filtration"],"t":"A drop of filtrate, deep nephron","p":"A drop of filtrate starts in the capsular space of a juxtamedullary nephron. Trace it to a minor calyx, naming each segment and saying whether it is in the cortex or the medulla at that moment.","k":[{"pt":"Capsular space and PCT in the deep cortex","why":"A juxtamedullary corpuscle sits in the deepest part of the cortex, and the PCT stays in the cortex."},{"pt":"Descending limb running deep into the medulla near the papilla","why":"Juxtamedullary nephrons have the long loops that reach deep into the medulla."},{"pt":"Ascending limb climbing back up to the cortex","why":"The loop is U shaped, so the filtrate comes back up toward the cortex on the other side."},{"pt":"Past the macula densa where the ascending limb meets the DCT","why":"The tubule passes its own corpuscle here, which is where the juxtaglomerular apparatus sits."},{"pt":"DCT in the cortex, then a collecting duct running down through a pyramid","why":"The DCT empties into a collecting duct, and the collecting ducts run down through the pyramids."},{"pt":"Papillary duct opening on the papilla, then the minor calyx","why":"The papillary ducts open on the tip of the pyramid, and the minor calyx catches what drains out."}]}]},{"id":"m5-urinary-tract","title":"Ureters, bladder, urethra and the path of urine","mw":"2026-11-18","tr":"2026-11-19","prompts":[{"id":"m5-urinary-tract-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[139,140],"cids":["w6-urine-path","w6-urinary-disorders"],"t":"Down the ureter","p":"Draw the ureter from the renal pelvis to the bladder. Mark its three narrow points and label the layers of its wall.","k":[{"pt":"Where the ureter leaves the renal pelvis, the first narrowing","why":"The wide funnel of the renal pelvis narrows into a tube only a few millimeters wide."},{"pt":"Where it crosses the iliac vessels at the pelvic brim, the second","why":"The ureter runs down the posterior abdominal wall and has to pass over the iliac vessels to enter the pelvis."},{"pt":"Where it enters the bladder wall at a slant, the third","why":"The ureter passes through the bladder wall at an angle, which makes another tight spot."},{"pt":"About 30 cm long, behind the peritoneum, entering the posterior wall of the bladder","why":"Like the kidney, the ureter is retroperitoneal as it runs down the back wall."},{"pt":"Wall: mucosa with transitional epithelium, smooth muscle, fibrous adventitia","why":"Transitional epithelium can stretch, and the smooth muscle pushes urine along by peristalsis."},{"pt":"The slanted entry is squeezed shut as the bladder fills, which stops backflow","why":"Pressure in a filling bladder closes the slanted part, so urine cannot flow back up toward the kidney."}]},{"id":"m5-urinary-tract-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[139],"cids":["w6-urine-path"],"t":"The bladder wall","p":"Draw the bladder in section and label its wall layers and its internal landmarks. Write what changes as it fills.","k":[{"pt":"Mucosa: transitional epithelium (urothelium) on a lamina propria","why":"Transitional epithelium is the lining that can stretch as the bladder fills."},{"pt":"Rugae that flatten as it fills","why":"The mucosa folds into rugae when the bladder is empty and stretches out smooth as it fills."},{"pt":"Detrusor muscle, layers of smooth muscle","why":"The detrusor is the muscle that contracts to squeeze urine out."},{"pt":"Adventitia outside, with peritoneum over the upper surface only","why":"The outer layer is fibrous connective tissue, and only the top of the bladder has a serosa."},{"pt":"Trigone between the two ureteral openings and the internal urethral orifice","why":"The ureters open at the back corners and the urethra begins at the front corner of the triangle."},{"pt":"Trigone stays smooth, no rugae","why":"The trigone has no rugae, so it looks smooth even when the rest of the empty bladder is folded."}]},{"id":"m5-urinary-tract-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[139],"cids":["w6-urine-path"],"t":"Sphincters and the two urethras","p":"Compare the two urethral sphincters by tissue, location, and control. Then compare the female and male urethra by length, what each carries, its parts, and where it opens.","k":[{"pt":"Internal urethral sphincter: a thickening of smooth muscle at the bladder neck, where the urethra begins","why":"It is part of the bladder's own smooth muscle, so it is involuntary."},{"pt":"External urethral sphincter: skeletal muscle around the urethra where it passes through the pelvic floor","why":"Being skeletal muscle is what gives conscious, voluntary control over urination."},{"pt":"Female urethra: about 4 cm, one short tube carrying urine only","why":"It serves only the urinary system."},{"pt":"Female external urethral orifice between the clitoris and the vaginal opening","why":"It opens in the vestibule, in front of the vaginal orifice."},{"pt":"Male urethra: about 20 cm, carrying urine and semen","why":"It is shared with the reproductive system, so it is much longer and does two jobs."},{"pt":"Male parts: prostatic urethra through the prostate, membranous urethra through the pelvic floor, spongy urethra through the corpus spongiosum, opening at the tip of the glans","why":"Each part is named for the structure it runs through on its way to the outside."}]},{"id":"m5-urinary-tract-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[139],"cids":["w6-urine-path"],"t":"Where the bladder sits","p":"Describe where the urinary bladder sits in the pelvis. Name what lies around it in a male and in a female, which surface the peritoneum covers, and where the tubes enter and leave it.","k":[{"pt":"Hollow muscular sac in the pelvic cavity, just behind the pubic symphysis","why":"The bladder sits low in the pelvic cavity, tucked just behind the front of the pelvis."},{"pt":"Male: in front of the rectum and above the prostate","why":"The prostate sits just below the bladder and wraps the first part of the urethra."},{"pt":"Female: in front of the vagina and below the uterus","why":"The uterus tips forward over the bladder, and the vagina lies behind it."},{"pt":"Peritoneum covers only the upper surface","why":"The rest of the bladder wall is covered by fibrous adventitia instead of a serosa."},{"pt":"Ureters enter its posterior wall","why":"The ureters come down the back wall of the body, so they reach the bladder from behind."},{"pt":"Urethra leaves from the internal urethral orifice at the bladder neck","why":"The internal urethral orifice is the front corner of the trigone, where the urethra begins."}]},{"id":"m5-urinary-tract-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[139,140],"cids":["w6-urine-path","w6-urinary-disorders"],"t":"A kidney stone on the move","p":"A kidney stone forms in a minor calyx and starts to move. Trace every space it passes on its way to the bladder, mark the three spots where it is most likely to stick, and predict what happens above it if it does.","k":[{"pt":"Minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis","why":"Stones form in the calyces and renal pelvis, which are the collecting spaces of the kidney."},{"pt":"Often little pain while it sits in the renal pelvis","why":"The renal pelvis is a wide funnel, so a stone there does not block or scrape much."},{"pt":"First tight spot: where the ureter leaves the renal pelvis","why":"Pain usually starts once the stone moves into the ureter, a tube only a few millimeters wide."},{"pt":"Second: where the ureter crosses the iliac vessels at the pelvic brim","why":"The ureter narrows where it passes over the iliac vessels into the pelvis."},{"pt":"Third: where the ureter enters the bladder wall","why":"The ureter passes through the bladder wall at a slant, which is the last narrow point."},{"pt":"A stuck stone backs urine up into the pelvis and calyces, called hydronephrosis","why":"Urine travels one narrow path, so a blockage swells every space above it."}]},{"id":"m5-urinary-tract-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[139,140],"cids":["w6-urine-path","w6-urinary-disorders"],"t":"An infection that climbs","p":"Bacteria from the intestine reach the female external urethral orifice. Trace every structure they would climb through to reach the kidney tissue, name the infection at the bladder and at the kidney, and explain why this happens more often in women.","k":[{"pt":"External urethral orifice, urethra, bladder","why":"A urinary tract infection climbs up the urine path in the opposite direction to the flow of urine."},{"pt":"Infection of the bladder is cystitis","why":"The infection inflames the lining of the bladder once it gets there."},{"pt":"Up a ureter to the renal pelvis","why":"The ureter is the only route from the bladder to the kidney."},{"pt":"Infection of the renal pelvis and kidney tissue is pyelonephritis","why":"Once it reaches the kidney itself, the infection has a different name."},{"pt":"The female urethra is short, about 4 cm","why":"A short tube gives bacteria a shorter climb to the bladder than the male urethra of about 20 cm."},{"pt":"It opens close to the anus","why":"Intestinal bacteria such as E. coli start out near the opening, which is one reason infections are more common in women."}]},{"id":"m5-urinary-tract-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[139,140,145],"cids":["w6-urine-path","w6-urinary-disorders","w6-male-disorders"],"t":"Three kinds of leaking","p":"Three patients leak urine. One leaks when coughing or lifting, one feels a sudden urge and cannot hold it, and one never empties fully and dribbles. Name the type of incontinence for each and the structure that is failing.","k":[{"pt":"Leaking with coughing or lifting is stress incontinence","why":"A sudden push on a full bladder shows up any weakness in what holds the outlet closed."},{"pt":"The weak structures are the pelvic floor and the external urethral sphincter","why":"The external sphincter is skeletal muscle in the pelvic floor, so a weak floor lets urine leak under pressure."},{"pt":"A sudden urge that cannot be held is urge incontinence","why":"Here the problem is the muscle that empties the bladder rather than the outlet."},{"pt":"The detrusor muscle contracts too readily","why":"The detrusor squeezes urine out, so when it contracts too easily the bladder empties before the person is ready."},{"pt":"Never emptying fully is overflow incontinence, where the bladder overfills and leaks","why":"A bladder that cannot empty keeps filling until it spills over."},{"pt":"In a male, an enlarged prostate squeezing the prostatic urethra is one cause","why":"The prostate wraps the urethra just below the bladder, so enlargement can keep the bladder from emptying."}]}]},{"id":"m5-male-repro","title":"Male reproductive system","mw":"2026-11-23","tr":"2026-11-24","prompts":[{"id":"m5-male-repro-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[141],"cids":["w6-scrotum-testes"],"t":"A testis in section","p":"Draw a testis cut open and label every structure. Then name the two cell types found in and between the seminiferous tubules, where each one sits, and what each does.","k":[{"pt":"Tunica vaginalis over the front and sides, a serous membrane","why":"It came from a pouch of peritoneum the testis carried with it into the scrotum, and it lets the testis move smoothly."},{"pt":"Tunica albuginea, the white capsule, with septa dividing the testis into lobules","why":"The tough capsule sends thin walls inward that split the testis into a few hundred wedge shaped lobules."},{"pt":"Seminiferous tubules coiled in each lobule","why":"These tubules are the only place sperm are made."},{"pt":"Rete testis at the back, efferent ductules, then the epididymis head, body, and tail","why":"The tubules empty toward the back of the testis, and the efferent ductules carry sperm out into the head of the epididymis."},{"pt":"Sustentacular (Sertoli) cells inside the tubule wall, supporting sperm and forming the blood testis barrier","why":"Sustentacular cells sustain the sperm, so they sit inside the wall with them and seal it with tight junctions."},{"pt":"Interstitial (Leydig) cells between the tubules, making testosterone","why":"Interstitial cells sit in the spaces between the tubules, outside the tubule walls."}]},{"id":"m5-male-repro-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[143],"cids":["w6-sperm-cell"],"t":"A sperm cell, front to back","p":"Draw a sperm cell and label every part from front to back. Beside each part, write what it holds or does.","k":[{"pt":"Head, holding the nucleus with 23 chromosomes","why":"The head is almost all nucleus, with almost no cytoplasm, because its job is delivering the chromosomes."},{"pt":"Acrosome, a cap of enzymes over the front of the head","why":"The enzymes let the sperm digest a path through the layers around the oocyte."},{"pt":"Neck, holding the centrioles","why":"The core of microtubules that runs the length of the tail starts here."},{"pt":"Middle piece, packed with mitochondria in a spiral","why":"The mitochondria make the ATP that powers the tail, and the middle piece is the only part packed with them."},{"pt":"Principal piece, the longest part of the tail","why":"This stretch does most of the whipping that moves the cell forward."},{"pt":"End piece, the short thin tip","why":"It finishes the flagellum, so the tail reads neck, middle piece, principal piece, end piece."}]},{"id":"m5-male-repro-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[144],"cids":["w6-male-glands-penis"],"t":"The penis in cross section","p":"Draw a cross section through the body of the penis and label the erectile columns and the urethra. Then list the three regions of the penis and the fold that covers the tip.","k":[{"pt":"Two corpora cavernosa on the dorsal side","why":"The two larger columns sit side by side above the urethra."},{"pt":"One corpus spongiosum on the ventral side, surrounding the spongy urethra","why":"Finding the urethra first tells you which column is the corpus spongiosum, because it wraps the urethra."},{"pt":"Erectile tissue is a sponge of blood spaces in connective tissue and smooth muscle","why":"When these spaces fill with blood the penis becomes erect."},{"pt":"Root: the bulb of the penis and the two crura, attached in the perineum","why":"The bulb is the back end of the corpus spongiosum, and the crura are the back ends of the corpora cavernosa fixed to the ischial and pubic rami."},{"pt":"Body, the free movable shaft","why":"The body is the part between the attached root and the tip."},{"pt":"Glans, the expanded end of the corpus spongiosum, covered by the prepuce","why":"The external urethral orifice opens on the glans, and the prepuce is the fold removed in circumcision."}]},{"id":"m5-male-repro-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[142,144],"cids":["w6-sperm-path","w6-male-glands-penis"],"t":"Sperm, start to exit","p":"Trace a sperm cell from the seminiferous tubule to the outside of the body. Name every duct and every accessory gland it passes, and say what each gland adds.","k":[{"pt":"Seminiferous tubule, rete testis, efferent ductules","why":"Sperm are made in the tubules and collected in the rete testis before the efferent ductules carry them out of the testis."},{"pt":"Epididymis, head to tail, where sperm are stored and gain the ability to swim","why":"The epididymis holds a single duct about 6 meters long coiled on the back of the testis."},{"pt":"Ductus deferens up the spermatic cord, through the inguinal canal, over the ureter, behind the bladder to its ampulla","why":"The route climbs out of the scrotum and into the pelvis before it turns down toward the prostate."},{"pt":"Ejaculatory duct through the prostate","why":"Each ampulla joins the duct of a seminal vesicle behind the bladder to form the short ejaculatory duct."},{"pt":"Prostatic, membranous, and spongy urethra to the external urethral orifice","why":"The urethra runs through the prostate, the pelvic floor, and the corpus spongiosum in that order."},{"pt":"Seminal vesicles add alkaline fluid rich in fructose, about 60 percent of semen, into the ejaculatory duct","why":"Their ducts form part of the ejaculatory duct, so their fluid joins the sperm right there."},{"pt":"Prostate adds milky fluid into the prostatic urethra, and bulbourethral glands add clear mucus into the spongy urethra","why":"Where each gland sits tells you where its fluid joins the duct system."}]},{"id":"m5-male-repro-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[141,142],"cids":["w6-scrotum-testes","w6-sperm-path"],"t":"Keeping the testes cool","p":"Compare the dartos and cremaster muscles: what tissue each is, where it sits, and what it does. Then list everything that travels in the spermatic cord.","k":[{"pt":"Dartos: smooth muscle in the subcutaneous tissue of the scrotum","why":"It is a thin layer inside the scrotal wall, and like all smooth muscle it works without conscious control."},{"pt":"Dartos wrinkles and tightens the scrotal skin to cut heat loss","why":"Because it sits in the scrotal wall, it works on the skin rather than pulling on the testis."},{"pt":"Cremaster: skeletal muscle from the internal oblique, running down the spermatic cord and looping around the testis","why":"It is a slip of an abdominal wall muscle that came down with the cord."},{"pt":"Cremaster pulls the testis up toward the warmer body wall","why":"In warm conditions both muscles relax and the testes hang lower."},{"pt":"The testes sit outside because sperm production needs a temperature a few degrees below the body core","why":"Both muscles exist to fine tune that temperature by moving the testes or tightening the skin."},{"pt":"Spermatic cord: ductus deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, lymphatic vessels and nerves, cremaster and fascia","why":"The cord is the bundle that suspends the testis and carries its duct, vessels, and nerves through the inguinal canal."}]},{"id":"m5-male-repro-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[144],"cids":["w6-male-glands-penis"],"t":"The three accessory glands","p":"Make a table of the three accessory glands. For each, write whether it is paired or single, where it sits, and where its fluid enters the duct system.","k":[{"pt":"Seminal vesicles: paired, on the posterior surface of the bladder","why":"They sit beside the ampullae of the ductus deferens, which is where they connect."},{"pt":"Each seminal vesicle duct joins the ductus deferens to form the ejaculatory duct","why":"Their fluid enters the duct system before the prostate, in the ejaculatory duct."},{"pt":"Prostate: single, about the size of a walnut, just below the bladder around the prostatic urethra","why":"Its back surface lies against the rectum."},{"pt":"Prostate ducts empty into the prostatic urethra","why":"The gland wraps that part of the urethra, so many small ducts open straight into it."},{"pt":"Bulbourethral glands: paired, pea sized, beside the membranous urethra in the pelvic floor","why":"They sit just below the prostate."},{"pt":"Bulbourethral ducts open into the spongy urethra","why":"Their clear mucus enters lower down than the other two glands, in the part of the urethra inside the penis."}]},{"id":"m5-male-repro-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[145,139],"cids":["w6-male-disorders","w6-urine-path"],"t":"A weak urine stream","p":"An older man has a weak urine stream and feels his bladder never empties. His prostate is enlarged but not cancerous. Explain the problem from the anatomy, then say where in the gland cancer more often starts and how a clinician can feel it.","k":[{"pt":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia, a noncancerous enlargement","why":"BPH is common in older men and is not a cancer."},{"pt":"The prostate sits just below the bladder and wraps the prostatic urethra","why":"Any growth of the gland presses on the tube that runs straight through it."},{"pt":"The enlargement is most often in the tissue right around the urethra, squeezing it","why":"Growth right next to the urethra narrows it more than growth farther out would."},{"pt":"The weak stream and incomplete emptying are urinary symptoms from a reproductive organ","why":"The duct system shares its final stretch with the urinary tract, so a reproductive organ can block urine."},{"pt":"Prostate cancer usually starts in the outer, posterior part of the gland","why":"That is a different region from where BPH usually grows."},{"pt":"The back of the prostate lies against the rectum, so it can be felt through the rectal wall","why":"Position decides the exam, because the posterior part is only a thin wall away from the rectum."}]},{"id":"m5-male-repro-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[145,142],"cids":["w6-male-disorders","w6-sperm-path"],"t":"An empty scrotal compartment","p":"A newborn's left scrotal compartment is empty. Trace the route a testis normally takes before birth, name the places it could have stopped, and predict what staying there would do.","k":[{"pt":"Testes develop high on the posterior abdominal wall near the kidneys","why":"This starting point is why each testicular artery branches from the abdominal aorta."},{"pt":"Late in fetal development each testis moves down through the inguinal canal into the scrotum","why":"The testis pulls its duct and vessels behind it, which forms the spermatic cord."},{"pt":"Cryptorchidism: the testis stopped in the abdomen or in the inguinal canal","why":"These are the two places along the normal route where a testis can stall."},{"pt":"An undescended testis stays at core body temperature","why":"The scrotum normally keeps the testes a few degrees cooler than the core."},{"pt":"Sperm production is impaired and the risk of testicular cancer rises","why":"If it is not corrected, the warmth can damage the sperm forming cells."}]},{"id":"m5-male-repro-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[145,141,142],"cids":["w6-male-disorders","w6-scrotum-testes","w6-sperm-path"],"t":"Three swollen scrotums","p":"Three men have a swollen scrotum. In one, fluid has collected around the testis. In one, the veins above the testis are swollen and twisted on the left. In one, a loop of bowel has slid down. Name each disorder and the exact structure involved.","k":[{"pt":"Fluid around the testis is a hydrocele","why":"The fluid is serous fluid, the same kind a serous membrane normally holds."},{"pt":"The fluid collects between the two layers of the tunica vaginalis","why":"The tunica vaginalis has a parietal and a visceral layer with a space between them."},{"pt":"Swollen, twisted veins above the testis are a varicocele of the pampiniform plexus","why":"The pampiniform plexus is the network of veins around the testicular artery in the spermatic cord."},{"pt":"Varicoceles are more common on the left","why":"The left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein rather than straight into the inferior vena cava."},{"pt":"Bowel in the scrotum is an inguinal hernia","why":"The inguinal canal is a weak spot in the abdominal wall left by the descent of the testis."},{"pt":"The bowel follows the spermatic cord down the inguinal canal","why":"The canal already carries the cord into the scrotum, so it gives the bowel a ready path."}]}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro","title":"Female reproductive system","mw":"2026-11-23","tr":"2026-11-24","prompts":[{"id":"m5-female-repro-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[146,147,148],"cids":["w6-ovary","w6-ovum-path","w6-uterus"],"t":"Female internal organs","p":"Draw the female internal reproductive organs and label every structure. Then trace the path of an oocyte from release to the uterus.","k":[{"pt":"Ovary against the lateral pelvic wall, just below the open end of a uterine tube","why":"Each ovary sits on one side of the uterus, close to the fimbriae that will catch the oocyte."},{"pt":"Uterine tube: fimbriae, infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus","why":"The tube runs from an upper corner of the uterus out toward the ovary inside the upper edge of the broad ligament."},{"pt":"Uterus: fundus, body, cervix","why":"The fundus is the top above the tube entries, and the cervix is the narrow part that projects into the vagina."},{"pt":"Wall layers: endometrium, myometrium, perimetrium","why":"The lining, the thick smooth muscle, and the outer serous layer are labeled from the inside out."},{"pt":"Vagina below the cervix, plus the broad, round, and ovarian ligaments","why":"The ligaments hold the uterus and ovaries in place in the pelvic cavity."},{"pt":"Ovulation releases the oocyte into the pelvic cavity, and the fimbriae sweep it into the infundibulum","why":"The outer end of the tube is not attached to the ovary, so the tube has to catch the oocyte."},{"pt":"Then ampulla, isthmus, intramural part, uterine cavity","why":"The tube narrows as it nears the uterus and passes through the uterine wall into the cavity."}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[146],"cids":["w6-ovary"],"t":"An ovary in section","p":"Draw an ovary cut open and label the follicle stages in order around the cortex, plus what is left after ovulation.","k":[{"pt":"Germinal epithelium, a single layer of cuboidal cells on the surface","why":"Despite its name, this surface layer does not make oocytes."},{"pt":"Tunica albuginea, then the cortex with the follicles, then the medulla with the vessels","why":"The follicles live in the cortex, and the loose medulla in the center carries the blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves."},{"pt":"Primordial follicle: oocyte with one layer of flat cells, the smallest and most numerous","why":"These sit in the outer cortex and are the starting stage."},{"pt":"Primary follicle: cuboidal granulosa cells and a zona pellucida","why":"The follicle cells change shape and a clear layer forms around the oocyte."},{"pt":"Secondary follicle: several layers of granulosa cells, a theca, and small fluid pockets","why":"The fluid pockets between the granulosa cells are the clue that sets it apart from a primary follicle."},{"pt":"Mature (vesicular or Graafian) follicle with one large antrum, bulging from the surface","why":"The small pockets have joined into one big cavity, and the oocyte sits to one side ringed by the corona radiata."},{"pt":"Corpus luteum after ovulation, then the corpus albicans, a pale scar","why":"The granulosa and theca cells left behind form the corpus luteum, which later breaks down into fibrous scar."}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[148],"cids":["w6-uterus"],"t":"The uterine wall","p":"Draw the uterus in section and label the three wall layers and the two parts of the innermost one. Then name the regions of the uterus from top to bottom.","k":[{"pt":"Perimetrium outside, the serous layer that is part of the visceral peritoneum","why":"The outer layer of the uterus is peritoneum that drapes over it."},{"pt":"Myometrium, thick smooth muscle, thickest in the fundus","why":"The muscle is the bulk of the wall and is heaviest at the top."},{"pt":"Endometrium with a stratum functionalis next to the cavity and a stratum basalis next to the myometrium","why":"The two layers are named for their jobs, one that comes and goes and one that stays."},{"pt":"Functional layer sheds in menstruation, and the basal layer rebuilds it each cycle","why":"The basal layer stays in place against the muscle, so it can regrow the layer that was lost."},{"pt":"Fundus above the tube entries, then the body holding the uterine cavity, then the isthmus and cervix","why":"The regions run from the rounded top down to the narrow part that projects into the vagina."},{"pt":"Cervical canal from the internal os to the external os","why":"The internal os opens into the uterine cavity and the external os opens into the vagina."}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[150],"cids":["w6-mammary"],"t":"The breast","p":"Draw the breast in sagittal section and label every structure inside it. Then trace milk from where it is made to the outside.","k":[{"pt":"15 to 20 lobes in a ring around the nipple, separated by fat","why":"The gland is built in nested units, and the lobe is the largest one."},{"pt":"Lobules inside each lobe, and alveoli inside each lobule","why":"Alveoli are the small sacs of gland cells that secrete milk, nested inside lobules inside lobes."},{"pt":"One lactiferous duct per lobe, widening into a lactiferous sinus before the nipple","why":"Each lobe drains by its own duct, and the sinus is where milk collects just behind the nipple."},{"pt":"Nipple, and the areola with areolar glands","why":"The ducts open on the nipple, and the bumps on the pigmented areola are a kind of sebaceous gland."},{"pt":"Suspensory (Cooper) ligaments from the skin to the fascia over the pectoralis major","why":"These bands run between the lobes and support the breast."},{"pt":"Adipose tissue mostly setting the size","why":"The amount of fat, not the amount of gland, mostly sets the size of the breast."},{"pt":"Milk path: alveoli, lobule ducts, lactiferous duct, lactiferous sinus, nipple","why":"The route follows the nesting outward from the smallest unit to the opening."}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro-L1-5","lv":1,"comps":[149,144],"cids":["w6-vagina-vulva","w6-male-glands-penis"],"t":"The perineum","p":"Draw the perineum and mark the two triangles. Name the boundaries and label the external structures in each sex.","k":[{"pt":"Diamond shaped region between the pubic symphysis in front and the coccyx behind","why":"These two bony points mark the front and back corners of the diamond."},{"pt":"Urogenital triangle in front, holding the external genitalia","why":"The front half of the diamond is where the genital and urinary openings are."},{"pt":"Anal triangle behind, holding the anus","why":"The back half holds only the anal opening."},{"pt":"Male: scrotum, and the root of the penis with the bulb and the two crura attached to the ischial and pubic rami","why":"The root is the part of the penis fixed to the pelvis inside the perineum."},{"pt":"Female: mons pubis, labia majora and minora, clitoris, vestibule","why":"Together these structures are the vulva."},{"pt":"Female midline, front to back: clitoris, external urethral orifice, vaginal orifice, then the anus","why":"The urethral and vaginal openings both sit in the vestibule, with the anus behind it in the anal triangle."}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[149,141,144,146],"cids":["w6-vagina-vulva","w6-scrotum-testes","w6-male-glands-penis","w6-ovary"],"t":"Matched pairs","p":"Build a mind map pairing male and female reproductive structures that match each other. Put the female structure on one side of each branch and its male match on the other.","k":[{"pt":"Ovary and testis, the gonads","why":"Both are the paired organs that make the sex cells."},{"pt":"Labia majora and scrotum","why":"Both are the outer covering, the labia majora as folds of skin and fat and the scrotum as a pouch of skin and subcutaneous tissue."},{"pt":"Clitoris and penis","why":"Both are erectile organs, and only the tip of the clitoris, its glans, shows on the surface."},{"pt":"Greater vestibular glands and bulbourethral glands","why":"Both are paired pea sized glands whose mucus lubricates the outlet."},{"pt":"Ovarian artery and testicular artery","why":"Each carries blood straight from the abdominal aorta to its gonad, the ovarian artery in the suspensory ligament and the testicular artery in the spermatic cord."}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[146,148],"cids":["w6-ovary","w6-uterus"],"t":"What holds the ovary and uterus","p":"List every ligament that holds the ovary or the uterus. For each, write what it runs from, what it runs to, and what it does.","k":[{"pt":"Broad ligament: a fold of peritoneum from the sides of the uterus to the lateral pelvic walls","why":"The same sheet also holds the tubes as the mesosalpinx and the ovaries as the mesovarium."},{"pt":"Ovarian ligament: ovary to the side of the uterus, just below where the uterine tube joins it","why":"It is a fibrous cord, so it tethers the ovary to the uterus rather than to the pelvic wall."},{"pt":"Suspensory ligament: ovary to the lateral pelvic wall, carrying the ovarian artery and vein","why":"It suspends the ovary from the wall, and the ovarian artery, a branch of the abdominal aorta, reaches the ovary through it."},{"pt":"Round ligament: uterus just below each tube, through the inguinal canal, ending in the labia majora","why":"It helps keep the uterus tipped forward over the bladder."},{"pt":"Cardinal ligaments: cervix and upper vagina to the lateral pelvic walls","why":"These are the main support that keeps the uterus from sliding down."},{"pt":"Uterosacral ligaments: cervix back to the sacrum","why":"They anchor the cervix to the back of the pelvis."}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[147],"cids":["w6-ovum-path"],"t":"Along the uterine tube","p":"Name the regions of the uterine tube from the ovary to the uterus. For each, write what it looks like and what happens to the oocyte there.","k":[{"pt":"Fimbriae on the edge of the funnel sweep over the ovary and draw the oocyte in","why":"The tube is not attached to the ovary, so these finger shaped projections have to catch the oocyte."},{"pt":"Infundibulum, the funnel shaped outer end, open to the pelvic cavity","why":"The open funnel carries the oocyte into the tube."},{"pt":"Ampulla, the widest and longest part, the usual site of fertilization","why":"This is where sperm usually meet the oocyte."},{"pt":"Isthmus, the narrow, thick walled part next to the uterus","why":"An isthmus of land is a narrow neck, and so is this part of the tube."},{"pt":"Intramural part passing through the uterine wall into the uterine cavity","why":"The last short stretch of the tube is inside the wall of the uterus itself."},{"pt":"Cilia beating toward the uterus and waves of smooth muscle move the oocyte along","why":"The lining and the wall work together to carry the oocyte over a few days."}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[149],"cids":["w6-vagina-vulva"],"t":"The vagina and vestibule","p":"Describe where the vagina sits and how it meets the cervix above and the vestibule below. Name the recess around the cervix, the fold at the opening, and the glands beside the opening.","k":[{"pt":"Thin walled fibromuscular tube about 10 cm long, from the cervix to the vestibule","why":"It runs down and forward from the cervix to the outside."},{"pt":"Behind the bladder and urethra, in front of the rectum","why":"Its neighbors explain why the urethral opening sits in front of the vaginal opening."},{"pt":"Lining of stratified squamous epithelium in folds called rugae, with smooth muscle around it","why":"A tough, folded lining fits a canal that has to stretch."},{"pt":"Fornix, the recess ringing the cervix, with the posterior fornix the deepest","why":"The cervix pokes into the top of the vagina, and the fornix is the space around it."},{"pt":"Hymen, a thin fold of mucous membrane partly covering the vaginal orifice","why":"Its shape and size vary a great deal from person to person."},{"pt":"Greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands on each side of the vaginal orifice, ducts opening into the vestibule","why":"Their mucus lubricates the vestibule, the space between the labia minora."}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[147,148,149],"cids":["w6-ovum-path","w6-uterus","w6-vagina-vulva"],"t":"An infection that reaches the pelvis","p":"An infection starts in the vagina and spreads into the pelvic cavity. Trace every structure it passes in order, and explain what feature of the uterine tube makes this possible.","k":[{"pt":"Vagina, then the external os and the cervical canal","why":"The cervix projects into the top of the vagina, so the external os is the first opening upward."},{"pt":"Internal os into the uterine cavity","why":"The cervical canal opens into the uterine cavity at the internal os."},{"pt":"Into the tube through the intramural part, then the isthmus and the ampulla","why":"Each tube opens into an upper corner of the uterine cavity."},{"pt":"Out through the infundibulum into the pelvic cavity","why":"The funnel at the outer end of the tube opens straight into the pelvic cavity near the ovary."},{"pt":"The infundibulum does not seal onto the ovary","why":"The same opening that lets the fimbriae catch an oocyte lets an infection out."},{"pt":"This spreading infection is pelvic inflammatory disease, PID","why":"The female tract is open to the pelvic cavity, so an infection can climb all the way out of it."}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[150],"cids":["w6-mammary"],"t":"A dimple over a lump","p":"A clinician sees a small dimple in the skin of a patient's breast over a lump. Explain from the anatomy what is pulling the skin in, and name where most breast cancers start.","k":[{"pt":"Suspensory (Cooper) ligaments run from the skin through the breast to the fascia over the pectoralis major","why":"These bands connect the skin to deeper tissue, so a pull on them reaches the surface."},{"pt":"They run between the lobes","why":"A lump growing among the lobes sits right beside these bands."},{"pt":"A tumor pulling on a suspensory ligament dimples the skin over it","why":"The ligament is anchored in the skin, so when it is tugged the skin is drawn inward."},{"pt":"Most breast cancer starts in the ducts","why":"The lactiferous ducts carry milk from each lobe toward the nipple."},{"pt":"Each lobe has one lactiferous duct running toward the nipple","why":"Knowing that the ducts run from each lobe to the nipple tells you where these cancers grow."}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[148,146],"cids":["w6-uterus","w6-ovary"],"t":"Problems by layer","p":"Three patients: one has noncancerous growths in the uterine muscle, one has endometrial tissue growing on an ovary, and one has a malignant tumor in the part of the uterus that projects into the vagina. Name each disorder and the layer or region of the uterus involved.","k":[{"pt":"Noncancerous growths in the muscle are uterine fibroids","why":"Fibroids are tumors of smooth muscle."},{"pt":"Fibroids grow in the myometrium","why":"The myometrium is the thick middle layer of smooth muscle in the uterine wall."},{"pt":"Endometrial tissue on the ovary is endometriosis","why":"Endometriosis means endometrial tissue growing outside the uterus, for example on the ovaries or pelvic peritoneum."},{"pt":"The endometrium normally lines only the uterine cavity","why":"It is the inner mucous lining, so finding it outside the uterus is what makes this a disorder."},{"pt":"A malignant tumor there is cervical cancer","why":"The cervix is the narrow lower part of the uterus that projects into the vagina."}]}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue","title":"Nervous system organization and nervous tissue","mw":"2026-11-30","tr":"2026-12-01","prompts":[{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[174],"cids":["w8-neuron-parts"],"t":"A neuron, labeled","p":"Draw a multipolar neuron and label every part. Add an arrow showing the direction a signal travels.","k":[{"pt":"Cell body with the nucleus and a large dark nucleolus","why":"The cell body is the metabolic center that makes the proteins the whole neuron needs."},{"pt":"Nissl bodies in the cell body and dendrites","why":"They are clusters of rough ER and ribosomes, which is where those proteins are made."},{"pt":"Dendrites, short and branched, carrying signals toward the cell body","why":"A multipolar neuron has many dendrites to receive incoming signals."},{"pt":"Axon hillock, the pale cone with no Nissl bodies","why":"That pale cone is how you find the axon on a slide, and impulses begin at the trigger zone where it meets the axon."},{"pt":"Axon, the single long process, with axon collaterals","why":"The axon carries the impulse away from the cell body, and collaterals are its side branches."},{"pt":"Axon terminals ending in synaptic end bulbs","why":"The end bulbs are where the signal passes to the next cell."},{"pt":"Arrow: dendrites to cell body to axon hillock to axon to terminals","why":"The signal always enters on the dendrites and leaves on the axon."}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[177],"cids":["w8-neuroglia"],"t":"Six kinds of glia","p":"List the six kinds of neuroglia. Sort them into CNS and PNS and write one job for each.","k":[{"pt":"CNS: astrocytes, star shaped and the most numerous, helping form the blood brain barrier","why":"Their processes wrap neurons and capillaries, which is how they hold neurons in place and take part in the barrier."},{"pt":"CNS: oligodendrocytes, making myelin for segments of several axons","why":"Each of their few long processes wraps a different axon."},{"pt":"CNS: microglia, phagocytes that clear microbes and debris","why":"They are the central nervous system's own immune defense."},{"pt":"CNS: ependymal cells lining the ventricles and central canal, helping make CSF","why":"In the choroid plexuses they make CSF, and their cilia help it circulate."},{"pt":"PNS: Schwann cells, making myelin around one segment of one axon","why":"A long PNS axon needs a whole row of Schwann cells along it."},{"pt":"PNS: satellite cells, flat cells around neuron cell bodies in ganglia, supporting them and regulating their surroundings","why":"They wrap the cell bodies, so they control the exchange of materials between those cell bodies and the fluid around them."}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[175],"cids":["w8-synapse-parts"],"t":"A chemical synapse","p":"Draw a chemical synapse and label every part on both sides of the gap. Then name the three synapse types by where the axon lands.","k":[{"pt":"Synaptic end bulb at the tip of the axon terminal","why":"The end bulb is the sending side of the synapse."},{"pt":"Synaptic vesicles inside the end bulb, storing neurotransmitter","why":"Only the presynaptic side has vesicles, which is why a chemical synapse passes its signal one way."},{"pt":"Presynaptic membrane facing the cleft","why":"This is the membrane of the end bulb where the vesicles release their contents."},{"pt":"Synaptic cleft, about 20 to 50 nanometers wide","why":"The two cells do not touch, so the signal has to cross the gap as a chemical."},{"pt":"Postsynaptic membrane with neurotransmitter receptors","why":"The receiving cell can be another neuron, a muscle fiber, or a gland cell."},{"pt":"Axodendritic (most common), axosomatic, axoaxonic","why":"Each name starts with axo for the sending axon and ends with the part it lands on."}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[173],"cids":["w8-ns-organization"],"t":"The nervous system outline","p":"Build an outline of the nervous system from the top down: CNS and PNS, then the sensory and motor sides, then every motor division. Write what each motor division controls.","k":[{"pt":"CNS: brain and spinal cord","why":"The CNS is where information is processed and commands are issued."},{"pt":"PNS: nerves, ganglia, sensory receptors, with 12 pairs of cranial and 31 pairs of spinal nerves","why":"The PNS is every neural structure outside the brain and cord and carries information between them and the body."},{"pt":"Sensory (afferent) division toward the CNS, motor (efferent) division away from it","why":"Afferent arrives at the CNS and efferent exits it."},{"pt":"Somatic nervous system: skeletal muscle, voluntary","why":"The motor divisions are named for what they run, and somatic runs the skeletal muscles."},{"pt":"Autonomic nervous system: smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands, with sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions","why":"It works without conscious control, and many organs receive both divisions."},{"pt":"Enteric nervous system: the wall of the digestive tract, able to run on its own","why":"The ANS connects to it and adjusts it, and some textbooks count it as part of the ANS."}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[176],"cids":["w8-neuron-classification"],"t":"Neurons by shape and by direction","p":"Classify neurons two ways, by shape and by direction. Give an example and a body location for each type.","k":[{"pt":"Multipolar: many dendrites and one axon, the most common type, such as an anterior horn motor neuron","why":"Pyramidal cells of the cerebral cortex and Purkinje cells of the cerebellum are also multipolar."},{"pt":"Bipolar: one dendrite and one axon, in the retina, olfactory epithelium, and inner ear","why":"Bipolar neurons are rare and found only in special sense organs."},{"pt":"Unipolar: one process that splits like a T, such as a sensory neuron with its cell body in a dorsal root ganglion","why":"One branch starts at a receptor and the other enters the CNS."},{"pt":"Sensory (afferent): toward the CNS, mostly unipolar, such as a touch neuron from a fingertip","why":"Sensory is afferent, which is half of the SAME memory tip."},{"pt":"Motor (efferent): away from the CNS, multipolar, such as an anterior horn neuron whose axon runs to the biceps brachii","why":"Motor is efferent, the other half of SAME."},{"pt":"Interneurons: entirely inside the CNS, multipolar, such as the cord neurons linking a withdrawal reflex","why":"Most neurons in the body are interneurons, connecting sensory neurons to motor neurons."}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[178,177],"cids":["w8-myelination","w8-neuroglia"],"t":"Myelin in the PNS and CNS","p":"Compare myelin in the PNS and the CNS: which cell makes it, how many axons each cell wraps, whether there is a neurolemma, and whether a cut axon can regrow.","k":[{"pt":"Myelin is many layers of glial membrane laid in segments, with nodes of Ranvier between","why":"The impulse jumps from node to node, which is saltatory conduction."},{"pt":"PNS: a Schwann cell wraps one segment of one axon","why":"A long PNS axon needs a row of Schwann cells along its length."},{"pt":"CNS: an oligodendrocyte wraps segments of several axons","why":"One oligodendrocyte sends processes out to many axons at once."},{"pt":"Neurolemma, the outer layer of a Schwann cell, is found only in the PNS","why":"It holds the Schwann cell's nucleus and cytoplasm outside the myelin, and oligodendrocytes have none."},{"pt":"PNS: a cut axon can regrow if the cell body survives, guided by a regeneration tube","why":"The Schwann cells line up and their neurolemma forms the tube that leads the axon back to its target."},{"pt":"CNS: regrowth is very limited","why":"There is no neurolemma to guide it, oligodendrocytes release substances that block it, and astrocytes fill the area with scar."}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[179,180],"cids":["w8-tissue-collections","w8-gray-white-matter"],"t":"Four names for groups of neurons","p":"Make a two by two grid with cell bodies and axons down the side and CNS and PNS across the top. Fill in the name for each box with an example, and mark which CNS boxes are gray matter and which are white.","k":[{"pt":"Cell bodies in the CNS: nucleus, such as a cranial nerve nucleus or the basal nuclei","why":"A nucleus here means a cluster of neuron cell bodies, not the nucleus inside one cell."},{"pt":"Cell bodies in the PNS: ganglion, such as a dorsal root ganglion","why":"Satellite cells surround the cell bodies in a ganglion."},{"pt":"Axons in the CNS: tract, such as the corticospinal tract","why":"Tracts are bundles of axons running inside the brain and cord."},{"pt":"Axons in the PNS: nerve, such as the sciatic or vagus nerve","why":"A nerve holds axons but no cell bodies, which sit in the CNS or in ganglia."},{"pt":"Nuclei are gray matter, and tracts make up the white matter","why":"Gray is where the cell bodies are and white is where the myelinated axons are."},{"pt":"Basal ganglia is an older name that breaks the rule","why":"They sit in the CNS, so by the rule they should be called basal nuclei."}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue-L2-5","lv":2,"comps":[180,151],"cids":["w8-gray-white-matter","w7-brain-organization"],"t":"Gray and white, brain and cord","p":"Draw a cross section of the spinal cord and a coronal slice of the cerebrum side by side. Shade the gray matter, leave the white matter blank, and write what each one is made of. Then say which of the two plans the cerebellum follows.","k":[{"pt":"Gray matter: neuron cell bodies, dendrites, unmyelinated axons, axon terminals, and glia","why":"Gray matter is where signals are received and processed."},{"pt":"White matter: mostly myelinated axons, pale because myelin is rich in lipid","why":"White matter carries signals from one region to another."},{"pt":"Spinal cord: gray inside in an H or butterfly shape, white outside in columns","why":"In the cord the gray core is surrounded by the tracts."},{"pt":"Cerebrum: gray cortex on the outside, only 2 to 4 mm thick, following every gyrus","why":"The arrangement in the cerebrum is the reverse of the cord, so name the organ before the layer."},{"pt":"Cerebrum: deep white matter with islands of gray, the basal nuclei and thalamus","why":"Deep nuclei are gray matter buried inside the white matter."},{"pt":"Cerebellum: same plan as the cerebrum, gray cortex outside and the arbor vitae inside","why":"The cerebellar white matter branches like a tree, which gives it its name."}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[178,177],"cids":["w8-myelination","w8-neuroglia"],"t":"A cut arm nerve and MS","p":"One patient cuts a nerve in the forearm. Another has multiple sclerosis, which destroys myelin around axons in the brain and spinal cord. Predict which one is more likely to recover lost function and explain why from the glial cells involved.","k":[{"pt":"The forearm nerve is in the PNS, myelinated by Schwann cells","why":"Any nerve out in the limb is part of the PNS."},{"pt":"The axon beyond the cut breaks down, but the cell body survives","why":"The part of an axon cut off from its cell body dies because it depends on the cell body for proteins."},{"pt":"Schwann cells line up and their neurolemma forms a regeneration tube","why":"The tube guides the regrowing axon back toward its target."},{"pt":"MS damages CNS myelin, which is made by oligodendrocytes","why":"Myelin in the brain and cord comes from oligodendrocytes, not Schwann cells."},{"pt":"The CNS has no neurolemma, oligodendrocytes block regrowth, and astrocytes form scar","why":"All three work against repair in the CNS."},{"pt":"The forearm nerve can regrow, while the CNS damage usually cannot be repaired","why":"The presence or absence of the neurolemma is the deciding difference."}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[177,174],"cids":["w8-neuroglia","w8-neuron-parts"],"t":"Where brain tumors come from","p":"A scan shows a primary tumor growing in the brain. Predict whether it more likely came from neurons or from glia, and explain from what each cell can and cannot do. Then predict which injury is more likely to kill a whole neuron, one close to the cell body or one far out on the axon.","k":[{"pt":"Mature neurons generally do not divide","why":"A tumor needs cells that can keep dividing."},{"pt":"Glia can still divide","why":"Unlike mature neurons, glial cells keep the ability to divide."},{"pt":"So most nervous tissue tumors come from glia, and a glioma is a tumor of glial cells","why":"The glioma is the most common primary malignant brain tumor."},{"pt":"The axon depends on the cell body for its proteins","why":"The cell body is the metabolic center that keeps the whole neuron alive."},{"pt":"An injury close to the cell body is more likely to kill the whole neuron","why":"Damage near the cell body threatens the part that supports everything else, while a cut far out only loses the distant piece of axon."}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[183,164],"cids":["w8-neuronal-pools","w7-medulla"],"t":"A stroke and the crossing","p":"A stroke damages the motor cortex of the left hemisphere. Predict which side of the body is weak and say where the motor fibers crossed. Then decide whether an anterior horn motor neuron that receives input from many brain regions shows convergence or divergence.","k":[{"pt":"The right side of the body is weak","why":"The main motor pathway crosses, so each side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body."},{"pt":"The corticospinal tract is a descending pathway from the cortex to the spinal cord","why":"Descending pathways carry motor commands down."},{"pt":"Most corticospinal axons cross at the pyramidal decussation in the medulla","why":"By the time they reach the spinal cord, most motor fibers are already on the other side."},{"pt":"Decussation is the crossing of a pathway to the opposite side of the CNS","why":"Spinothalamic axons also cross, but in the spinal cord close to where they enter."},{"pt":"The anterior horn motor neuron shows convergence","why":"Several presynaptic neurons feed onto one neuron, which combines their input into a single output to a muscle."},{"pt":"Divergence is the opposite, one neuron spreading its signal to many","why":"A single motor neuron in the brain reaching many motor neurons in the cord is divergence."}]}]},{"id":"m5-cerebrum","title":"Cerebrum: lobes, cortex areas and deep structures","mw":"2026-11-30","tr":"2026-12-01","prompts":[{"id":"m5-cerebrum-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[153,154],"cids":["w7-cerebrum-surface","w7-cortex-functional-areas"],"t":"Lobes and landmarks","p":"Draw the brain from the side and label all the lobes, the two major sulci, the gyri on either side of the central sulcus, and the two language areas. Write what each of those gyri and areas does.","k":[{"pt":"Frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes, with the insula named deep in the lateral sulcus","why":"Four lobes are named for the bone over them, and the fifth is hidden under the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes."},{"pt":"Central sulcus between the frontal and parietal lobes","why":"It runs from the top of the hemisphere down toward the lateral sulcus without a break."},{"pt":"Lateral sulcus with the temporal lobe below it","why":"It is the deepest groove on the side, so find it first on a specimen."},{"pt":"Precentral gyrus, the primary motor cortex, in front of the central sulcus","why":"Pre means before, so this gyrus comes before the sulcus and moves the body."},{"pt":"Postcentral gyrus, the primary somatosensory cortex, behind it","why":"Post means after, and this gyrus receives touch, pressure, pain, temperature, and position."},{"pt":"Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus and Wernicke's area in the posterior superior temporal gyrus, usually on the left","why":"One plans the movements of speech and the other is needed to understand language."}]},{"id":"m5-cerebrum-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[151,155,157,158,159,161],"cids":["w7-brain-organization","w7-white-matter-tracts","w7-diencephalon","w7-cerebellum","w7-limbic-system","w7-brainstem-regions"],"t":"Brain, midsagittal","p":"Draw the brain in midsagittal view and label every structure you can. Then bracket the three regions of the brainstem.","k":[{"pt":"Cerebrum with its gray cortex and deep white matter, and the corpus callosum as the C shaped band","why":"The corpus callosum is the arched white band above the diencephalon that joins the two hemispheres."},{"pt":"Septum pellucidum hanging below the corpus callosum, with the fornix along its lower edge","why":"The septum is the thin sheet that separates the two lateral ventricles."},{"pt":"Thalamus with the massa intermedia, hypothalamus below it, pineal gland, pituitary on the infundibulum","why":"The diencephalon is hidden by the hemispheres, so the midsagittal cut is where you see it best."},{"pt":"Midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata bracketed as the brainstem","why":"Read them top down, M, P, M, and the medulla is the one that becomes the spinal cord."},{"pt":"Cerebellum with the vermis and arbor vitae, and the fourth ventricle in front of it","why":"The cut runs down the vermis, so the branching white matter shows clearly."},{"pt":"Cingulate gyrus, the first gyrus above the corpus callosum","why":"It is part of the limbic ring on the medial surface."}]},{"id":"m5-cerebrum-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[156,155],"cids":["w7-basal-ganglia","w7-white-matter-tracts"],"t":"Deep gray matter","p":"Draw a coronal slice of the brain and label the basal nuclei and the internal capsule. Name each nucleus and write what the group is broadly for.","k":[{"pt":"Caudate nucleus bulging into the lateral wall of the lateral ventricle","why":"Its large head sits in the wall of the anterior horn, and its body and tail follow the ventricle around."},{"pt":"Putamen, the outer part of the lentiform nucleus","why":"It is the darker part that wraps around the outside."},{"pt":"Globus pallidus, the inner, paler part","why":"Pallidus means pale, which is how you tell it from the putamen."},{"pt":"Lentiform nucleus is putamen plus globus pallidus, and the striatum is caudate plus putamen","why":"The striatum is named for the striped bands of gray that cross the internal capsule between them."},{"pt":"Internal capsule between the caudate and thalamus on its inner side and the lentiform nucleus on its outer side","why":"The capsule is white matter running between the gray masses."},{"pt":"Thalamus beside them, not part of the basal nuclei","why":"The thalamus is part of the diencephalon even though it is deep gray matter too."},{"pt":"They work with the motor cortex to start wanted movements, hold back unwanted ones, and set muscle tone","why":"This is why loss in these movement circuits shows up as a movement problem."}]},{"id":"m5-cerebrum-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[155],"cids":["w7-white-matter-tracts"],"t":"Three kinds of white matter","p":"Compare the three classes of cerebral white matter fibers. For each, write what it connects, one named example, and where you would see it on a specimen.","k":[{"pt":"Association fibers stay within one hemisphere, gyrus to gyrus or lobe to lobe","why":"They never cross the midline."},{"pt":"Example: the arcuate fasciculus linking Broca's and Wernicke's areas","why":"It connects a frontal lobe area to a temporal lobe area on the same side."},{"pt":"Commissural fibers cross the midline and join matching areas of the two hemispheres","why":"Commissure means a joining."},{"pt":"Corpus callosum, the largest, seen on the midsagittal cut, plus the anterior and posterior commissures","why":"The corpus callosum is the arched band above the diencephalon and the roof of the lateral ventricles."},{"pt":"Projection fibers connect the cortex with the diencephalon, brainstem, and cord in both directions","why":"They project down from the cortex or up to it."},{"pt":"Corona radiata fanning under the cortex, gathering into the internal capsule, continuing as the crus cerebri","why":"The internal capsule is seen on a coronal or horizontal section, bent into a V between the deep nuclei."}]},{"id":"m5-cerebrum-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[154],"cids":["w7-cortex-functional-areas"],"t":"Map the cortex","p":"Make a table of the functional areas of the cortex. For each, write its lobe, the gyrus or landmark where it sits, and whether it is a motor, primary sensory, or association area.","k":[{"pt":"Primary motor cortex: motor, precentral gyrus, frontal lobe, with the premotor cortex just in front of it","why":"Front moves, so the motor strip is in front of the central sulcus."},{"pt":"Primary somatosensory cortex: primary sensory, postcentral gyrus, parietal lobe, with its association area just behind","why":"Back feels, so the sensory strip is behind the central sulcus."},{"pt":"Primary visual cortex: primary sensory, along the calcarine sulcus on the medial occipital lobe, ringed by the visual association area","why":"It sits on the medial surface, so you will not find it from the side view."},{"pt":"Primary auditory cortex: primary sensory, superior temporal gyrus along the lower edge of the lateral sulcus","why":"The auditory area sits right at the top of the temporal lobe."},{"pt":"Gustatory cortex in the insula and lower postcentral gyrus, and olfactory cortex on the medial temporal lobe near the uncus, both primary sensory","why":"Smell reaches its cortex from the olfactory bulb and tract under the frontal lobe."},{"pt":"Broca's area, a motor area in the inferior frontal gyrus, and Wernicke's area, an association area in the posterior superior temporal gyrus, both usually left","why":"Broca plans the movements of speech, and Wernicke is needed to understand language."},{"pt":"Prefrontal cortex: association, at the front of the frontal lobe","why":"This large association area handles planning, judgment, and personality."}]},{"id":"m5-cerebrum-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[152],"cids":[],"t":"From neural tube to brain","p":"Trace brain development from the neural tube. Name the three primary vesicles, the five secondary vesicles, and the adult structures and ventricle that come from each secondary vesicle.","k":[{"pt":"Neural tube from ectoderm, the front end becoming the brain and the rest the spinal cord","why":"The tube stays hollow, and its cavity becomes the ventricles and the central canal."},{"pt":"Primary vesicles: prosencephalon, mesencephalon, rhombencephalon","why":"These are the forebrain, midbrain, and hindbrain swellings of about the fourth week."},{"pt":"Telencephalon: cerebrum, with the lateral ventricles","why":"The telencephalon grows far more than the rest and covers the diencephalon."},{"pt":"Diencephalon: thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus, with the third ventricle","why":"Both the telencephalon and the diencephalon split off from the prosencephalon."},{"pt":"Mesencephalon: midbrain, with the cerebral aqueduct","why":"The mesencephalon is the one primary vesicle that does not divide."},{"pt":"Metencephalon: pons and cerebellum, with the upper fourth ventricle","why":"Met means after, and this vesicle comes off the rhombencephalon."},{"pt":"Myelencephalon: medulla oblongata, with the lower fourth ventricle leading into the central canal","why":"Myel means marrow, for the part next to the spinal cord."}]},{"id":"m5-cerebrum-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[159],"cids":["w7-limbic-system"],"t":"The limbic ring","p":"Name the limbic structures and place each one around the diencephalon. Write where each sits and its main role.","k":[{"pt":"Cingulate gyrus arching over the corpus callosum on the medial surface","why":"It is the first gyrus above the corpus callosum and helps connect emotion with behavior."},{"pt":"Parahippocampal gyrus continuing it into the temporal lobe, with its front end hooked into the uncus","why":"Together these gyri make the C shaped ring that gives the system its name."},{"pt":"Hippocampus in the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle, needed to form new long term memories","why":"It is a curved ridge of cortex rolled into the medial temporal lobe, shaped like a seahorse."},{"pt":"Fornix carrying hippocampal output under the corpus callosum to the mammillary bodies","why":"It runs along the lower edge of the septum pellucidum on a midsagittal section."},{"pt":"Amygdala deep to the uncus, just in front of the hippocampus, for fear and emotion","why":"Amygdala means almond, and it links emotion to memory."},{"pt":"Limbus means border or ring","why":"These structures ring the diencephalon and the top of the brainstem."}]},{"id":"m5-cerebrum-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[154,155],"cids":["w7-cortex-functional-areas","w7-white-matter-tracts"],"t":"Slow, effortful speech","p":"A patient's speech is slow and effortful, but she understands everything you say. Name the area that is damaged with its gyrus, lobe, and usual side. Then name the area whose damage would cause the opposite problem and the tract that links the two.","k":[{"pt":"Broca's area","why":"Broca's area plans the movements of speech, so damage makes speaking hard."},{"pt":"Inferior frontal gyrus of the frontal lobe","why":"Broca's area is a motor area, and motor areas sit in the frontal lobe."},{"pt":"Usually the left hemisphere","why":"Both language areas are usually on the left."},{"pt":"Understanding is largely spared","why":"Understanding language depends on a different area farther back."},{"pt":"Wernicke's area, in the posterior superior temporal gyrus, needed to understand language","why":"Damage there affects understanding of spoken and written language."},{"pt":"The arcuate fasciculus, an association tract, links the two","why":"It stays in one hemisphere, which is what makes it association fibers."}]},{"id":"m5-cerebrum-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[155,156,164],"cids":["w7-white-matter-tracts","w7-basal-ganglia","w7-medulla"],"t":"A tiny stroke, a big loss","p":"A tiny stroke in the right internal capsule weakens the whole left side of the body. Explain why such a small lesion does so much. Name the fiber class, the structures on each side of the capsule, and why the weakness is on the left.","k":[{"pt":"The internal capsule is made of projection fibers","why":"Projection fibers carry signals between the cortex and the brainstem and cord."},{"pt":"Most traffic between the cortex and the body passes through this one narrow band","why":"Cutting a compact band cuts a great deal of wiring at once."},{"pt":"Thalamus and caudate on its medial side, lentiform nucleus on its lateral side","why":"These deep nuclei squeeze the fibers into a narrow path between them."},{"pt":"Above, the fibers fan out as the corona radiata, and below they continue as the crus cerebri","why":"The capsule is the narrow waist between the wide fan and the brainstem."},{"pt":"Motor fibers cross at the decussation of the pyramids in the medulla","why":"The crossing happens below the capsule, so damage on the right weakens the left side."}]},{"id":"m5-cerebrum-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[153,154],"cids":["w7-cerebrum-surface","w7-cortex-functional-areas"],"t":"What each patient lost","p":"Predict what each patient lost. One has damage along the calcarine sulcus. One has damage to the top of the postcentral gyrus where it turns onto the medial surface inside the longitudinal fissure. One has damage to the prefrontal cortex.","k":[{"pt":"The calcarine sulcus holds the primary visual cortex of the occipital lobe","why":"It sits on the medial surface near the back of the hemisphere."},{"pt":"So vision is affected","why":"Damage to the occipital lobe affects vision."},{"pt":"The postcentral gyrus is the primary somatosensory cortex","why":"It receives touch, pressure, pain, temperature, and body position."},{"pt":"The leg and foot are mapped on the medial surface inside the longitudinal fissure","why":"The body map runs upside down along the gyrus, with the face lowest near the lateral sulcus."},{"pt":"So sensation from the opposite leg and foot is lost","why":"The sensory cortex receives input from the opposite side of the body."},{"pt":"The prefrontal cortex is association cortex at the front of the frontal lobe, so planning, judgment, and personality change","why":"Each cortical area has a fixed location, so the site of damage predicts the loss."}]}]},{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem","title":"Diencephalon, brainstem and cerebellum","mw":"2026-11-30","tr":"2026-12-01","prompts":[{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[157,159],"cids":["w7-diencephalon","w7-limbic-system"],"t":"The diencephalon","p":"Draw a midsagittal brain and label the diencephalon and everything in it. Then name two limbic structures and where they sit.","k":[{"pt":"Thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus around the third ventricle","why":"The three parts of the diencephalon enclose the third ventricle between them."},{"pt":"Massa intermedia crossing the third ventricle","why":"It is a small bridge of gray matter joining the two thalami, and some people do not have one."},{"pt":"Pineal gland on the epithalamus, and the pituitary below the hypothalamus on the infundibulum","why":"The pineal gland projects back from the roof of the third ventricle, and the infundibulum is the stalk to the pituitary."},{"pt":"Optic chiasm in front, then the infundibulum, then the mammillary bodies","why":"These three landmarks on the underside belong to the hypothalamus, front to back."},{"pt":"Hippocampus in the medial temporal lobe, with the amygdala just in front of it, deep to the uncus","why":"Both sit in the medial temporal lobe, and the amygdala is the one in front."},{"pt":"Cingulate gyrus above the corpus callosum, and the fornix below it","why":"These complete the limbic ring around the diencephalon."}]},{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[158],"cids":["w7-cerebellum"],"t":"The cerebellum","p":"Draw the cerebellum from behind and in section. Label every part and name what it connects to the brainstem with.","k":[{"pt":"Two cerebellar hemispheres joined by the vermis","why":"The vermis is the narrow midline strip between the hemispheres."},{"pt":"Primary fissure dividing the anterior and posterior lobes, with the small flocculonodular lobe underneath","why":"The deep primary fissure is the landmark that splits the lobes."},{"pt":"Folia, thin parallel folds much finer than gyri","why":"Folia means leaves, which is how the fine folds look."},{"pt":"Gray cerebellar cortex outside, with the arbor vitae of white matter inside","why":"The cerebellum follows the same plan as the cerebrum, and the white matter branches like a tree."},{"pt":"Deep nuclei, lateral to medial: dentate, emboliform, globose, fastigial","why":"Most signals leaving the cerebellum start in these nuclei."},{"pt":"Superior peduncle to the midbrain, middle to the pons, inferior to the medulla","why":"Every fiber entering or leaving the cerebellum passes through one of these three pairs."},{"pt":"Coordinates movement, posture, and balance","why":"It adjusts timing and accuracy without our awareness."}]},{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[162,163,164,166],"cids":["w7-midbrain","w7-pons","w7-medulla","w7-reticular-formation"],"t":"Through the brainstem","p":"Draw the brainstem from the front and from the back and label every surface feature. Then add the fluid channel and chamber inside or behind it, and the net of gray matter that runs through its core.","k":[{"pt":"Midbrain: cerebral peduncles in front and the superior and inferior colliculi behind","why":"The peduncles carry descending motor tracts, and the colliculi sit on the tectum, the roof."},{"pt":"Pons: basilar pons with crosswise fibers curving back into the middle cerebellar peduncles","why":"The crosswise fibers are the axons of the pontine nuclei heading to the cerebellum."},{"pt":"Medulla: pyramids, their decussation, and the olives","why":"The pyramids are the corticospinal tracts, and each olive sits just lateral to a pyramid."},{"pt":"Fourth ventricle behind the pons and upper medulla","why":"The cerebellum lies over its roof."},{"pt":"Cerebral aqueduct through the midbrain from the third to the fourth ventricle","why":"The aqueduct is the landmark that separates the roof of the midbrain from its floor."},{"pt":"Reticular formation running through the core of all three regions","why":"It is a loose net of gray matter in the tegmentum with no surface landmark."}]},{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[161,162,163,164,165],"cids":["w7-brainstem-regions","w7-midbrain","w7-pons","w7-medulla","w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"],"t":"The brainstem, region by region","p":"Name the three brainstem regions top to bottom. For each, name a structure or nucleus you can see or recall and one thing that region handles.","k":[{"pt":"Top to bottom: midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata","why":"The medulla becomes the spinal cord at the foramen magnum."},{"pt":"Midbrain: tectum with the superior and inferior colliculi, cerebral peduncles, substantia nigra, cerebral aqueduct","why":"The colliculi are reflex centers for sight and sound, and the substantia nigra works with the basal nuclei."},{"pt":"Pons: basilar pons with pontine nuclei, plus nuclei that help adjust the breathing rhythm","why":"The pontine nuclei relay motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum."},{"pt":"Medulla: pyramids and their decussation, olives, and the cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers","why":"The vital centers sit in the core of the medulla and are not visible on the surface."},{"pt":"Cranial nerves: III and IV from the midbrain, V from the pons, VI to VIII at the pontomedullary junction, IX, X, and XII from the medulla","why":"A nerve usually leaves near the level of its nucleus, so the attachment tells you the region."}]},{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[157],"cids":["w7-diencephalon"],"t":"Thalamus and hypothalamus","p":"Draw the diencephalon enlarged. Mark the thalamus and the hypothalamus, label what sits in each, and write the one line job of each.","k":[{"pt":"Thalamus: paired egg shaped masses of gray matter, about 80 percent of the diencephalon","why":"It is made of many nuclei on each side of the third ventricle."},{"pt":"Thalamus relays most sensory input on its way to the cerebral cortex","why":"Sensory signals stop here before reaching the primary areas of the cortex."},{"pt":"Massa intermedia joining the two thalami across the third ventricle","why":"On a midsagittal cut it shows as a small oval in the middle of the thalamus."},{"pt":"Hypothalamus below the hypothalamic sulcus, forming the floor and lower walls of the third ventricle","why":"Hypo means below, so it sits under the thalamus."},{"pt":"Optic chiasm, infundibulum, mammillary bodies, and the pituitary below","why":"These are the hypothalamic landmarks on the underside of the brain."},{"pt":"Hypothalamus as the main control center for homeostasis","why":"It controls the pituitary and the autonomic nervous system and helps regulate temperature, hunger, thirst, and sleep."}]},{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[165],"cids":["w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"],"t":"Where the nuclei live","p":"Use the 2, 2, 4, 4 rule to sort cranial nerves I to XII by where they attach. Then name the three nerves whose nuclei break the simple pattern and say how.","k":[{"pt":"I and II attach above the brainstem, so they have no brainstem nucleus","why":"They connect to the forebrain, the cerebrum and the diencephalon."},{"pt":"III and IV in the midbrain","why":"The nuclei line up top to bottom in the same order as the nerve numbers."},{"pt":"V, VI, VII, and VIII in the pons","why":"The pons is the middle region, so it holds the middle numbers."},{"pt":"IX, X, XI, and XII grouped with the medulla","why":"The lowest region holds the highest numbers."},{"pt":"V: its sensory nuclei stretch up into the midbrain and down through the medulla","why":"It is assigned to the pons, where its main motor and sensory nuclei sit."},{"pt":"VIII sits at the pons and medulla junction, and XI's motor nucleus is in the upper cervical cord","why":"XI's rootlets rise through the foramen magnum and then leave the skull with the vagus."}]},{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[166],"cids":["w7-reticular-formation"],"t":"Two directions of the reticular formation","p":"Compare the two components of the reticular formation. Say where the reticular formation sits, which way each component sends its fibers, and what each one does.","k":[{"pt":"A loose net of gray matter through the core of the medulla, pons, and midbrain","why":"Its name comes from the Latin for net."},{"pt":"It fills the tegmentum, behind the big motor tracts and in front of the ventricle or aqueduct, with no surface landmark","why":"It is small clusters of neurons scattered among white matter, so it has no sharp borders."},{"pt":"Ascending reticular activating system: up through the thalamus to the cortex","why":"Ascending means awake, because this part wakes up the cortex."},{"pt":"RAS keeps the cortex awake and alert, holds attention, and filters out unimportant input","why":"Its activity falls during sleep."},{"pt":"Descending component: down to the spinal cord, setting resting muscle tone and posture","why":"Descending means down to the muscles."},{"pt":"The cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers of the medulla are part of it","why":"That is how the descending output links the brainstem to heart rate, breathing, and vessel diameter."}]},{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[164,161],"cids":["w7-medulla","w7-brainstem-regions"],"t":"A small injury, a big danger","p":"Explain why a small injury to the medulla oblongata can be life threatening when a much larger injury to one area of the cerebral cortex is not. Say exactly where the medulla sits and name the centers involved.","k":[{"pt":"The medulla is the lowest brainstem region, from the pontomedullary junction down to the foramen magnum","why":"It sits at the base of the brain right where the brain becomes the spinal cord."},{"pt":"Cardiac center, adjusting the rate and force of the heartbeat","why":"This center is one reason the medulla is called vital."},{"pt":"Respiratory center, setting the basic rhythm of breathing","why":"The pons helps adjust the breathing rhythm, but the medulla sets it."},{"pt":"Vasomotor center, adjusting vessel diameter and so blood pressure","why":"All three centers keep the body alive from moment to moment."},{"pt":"The centers are in the core of the medulla as part of the reticular formation, not on the surface","why":"A small injury to that small region can reach all of them at once."},{"pt":"Damage to one cortical area takes away that area's job, such as vision or speech, but not the heartbeat or breathing","why":"Each area has a fixed job, so the location of the damage predicts what is lost."}]},{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[166,162,156],"cids":["w7-reticular-formation","w7-midbrain","w7-basal-ganglia"],"t":"A coma and Parkinson disease","p":"Two patients: one is in a coma even though scans show the cerebral cortex is not injured, and the other has Parkinson disease. For each, name the structure involved and exactly where it sits in the brainstem.","k":[{"pt":"The coma comes from damage to the ascending reticular activating system","why":"The RAS keeps the cortex awake, so losing it can cause a coma even with a healthy cortex."},{"pt":"The RAS runs from the upper brainstem up through the thalamus to the cortex","why":"It is the ascending part of the reticular formation in the brainstem core."},{"pt":"Parkinson disease is the loss of the pigmented neurons of the substantia nigra","why":"Their dark pigment is why the substantia nigra looks black in a fresh section."},{"pt":"The substantia nigra is in the midbrain, between the crus cerebri and the tegmentum","why":"It is the dark band across the midbrain on a cross section."},{"pt":"It works with the basal nuclei in movement circuits","why":"That is why it is grouped with the basal nuclei by function even though it sits in the midbrain."}]},{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[165,161,190],"cids":["w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei","w7-brainstem-regions","w8-cranial-nerves"],"t":"A tumor at the junction","p":"A tumor presses on the pontomedullary junction on one side. Name the cranial nerves it could affect, in order from medial to lateral, and the jobs that could be lost.","k":[{"pt":"The pontomedullary junction is the groove between the pons and the medulla on the front","why":"It is a useful landmark because three cranial nerves leave along it."},{"pt":"VI abducens, closest to the midline","why":"VI leaves near the midline at the junction."},{"pt":"VII facial, just lateral to VI","why":"VII leaves lateral to VI on the same groove."},{"pt":"VIII vestibulocochlear, lateral to VII","why":"VIII is the most lateral of the three."},{"pt":"Jobs at risk: turning the eye outward, facial expression and taste from the front of the tongue, hearing and balance","why":"Each nerve's job is lost on the side of the tumor."},{"pt":"VI and VII have their nuclei in the pons, and VIII has its nuclei at the junction","why":"The nuclei sit near where the nerves leave, so the tumor's level predicts which ones are hit."}]}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf","title":"Meninges, ventricles, CSF and brain blood supply","mw":"2026-11-30","tr":"2026-12-01","prompts":[{"id":"m5-meninges-csf-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[167,168],"cids":["w7-cranial-meninges","w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"],"t":"Meninges and spaces","p":"List the meninges from the skull inward. Name the space above or below each one and say what is in it.","k":[{"pt":"Dura mater outermost, with periosteal and meningeal layers around the brain","why":"The periosteal layer is attached to the skull, and the two layers are fused except in a few places."},{"pt":"Dural venous sinuses where the two dural layers separate","why":"The spaces between the layers collect venous blood from the brain."},{"pt":"Epidural space between bone and dura: real and full of fat and veins in the spine, only potential in the skull","why":"In the skull the dura is stuck to the bone, so the space opens only if something like blood pushes them apart."},{"pt":"Subdural space between the dura and arachnoid, a potential space","why":"The two membranes normally touch with only a thin film of fluid between them."},{"pt":"Arachnoid mater, with arachnoid granulations pushing into the sinuses","why":"The granulations are where CSF returns to the blood."},{"pt":"Subarachnoid space with CSF, arachnoid strands, and the larger surface vessels","why":"This is the real, fluid filled space between the arachnoid and pia."},{"pt":"Pia mater on the brain surface, following every sulcus","why":"It is very thin and rich in small vessels, and unlike the arachnoid it dips into every groove."}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[170],"cids":["w7-ventricles"],"t":"The ventricles","p":"Draw the ventricular system as a single connected shape seen from the side. Name every chamber and every passage between them.","k":[{"pt":"Two C shaped lateral ventricles, one in each hemisphere, with an anterior horn and an inferior horn","why":"Each curves from the frontal lobe back and then down into the temporal lobe."},{"pt":"Septum pellucidum separating the two lateral ventricles","why":"It is the thin sheet hanging below the corpus callosum in the midline."},{"pt":"Interventricular foramina (of Monro), one on each side","why":"Each lateral ventricle opens into the third ventricle through its own small hole."},{"pt":"Third ventricle, a narrow slit between the halves of the thalamus","why":"It sits in the midline of the diencephalon."},{"pt":"Cerebral aqueduct (of Sylvius) through the midbrain","why":"The narrow channel carries CSF from the third ventricle down to the fourth."},{"pt":"Fourth ventricle between the brainstem and cerebellum, with one median and two lateral apertures to the subarachnoid space","why":"Three openings in all lead out of the fourth ventricle to the space around the brain."},{"pt":"Central canal continuing straight down from the fourth ventricle into the cord","why":"The apertures lead only to the subarachnoid space, and the central canal opens only from the fourth ventricle."}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[160],"cids":["w7-circle-of-willis"],"t":"The circle of Willis","p":"Draw the circle of Willis as seen from the base of the brain. Name every artery in the ring and every artery joining it, and write what the ring is for.","k":[{"pt":"Two internal carotid arteries arriving at the front beside the optic chiasm","why":"They form the anterior circulation."},{"pt":"Two vertebral arteries uniting at the junction of the medulla and pons to form the basilar artery","why":"The vertebral arteries form the posterior circulation."},{"pt":"Anterior cerebral arteries joined by the anterior communicating artery","why":"The short anterior communicating artery closes the front of the ring."},{"pt":"Posterior cerebral arteries as the two end branches of the basilar artery","why":"The basilar artery divides at the top of the pons."},{"pt":"Posterior communicating arteries joining each internal carotid to a posterior cerebral artery","why":"These two vessels link the anterior and posterior circulations."},{"pt":"Middle cerebral arteries branching off the internal carotids, not part of the ring","why":"The middle cerebral is the largest branch of the internal carotid but sits outside the circle."},{"pt":"A backup route if a vessel feeding the ring narrows slowly, but no help for a blockage beyond the ring","why":"Blood can reroute through the communicating arteries only for vessels that feed the circle."}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf-L1-4","lv":1,"comps":[172,177],"cids":["w7-bbb","w8-neuroglia"],"t":"The blood brain barrier","p":"Name the three parts of the blood brain barrier in order from the blood outward and say what each one contributes. Then say where the barrier is missing and why.","k":[{"pt":"Capillary endothelium joined by tight junctions, the actual seal","why":"Tight junctions close the gaps between neighboring endothelial cells."},{"pt":"Brain capillaries lack the pores and wide clefts found elsewhere","why":"With no gaps or pores, most substances can only cross by passing through the cells themselves."},{"pt":"Basement membrane around the endothelium","why":"It is a thin layer of extracellular material that surrounds and supports the capillary wall."},{"pt":"Astrocyte end feet wrapping the outside of the capillary","why":"Astrocytes are star shaped glia whose processes end in flattened feet over the basement membrane."},{"pt":"The end feet support the endothelial cells and help keep the tight junctions in place","why":"The glia hold up the seal that the endothelium makes."},{"pt":"Missing in small areas around the ventricles, such as parts of the hypothalamus","why":"In those spots the brain needs to sample the blood directly."}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[171,170],"cids":["w7-csf-circulation","w7-ventricles"],"t":"CSF, made to absorbed","p":"Trace CSF from where it is made to where it is absorbed. Name every ventricle, opening, and structure it passes in order.","k":[{"pt":"Choroid plexus, capillaries covered by ependymal cells, most of it in the lateral ventricles","why":"The ependymal cells make CSF from the plasma in the capillaries, and there is choroid plexus in all four ventricles."},{"pt":"Lateral ventricles, interventricular foramina, third ventricle","why":"The paired foramina are the openings from each lateral ventricle into the midline ventricle."},{"pt":"Cerebral aqueduct, then the fourth ventricle, with a little going on into the central canal","why":"The aqueduct is the narrow channel through the midbrain."},{"pt":"Median and lateral apertures into the subarachnoid space","why":"This is where CSF leaves the inside of the brain and bathes the outside of the brain and cord."},{"pt":"Arachnoid granulations into the dural venous sinuses, mainly the superior sagittal sinus","why":"CSF is made inside the brain and reabsorbed outside it, into the venous blood."},{"pt":"Out of the skull with the venous blood through the internal jugular veins","why":"Once it is in a sinus, CSF follows the same drainage as the blood."}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[167],"cids":["w7-cranial-meninges"],"t":"Dural folds and sinuses","p":"Draw the inside of the skull and label the dural folds. Then name the venous sinuses and trace blood from the superior sagittal sinus out of the skull.","k":[{"pt":"Falx cerebri in the longitudinal fissure between the cerebral hemispheres","why":"The folds are the meningeal layer of the dura turned inward as sheets that steady the brain."},{"pt":"Tentorium cerebelli in the transverse fissure, like a tent over the cerebellum","why":"It separates the cerebellum from the occipital lobes above it."},{"pt":"Falx cerebelli below the tentorium, between the cerebellar hemispheres","why":"It is the small midline fold in the back of the skull."},{"pt":"Superior sagittal sinus in the top edge of the falx cerebri, and the inferior sagittal sinus in its lower edge draining to the straight sinus","why":"The main sinuses run in the edges of the folds."},{"pt":"Confluence of sinuses at the internal occipital protuberance","why":"The superior sagittal and straight sinuses meet here at the back of the skull."},{"pt":"Transverse sinus, then sigmoid sinus to the jugular foramen","why":"The paired transverse sinuses run along the back edge of the tentorium and curve down as the sigmoid sinuses."},{"pt":"Internal jugular vein","why":"The sigmoid sinus passes through the jugular foramen and becomes the internal jugular vein."}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[167,171,172],"cids":["w7-cranial-meninges","w7-csf-circulation","w7-bbb"],"t":"Layers of protection","p":"Build a mind map of what protects the brain, working from the skull inward. On each branch, name the layer and what it protects against or does.","k":[{"pt":"Skull, with the periosteal dura attached to its inner surface","why":"The bone is the hard outer case, and the dura is fixed tight against it, so the cranial epidural space is only a potential space."},{"pt":"Dura, arachnoid, pia, in that order","why":"Tough mother, spider layer, and tender mother wrap the brain from outside to inside."},{"pt":"Dural folds, the falx cerebri, tentorium cerebelli, and falx cerebelli, divide the cranial cavity and steady the brain","why":"They are sheets of the meningeal dura folded inward between the hemispheres and between the cerebrum and cerebellum."},{"pt":"Subarachnoid space filled with CSF","why":"The fluid layer sits between the arachnoid and the pia all around the brain and cord."},{"pt":"CSF floats the brain, cushions it against jolts, and keeps a steady chemical environment around the neurons","why":"Floating lowers the brain's effective weight so it does not press down on its own base."},{"pt":"Blood brain barrier at the capillaries: tight junction endothelium, basement membrane, astrocyte end feet, keeping out many drugs and germs","why":"This inner layer protects against harmful substances in the blood rather than against blows."}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[160,154],"cids":["w7-circle-of-willis","w7-cortex-functional-areas"],"t":"Three territories","p":"Draw the brain from the side and from the medial surface. Shade and label the region supplied by the anterior, middle, and posterior cerebral arteries, and write where each one comes off the circle of Willis.","k":[{"pt":"Anterior cerebral: medial surface of the frontal and parietal lobes, including the leg and foot areas","why":"The artery runs into the longitudinal fissure and arches over the corpus callosum, which is where the leg is mapped."},{"pt":"Middle cerebral: most of the lateral surface, including the face and arm areas, the auditory cortex, the insula, and Broca's and Wernicke's areas","why":"It runs out into the lateral sulcus, which puts it over the side of the hemisphere."},{"pt":"Posterior cerebral: occipital lobe with the visual cortex, and the inferior temporal surface","why":"It curves around the midbrain to reach the back and underside of the hemisphere."},{"pt":"Anterior and middle cerebral arteries come from the internal carotid","why":"The internal carotid ends by dividing into these two branches."},{"pt":"Posterior cerebral arteries are the end branches of the basilar artery","why":"They belong to the posterior circulation, fed by the vertebral arteries."}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[167,168],"cids":["w7-cranial-meninges","w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"],"t":"Two kinds of head bleed","p":"Two head injury patients. In one, a skull fracture tears an artery and blood pushes the dura away from the bone. In the other, torn veins crossing to the sinuses bleed just beneath the dura. Name each bleed and the layers on either side of the blood, and explain why the first space normally does not exist.","k":[{"pt":"The first is an epidural hematoma, between the skull and the dura","why":"The blood has pushed the dura off the inside of the skull."},{"pt":"In the skull the periosteal dura is attached to the bone, so the cranial epidural space is only a potential space","why":"It opens only when something like blood separates the dura from the skull."},{"pt":"The second is a subdural hematoma, between the dura and the arachnoid","why":"Veins crossing to the dural sinuses tear and bleed into this space."},{"pt":"The subdural space is also normally a potential space","why":"The dura and arachnoid normally touch with only a thin film of fluid between them."},{"pt":"A subarachnoid hemorrhage would be different, with blood from a surface artery mixing into the CSF","why":"The subarachnoid space is a real space that already holds CSF and the larger surface vessels."}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[170,171],"cids":["w7-ventricles","w7-csf-circulation"],"t":"The aqueduct squeezed shut","p":"A tumor squeezes the cerebral aqueduct shut. Predict which ventricles enlarge and which do not, and explain using the path of CSF.","k":[{"pt":"Most CSF is made in the lateral ventricles","why":"The largest amounts of choroid plexus are in the lateral ventricles."},{"pt":"CSF flows from the lateral ventricles through the interventricular foramina into the third ventricle","why":"Those spaces are all above the block."},{"pt":"The aqueduct is the only route from the third ventricle to the fourth","why":"It is one of the narrow points on the CSF route."},{"pt":"The lateral and third ventricles enlarge","why":"CSF keeps being made but builds up behind the blockage."},{"pt":"The fourth ventricle does not enlarge from this block","why":"It sits past the block and still drains through its median and lateral apertures."},{"pt":"This buildup is hydrocephalus","why":"Hydrocephalus is CSF building up behind a blockage or from poor reabsorption, with the ventricles enlarging."}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[160,154],"cids":["w7-circle-of-willis","w7-cortex-functional-areas"],"t":"Which artery is blocked","p":"One stroke patient has weakness of the right face and arm and her speech is affected, but her right leg is fine. Another has a weak left leg but a normal left arm. Name the artery blocked in each and explain why the pattern fits.","k":[{"pt":"The first is the left middle cerebral artery","why":"The left hemisphere controls the right side of the body."},{"pt":"The middle cerebral supplies the face and arm areas on the lateral surface","why":"The leg is mapped on the medial surface, outside this artery's territory, so the leg is spared."},{"pt":"It also supplies Broca's and Wernicke's areas, usually on the left","why":"That is why her speech is affected too."},{"pt":"The second is the right anterior cerebral artery","why":"The right hemisphere controls the left side of the body."},{"pt":"The anterior cerebral supplies the medial surface with the leg and foot areas","why":"A stroke in this artery affects the opposite leg most."},{"pt":"A block in a cerebral artery beyond the ring has no backup through the circle of Willis","why":"The circle only helps when a vessel feeding the ring narrows slowly."}]}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord","title":"The spinal cord","mw":"2026-12-02","tr":"2026-12-03","prompts":[{"id":"m5-spinal-cord-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[186,185,180],"cids":["w8-cord-internal","w8-cord-roots","w8-gray-white-matter"],"t":"Spinal cord in cross section","p":"Draw the spinal cord in cross section and label the horns, the columns, the roots, and the central canal. Mark which way information travels on each side.","k":[{"pt":"Gray matter: posterior, lateral, and anterior horns joined by the gray commissure","why":"The gray matter forms an H or butterfly in the center of the cord."},{"pt":"Central canal in the middle of the gray commissure","why":"The canal is filled with CSF, lined by ependymal cells, and continuous with the fourth ventricle."},{"pt":"White matter: posterior, lateral, and anterior columns","why":"The horns mark the borders between the columns."},{"pt":"Wide anterior median fissure in front and shallow posterior median sulcus behind","why":"The wide groove tells you which side of the section is anterior."},{"pt":"Posterior root with its ganglion, carrying sensory signals in","why":"The ganglion holds the sensory cell bodies, which is why only this root has one."},{"pt":"Anterior root carrying motor signals out, joining the posterior root as the spinal nerve","why":"The two roots join just outside the cord, so every spinal nerve is mixed."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[184],"cids":["w8-cord-external"],"t":"The cord, top to bottom","p":"Draw the whole spinal cord from behind, from the foramen magnum down. Label both enlargements with their levels, where the cord ends, and what continues below it.","k":[{"pt":"Continuous with the medulla oblongata at the foramen magnum, about 42 to 45 cm long","why":"The cord is the part of the CNS that sits inside the vertebral canal."},{"pt":"Cervical enlargement, about C4 to T1, where the upper limb nerves leave","why":"A limb has many muscles and a large area of skin, so its cord segments need more neurons."},{"pt":"Lumbar enlargement, about T9 to T12, where the lower limb nerves leave","why":"Texts give slightly different levels for this one, some a little lower."},{"pt":"Conus medullaris, the tapered end, near the L1 to L2 disc","why":"The cord is shorter than the vertebral column, so it ends well above the bottom of the canal."},{"pt":"Filum terminale running from the conus to the coccyx","why":"It is a thin strand of pia that anchors the cord."},{"pt":"Cauda equina, the lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal roots below the conus","why":"The long roots look like a horse's tail, with the filum terminale down their middle."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[185,176,179],"cids":["w8-cord-roots","w8-neuron-classification","w8-tissue-collections"],"t":"Roots and rootlets","p":"Draw one segment of the spinal cord with its roots on one side. Label the rootlets, both roots, the root ganglion, the spinal nerve, and the opening it leaves through.","k":[{"pt":"Posterior rootlets along the back of the cord and anterior rootlets along the front","why":"The rootlets of one segment merge to form one root."},{"pt":"Posterior (dorsal) root, carrying sensory axons only","why":"It carries signals from receptors into the cord."},{"pt":"Posterior root ganglion, holding the cell bodies of the sensory neurons","why":"These sensory neurons are unipolar, with one branch out to a receptor and one into the cord."},{"pt":"Anterior (ventral) root, carrying motor axons only, with no ganglion","why":"The motor cell bodies sit inside the cord in the gray matter, so the root needs no ganglion."},{"pt":"The two roots join just outside the cord as a mixed spinal nerve","why":"Every spinal nerve carries both sensory and motor axons."},{"pt":"Intervertebral foramen between two neighboring vertebrae, holding the ganglion","why":"The spinal nerve leaves the vertebral canal through this opening."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[181,182,185],"cids":["w8-reflex-arc","w8-reflex-types","w8-cord-roots"],"t":"A reflex arc","p":"Draw a reflex arc with all five components labeled. Then write whether it is monosynaptic or polysynaptic and name a real example.","k":[{"pt":"Receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, effector","why":"Every reflex arc has these five parts in the same order, in, through, and out."},{"pt":"Posterior root ganglion holding the sensory cell body","why":"In a spinal reflex the sensory axon enters the cord through the dorsal root."},{"pt":"Integration center in the gray matter of the cord or brainstem","why":"One or more synapses here pass the signal to the next neuron."},{"pt":"Anterior horn holding the motor cell body, with its axon leaving through the ventral root","why":"In a somatic spinal reflex the motor neuron runs out to skeletal muscle."},{"pt":"Monosynaptic: the stretch reflex, such as the patellar reflex","why":"The sensory neuron synapses directly on the motor neuron, so there is only one synapse."},{"pt":"Polysynaptic: the withdrawal reflex, with an interneuron","why":"Each extra synapse adds a short delay, so the monosynaptic reflex is the faster one."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[184,169],"cids":["w8-cord-external","w7-cord-termination-lp"],"t":"The end of the cord","p":"Draw the lower end of the spinal cord in detail and label the conus medullaris, cauda equina, and filum terminale. Write why the roots have to travel so far.","k":[{"pt":"Cord ends at the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc in an adult","why":"The vertebral column continues well below that point."},{"pt":"Cauda equina, the loose bundle of lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal roots","why":"These roots leave the cord high up, at or above the conus."},{"pt":"Filum terminale, a strand of pia anchoring the cord to the coccyx","why":"It runs down the middle of the cauda equina."},{"pt":"The cord stops growing in length at about 4 to 5 years while the column keeps growing","why":"This difference in growth is what leaves the adult cord shorter than the column."},{"pt":"Roots must travel down the canal to reach their own exits lower in the column","why":"Each spinal nerve still leaves at its own intervertebral foramen, far below where its roots started."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[186,183],"cids":["w8-cord-internal","w8-neuronal-pools"],"t":"Tracts up and down","p":"Draw the spinal cord in cross section and mark which white columns hold ascending tracts, descending tracts, or both. Name one ascending and one descending tract, what each carries, and where each crosses to the other side.","k":[{"pt":"Posterior, lateral, and anterior white columns, with the horns marking their borders","why":"Each column is made of tracts, bundles of axons sharing the same origin and destination."},{"pt":"Posterior columns hold mainly ascending tracts","why":"Ascending tracts carry sensory signals up to the brain."},{"pt":"Lateral and anterior columns hold both ascending and descending tracts","why":"Descending tracts carry motor signals down from the brain."},{"pt":"Spinothalamic tract: ascending, carrying pain and temperature, crossing in the cord close to where the fibers enter","why":"Its name says it runs from the spinal cord to the thalamus."},{"pt":"Corticospinal tract: descending, for voluntary movement, with most axons crossed in the medulla","why":"Its name says it runs from the cerebral cortex to the spinal cord."},{"pt":"Anterior white commissure just in front of the gray commissure, where axons cross to the other side of the cord","why":"A pathway that crosses inside the cord, like the spinothalamic tract, needs this band of white matter to get to the other side."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord-L2-4","lv":2,"comps":[186],"cids":["w8-cord-internal"],"t":"Three gray horns","p":"Compare the three gray horns: where each points, its shape, and what it holds. Then explain why only some segments have a lateral horn.","k":[{"pt":"Posterior horn, thin and narrow, pointing back: incoming sensory axons and interneuron cell bodies","why":"It sits on the side where the posterior root brings sensory axons in."},{"pt":"Anterior horn, wide and rounded, pointing forward: somatic motor neuron cell bodies","why":"Their axons leave in the anterior root to skeletal muscle."},{"pt":"Lateral horn, a small point to the side between the other two: autonomic (sympathetic) motor cell bodies","why":"These are the preganglionic sympathetic neurons."},{"pt":"The lateral horn is found only from T1 to L2","why":"Only those segments send out sympathetic neurons."},{"pt":"S2 to S4 hold parasympathetic neurons in a similar spot but form no visible horn","why":"So those segments still send out autonomic axons, but only T1 to L2 show a lateral horn on a slide."},{"pt":"Gray matter is largest in the enlargements","why":"The limbs need many motor neurons, so those segments have bigger anterior horns."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord-L2-5","lv":2,"comps":[168],"cids":["w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"],"t":"Coverings of the cord","p":"Compare the meninges and spaces around the spinal cord with those around the brain. Name each layer and space, what it holds, and what anchors the cord in place.","k":[{"pt":"Spinal dura is a single layer, not attached to the vertebrae","why":"It matches the meningeal layer of the cranial dura, so there is no periosteal layer stuck to the bone."},{"pt":"The dural sac ends at about S2","why":"The dura forms a tube that runs well below the end of the cord."},{"pt":"Epidural space is a real space of fat and veins in the spine, but only potential in the skull","why":"Because the spinal dura is not stuck to bone, there is room for fat and veins, and that is where an epidural injection goes."},{"pt":"Subdural space is potential, and the subarachnoid space is real and holds CSF","why":"The same layering continues down from the brain."},{"pt":"Denticulate ligaments, tooth shaped flaps of pia between the roots, attach to the dura","why":"They keep the cord centered in the dural sac."},{"pt":"Filum terminale, a pia strand through the lumbar cistern that picks up dura and anchors to the coccyx","why":"It anchors the bottom of the cord, while the denticulate ligaments anchor its sides."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[169,168],"cids":["w7-cord-termination-lp","w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"],"t":"Where to put the needle","p":"Draw the lower spinal cord in the canal. Mark where the cord ends, where the subarachnoid space ends, and the level you would enter for a lumbar puncture, and write why that level is safe. Then list, in order, the layers the needle crosses from the skin to the CSF.","k":[{"pt":"Conus medullaris ending near the L1 to L2 disc in an adult","why":"The cord stops here, so anything lower avoids it."},{"pt":"Lumbar cistern below it, down to about S2, holding CSF, the cauda equina, and the filum terminale","why":"The dural sac and subarachnoid space keep going after the cord stops."},{"pt":"Puncture between the L3 and L4 or the L4 and L5 spines","why":"A line across the tops of the iliac crests crosses the spine at about L4, which helps find the level."},{"pt":"Safe because it is well below the end of the cord","why":"Only the floating roots of the cauda equina lie in the path, and they slide aside."},{"pt":"Needle path: skin, subcutaneous tissue, supraspinous, interspinous, ligamentum flavum","why":"The firm ligamentum flavum giving way is a landmark the clinician feels."},{"pt":"Then epidural space, dura and arachnoid together, and into the subarachnoid space","why":"The arachnoid is pressed against the dura, so the needle passes the two together before CSF appears."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[183,154,155,185,186],"cids":["w8-neuronal-pools","w7-cortex-functional-areas","w7-white-matter-tracts","w8-cord-roots","w8-cord-internal"],"t":"Skin to brain and back","p":"Build a mind map following a signal from a pinprick on a fingertip up to the brain, and then a command back down that moves the hand. Name every structure it passes.","k":[{"pt":"Free nerve ending in the skin, sensory neuron through the spinal nerve, cell body in the posterior root ganglion","why":"The sensory neuron is unipolar, with one branch at the receptor and one entering the cord."},{"pt":"In through the posterior root to the posterior horn","why":"The posterior horn receives the incoming sensory axons and holds the interneurons they connect to."},{"pt":"Crossing in the cord close to where it enters, then up the spinothalamic tract","why":"The spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature from the spinal cord to the thalamus, and its axons cross soon after they enter."},{"pt":"Thalamus, then the postcentral gyrus of the opposite hemisphere","why":"The thalamus relays most sensory input up to the primary somatosensory cortex."},{"pt":"Precentral gyrus down the corticospinal tract through the internal capsule and the pyramids, crossing at the decussation in the medulla","why":"The command starts in the primary motor cortex and crosses back before it reaches the cord."},{"pt":"Anterior horn motor neuron, out the anterior root and spinal nerve to the hand muscle","why":"The motor axon leaves through the front of the cord and travels out with the sensory axons in the same nerve."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[184,186,191],"cids":["w8-cord-external","w8-cord-internal","w8-nerve-plexuses"],"t":"A complete cord injury","p":"A patient's spinal cord is completely cut at the T6 segment. Predict what is lost and what is spared, and explain from the tracts and the enlargements why the arms still work.","k":[{"pt":"The ascending tracts carrying sensation up to the brain are cut","why":"Signals from below the cut can no longer reach the brain."},{"pt":"The descending tracts carrying motor commands down are cut","why":"Commands from the brain can no longer reach the cord below the cut."},{"pt":"Sensation and movement are lost below the injury","why":"Everything served by segments below T6 is cut off from the brain."},{"pt":"Function above the injury is spared","why":"Tracts above the cut still connect to the brain."},{"pt":"The arms work because their nerves leave at the cervical enlargement, C4 to T1, above the cut","why":"The brachial plexus forms from C5 to T1, all above T6."},{"pt":"The legs are affected because their nerves leave at the lumbar enlargement, below the cut","why":"The lower limb plexuses come off segments that are now cut off from the brain."}]}]},{"id":"m5-cranial-nerves","title":"The cranial nerves","mw":"2026-12-02","tr":"2026-12-03","prompts":[{"id":"m5-cranial-nerves-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[190],"cids":["w8-cranial-nerves"],"t":"Twelve nerves, in order","p":"List all twelve cranial nerves by number and name. Beside each write sensory, motor, or both.","k":[{"pt":"I olfactory, II optic, III oculomotor, IV trochlear","why":"The numbers run front to back in the order the nerves attach to the brain."},{"pt":"V trigeminal, VI abducens, VII facial, VIII vestibulocochlear","why":"\"On Old Olympus' Towering Tops A Finn And German Viewed Some Hops\" gives the first letters in order."},{"pt":"IX glossopharyngeal, X vagus, XI accessory, XII hypoglossal","why":"The last four attach lowest, at the medulla and upper cervical cord."},{"pt":"Sensory only: I, II, VIII","why":"These three carry the special senses of smell, vision, hearing, and balance."},{"pt":"Motor: III, IV, VI, XI, XII","why":"These mainly carry signals out to muscles."},{"pt":"Both: V, VII, IX, X","why":"\"Some Say Marry Money But My Brother Says Big Brains Matter Most\" gives the pattern in order."}]},{"id":"m5-cranial-nerves-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[165,190],"cids":["w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei","w8-cranial-nerves"],"t":"Nerves off the base","p":"Draw the base of the brain and mark where each cranial nerve emerges. Name every nerve at its exit point.","k":[{"pt":"I from the olfactory bulb under the frontal lobe, and II into the optic chiasm","why":"These two attach to the forebrain rather than the brainstem."},{"pt":"III from the front of the midbrain, between the cerebral peduncles","why":"It leaves in the gap between the two stalks."},{"pt":"IV from the back of the midbrain, just below the inferior colliculi","why":"IV is the only cranial nerve that leaves the back of the brainstem, so draw it wrapping around."},{"pt":"V from the side of the pons","why":"It is the largest cranial nerve, coming off the bulge of the pons."},{"pt":"VI, VII, and VIII at the pontomedullary junction, medial to lateral","why":"Three nerves line up along the groove between the pons and medulla."},{"pt":"IX and X in the groove behind the olive, and XII between the pyramid and the olive","why":"The olive is the landmark that separates these medulla nerves."},{"pt":"XI from the upper cervical cord, with rootlets rising through the foramen magnum","why":"Most of XI comes from the cord, with only a small part from the medulla."}]},{"id":"m5-cranial-nerves-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[190],"cids":["w8-cranial-nerves"],"t":"Nerve, hole, job","p":"For as many cranial nerves as you can, write the nerve, the skull opening it passes through, and its main job.","k":[{"pt":"I: olfactory foramina in the cribriform plate, smell","why":"Small bundles of axons pass up from the nasal lining to the olfactory bulb."},{"pt":"II: optic canal, vision","why":"The optic nerve leaves the back of the eye and meets its partner at the optic chiasm."},{"pt":"III, IV, VI, and V1: superior orbital fissure, eye movement and sensation from the face","why":"This opening leads into the orbit, so the eye nerves share it."},{"pt":"V2 through the foramen rotundum and V3 through the foramen ovale, face sensation and chewing","why":"The trigeminal has three branches that each use a different opening."},{"pt":"VII and VIII: internal acoustic meatus, with VII leaving through the stylomastoid foramen for facial expression and taste, and VIII for hearing and balance","why":"Both enter the temporal bone together, but only the facial nerve comes back out to the face."},{"pt":"IX, X, and XI: jugular foramen, for swallowing and taste from the back of the tongue, the organs and voice, and the neck and shoulder","why":"Three nerves leave the skull side by side here."},{"pt":"XII: hypoglossal canal, moves the tongue","why":"It has its own canal at the base of the skull."}]},{"id":"m5-cranial-nerves-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[190],"cids":["w8-cranial-nerves"],"t":"Nerves by job","p":"Build a mind map grouping the cranial nerves by what they do rather than by number. Use branches for eye movement, face, throat and neck, and special senses.","k":[{"pt":"Special senses: I smell, II vision, VIII hearing and balance","why":"These are the three purely sensory nerves."},{"pt":"Eye movement: III, IV, VI, with III also raising the upper eyelid and constricting the pupil","why":"Three nerves share the work of moving each eye."},{"pt":"Face: V for sensation and chewing, VII for expression and taste from the front two thirds of the tongue","why":"Both are mixed nerves serving the face."},{"pt":"Throat and neck: IX swallowing and taste from the back third, X pharynx and larynx, XI neck and shoulder, XII tongue","why":"These four attach lowest, at the medulla and the upper cervical cord."},{"pt":"X also carries the parasympathetic supply to the thoracic and abdominal organs","why":"The vagus reaches far past the throat, which is why it does not fit a single branch neatly."},{"pt":"III, VII, IX, and X also carry parasympathetic axons to smooth muscle and glands","why":"They are the cranial part of the craniosacral division, which is why the pupil, tears, and saliva are run by cranial nerves."}]},{"id":"m5-cranial-nerves-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[190,193],"cids":["w8-cranial-nerves","w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"],"t":"Four parasympathetic nerves","p":"Name the four cranial nerves that carry parasympathetic axons. For each, name the ganglion or target it reaches.","k":[{"pt":"III to the ciliary ganglion, for the muscles that constrict the pupil and focus the lens","why":"This is the parasympathetic part of the oculomotor nerve's job."},{"pt":"VII to the pterygopalatine ganglion, for the lacrimal gland and nasal glands","why":"The facial nerve is behind tears as well as facial expression."},{"pt":"VII to the submandibular ganglion, for the submandibular and sublingual glands","why":"VII is the only one of the four that reaches two named head ganglia."},{"pt":"IX to the otic ganglion, for the parotid gland","why":"The glossopharyngeal nerve serves the largest salivary gland."},{"pt":"X to terminal ganglia in the walls of the thoracic and abdominal organs","why":"The vagus carries most of the parasympathetic supply to the chest and abdomen."},{"pt":"These ganglia sit close to or inside the organ, so the postganglionic axons are short","why":"Terminal ganglia are what make the parasympathetic division long before and short after."}]},{"id":"m5-cranial-nerves-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[190,165],"cids":["w8-cranial-nerves","w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"],"t":"What breaks when","p":"For cranial nerves III, V, VII, VIII, X, and XII, write one thing you would see if that nerve stopped working, and where it attaches to the brainstem.","k":[{"pt":"III: drooping upper lid, a pupil that does not constrict, and an eye that cannot move in most directions, from the midbrain","why":"III raises the lid, constricts the pupil, and moves four of the six eye muscles."},{"pt":"V: numb face and weak chewing, from the side of the pons","why":"V carries sensation from the face and moves the muscles of chewing."},{"pt":"VII: one whole side of the face droops, as in Bell's palsy, at the pontomedullary junction","why":"VII moves the muscles of facial expression."},{"pt":"VIII: hearing loss or balance problems, at the pontomedullary junction","why":"VIII has a cochlear branch for hearing and a vestibular branch for balance."},{"pt":"X: trouble swallowing and a changed voice, from the groove behind the olive","why":"X moves the muscles of the pharynx and larynx."},{"pt":"XII: a tongue that cannot move normally, from between the pyramid and the olive","why":"XII is the motor nerve of the tongue."}]},{"id":"m5-cranial-nerves-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[190,167],"cids":["w8-cranial-nerves","w7-cranial-meninges"],"t":"A tumor at the jugular foramen","p":"A tumor grows at the jugular foramen. Name every cranial nerve it could squeeze and predict the cluster of signs. Then name the vessel that also leaves the skull through that opening.","k":[{"pt":"IX glossopharyngeal, X vagus, and XI accessory pass through the jugular foramen","why":"The three nerves sit side by side in this one opening."},{"pt":"IX: trouble swallowing and loss of taste from the back third of the tongue","why":"IX helps with swallowing and carries taste and sensation from the back of the tongue and pharynx."},{"pt":"X: swallowing and voice problems","why":"X moves the muscles of the pharynx and larynx."},{"pt":"XI: weakness of the neck and shoulder muscles","why":"XI moves the muscles of the neck and shoulder."},{"pt":"The result is a cluster of swallowing, voice, and shoulder signs together","why":"Because the nerves share an opening, one tumor can affect all three at once."},{"pt":"The sigmoid sinus passes through it and becomes the internal jugular vein","why":"The same opening is the exit for venous blood draining from the brain."}]},{"id":"m5-cranial-nerves-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[190,165],"cids":["w8-cranial-nerves","w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"],"t":"A drooping face, a stabbing pain","p":"One patient suddenly has one side of the face drooping, and another has stabbing pain along the face. Name the nerve in each, whether it is sensory, motor, or mixed, where it attaches to the brainstem, and the skull openings it uses.","k":[{"pt":"The drooping face is Bell's palsy, a sudden weakness of VII facial","why":"VII moves the muscles of facial expression."},{"pt":"VII is mixed and leaves at the pontomedullary junction","why":"Its nucleus sits in the pons."},{"pt":"VII enters the internal acoustic meatus and comes out at the stylomastoid foramen","why":"It runs through the temporal bone before it reaches the face."},{"pt":"The stabbing pain is trigeminal neuralgia, along the branches of V trigeminal","why":"V carries sensation from the face, scalp, teeth, mouth, and nose."},{"pt":"V is mixed, the largest cranial nerve, leaving the side of the pons","why":"It also moves the muscles of chewing."},{"pt":"V1 uses the superior orbital fissure, V2 the foramen rotundum, and V3 the foramen ovale","why":"The path of each branch predicts where on the face the pain shows up."}]}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-nerves","title":"Spinal nerves and nerve plexuses","mw":"2026-12-02","tr":"2026-12-03","prompts":[{"id":"m5-spinal-nerves-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[188,191],"cids":["w8-spinal-nerves-rami","w8-nerve-plexuses"],"t":"Spinal nerves and plexuses","p":"Write the number of spinal nerve pairs by region. Then name the plexuses and one major nerve coming out of each.","k":[{"pt":"8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, 1 coccygeal, 31 pairs total","why":"C1 leaves between the skull and the atlas and C8 leaves below C7, which is how 7 cervical vertebrae end up with 8 cervical nerves."},{"pt":"Cervical plexus, C1 to C4: phrenic nerve","why":"The phrenic nerve, from C3 to C5, is the motor supply to the diaphragm."},{"pt":"Brachial plexus, C5 to T1: axillary, musculocutaneous, radial, median, ulnar","why":"This plexus serves the shoulder and the whole upper limb."},{"pt":"Lumbar plexus, L1 to L4: femoral and obturator nerves","why":"It serves the lower abdominal wall and the front and inner thigh."},{"pt":"Sacral plexus, L4 to S4: sciatic nerve, plus gluteal and pudendal nerves","why":"The sciatic nerve is the largest nerve in the body."},{"pt":"Coccygeal plexus, S4, S5, and Co1: small nerves to the skin over the coccyx","why":"It is the smallest plexus and serves only that small patch of skin."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-nerves-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[187,179],"cids":["w8-nerve-structure","w8-tissue-collections"],"t":"A nerve in cross section","p":"Draw a nerve in cross section and label every layer from one axon out to the whole nerve. Write what each wrapping surrounds.","k":[{"pt":"Axon, or nerve fiber, with its Schwann cell if it is myelinated","why":"The single axon is the smallest unit in a nerve."},{"pt":"Endoneurium around each single axon","why":"Endo means within, and this thin layer sits inside the fascicle around one fiber."},{"pt":"Fascicle, a bundle of axons","why":"On a slide the fascicles are the round clusters inside the nerve."},{"pt":"Perineurium around each fascicle","why":"Peri means around, and this middle wrapping outlines each bundle."},{"pt":"Epineurium, tough dense irregular tissue around the whole nerve, carrying its blood vessels","why":"Epi means upon, and this thick outer ring also fills the spaces between fascicles."},{"pt":"Same pattern as skeletal muscle: endomysium, perimysium, epimysium","why":"Learning one set gives you the other, since the prefixes mean the same thing."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-nerves-L1-3","lv":1,"comps":[188],"cids":["w8-spinal-nerves-rami"],"t":"Branches of a spinal nerve","p":"Draw a spinal nerve leaving the intervertebral foramen and label every branch it gives off. Write the territory each branch supplies.","k":[{"pt":"Posterior ramus: deep muscles of the back and the skin of the back of the neck and trunk","why":"It turns toward the back right after the nerve leaves the foramen."},{"pt":"Anterior ramus, the larger branch: the front and sides of the trunk and all of the limbs","why":"It runs forward and around the body, so it has the larger territory."},{"pt":"Most anterior rami join plexuses, but T2 to T12 run between the ribs as intercostal nerves","why":"The thoracic region is the exception to the plexus pattern."},{"pt":"Meningeal branch turning back through the foramen to the vertebrae, ligaments, cord vessels, and meninges","why":"It serves the structures around the cord itself."},{"pt":"White ramus communicans into a sympathetic trunk ganglion, only on T1 to L2","why":"Only those segments have lateral horns sending out sympathetic axons."},{"pt":"Gray ramus communicans from the ganglion back to the spinal nerve, on every spinal nerve","why":"This is how sympathetic axons get into every nerve's territory."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-nerves-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[188,193],"cids":["w8-spinal-nerves-rami","w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"],"t":"White and gray rami","p":"Compare the white and gray rami communicantes: which way each carries axons, whether those axons are myelinated, which spinal nerves have them, and why.","k":[{"pt":"White ramus: from the spinal nerve into the sympathetic trunk ganglion","why":"It carries the preganglionic axons into the chain."},{"pt":"White ramus axons are myelinated preganglionic axons, which make it look white","why":"White means myelinated, the same rule as white matter."},{"pt":"White rami only on T1 to L2","why":"Only those cord segments have lateral horns, the source of sympathetic preganglionic neurons."},{"pt":"Gray ramus: from the trunk ganglion back out to the spinal nerve","why":"It carries the postganglionic axons back into the nerve."},{"pt":"Gray ramus axons are unmyelinated postganglionic axons","why":"Gray means unmyelinated."},{"pt":"Gray rami on all 31 pairs, their axons riding out to sweat glands, arrector pili muscles, and vessels of the skin and body wall","why":"Every nerve's territory needs sympathetic supply, even where no white ramus enters."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-nerves-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[189],"cids":["w8-sensory-receptors"],"t":"Three ways to sort receptors","p":"Classify sensory receptors three ways: by stimulus, by location, and by structure. Then give a muscle spindle and a lamellar corpuscle one label from each scheme.","k":[{"pt":"By stimulus: mechanoreceptors, thermoreceptors, photoreceptors, chemoreceptors, nociceptors","why":"Each name tells you the kind of change the receptor detects."},{"pt":"By location: exteroceptors, interoceptors, proprioceptors","why":"These say whether the receptor watches the outside, the organs, or the body's own position."},{"pt":"By structure: free nerve endings, encapsulated nerve endings, specialized receptor cells","why":"These say whether the dendrite is bare, wrapped in a capsule, or helped by a separate cell."},{"pt":"Muscle spindle: mechanoreceptor, proprioceptor, encapsulated","why":"It sits among muscle fibers and senses stretch of the muscle."},{"pt":"Lamellar corpuscle: mechanoreceptor, exteroceptor in the skin or proprioceptor in a joint capsule, encapsulated","why":"Lamellar means layered, and on a slide it looks like a cut onion around one dendrite."},{"pt":"Every receptor gets one label from each scheme","why":"The three schemes answer different questions, so they do not compete."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-nerves-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[189],"cids":["w8-sensory-receptors"],"t":"Receptors in the skin","p":"Compare the named receptors in the skin from the surface down. For each, write where it sits and what it senses.","k":[{"pt":"Tactile (Merkel) discs in the stratum basale: light touch","why":"They sit in the deepest layer of the epidermis, so they are among the shallowest receptors in the skin."},{"pt":"Free nerve endings throughout the epithelium and connective tissue: pain, temperature, and some touch","why":"They are bare dendrite tips with no capsule."},{"pt":"Tactile (Meissner) corpuscles in the dermal papillae of hairless skin: light, fine touch","why":"They sit high in the dermis close to the surface, crowded in the fingertips and lips."},{"pt":"Hair follicle receptors wrapped around the base of hair follicles: bending of a hair","why":"A moving hair is a form of light touch."},{"pt":"Lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscles in the deep dermis and hypodermis: deep pressure and vibration","why":"They sit deep in the skin, which fits a receptor for deep pressure."},{"pt":"Bulbous (Ruffini) corpuscles in the deep dermis and hypodermis: steady pressure and stretching of the skin","why":"They sit deep like the lamellar corpuscles but respond to steady stretch."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-nerves-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[191],"cids":["w8-nerve-plexuses"],"t":"Which region is lost","p":"Predict the region affected for each injury: a stab wound that damages the brachial plexus, a pelvic fracture that damages the sacral plexus, and a neck injury that damages the phrenic nerve. Name the spinal nerves that form each plexus.","k":[{"pt":"Brachial plexus: C5 to C8 and T1","why":"Name the plexus first, then the region it serves."},{"pt":"Weakness and sensory loss across the shoulder and the whole upper limb on that side","why":"The brachial plexus serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb."},{"pt":"Sacral plexus: L4 to L5 and S1 to S4","why":"It is built from the lower lumbar and the sacral spinal nerves."},{"pt":"Weakness and sensory loss of the buttock, the back of the thigh, and the leg and foot","why":"The sciatic, gluteal, and pudendal nerves all leave this plexus."},{"pt":"Phrenic nerve: from the cervical plexus, C3 to C5","why":"\"C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive\" is the reminder."},{"pt":"The diaphragm on that side is weak and breathing is affected","why":"The phrenic nerve is the only motor supply to the diaphragm."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-nerves-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[191,188],"cids":["w8-nerve-plexuses","w8-spinal-nerves-rami"],"t":"One root or a whole plexus","p":"A herniated disc presses on one spinal nerve root in the lower back. Another patient's whole lumbar plexus is crushed. Predict which one loses more function and explain from how a plexus is built.","k":[{"pt":"In a plexus, the anterior rami of neighboring spinal nerves join and regroup","why":"Axons are sorted and leave the plexus in named nerves."},{"pt":"Each nerve leaving a plexus carries axons from several spinal nerves","why":"So any one body region gets axons from more than one spinal nerve."},{"pt":"One damaged root weakens a region but rarely wipes out its function","why":"The other spinal nerves feeding that region still get through."},{"pt":"Plexus damage takes out axons from several spinal nerves at once, so a whole region can be lost","why":"The plexus patient loses more."},{"pt":"The lumbar plexus, L1 to L4, serves the lower abdominal wall, the external genitals, and the front and inner thigh","why":"That is the region the crushed plexus patient loses."},{"pt":"The loss is on the same side as the injury","why":"A plexus serves its own side of the body."}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-nerves-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[185,188],"cids":["w8-cord-roots","w8-spinal-nerves-rami"],"t":"A band of rash","p":"A patient has a painful rash in a band around one side of the chest, following one rib. Explain where the virus was hiding, why the rash stays in one strip on one side, and which branch of the spinal nerve serves that skin.","k":[{"pt":"The varicella zoster virus stays dormant in a posterior root ganglion","why":"This is the chickenpox virus, and it can hide there for decades before it reactivates as shingles."},{"pt":"That ganglion holds the sensory cell bodies for one spinal nerve","why":"Each posterior root has its own ganglion for its own spinal nerve."},{"pt":"The rash follows the strip of skin served by that one spinal nerve","why":"The ganglion belongs to one spinal nerve, so only the skin that nerve serves is affected."},{"pt":"It stays on one side because each spinal nerve and ganglion belongs to one side","why":"The nerves come in right and left pairs."},{"pt":"Chest skin along a rib is served by an anterior ramus running as an intercostal nerve, T2 to T12","why":"The thoracic anterior rami do not form plexuses, so each one stays in its own space between ribs."}]}]},{"id":"m5-ans","title":"The autonomic nervous system","mw":"2026-12-07","tr":"2026-12-08","prompts":[{"id":"m5-ans-L1-1","lv":1,"comps":[192],"cids":["w8-ans-pathway"],"t":"Two neurons to the organ","p":"Draw the autonomic motor pathway from the CNS to an organ and label every part. Then draw a somatic motor pathway under it for comparison.","k":[{"pt":"Preganglionic neuron with its cell body in a brainstem nucleus or the lateral gray matter of the cord","why":"Pre means before the ganglion, so this first neuron starts in the CNS."},{"pt":"Its thin, lightly myelinated axon leaving in a cranial nerve or an anterior root","why":"It ends in an autonomic ganglion."},{"pt":"Autonomic ganglion where the synapse happens","why":"An autonomic ganglion holds motor cell bodies and is the place the two neurons meet."},{"pt":"Postganglionic neuron with its cell body in the ganglion and an unmyelinated axon","why":"Post means after the ganglion, and this axon carries the signal the rest of the way."},{"pt":"Effector: cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, or a gland","why":"These are the only effectors the autonomic system controls."},{"pt":"Somatic: one myelinated neuron from the anterior horn or a brainstem motor nucleus straight to skeletal muscle","why":"The somatic system does the whole job with one neuron and no ganglion."}]},{"id":"m5-ans-L1-2","lv":1,"comps":[193,110],"cids":["w8-ans-ganglia-divisions","w5-enteric-plexuses"],"t":"Ganglia and plexuses","p":"List every named autonomic ganglion and plexus you can. Sort them into sympathetic trunk ganglia, prevertebral ganglia, terminal ganglia, and plexuses, and write where each sits.","k":[{"pt":"Sympathetic trunk ganglia in a chain on each side of the vertebral column, from the base of the skull to the coccyx","why":"They are also called paravertebral or chain ganglia, and axons link them into the trunk."},{"pt":"Prevertebral ganglia in front of the vertebral column: celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, aorticorenal, renal","why":"They sit close to the large branches of the abdominal aorta."},{"pt":"Terminal ganglia close to or in the organ wall, including the ciliary, pterygopalatine, submandibular, and otic ganglia in the head","why":"These are the parasympathetic ganglia."},{"pt":"Autonomic plexuses: cardiac, pulmonary, celiac, superior and inferior mesenteric, renal, hypogastric","why":"They are networks of autonomic axons around the large blood vessels."},{"pt":"The celiac plexus is the largest, around the celiac trunk, and holds the celiac ganglia","why":"Some plexuses hold sympathetic ganglia inside them."},{"pt":"Enteric plexuses: myenteric in the muscularis externa and submucosal in the submucosa","why":"These networks live in the wall of the digestive tract itself."}]},{"id":"m5-ans-L2-1","lv":2,"comps":[193],"cids":["w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"],"t":"Two autonomic outflows","p":"Draw the body and mark where the sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers leave the central nervous system. Name the ganglia each uses and write the length of the fibers.","k":[{"pt":"Sympathetic: thoracolumbar, from the lateral horns of T1 to L2","why":"The other name maps the origin."},{"pt":"Sympathetic ganglia close to the cord: the sympathetic trunk ganglia and the prevertebral ganglia","why":"The ganglia follow the origin, staying near the spinal cord."},{"pt":"Sympathetic: short preganglionic and long postganglionic fibers","why":"A ganglion near the cord leaves a long way still to go to the organ."},{"pt":"Parasympathetic: craniosacral, from the nuclei of III, VII, IX, and X and the S2 to S4 segments","why":"Cranio for the brainstem and sacral for S2 to S4."},{"pt":"Parasympathetic: terminal ganglia near or in the organ, with long preganglionic and short postganglionic fibers","why":"A ganglion at the organ means the first fiber does most of the traveling."},{"pt":"The vagus carries most of the parasympathetic supply to the thorax and abdomen, and the pelvic splanchnic nerves serve the pelvic organs","why":"The vagus's long reach is why one nerve can affect so many organs."}]},{"id":"m5-ans-L2-2","lv":2,"comps":[192,173],"cids":["w8-ans-pathway","w8-ns-organization"],"t":"Somatic or autonomic","p":"Compare the somatic and autonomic motor systems: their effectors, control, number of motor neurons, where the cell bodies sit, and myelin.","k":[{"pt":"Somatic effector is skeletal muscle, and autonomic effectors are cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands","why":"The effector is what decides which system is in charge."},{"pt":"Somatic control is voluntary and normally conscious, and autonomic control is involuntary","why":"You decide to move a skeletal muscle, but not to change your heart rate."},{"pt":"Somatic uses one motor neuron, and autonomic uses two with a synapse in a ganglion","why":"The ganglion is the key structural difference."},{"pt":"Somatic cell bodies sit in the anterior horn or a brainstem motor nucleus","why":"From there one axon runs all the way to the muscle."},{"pt":"Autonomic: first cell body in the brainstem or cord, second in the ganglion","why":"The pathway is split into two neurons at the ganglion."},{"pt":"Somatic axon is myelinated, and the autonomic axons are lightly myelinated then unmyelinated","why":"The first autonomic axon is thin with little myelin, and the second has none."},{"pt":"Somatic always excites, while autonomic can excite or inhibit","why":"That is why an organ can be pushed either way by its autonomic supply."}]},{"id":"m5-ans-L2-3","lv":2,"comps":[110,193],"cids":["w5-enteric-plexuses","w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"],"t":"Nerves in the gut wall","p":"Draw the wall of the gut and place the two enteric plexuses in their layers. Write what each one mainly controls and how the ANS connects to them.","k":[{"pt":"Submucosal plexus in the submucosa, just deep to the mucosa","why":"It sits next to the lining it controls."},{"pt":"The submucosal plexus mainly controls secretions and blood flow to the mucosa","why":"Its position right next to the mucosal glands fits its job."},{"pt":"Myenteric plexus in the muscularis externa, between the circular and longitudinal layers","why":"It sits right between the two muscle layers it runs."},{"pt":"The myenteric plexus mainly controls the contractions that move food along","why":"Its position between the muscle layers fits that job."},{"pt":"Together they run from the esophagus to the anus with about 100 million neurons and can work on their own","why":"That independence is why the enteric system is called the brain of the gut."},{"pt":"The ANS adjusts them, with parasympathetic fibers mostly from the vagus plus sympathetic fibers","why":"The plexuses can run alone, but the ANS connects to them and adjusts what they do."}]},{"id":"m5-ans-L3-1","lv":3,"comps":[193,188],"cids":["w8-ans-ganglia-divisions","w8-spinal-nerves-rami"],"t":"To a sweat gland","p":"Trace a sympathetic signal from the spinal cord to a sweat gland in the skin. Name every structure in order and mark where the synapse happens.","k":[{"pt":"Preganglionic cell body in the lateral horn of a segment from T1 to L2","why":"Sympathetic neurons only start in the thoracolumbar segments."},{"pt":"Out through the anterior root into the spinal nerve","why":"The anterior root carries motor axons, including autonomic ones, out of the cord."},{"pt":"Through the white ramus communicans into the sympathetic trunk","why":"The white ramus carries myelinated preganglionic axons in."},{"pt":"Synapse in a sympathetic trunk ganglion","why":"This is where the preganglionic neuron meets the postganglionic neuron."},{"pt":"Postganglionic axon back through the gray ramus communicans to the spinal nerve","why":"The gray ramus carries unmyelinated axons back out, and every spinal nerve has one."},{"pt":"Out in a ramus to the sweat gland","why":"The axon rides the posterior or anterior ramus to the skin it serves."}]},{"id":"m5-ans-L3-2","lv":3,"comps":[193,131],"cids":["w8-ans-ganglia-divisions","w6-adrenal"],"t":"To the gut, and the adrenal medulla","p":"Trace a sympathetic signal from the spinal cord to the small intestine and point out where it differs from the path to a sweat gland. Then explain why the adrenal medulla is described as working like a sympathetic ganglion.","k":[{"pt":"Lateral horn, anterior root, and white ramus into the trunk, the same as the sweat gland route","why":"Every sympathetic preganglionic axon leaves the cord the same way."},{"pt":"The axon passes through the trunk ganglion without synapsing","why":"This is the first difference from the sweat gland route."},{"pt":"It travels in a splanchnic nerve to a prevertebral ganglion such as the celiac or superior mesenteric","why":"Prevertebral ganglia sit in front of the vertebral column near the big branches of the aorta."},{"pt":"It synapses there, and the postganglionic axon follows the arteries to the organ","why":"The synapse is out in front of the spine instead of in the chain."},{"pt":"Adrenal medulla chromaffin cells are modified sympathetic neurons with no axons or dendrites","why":"The medulla develops from the neural crest, the same tissue that forms the sympathetic ganglia."},{"pt":"They are supplied by preganglionic sympathetic fibers and release their product straight into the blood","why":"A preganglionic fiber ending on them is just what happens in a ganglion, which is why the medulla works like one."}]},{"id":"m5-ans-L3-3","lv":3,"comps":[192,185],"cids":["w8-ans-pathway","w8-cord-roots"],"t":"A labeling mistake","p":"A student labels the swelling on the posterior root of a spinal nerve as the place where autonomic neurons synapse. Explain what is wrong by comparing what each kind of ganglion holds and whether a synapse happens there.","k":[{"pt":"The swelling on the posterior root is the posterior root ganglion","why":"It sits in or beside the intervertebral foramen on the sensory root."},{"pt":"It holds the cell bodies of sensory neurons","why":"These unipolar neurons carry signals into the cord."},{"pt":"There are no synapses in a posterior root ganglion","why":"The signal passes the sensory cell body on its way in without changing neurons."},{"pt":"An autonomic ganglion holds motor cell bodies, the postganglionic neurons","why":"It sits on the motor pathway, not the sensory one."},{"pt":"The synapse happens in the autonomic ganglion, where the preganglionic axon meets the postganglionic neuron","why":"That synapse is the whole reason the autonomic pathway has two neurons."},{"pt":"Both are called ganglia because both are clusters of cell bodies outside the CNS","why":"The shared name is what makes them easy to mix up."}]}]}]}]};
